= Thermodynamic system
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A thermodynamic system is the part of the physical world selected for an energy and matter balance. Its boundary determines which transfers count as <heat>, <work>, or transported matter.

= Simple compressible system
{parent=Thermodynamic system}

A <thermodynamic system> for which the reversible mechanical <work> is entirely <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> work. At fixed composition its <internal energy> is specified by <entropy> and <volume (thermodynamics)>; additional work modes require additional state variables.

= Thermodynamic equilibrium
{parent=Thermodynamic system}
{wiki}

A <thermodynamic system> is in thermodynamic equilibrium when its macroscopic state has no spontaneous tendency to change through any exchange allowed by its constraints. For an <isolated system>, stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> maximizes <entropy> over the accessible states at fixed total <energy> and conserved material inventories. A wall can forbid an exchange, so always specify what can cross or move. Thermal, mechanical and chemical conditions answer different questions about that same constrained state. <Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>{ref}

For example, two compartments separated by a rigid, impermeable wall that transmits <heat> can reach <thermal equilibrium> while retaining different <pressures> and compositions. Allowing an unloaded piston to move adds a <mechanical equilibrium> condition. Allowing one <chemical species> to cross adds <chemical potential equality for particle exchange>; allowing a <chemical reaction> adds its <chemical equilibrium> condition. A fixed barrier can therefore support a constrained <thermodynamic equilibrium> that ceases to be an equilibrium when the barrier is removed.

Uniformity is not the definition of <thermodynamic equilibrium>. An <isothermal atmosphere> in <hydrostatic equilibrium> can have a common <temperature> while its <pressure> and <mass density> vary with height: the <pressure gradient> balances <gravity>. In an external field, the exchange condition must include the relevant potential <energy>; for charged matter this leads to the <electrochemical potential>. Relativistic gravitational corrections to <thermal equilibrium> are described separately by the <Tolman temperature law>.

A <steady state> only says that the macroscopic description is constant in time. A bar maintained between hot and cold <thermal reservoirs> has steady <heat flux> and produces <entropy>, so it is not in <thermodynamic equilibrium>. Even matching <temperatures> at one perfect-contact interface can coexist with <heat transfer> through the surrounding material; matching those two local values does not establish equilibrium of the whole apparatus. See <thermal conduction> for that spatial transport problem.

The useful simplification is that equilibrium conditions predict an endpoint without computing every collision or flow during relaxation. The <first law of thermodynamics> supplies the conserved quantities, and the <second law of thermodynamics> supplies the direction of change. For a system held at fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>, the corresponding criterion is minimization of <Helmholtz free energy>; at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, it is minimization of <Gibbs free energy>, with the material and exchange constraints still imposed.

= Mechanical equilibrium
{parent=Thermodynamic equilibrium}
{wiki}

Mechanical equilibrium requires <force balance> for the mechanical displacements that are allowed. Two uniform fluids separated by a freely moving, unloaded, frictionless piston with equal face <areas> require equal <pressures>. A supported piston can sustain a <pressure> difference, and a fluid in <gravity> instead obeys <hydrostatic equilibrium>. For an upward height coordinate $z$, that balance is $dp/dz=-\rho g$, where $\rho$ is <mass density> and $g$ is the downward gravitational acceleration. Mechanical equilibrium alone does not establish <thermal equilibrium> or <chemical equilibrium>.

= Closed system
{parent=Thermodynamic system}

A closed thermodynamic system exchanges no matter with its surroundings, but can exchange <energy> through <heat> or <work>. A sealed cylinder with a moving piston is a typical idealized example.

= Isolated system
{parent=Thermodynamic system}

An isolated thermodynamic system exchanges neither matter nor <energy> with its surroundings. Its total <internal energy> is fixed, while the <second law of thermodynamics> constrains its total <entropy>.

= Quasistatic process
{parent=Thermodynamics}

= Quasi-static process
{synonym}

A quasistatic process is an ideal limit in which a changing <thermodynamic system> passes through a sequence of states arbitrarily close to <thermodynamic equilibrium>. Its intermediate <pressure>, <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)> are therefore well defined and obey its <equation of state>. Driving must be slow compared with the system's own equilibration, rather than merely taking a long time on a clock. A <reversible thermodynamic process> additionally allows both system and surroundings to be restored without net change; <friction> can produce <entropy> even in a <quasistatic process>.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§§4.2–4.3: quasi-static and reversible processes]{ref}

For a computed example, take a fixed <amount of substance> $n$ of <ideal gas> in a sealed cylinder with a frictionless piston, in contact with a <heat bath> at absolute <temperature> $T$. Reduce the external load in infinitesimal steps so that the piston expands from $V_0$ to $2V_0$. In the reversible limit, the gas <pressure> exceeds the external <pressure> by an infinitesimal amount during expansion, and the <heat transfer> occurs across an infinitesimal <temperature> difference. Exact <mechanical equilibrium> would give no driving <force>; the limiting imbalance supplies motion while preserving equilibrium intermediate states.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.4 Thermodynamic Processes>[§3.4: a piston unloaded in infinitesimal steps]{ref}

Along this reversible <isothermal expansion>, the <ideal gas law> gives
$$
p(V)=\frac{nRT}{V},\qquad nRT=1\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad V_0=1\,\mathrm L=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^3},\qquad
p_0=\frac{nRT}{V_0}=1\,\mathrm{MPa}.
$$
Here $R$ is the <molar gas constant>; specifying $nRT$ fixes an <energy> scale, without choosing $n$ and $T$ separately. The initial and final equilibrium states are $(V_0,p_0,T)$ and $(2V_0,p_0/2,T)$. Count <work> delivered to the surroundings as positive. The reversible <work> is the shaded <area> beneath the <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> curve:
$$
W_{\rm out,rev}=\int_{V_0}^{2V_0}p(V)\,dV
=nRT\ln2\approx0.693147\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
The <integral> of $1/V$ supplies the <natural logarithm>. Since <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>, $\Delta U=0$; the <first law of thermodynamics>, $\Delta U=Q_{\rm in}-W_{\rm out}$, gives $Q_{\rm in,rev}=0.693147\,\mathrm{kJ}$ to the displayed precision. The <heat bath> replaces the <energy> delivered as <work>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.2 Work, Heat, and Internal Energy>[§3.2: pressure-volume work and the isothermal integral]{ref}

\Image[quasistatic-isothermal-pv-work.png]
{title=Reversible <isothermal expansion>: <work> is the <area> beneath the equilibrium <pressure> curve}
{description=Fixed <amount of substance> of <ideal gas>, with $nRT=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $V_0=1\,\mathrm L$. The blue curve gives every intermediate equilibrium state; its shaded <area> is $0.693147\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The arrow indicates expansion, without specifying a time dependence.}

Now start from the same initial state, but abruptly lower the external <pressure> to the constant value $p_{\rm ext}=p_0/2=0.5\,\mathrm{MPa}$. There is a finite initial <pressure> imbalance, so this is an irreversible expansion rather than a <quasistatic process>. Assume the piston begins and ends at rest, has no net change in stored <energy>, and has no external <friction>; relaxation occurs within the <gas> and its <heat bath>. After motion has settled and the <heat bath> has restored <temperature> $T$, <mechanical equilibrium> and the <ideal gas law> give $V_f=nRT/p_{\rm ext}=2V_0$. The <work> delivered to the constant load is
$$
W_{\rm out,load}=p_{\rm ext}(V_f-V_0)
=(0.5\,\mathrm{MPa})(1\,\mathrm L)=0.5\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad
\Delta U=0,\qquad Q_{\rm in,load}=0.5\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
This <work> follows directly from load <force> times piston <displacement>, using $dV=A\,dx$ for piston <area> $A$. Intermediate gas states need not have a single equilibrium <pressure> or <temperature>. In particular, neither $p(V)=nRT/V$ at the bath <temperature> nor $p(V)=p_{\rm ext}$ describes the actual gas during this irreversible motion. The rectangle below plots the known external load; its upper edge is not an equilibrium gas path.

\Image[reversible-versus-constant-load-work.png]
{title=The same equilibrium endpoints give different <work> for reversible and constant-load expansion}
{description=The blue curve is the reversible <ideal gas> path. The dashed orange line is constant external <pressure>, whose rectangle gives $0.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of load <work>. The additional blue shading is $0.193147\,\mathrm{kJ}$ available in the reversible case. Black points mark the common gas endpoints; the open orange point marks the initial external load, and the dotted gap indicates the initial imbalance. No irreversible internal gas path is drawn.}

The <entropy> accounting makes the difference precise. Because <entropy> is a <state function>, both expansions have the same gas <entropy> change, computed along the reversible <isothermal expansion>:
$$
\Delta S_{\rm gas}=\int_{V_0}^{2V_0}\frac{\delta Q_{\rm rev}}{T}=nR\ln2.
$$
The <thermal reservoir> stays at $T$ and loses <heat> $Q_{\rm in}=W_{\rm out}$, so $\Delta S_{\rm bath}=-W_{\rm out}/T$. Assume the ideal load stores <work> without an <entropy> change. The combined <entropy production> is therefore
$$
\Delta S_{\rm total}=nR\ln2-\frac{W_{\rm out}}{T}
=\begin{cases}
0,&\text{reversible expansion},\\
nR(\ln2-1/2)>0,&\text{constant external load}.
\end{cases}
$$
No numerical <entropy> unit is implied by $nRT=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ alone: $nR$ or $T$ must also be specified. The lost <work> opportunity is $T\Delta S_{\rm total}=0.193147\,\mathrm{kJ}$ for this comparison. This calculation uses equilibrium endpoints and the reservoir's <temperature>, without assigning equilibrium intermediate states to the irreversible gas. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-6-entropy[OpenStax, §4.6: entropy as a state function and reversible reference paths]{ref}

For another boundary choice, let the same <ideal gas> expand into an initially evacuated compartment of <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_0$ inside a rigid, insulated vessel of total <volume (thermodynamics)> $2V_0$. Removing the partition causes <free expansion>: no load is moved and no <heat> crosses the outer boundary, so $W_{\rm out}=Q_{\rm in}=0$ and $\Delta U=0$. After equilibration the <temperature> is again $T$, the <pressure> is $p_0/2$, and the gas <entropy> has increased by $nR\ln2$. The outer vessel has fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>; the gas's accessible <volume (thermodynamics)> doubles. Restoring the initial gas state requires compression and removal of <heat>, changing the surroundings. Equal endpoints do not make <free expansion> reversible. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-1-reversible-and-irreversible-processes[OpenStax, §4.1: insulated free expansion and restoration]{ref}

A slowly moving piston with dry <friction> supplies a different caution: its gas can remain arbitrarily close to <thermodynamic equilibrium>, yet rubbing still converts <work> into <heat> and produces <entropy>. If the frictional <force> has a nonzero slow-motion limit, its dissipated <energy> over a fixed travel does not vanish with speed. Reversing the motion reverses <friction>, so infinitesimally changing the external load cannot retrace the same operation and restore the surroundings. The <heat capacity> calculations additionally exclude <friction> and other dissipative <work>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.4 Thermodynamic Processes>[§3.4: a quasi-static piston with friction]{ref}

The usefulness of a <quasistatic process> is that it replaces detailed motion by a readable sequence of equilibrium states. An <equation of state> and an <integral> then turn a <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> drawing into an <energy> budget. The beautiful distinction is that <internal energy> and <entropy> depend on the endpoints, while <heat> and <work> record how the change was performed. Reversible benchmarks expose how much <work> an irreversible device sacrifices, while <entropy production> explains why simply making it slower need not recover that opportunity.

= State function
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A state function depends on the current equilibrium state, rather than the path used to reach it. <Internal energy> and <entropy> are state functions; transferred <heat> and <work> depend on the process.

= Heat engine
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A heat engine uses a repeating <thermodynamic cycle> to turn part of an incoming <heat> transfer into net outgoing <work>. In the two-<thermal reservoir> model, the hot <thermal reservoir> supplies <heat> $Q_h>0$ at fixed <temperature> $T_h$, and the cold <thermal reservoir> receives <heat> of magnitude $Q_c>0$ at fixed <temperature> $T_c<T_h$. A <Stirling engine> makes the repeated motion visible; the abstract <energy> budget applies independently of its particular mechanism.<Thermodynamics and Propulsion/3.2 Generalized Representation of Thermodynamic Cycles>[§3.2, Figure 3.3: two reservoirs and net work output]{ref}

Choose the entire cyclic device as a <closed system>, with no net change in bulk <kinetic energy> or <potential energy>. Count <heat> entering as positive and net <work> delivered as positive, as in the <first law of thermodynamics>. Thus $Q_h$ enters with a plus sign and $Q_c$, although a positive magnitude, enters with a minus sign. Assume these two <heat> transfers and the net <work> are the only transfers across the boundary. Over one complete <thermodynamic cycle>, all stored <energy>, including <internal energy> $U$, returns to its starting value:
$$
\Delta U=Q_h-Q_c-W=0,\qquad W=Q_h-Q_c.
$$
The device does not accumulate <heat> from cycle to cycle. More precisely, <heat> is a transfer, while <internal energy> is what can be stored; even a component that temporarily stores <internal energy> must return to its initial state for this boundary to be cyclic.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.2 Work, Heat, and Internal Energy>[§3.2: heat, work and changes in stored internal energy]{ref}

For engine operation, $Q_h>Q_c$ and $W>0$. Its <thermal efficiency> is
$$
\eta=\frac{W}{Q_h}=1-\frac{Q_c}{Q_h}.
$$
Use net <work>, after subtracting any <work> supplied during compression or required by auxiliaries included in the chosen device. For periodic operation, average <power> satisfies $P_{\rm out}=\dot Q_h-\dot Q_c$ and $\eta=P_{\rm out}/\dot Q_h$, with the rates averaged over complete <thermodynamic cycles>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/4.2 Heat Engines>[§4.2, equations 4.1–4.2: heat-engine balance and efficiency]{ref}

For a stipulated model at $T_h=600\,\mathrm K$ and $T_c=300\,\mathrm K$, let $Q_h=1000\,\mathrm J$ and $Q_c=600\,\mathrm J$ per <thermodynamic cycle>. Then $W=400\,\mathrm J$ and $\eta=2/5=40\%$. At two <thermodynamic cycles> per second, these become $2000\,\mathrm W$ incoming <heat transfer>, $1200\,\mathrm W$ outgoing <heat transfer>, and $800\,\mathrm W$ net <power>. These are constructed values, not measurements.

\Image[heat-engine-cyclic-energy-flows.png]
{title=Two-<thermal reservoir> <heat engine>: $1000\,\mathrm J$ in, $400\,\mathrm J$ of net <work>, and $600\,\mathrm J$ rejected}
{description=Illustrative transfers per complete <thermodynamic cycle>, with positive magnitudes labeled on the arrows. The <closed system> returns to its initial state. Arrow widths represent the <energy> split; the <thermal reservoirs> remain at $600\,\mathrm K$ and $300\,\mathrm K$.}

Different signs describe different tasks. With the same directions of <heat transfer>, $Q_h=Q_c$ gives $W=0$: a device transmitting <heat> without net <work> output. If $Q_c>Q_h$, net <work> is supplied to that device. A <refrigerator> or <heat pump> instead extracts <heat> from the cold side and delivers <heat> to the hot side, using positive input <work> $W_{\rm in}$ and the balance $Q_h=Q_c+W_{\rm in}$. Their useful output is <heat transfer>, measured by a <coefficient of performance>. Finally, a one-time <isothermal expansion> can have $Q=W$ and $\Delta U=0$ while leaving the <volume (thermodynamics)> changed: it is not a complete <thermodynamic cycle>. A warming device also fails the cyclic assumption, and a fuel-consuming device requires an accounting of matter crossing its boundary.

For two fixed <thermal reservoirs> at positive <thermodynamic temperatures>, the <second law of thermodynamics> strengthens the <energy> balance to
$$
\eta\le\eta_C=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}.
$$
Equality requires the overall operation to be a <reversible thermodynamic process>, including its <heat transfer> to the <thermal reservoirs>. The <Carnot cycle> explains how the reversible benchmark is constructed, and <Carnot's theorem> explains its universality and the <entropy production> penalty. Use <kelvin> values, never ratios of <degree Celsius> readings. The example above lies below the $50\%$ bound: its total <entropy production> is $-1000/600+600/300=1/3\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$ per <thermodynamic cycle>.

\Image[heat-engine-efficiency-temperature-model.png]
{title=<Thermal efficiency> versus hot <thermal reservoir> <temperature> at $T_c=300\,\mathrm K$}
{description=The upper curve is the reversible bound in <Carnot's theorem>. The lower curve is the stipulated model $\eta=0.8\eta_C$, not experimental data or a universal engine law. At $T_h=600\,\mathrm K$, the curves give $50\%$ and $40\%$, matching the <energy> budget above. The endpoint $T_h=T_c$ is a zero-output limit.}

This is useful before building a machine: warmer cooling surroundings reduce the possible <thermal efficiency>, even if the mechanism is unchanged. A small observed <thermal efficiency> does not by itself test the <second law of thermodynamics>; <heat> leaks, <friction>, incomplete recovery of <work>, and the measurement boundary can all reduce the reported ratio. A meaningful comparison requires the actual <thermal reservoir> <temperatures>, matched <heat> and net <work> measurements, and the hypotheses of <Carnot's theorem>. Finite bodies whose <temperatures> change are treated separately there.

The practical question already appears in the 1824 <Réflexions sur la puissance motrice du feu et sur les machines propres à développer cette puissance by Sadi Carnot>: pp. 6–8 ask whether machines driven by <heat> have a fundamental limit and whether changing the working substance can improve their output. Pages 9–11 describe heating and cooling in real machines, but use the historical <caloric theory>, rather than the modern <first law of thermodynamics> balance. The historical discussion under <Carnot's theorem> explains that distinction.

= Stirling engine
{c}
{parent=Heat engine}
{wiki}

A Stirling engine repeatedly heats and cools a confined <gas>, whose expansion and compression drive a moving mechanical load. This makes it a particularly accessible example of a <heat engine>: both the repeating motion and the two regions exchanging <heat> can be observed.

In the published <Demonstration experiments with a Stirling engine by Christopher G Deacon and others>, an electrical heater warms one end of a cylinder and flowing <water> cools the other at $12\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. A moving displacer shuttles <gas> through <copper> wool that temporarily stores and returns <internal energy>. A <laser> spot traces a closed <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> path on a screen: mechanical motion controls one coordinate, <pressure> the other. Calibrating both scales converts the enclosed <area> to <work> per <thermodynamic cycle>; multiplying by rotation <frequency> gives gas-cycle <power>. At $97\,\mathrm W$ heater input, the paper reports $4.4\,\mathrm W$ gas-cycle <power>, while a separate <Prony brake> measures $3.4\,\mathrm W$ shaft output. The cold-side <temperature> is controlled and two higher heater settings are compared. The gas has a nonuniform <temperature>, and heater <energy> also escapes to the room: neither output/input ratio measures $W/Q_h$ for an ideal two-<thermal reservoir> engine. These observations demonstrate <work> production and losses, not a direct measurement of the bound in <Carnot's theorem>. The inspected apparatus, readouts and results are on pp. 180–182, Figures 1–3 and Table 1.

= Prony brake
{c}
{parent=Stirling engine}
{wiki}

A Prony brake measures shaft <power> using a resisting band: its <force> difference gives <torque> $\tau=r(F_1-F_2)$, and rotation gives $P=\tau\omega$, where $\omega$ is <angular velocity>. The <Stirling engine> experiment above uses this independent load measurement to distinguish shaft output from gas-cycle <work>.

= Heat-engine calorimetry
{parent=Heat engine}

Heat-engine calorimetry measures <heat> transfers so that observed <work> can be compared with the correct <energy> input. Measuring motion alone cannot supply the denominator of <thermal efficiency>.

The institutional <Laboratory 16: Stirling Engine> describes a modern teaching arrangement: an <ethanol> lamp drives a <Stirling engine> coupled to an electrical output device, which supplies a <resistor> immersed in <water> in a <calorimeter>. The prescribed readouts include fuel <mass> consumed, the <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> loop, electrical readings, and <water> <temperature>. The sequence separates fuel input, gas-cycle <work>, electrical output, and <Joule heating> of the <water>. Warming of the <water> is the final useful output; it is not the engine's rejected $Q_c$. The handout distinguishes warm-up from steady operation and asks students to compare the final warming with direct heating by the lamp. It gives a procedure, not a table of measured efficiencies. Its pp. 1–3 therefore establish an apparatus and measurement design without supplying a new experimental result.

For a proposed calibration, use a known input of electrical <work> to determine the effective <heat capacity> $C_{\rm eff}$ of the <water>, vessel and immersed parts; stir to make the measured <temperature> representative. A matched run with no input estimates exchange with the room. For constant $C_{\rm eff}$ and no <phase transition>, the resulting model is
$$
E_{\rm in}=C_{\rm eff}(T_f-T_i)+Q_{\rm leak,out},
$$
where $Q_{\rm leak,out}$ is the signed net <heat> lost to the room during the measurement. Here $E_{\rm in}$ is supplied electrical <work> when the <resistor> lies inside the calibration boundary; for a separate external <heat> input it is the received <heat>. These are proposed controls, not controls reported in that handout. Slow cooling, changing material properties and imperfectly matched room exchange limit this inference; the approach to <thermal equilibrium> and <heat capacity> matter as much as the thermometer reading.

To measure the actual engine-input $Q_h$, one must instrument its hot-side transfer separately, or close a calibrated <energy> balance around a specified heater boundary. A heater's electrical <work> includes changes in its stored <internal energy> and <heat transfer> that bypasses the engine. At periodic operation a cold-side flowing-<water> measurement can instead estimate $\dot Q_c=\dot m c_p(T_{\rm out}-T_{\rm in})$, if <mass flow rate> $\dot m$ and <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_p$ are known, with negligible storage and other <heat> exchange in that measurement boundary. It measures only the rejection captured by that stream. Include all remaining transfers before using the residual to infer $\dot Q_h=P_{\rm out}+\dot Q_c$. This measurement discipline makes a comparison with <Carnot's theorem> meaningful.

= Calorimetry
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Calorimetry measures <heat> transfers. A <calorimeter>, a calibrated <heat capacity> and an accounting of unwanted <heat transfer> connect an observed <temperature> change to an <energy> balance, as in <heat-engine calorimetry> and <heat-pump calorimetry>.

The central idea is to measure a response that is easier to observe than the transfer itself: a known <heat capacity> turns a <temperature> change into an <energy> estimate. <Mixing calorimetry> can determine an unknown <specific heat capacity>, while a <chemical reaction> can be studied by measuring the response of its surroundings. The useful observable is a change in <temperature>, together with a stated <thermodynamic system> boundary; a hot object does not contain a separately conserved store of <heat>.

For a <closed system> with unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, let $Q_{\rm in}$ be net inward <heat> and $W_{\rm on}$ net inward <work>. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
\Delta U=Q_{\rm in}+W_{\rm on}.
$$
If the boundary includes a hot sample, receiving <water> and the participating vessel, their mutual <heat transfer> is internal. With no external input, the total storage change is minus the signed outward <heat> leakage, not the positive amount transferred from sample to <water>. A <calorimeter calibration> with an immersed <resistor> instead supplies electrical <work> across this boundary; <Joule heating> converts it into <internal energy>. Choosing only the receiving <water> as the boundary changes which transfers count as external.

The mechanical constraint decides which storage quantity the readout represents. For a <closed system> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, with no other <work>, $Q_V=\Delta U$. At constant external <pressure>, with only expansion <work> $p\Delta V$, $Q_p=\Delta H$, where $H=U+pV$ is <enthalpy>. Thus the corresponding warming balances use <heat capacity at constant volume> or <heat capacity at constant pressure>. Electrical or shaft <work> must be included separately when present. These endpoint balances do not require the actual exchange to be a <reversible thermodynamic process>.

A covered cup open to atmospheric <pressure> approximately realizes the second constraint when escaping material is negligible. A sealed rigid reaction vessel realizes the first; its surrounding <water>, vessel and immersed parts form the calibrated receiver. The reaction's outward <heat> equals the receiver's gain only after losses and ignition input have been accounted for. The distinction concerns the reaction boundary, even if its receiver is open to atmospheric <pressure>.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/5.2 Calorimetry>[§5.2, paragraph beginning “If the amount of heat absorbed by a calorimeter”, and Figure 5.17: vessel storage, constant-pressure versus constant-volume apparatus and calibration]{ref}

A historical quantitative result appears in <James Prescott Joule>'s <On the Mechanical Equivalent of Heat> (1850): Table IX gives $772.692$ foot-pounds of mechanical <work> per warming of one pound of <water> by one degree Fahrenheit, with weighing reduced to vacuum. His final rounded statement specifies <water> between $55$ and $60$ degrees Fahrenheit. He acknowledged residual vibration and sound as possible upward bias. This is an original reported conversion result, not the definition of the modern <joule>; the apparatus and resting controls are described in <Joule's paddle-wheel experiment>.<On the Mechanical Equivalent of Heat by James Prescott Joule>[p. 82, Table IX and concluding paragraphs: water-series equivalent and residual losses]{ref}

= Mixing calorimetry
{parent=Calorimetry}
{title2=$m_xc_x(T_x-T_f)=(m_wc_w+C_{\rm cal})(T_f-T_w)+Q_{\rm loss,out}$}

Mixing calorimetry determines <heat capacities> by bringing bodies at different <temperatures> into thermal contact. The striking feature is that the final <temperature> is a <weighted arithmetic mean> with <heat capacities> as weights, rather than weights given by <mass>. The vessel participates in the exchange even though it is not the sample being studied.

For a hot nonreacting sample of <mass> $m_x$, initial <temperature> $T_x$ and unknown <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_x$, let receiving <water> have <mass> $m_w$, initial <temperature> $T_w$ and known <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_w$. Let the vessel and immersed parts start at $T_w$ and have combined <heat capacity> $C_{\rm cal}$, excluding that <water>. Assume a common final <temperature> $T_f$, approximately constant <pressure> and <heat capacities>, no <phase transition>, no <chemical reaction> and negligible external nonexpansion <work>. For signed outward <heat> loss $Q_{\rm loss,out}$, the combined <enthalpy> balance is
$$
m_xc_x(T_f-T_x)+(m_wc_w+C_{\rm cal})(T_f-T_w)=-Q_{\rm loss,out}.
$$
Hence
$$
c_x=\frac{(m_wc_w+C_{\rm cal})(T_f-T_w)+Q_{\rm loss,out}}{m_x(T_x-T_f)}.
$$
The loss is positive if the room receives net <heat>, negative if the room supplies it. If the vessel starts at a different <temperature> $T_{{\rm cal},i}$, replace its warming term by $C_{\rm cal}(T_f-T_{{\rm cal},i})$; do not assume the vessel shared the <water>'s initial <temperature>. For variable material properties, replace each $mc\Delta T$ by the corresponding <integral> $m\int c_p(T)\,dT$ and each vessel term by $\int C_{\rm cal}(T)\,dT$. The inferred constant $c_x$ then represents an average over the sample's cooling interval.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/5.2 Calorimetry>[Figure 5.14 and Example 5.4: exchange between metal and water and inference of unknown specific heat]{ref}

For a synthetic no-loss example, mix $0.100\,\mathrm{kg}$ of hot <water> at $80\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ with $0.200\,\mathrm{kg}$ of cold <water> at $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ in a vessel initially at $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Stipulate $c_w=4180\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $C_{\rm cal}=100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. The <enthalpy> balance gives
$$
T_f=\frac{(418)(80)+(836+100)(20)}{418+836+100}\,{}^\circ\mathrm C
\approx38.523\,{}^\circ\mathrm C.
$$
The hot <water> releases $17.337\,\mathrm{kJ}$: the cold <water> receives $15.485\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and the vessel $1.852\,\mathrm{kJ}$. Omitting the vessel predicts $40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, despite perfect insulation. These are computed numbers from stipulated properties, not laboratory observations.

\Image[calorimetry-mixing-temperature-energy-balance.png]
{id=calorimetry-mixing-temperature-energy-balance}
{title=The vessel changes the mixing <temperature> by sharing the <heat> transfer}
{description=Original computed no-loss <water> example. Left: transfers sum to the hot portion's released <heat>. Right: increasing the initially cold vessel's <heat capacity> lowers the common final <temperature>; the marked point uses $C_{\rm cal}=100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Each vessel setting is a separate modeled experiment.}

For an unknown-sample example using the same cold <water> and vessel, stipulate $m_x=0.100\,\mathrm{kg}$, $T_x=80\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and $c_x=450\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. With no loss, $T_f\approx22.752\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Inverting the full balance returns $450\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$; ignoring vessel storage returns about $402\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. With a positive outward loss, ignoring that loss also biases the inferred $c_x$ downward under these hot-sample conditions. A small <water> <temperature> rise makes sensor offsets and loss estimates relatively influential; <propagation of uncertainty> must include the measured <temperatures>, <masses> and calibrated vessel term.

= Calorimeter
{parent=Calorimetry}
{wiki}

A calorimeter infers transferred <heat> from a measured change, such as a <temperature> change and a calibrated <heat capacity>. The <heat-engine calorimetry> example requires accounting for the vessel and <heat transfer> to the surroundings as well as the <water>.

A practical receiver has an inner vessel, insulation or an outer shield, a lid, a <thermometer> and a stirrer. Its participating <heat capacity> includes the immersed parts that follow the <water>'s <temperature>. Insulation reduces <heat transfer> to the room; stirring makes a single reading more representative of the contents. These serve different purposes: a well-insulated vessel can still have substantial <heat capacity>, and a uniform <temperature> does not imply zero external <heat transfer>.

The University of Toronto's <Heat Capacity (University of Toronto laboratory handout)> describes a <copper> inner vessel, outer shield, <thermometer> and heated metal block. Weigh the block and receiving <water>, heat the block in a bath, then transfer it quickly into enough <water> to cover it. The observed <temperature> rises, reaches a maximum, then falls. The inferred <specific heat capacity> comes from the block's cooling and the warming of both <water> and vessel. In its cooling exercise the handout deliberately exposes the inner vessel to room air, recommends readings every $15\,\mathrm s$, and uses the later common cooling to estimate the earlier loss. Starting below room <temperature> reduces exchange without guaranteeing cancellation. The handout notes limited time and thermometry for small corrections; it supplies no dataset.<Heat Capacity (University of Toronto laboratory handout)>[pp. 1–3, The Method of Mixtures and Cooling Correction, Figure 1]{ref}

For a proposed modern version, independently perform <thermometer calibration> and <calorimeter calibration>, record bath and room <temperatures>, and keep immersion depth, lid position and stirring rate fixed. Ensure the block actually reaches bath <temperature>; minimize and account for adhering hot bath <water>. Compare repeated transfers with different delays and a reference sample of known <specific heat capacity>. A resting run tests environmental <heat transfer>, while a stirred run with no added sample checks mechanical input. These are proposed controls, not claimed observations from the handout. Sensor delay, changing <heat capacity>, incomplete <thermal equilibrium> and material escaping the boundary limit the resulting estimate.

= Calorimeter calibration
{parent=Calorimeter}
{title2=$C_{\rm eff}=m_wc_w+C_{\rm cal}$}

Calorimeter calibration establishes the response of the actual measurement assembly to a known <energy> input. It determines a participating <heat capacity>, not just the empty vessel's <mass>. For an electrical calibration with an immersed <resistor>, measure <voltage> $V(t)$ across that <resistor> and <electric current> $I(t)$ through it. If that electrical <work> is deposited inside the chosen boundary, its input is
$$
E_{\rm el}=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}V(t)I(t)\,dt
=C_{\rm eff}(T_1-T_0)+Q_{\rm loss,out},\qquad
C_{\rm eff}=m_wc_w+C_{\rm cal}.
$$
For a covered receiver at constant <pressure>, $C_{\rm eff}$ describes <enthalpy> storage; for a rigid fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> assembly, use the matching <heat capacity at constant volume>. Negligible endpoint mechanical storage and no escaping material are assumed. Thus $E_{\rm el}/(T_1-T_0)$ overestimates $C_{\rm eff}$ if outward loss is ignored. Account for lead losses outside the boundary and maintain the same <water> charge and immersed hardware in calibration and sample runs.

An alternative calibration mixes known hot and cold <water> portions. With the vessel initially at the cold portion's <temperature> $T_c$, the <mixing calorimetry> balance gives
$$
C_{\rm cal}=\frac{m_hc_w(T_h-T_f)-Q_{\rm loss,out}}{T_f-T_c}-m_cc_w.
$$
The previous synthetic <water> example returns $100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. The sign matters: attributing room losses to the vessel makes this estimate too large. With both $C_{\rm cal}$ and the loss unknown, one final <temperature> supplies only one equation; separate cooling information or controlled independent runs are needed.

A <calorimeter heat-loss correction> can make the electrical balance quantitative. For a second synthetic example, stipulate $0.100\,\mathrm{kg}$ of <water> with $c_w=4180\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and immersed hardware with $C_{\rm cal}=82\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, so $C_{\rm eff}=500\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Supply $10\,\mathrm W$ for $300\,\mathrm s$, starting at a room <temperature> of $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, with loss coefficient $G=1\,\mathrm{W\,K^{-1}}$. The model below gives $T_1\approx24.512\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, storage $2255.942\,\mathrm J$ and outward loss $744.058\,\mathrm J$. The uncorrected ratio gives about $665\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$; subtracting the loss recovers $500\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$.

\Image[calorimeter-electrical-calibration-cooling-balance.png]
{id=calorimeter-electrical-calibration-cooling-balance}
{title=Electrical input splits into thermal storage and room losses during calibration}
{description=Original synthetic computed traces for the stated constant-<heat capacity>, constant-loss model. The heater switches off at $300\,\mathrm s$; stored <energy> then declines while cumulative outward <heat> increases. The no-loss comparison reaches $26\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. No points represent experimental measurements.}

= Calorimeter heat-loss correction
{parent=Calorimeter}
{title2=$Q_{\rm loss,out}=G\int(T-T_a)\,dt$}

A calorimeter heat-loss correction estimates the signed <heat> exchanged with the room during the measurement. Under <Newton's law of cooling>, a constant room <temperature> $T_a$ and a single representative assembly <temperature> $T$ give the model
$$
C_{\rm eff}\frac{dT}{dt}=P(t)-G(T-T_a),\qquad
Q_{\rm loss,out}=G\int_{t_0}^{t_1}(T(t)-T_a)\,dt.
$$
Here $P(t)$ is deposited input <power>, and $G$ is the apparatus loss coefficient in $\mathrm{W\,K^{-1}}$. After input ceases, the <temperature> excess shows <exponential decay> with <time constant> $\tau=C_{\rm eff}/G$:
$$
T(t)-T_a=(T(t_1)-T_a)e^{-(t-t_1)/\tau}.
$$
A cooling trace determines the ratio $G/C_{\rm eff}$, not the two factors separately. Combining the cooling trace with a known electrical <energy> input supplies the missing information. For constant input $P_0$, starting at $T_a$, the heating solution is $T(t)-T_a=(P_0/G)(1-e^{-t/\tau})$; these solutions generate the calibration figure.

For <mixing calorimetry>, infer cooling behavior only after the sample, <water> and vessel approach a common <temperature>. Applying a single-body cooling model while the sample remains much hotter than the <water> can misestimate the earlier loss. The Toronto handout's area correction assumes the same loss mechanism represents both the mixing interval and subsequent cooling.<Heat Capacity (University of Toronto laboratory handout)>[p. 3, Figure 1 and the area-ratio cooling correction]{ref} A backward extrapolation to the transfer time is therefore a model-dependent estimate, not the ultimate equilibrium with the room. Extend the model or report a limitation if sensor delay, substantial <temperature gradients>, changing room <temperature>, or escaping material invalidate that assumption. In particular, the relevant $C_{\rm eff}$ after adding a sample includes its <heat capacity>; an empty-receiver cooling rate need not apply to the loaded receiver.

= Caloric theory
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The historical caloric theory treated <heat> as a conserved substance. The modern <first law of thermodynamics> conserves <energy>, allowing net <heat> input to become <work> in a <heat engine>.

= Refrigerator
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A refrigerator removes <heat> from the region we want to keep cold and releases <heat> to warmer surroundings. Here we consider a work-driven cyclic device: its useful output is the cold-side extraction $Q_c$, and its input is net <work> $W$. A food compartment is a familiar example; the same cooling objective applies when removing <heat> from a room. The <coefficient of performance> measures extraction per unit <work>, rather than the fraction of input <energy> converted into output. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-3-refrigerators-and-heat-pumps[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §4.3: refrigerators and useful output]{ref}

Choose the complete machine as the <thermodynamic system>. Let $Q_c>0$ be <heat> entering from the colder side, $Q_h>0$ be <heat> delivered to the hotter side, and $W>0$ be net <work> supplied, all over the same complete cycle. Assume these are its only <energy> transfers, no matter crosses the outer boundary, and there is no net change in stored <energy>. Returning to the initial state gives $\Delta U=0$, so the <first law of thermodynamics> reads
$$
0=Q_c-Q_h+W,\qquad Q_h=Q_c+W.
$$
The <heat> leaving the warmer side includes both the extracted <heat> and the supplied <work>. For instance, stipulated transfers $Q_c=3\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $W=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ require $Q_h=4\,\mathrm{kJ}$; the cooling <coefficient of performance> is $3$. This is an illustrative budget, not measured performance. At steady operation the same balance holds for average transfer rates, $\dot Q_h=\dot Q_c+P_{\rm in}$, where $P_{\rm in}$ is input <power>.

This budget explains a useful counterexample: operating a <refrigerator> with its door open inside an otherwise isolated room cannot cool the room as a whole. If both <heat> transfers occur within the room and the electrical input comes from outside, the room gains $Q_h-Q_c=W$ each cycle. Locally cold air does not imply net cooling. The <second law of thermodynamics> permits cold-to-hot transfer because <work> is supplied; it forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is that transfer. See the <Clausius statement of the second law>.

= Vapor-compression refrigeration
{parent=Refrigerator}
{wiki}

= Vapour-compression refrigeration
{synonym}

Vapor-compression refrigeration circulates a working <fluid> through cold and hot coils. In the cold coil, <liquid> becomes <gas> while absorbing <heat> from the refrigerated region. Compression supplies <work> and raises the <pressure> and <temperature>; the hot coil releases <heat> as the <gas> returns to <liquid>. A restriction reduces the <pressure> before the next cold-side passage. Changing the <pressure> changes the <temperature> of the <phase transition>, letting one circulating <fluid> receive and release <heat> at different <temperatures>. This physical implementation serves both <refrigerators> and <heat pumps>; its ordinary dissipative operation differs from the reversible <Carnot cycle> used to bound performance. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-3-refrigerators-and-heat-pumps[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §4.3, Figure 4.7: circulating coolant]{ref} https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node25.html[MIT, Refrigerators and heat pumps, refrigerator hardware]{ref}

= Heat pump
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A heat pump uses <work> to deliver <heat> to the region we want to keep warm. Its useful output is the hot-side delivery $Q_h$. The same operating machine can be described as a <refrigerator> when the cold-side extraction $Q_c$ is the objective: which output is useful changes the <coefficient of performance>, while the <energy> balance stays $Q_h=Q_c+W$. Heating a water bath by taking <heat> from a colder water bath makes this distinction visible in the <heat-pump calorimetry> experiment below. https://labs.phys.utk.edu/mbreinig/phys136core/modules/m3/refrigerators.html[University of Tennessee, Refrigerators, air conditioners, and heat pumps: heating objective]{ref}

In the illustrative budget from <refrigerator>, supplying $1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <work> delivers $4\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of useful <heat>, giving a heating <coefficient of performance> of $4$. The additional $3\,\mathrm{kJ}$ came from the colder region. Compare an ideal electrical heater whose entire input <work> becomes <heat> in the target region: it delivers $1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ for that input. The appeal of a <heat pump> is this ability to move existing <energy>, while the <second law of thermodynamics> limits the <work> saving for a given pair of <temperatures>.

\Image[heat-pump-energy-and-carnot-bounds.png]
{title=One <energy> budget, two useful outputs, and <Carnot's theorem> bounds on the <coefficient of performance>}
{description=Original quantitative diagram, with stipulated $Q_c=3\,\mathrm{kJ}$, $W=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $Q_h=4\,\mathrm{kJ}$. Arrow widths follow the $3:1:4$ transfer magnitudes. The curves are reversible bounds for fixed cold <temperature> $T_c=280\,\mathrm K$ and varying hot <temperature> $T_h=T_c+\Delta T$; they are not measured performance. At $\Delta T=40\,\mathrm K$, the bounds are $7$ for cooling and $8$ for heating, above the illustrative machine's $3$ and $4$.}

= Coefficient of performance
{parent=Heat pump}
{title2=$\mathrm{COP}=Q_{\rm useful}/W$}
{wiki}

= COP
{c}
{synonym}

For the cyclic two-side <energy> balance in <refrigerator>, define the dimensionless cooling and heating coefficients by
$$
\mathrm{COP}_{\rm r}=\frac{Q_c}{W},\qquad
\mathrm{COP}_{\rm heat}=\frac{Q_h}{W}
=\frac{Q_c+W}{W}=\mathrm{COP}_{\rm r}+1.
$$
This identity applies even to irreversible machines, provided both coefficients refer to the same operating point, <work> input and boundary with no additional <energy> transfer or storage. It does not compare two separately operated appliances. Unlike <thermal efficiency> of a <heat engine>, a <COP> can exceed one: its numerator counts <heat> moved, including <energy> taken from another region. https://labs.phys.utk.edu/mbreinig/phys136core/modules/m3/refrigerators.html[University of Tennessee, Refrigerators, air conditioners, and heat pumps: the two performance ratios]{ref}

To obtain temperature-only bounds, impose stronger assumptions: a cyclic machine exchanges <heat> only with two ideal <thermal reservoirs> maintained at uniform, fixed absolute <thermodynamic temperatures> $0<T_c<T_h$, and receives <work> through an ideal input carrying no <entropy>. Include the machine and the <heat transfer> interfaces in the <entropy production> accounting. Because its own <entropy> returns to its initial value each cycle, the <second law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
S_{\rm gen}=\frac{Q_h}{T_h}-\frac{Q_c}{T_c}\geq0.
$$
Combining this inequality with $Q_h=Q_c+W$ gives
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{COP}_{\rm r}\leq\frac{T_c}{T_h-T_c}},\qquad
\boxed{\mathrm{COP}_{\rm heat}\leq\frac{T_h}{T_h-T_c}}.
$$
Equality requires a <reversible thermodynamic process> for the entire cycle and its contacts, as in a reversed <Carnot cycle>. These are <Carnot's theorem> bounds independent of the working substance. Use <kelvin> values, not <degree Celsius> readings, in the numerators; the <temperature> difference can be expressed in either unit. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node25.html[MIT, Refrigerators and heat pumps: reversed Carnot cycle and absolute-temperature cooling bound]{ref} https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-5-the-carnot-cycle[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §4.5: reversible reservoir entropy balance]{ref}

The diagram fixes $T_c=280\,\mathrm K$ and sets $T_h=320\,\mathrm K$, so the reversible bounds are $280/40=7$ and $320/40=8$. The stipulated machine with $\mathrm{COP}_{\rm r}=3$ and $\mathrm{COP}_{\rm heat}=4$ satisfies both. Its reservoir <entropy production> per cycle is $4000/320-3000/280=25/14\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}>0$. Increasing the <temperature> lift $\Delta T=T_h-T_c$ at fixed $T_c$ lowers both bounds. Equivalently, delivering fixed $Q_h$ needs at least
$$
W_{\min}=Q_h\left(1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}\right).
$$
These expressions reveal why a small <temperature> lift is attractive. Their divergence as $T_h-T_c\to0^+$ is an ideal reversible limit, not a promise of infinite heating or cooling <power>. For finite <heat transfer> rates, the working <fluid> must be colder than the cold bath and hotter than the hot bath; these contact differences produce <entropy> and increase the internal <temperature> lift. Dissipative compression and the pressure-reducing restriction of <vapor-compression refrigeration> supply further departures from reversibility. Thus bath <temperatures> set an outer bound, not a prediction of real <COP>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node25.html[MIT, Refrigerator hardware: working-fluid temperatures on either side of the baths]{ref}

= Heat-pump calorimetry
{parent=Heat pump}

A motivating bench experiment asks whether a hot bath receives more <heat> than the electrical <work> supplied to a <heat pump>. PHYWE's authored operating instructions, section 4, describe a sealed <vapor-compression refrigeration> apparatus with its cold and hot coils immersed in separate <water> vessels. The quantitative procedure calls for insulated vessels, stirring to reduce <temperature> gradients, timed <temperature> readings, and a wattmeter for the input. It waits about five minutes for operating conditions to settle before measurements and explicitly includes the glass vessel and immersed copper pipe when calculating the heated body's <heat capacity>. One bath cooling while the other warms makes the origin of the delivered <energy> observable. https://phywe-itemservice.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/sites/DMS-Phywe/PROD/de-DE/item/phy_operatingmanuals/04/04373-93/0437393e.pdf[PHYWE Systeme, Heat pump, compression principle 04373-93, operating instructions, §4, p. 2]{ref}

The following analysis extends that procedure to simultaneous measurements of both finite baths. Weigh the <water> in each vessel; determine the effective <heat capacities> $C_c$ and $C_h$, including immersed hardware, by a separate known electrical-input calibration over the working <temperature> range. A matched pair of calibrated <thermometers> placed in stirred <water> measures bulk $T_c(t)$ and $T_h(t)$; keeping probes away from the coils avoids mistaking a local contact <temperature> for the whole bath. Record the room <temperature> and synchronise the electrical meter and thermometry. Use short intervals after startup, with no boiling or freezing and small enough <temperature> changes that constant effective <heat capacities> are a reasonable approximation. These are proposed measurement controls, rather than reported results from the manual.

Over a common interval $[t_0,t_1]$, the uncorrected bath estimates and measured electrical input are
$$
Q_{h,\rm raw}=C_h[T_h(t_1)-T_h(t_0)],\qquad
Q_{c,\rm raw}=-C_c[T_c(t_1)-T_c(t_0)],\qquad
W_{\rm el}=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}P_{\rm el}(t)\,dt.
$$
Here the wattmeter records real electrical <power> $P_{\rm el}$. The <integral> handles changing input, and the signs make both estimated useful <heat> quantities positive during heating and cooling. The effective <heat capacity> includes <water> <mass> times its <specific heat capacity> plus the response of the vessel and immersed hardware. Without that correction, equal <temperature> changes need not imply equal <heat> transfers.

Insulation reduces heat leaks but does not establish an <isolated system>. A warmer bath can lose <heat> to the room while a colder bath gains it. As a proposed control, measure pump-off drift at matched bath and room <temperatures>, with the same stirring and insulation. Writing $L_h>0$ for outward hot-bath leak <power> and $L_c>0$ for inward cold-bath leak <power>, the bath balances give
$$
\dot Q_h=C_h\frac{dT_h}{dt}+L_h,\qquad
\dot Q_c=-C_c\frac{dT_c}{dt}+L_c.
$$
Integrate these rates over the same interval before dividing by $W_{\rm el}$. For example, in a pump-off hot-bath control, $L_h=-C_h\,dT_h/dt$; in the cold-bath control, $L_c=C_c\,dT_c/dt$. Check that a stopped machine does not create a different leak path from the running one; otherwise use an independent bath calibration and keep that discrepancy as an uncertainty. Repeat at the same starting <temperatures> to distinguish drift from a change in <COP> as the baths separate.

\Image[heat-pump-bath-calorimetry.png]
{title=<Heat-pump calorimetry>: bath <temperature> slopes, electrical <work>, and heat-leak corrections}
{description=Original apparatus schematic and stipulated constant-rate calculation, not measured data and not a performance claim for the cited apparatus. Each finite bath has effective <heat capacity> $4.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$. The hypothetical machine takes $50\,\mathrm W$, extracts $100\,\mathrm W$, and delivers $150\,\mathrm W$; hot-bath loss is $15\,\mathrm W$ and cold-bath gain is $5\,\mathrm W$. Over $180\,\mathrm s$ the hot bath warms by $5.4\,\mathrm K$ and the cold bath cools by $3.8\,\mathrm K$. Correcting the two bath estimates restores the machine's $3$ heating <COP> and $2$ cooling <COP>.}

In the illustrated toy model, $C_h=C_c=4500\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, $P_{\rm el}=50\,\mathrm W$, $\dot Q_h=150\,\mathrm W$, $\dot Q_c=100\,\mathrm W$, $L_h=15\,\mathrm W$, and $L_c=5\,\mathrm W$ are stipulated constants for $180\,\mathrm s$. The slopes are $dT_h/dt=0.030\,\mathrm{K\,s^{-1}}$ and $dT_c/dt=-19/900\,\mathrm{K\,s^{-1}}$. Consequently,
$$
\begin{aligned}
W_{\rm el}&=9.0\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{h,\rm raw}&=24.3\,\mathrm{kJ},&Q_h&=24.3+2.7=27.0\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{c,\rm raw}&=17.1\,\mathrm{kJ},&Q_c&=17.1+0.9=18.0\,\mathrm{kJ}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The raw heating and cooling ratios are $2.7$ and $1.9$, whose difference is $0.8$. Correcting the bath leaks gives $3$ and $2$, whose difference is one. The apparent failure of $Q_h=Q_c+W$ was incomplete measurement of <heat>, not a violation of the <first law of thermodynamics>.

Real apparatus can also release motor <heat> and pipe <heat> directly to the room, and store <internal energy> during startup. For a machine boundary that receives electrical <work>, an additional outward <heat> transfer $Q_{\rm room}$ gives $Q_h+Q_{\rm room}=Q_c+W_{\rm el}-\Delta U_{\rm machine}$. The residual $Q_h-Q_c-W_{\rm el}$ therefore tests the boundary and corrections; do not force it to zero. Include any auxiliary electrical <work> in the input if quoting whole-apparatus <COP>, and account separately for stirring <work> deposited in a bath. Ignoring vessel <heat capacity>, sensor lag or room leaks can bias the inferred result. These limitations follow from extending the measured <energy> budget, and the manual's explicit hardware correction illustrates why such boundaries matter.

Finally, these vessels are finite bodies whose <temperatures> change; they are not fixed <thermal reservoirs>. Their ratio of integrated useful <heat> to integrated input <work> is an interval <COP>. A <Carnot's theorem> comparison using simultaneous bath <temperatures> is a local approximation only when the machine settles much faster than the baths change. An exact reversible comparison for changing bath <temperatures> instead uses the bath <entropy> changes, for example $C_h\ln(T_{h,1}/T_{h,0})+C_c\ln(T_{c,1}/T_{c,0})$ for constant <heat capacities>, negligible net machine <entropy> change and no room leaks. The experiment's beauty is that two <thermometers>, known <heat capacities> and an electrical-energy measurement expose both <conservation of energy> and the practical gap from reversibility.

= Free expansion
{parent=Thermodynamics}

Free expansion occurs when a <gas> spreads into an initially evacuated region without driving an external load. The standard idealization uses a rigid, insulated vessel: initially a partition confines the <gas> to <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_1$, while the remaining compartment contains <vacuum>. Open a passage and wait for <thermodynamic equilibrium>; the same fixed <amount of substance> $n$ now occupies the larger accessible <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_2$. The outer vessel never changes size. This simple release separates three questions: what <energy> is conserved, whether <temperature> changes, and why <entropy> increases. <Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Free Expansion of Gas>{ref}

\Image[free-expansion-vessel-and-thermometry.png]
{title=<Free expansion> changes the accessible <volume (thermodynamics)>; the apparatus affects the observed <temperature>}
{description=Original schematic, with equal compartments chosen for illustration. The top row assumes an insulated <gas> with negligible wall <heat capacity>. The bottom left sketches the water-bath arrangement of <Joule's free-expansion experiment>, rather than a reconstruction to scale. The bottom right uses stipulated <heat capacities> and an illustrative <energy> demand to show signal suppression; it contains no measured data.}

Choose the <gas> as the <closed system>, neglect <heat> exchange with the walls, and assume negligible changes in bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> between the equilibrium endpoints. Insulation gives $Q_{\rm in}=0$; no moving outer boundary or external load receives <work>, so $W_{\rm out}=0$. The <first law of thermodynamics> therefore gives
$$
\Delta U=Q_{\rm in}-W_{\rm out}=0.
$$
The gas can develop internal motion during the release; this does not supply external <work>. The intermediate state need not have one equilibrium <pressure> or <temperature>, so there is no actual <quasistatic process> curve $p(V)$ to integrate. In particular, substituting the equilibrium <ideal gas law> into $\int p\,dV$ would describe a different process. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-1-reversible-and-irreversible-processes[OpenStax, section 4.1: free expansion and the absence of equilibrium intermediate states]{ref}

For an <ideal gas>, <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>. With positive <heat capacity at constant volume>, $U(T_2)=U(T_1)$ implies $T_2=T_1$, and the <ideal gas law> gives $p_2/p_1=V_1/V_2$. Doubling the accessible <volume (thermodynamics)> thus halves the final <pressure> without changing the final <temperature>. This is an endpoint statement; it does not assert a spatially uniform, constant <temperature> during the rush. Insulation alone does not imply constant <temperature>: a reversible <adiabatic expansion> cools an <ideal gas> because it supplies <work>. <Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>{ref}

For a <real gas>, $U(T,V)$ can depend on <volume (thermodynamics)> as well as <temperature>, so unchanged <internal energy> need not mean unchanged <temperature>. For a fixed-composition <simple compressible system> in a single <thermodynamic phase>, equilibrium identities using a <Maxwell relation> give
$$
dU=C_V\,dT+\left[T\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V-p\right]dV,
\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial V}\right)_U=
\frac{p-T(\partial p/\partial T)_V}{C_V}.
$$
Here $C_V$ is the whole sample's <heat capacity at constant volume>. The sign depends on the <equation of state>; for an <ideal gas> the numerator vanishes. For a finite <free expansion>, solve $U(T_2,V_2)=U(T_1,V_1)$, or integrate this slope along equilibrium states of equal <internal energy>; that mathematical curve does not describe the actual release. <Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Free Expansion of Gas>{ref}

Free expansion is useful because it tests how <internal energy> depends on <number density> while eliminating external expansion <work>. Its beautiful surprise is that an <ideal gas> can finish with the same <temperature> and <internal energy> but greater <entropy>: a <thermometer> alone cannot diagnose reversibility. See <entropy change in free expansion> for the calculation and <temperature sensitivity of a free-expansion experiment> for the experimental qualification. The continuous-flow throttling associated with the <Joule-Thomson coefficient> has a different balance: under its usual assumptions it preserves <enthalpy>, whereas the gas-only idealization here preserves <internal energy>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/sm1/Thermalhtml/node68.html[Fitzpatrick, Joule-Thompson Throttling: steady-flow enthalpy balance]{ref}

= Joule's free-expansion experiment
{c}
{parent=Free expansion}

In his paper published in 1845, Joule connected two copper receivers with stopcocks, filled one with compressed dry air, and evacuated the other with an air pump. He immersed the connected receivers in water in an insulated arrangement, stirred the water, and read a sensitive <thermometer>. He then opened the stopcocks, let the air redistribute, stirred again, and compared the water's <temperature> with its initial value. Alternating control trials accounted for effects such as stirring and evaporation. The observation was no detectable net cooling of the common bath, rather than a record of the <gas> <temperature> at every instant. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/The_free_expansion_of_gases-_memoirs_by_Gay-Lussac%2C_Joule%2C_and_Joule_and_Thomson%3B_%28IA_cu31924012329300%29.pdf#page=37[J. P. Joule, On the Changes of Temperature Produced by the Rarefaction and Condensation of Air (1845), reprinted in Ames's 1898 collection, pp. 24–26]{ref} https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360093/[John Young (2015), section 5: Joule's paired-cylinder experiments and common-bath observation]{ref}

Joule also immersed the receivers and their connecting piece in separate water vessels. The source receiver cooled while the receiving side warmed, with nearly cancelling thermal effects. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/The_free_expansion_of_gases-_memoirs_by_Gay-Lussac%2C_Joule%2C_and_Joule_and_Thomson%3B_%28IA_cu31924012329300%29.pdf#page=39[Joule (1845), separate-water-vessel comparison, reprint pp. 26–27]{ref}

This distinguishes local transient changes from the final observation for the combined apparatus. Insulating its outer boundary suppresses external <heat> exchange; it does not prevent <heat> exchange between the <gas>, the receivers and the water inside that boundary.

An idealized modern version can use two rigid chambers joined by a valve, with <thermometers> in the chambers and on the walls. Prepare one chamber with <gas> and the other evacuated, disconnect the pump, close the apparatus to matter exchange, and let the initial <temperature> settle. Record initial and final equilibrium <pressure> and <temperature>, along with the wall response. Repeated closed-valve runs can reveal drift and valve-actuation effects; independent heating calibrations can estimate the <heat capacity> of the participating apparatus. This is a proposed measurement protocol, not a claim of a particular measured result. Fast local readings can characterize the release, but they cannot replace the final equilibrium readings in the endpoint <internal energy> balance.

= Temperature sensitivity of a free-expansion experiment
{parent=Joule's free-expansion experiment}

The <gas>-only condition $\Delta U=0$ requires negligible <heat> exchange with the apparatus. If the insulated <isolated system> instead includes the walls, sensors and water, the <first law of thermodynamics> fixes their combined <energy>. Once everything returns to a common final <temperature>, a constant apparatus <heat capacity> $C_a$ gives
$$
U_g(T_2,V_2)-U_g(T_1,V_1)+C_a(T_2-T_1)=0.
$$
Define $A=U_g(T_1,V_2)-U_g(T_1,V_1)$, the gas's <internal energy> increase for the same expansion evaluated at the initial <temperature>. For a small <temperature> change, linearizing at the final <volume (thermodynamics)> gives
$$
\Delta T\simeq-\frac{A}{C_{V,g}+C_a}.
$$
Here $C_{V,g}$ is the whole gas sample's <heat capacity at constant volume> at the reference state. Compared with an isolated gas sample's $-A/C_{V,g}$, the common final signal is reduced by $C_{V,g}/(C_{V,g}+C_a)$. This <energy> accounting explains why a massive bath can conceal a real-gas effect. Fitzpatrick gives the corresponding apparatus correction for the <Van der Waals equation>. <Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Van der Waals Gas>{ref}

For a toy calculation, stipulate $A=5\,\mathrm J$, $C_{V,g}=10\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$ and $C_a=990\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, all constant in this example. The predicted cooling magnitude changes from $0.5\,\mathrm K$ for the gas alone to $0.005\,\mathrm K$ for the combined apparatus. An illustrative effective detection threshold of $0.01\,\mathrm K$ would miss the latter. These are chosen numbers, not Joule's measurements. In this approximation a null observation only bounds $|A|\lesssim(C_{V,g}+C_a)\epsilon_T$, where $\epsilon_T$ is the detectable <temperature> change after allowing for drift and other experimental uncertainties; a display's smallest digit is not automatically this threshold.

The historical limitation was recognized explicitly: Joule and Thomson later estimated that the common apparatus in Joule's experiment would cool by only about $0.003\,\mathrm K$, explaining why the earlier external thermal effect was undetectable. This was a later calculation about the apparatus, not a measured cooling trace. https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/The_free_expansion_of_gases-_memoirs_by_Gay-Lussac%2C_Joule%2C_and_Joule_and_Thomson%3B_%28IA_cu31924012329300%29.pdf#page=75[Joule and Thomson, On the Thermal Effects of Elastic Fluids, Part II, reprint p. 63: estimated temperature change of the copper-and-water apparatus]{ref}

= Entropy change in free expansion
{parent=Free expansion}
{title2=$\Delta S=nR\ln(V_2/V_1)$ for an ideal gas}

For the insulated gas-only <free expansion> of an <ideal gas>, the equilibrium endpoints are $(T,V_1)$ and $(T,V_2)$ with $V_2>V_1$. Because <entropy> is a <state function>, calculate their difference along a hypothetical <reversible thermodynamic process>: an <isothermal expansion> using a frictionless piston and a <thermal reservoir> at $T$. This comparison process receives <heat> and delivers <work>, even though the actual <free expansion> does neither. Its reversible <heat> increment is $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=p\,dV=nRT\,dV/V$, so
$$
\Delta S=\int_1^2\frac{\delta Q_{\rm rev}}{T}
=nR\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\frac{dV}{V}
=nR\ln\frac{V_2}{V_1}>0.
$$
Here $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, and the <logarithm> acts on a dimensionless ratio. The reversible reference gives the correct endpoint <entropy> difference because <entropy> is a <state function>; putting the actual $Q=0$ into $\Delta S=Q/T$ would misuse a relation requiring reversible <heat> transfer. For the actual isolated gas, all this increase is <entropy production>. Thus an <adiabatic process> can increase <entropy> and need not be an <isentropic process>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-6-entropy[OpenStax, section 4.6: reversible reference path for the entropy change of free expansion]{ref}

\Image[free-expansion-entropy-reference-path.png]
{title=The reversible reference calculates <entropy> without supplying a path for the actual <free expansion>}
{description=Toy <ideal gas> example with $n=1\,\mathrm{mol}$, $T=300\,\mathrm K$, $V_1=0.025\,\mathrm{m^3}$ and stipulated $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The plotted curve belongs only to the hypothetical reversible <isothermal expansion>; the actual release has the same equilibrium endpoints but no equilibrium curve between them. The table refers to the <gas>, with <work> positive outward. No points are experimental data.}

In the figure's volume doubling, the <ideal gas law> gives $p_1=99.768\,\mathrm{kPa}$ and $p_2=49.884\,\mathrm{kPa}$, while $\Delta S=R\ln2\simeq5.763\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. The reference process receives and delivers $nRT\ln2\simeq1728.85\,\mathrm J$ as <heat> and <work>; its <thermal reservoir> loses the same <entropy> that the <gas> gains. The actual <free expansion> has $Q_{\rm in}=W_{\rm out}=0$ and produces that <entropy> internally. Reversibly compressing the <gas> back at $300\,\mathrm K$ would require $1728.85\,\mathrm J$ of <work> input and rejection of equal <heat>. Restoring the gas alone therefore changes the surroundings: the actual release is irreversible.

For a <real gas> the final <temperature> may differ, so the ideal-gas <logarithm> formula cannot simply be copied. Evaluate $S(T_2,V_2)-S(T_1,V_1)$ using that gas's equilibrium properties or a suitable reversible comparison path. If the walls and water exchange <heat> with it, the <second law of thermodynamics> constrains the total <entropy> of the isolated apparatus; the gas alone is then not an <isolated system>.

= Thermodynamic phase
{parent=Thermodynamics}

A thermodynamic phase is a form of macroscopic equilibrium characterized by its structure, symmetries and thermodynamic response. Away from <phase transitions>, its <free-energy density> varies smoothly with the control parameters. Different phases can share a <phase coexistence> boundary. An ordered magnetic phase is distinguished from a disordered phase by its spontaneous <order parameter>; positive and negative ordered states are different <pure thermodynamic phases> related by <spin inversion symmetry>.

= Phase fraction
{parent=Thermodynamic phase}
{title2=$x_\beta=m_\beta/m$}

A phase fraction is the proportion of a mixture occupying a specified <thermodynamic phase>. State the basis: by <mass>, $x_\beta=m_\beta/\sum_\alpha m_\alpha$; by <volume (thermodynamics)>, divide the occupied <volume (thermodynamics)> by the total <volume (thermodynamics)>. These fractions need not agree when the <mass densities> differ. The <latent heat> <heating curve> uses the <liquid> fraction by <mass>, whereas <liquid fraction> in a <mushy layer> is defined by <volume (thermodynamics)>.

= Laws of thermodynamics
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The laws organize the equilibrium and process constraints of <thermodynamics>: the <zeroth law of thermodynamics> makes thermal equilibrium transitive, the <first law of thermodynamics> expresses energy balance, the <second law of thermodynamics> restricts entropy changes, and the <third law of thermodynamics> describes the zero-temperature limit. Their analogy with horizon mechanics becomes quantitative through <Hawking temperature> and <Bekenstein-Hawking entropy>.

= Zeroth law of thermodynamics
{parent=Laws of thermodynamics}
{wiki}

If two <thermodynamic systems> are each in <thermal equilibrium> with the same third system in the same equilibrium state, they are in <thermal equilibrium> with each other. Write $A\sim B$ for this relation. The <zeroth law of thermodynamics> supplies <transitivity>:
$$
A\sim C\quad\text{and}\quad B\sim C\quad\Longrightarrow\quad A\sim B.
$$
Its <symmetric relation> property and self-equilibrium (<reflexivity>), together with <transitivity>, make <thermal equilibrium> an <equivalence relation>. Its <equivalence classes> can be labelled by a common <empirical temperature>; choosing an absolute numerical scale requires the further structure of <thermodynamic temperature>.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§4.1, printed pp. 109–110: thermal contact, transitivity and temperature labels]{ref}

The comparison assumes internally equilibrated macroscopic bodies, unchanged composition and mechanical constraints, and contact permitting only <heat> exchange. Use rigid, impermeable <diathermal walls> so that neither matter transfer nor boundary <work> spoils the test. The same state of $C$ matters: letting $C$ warm while measuring $A$, then cool while measuring $B$, does not supply the premise. Ordinary nonrelativistic laboratory <thermodynamics> is assumed; the <Tolman temperature law> treats gravitational qualifications. Equal <temperatures> establish the thermal condition, without requiring <mechanical equilibrium> or <chemical equilibrium>.

\Image[zeroth-law-thermal-contact-comparison.png]
{id=zeroth-law-thermal-contact-comparison}
{title=<Thermal equilibrium> is transitive; an <adiabatic wall> supplies no equality test}
{description=The top row compares $A$ and $B$ separately with the same equilibrium state of reference $C$. The lower left shows the predicted absence of net <heat transfer> when $A$ and $B$ are put in <thermal contact>. The lower right is a counterexample to inferring equality from insulation: a rigid <adiabatic wall> can separate bodies at different <temperatures>. All numerical labels are illustrative.}

This explains why a <thermometer> can compare unlike materials: it supplies a reusable third system. A <temperature> is a property of an equilibrium state, while <heat> is <energy> transferred because of a <temperature> difference. Two bodies at the same <temperature> can have different <internal energies> and <heat capacities>. A <thermometer> therefore does not measure a body's stored amount of <heat>; the finite-body disturbance and waiting-time calculations in <thermometer> describe how making the comparison can change the state being measured.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§4.2, printed p. 111: heat as energy transfer, rather than a stored state quantity]{ref}

The name “zeroth” reflects logical order rather than the date of the first <thermometer>: this premise about <thermal equilibrium> precedes the already numbered <first law of thermodynamics>, <second law of thermodynamics> and <third law of thermodynamics>. This is the naming explanation given in <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others> (§1.1, “Thermal Equilibrium”).<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[§1.1, paragraph introducing the zeroth law: retrospective numbering; the textbook credits the name to Ralph Fowler in the 1930s]{ref}

The corresponding <zeroth law of black-hole mechanics> states constancy of <surface gravity> over an equilibrium horizon; <Hawking radiation> identifies that quantity with physical temperature up to its universal conversion factor.

= Thermal contact
{parent=Zeroth law of thermodynamics}

Two <thermodynamic systems> are in thermal contact when their boundary permits <heat transfer>. They need not already be in <thermal equilibrium>: contact between unequal <temperatures> is precisely how equilibration can begin. Contact specifies an allowed exchange; equilibrium specifies its balance.

= Diathermal wall
{parent=Thermal contact}

A diathermal wall permits <heat transfer>. For a clean <zeroth law of thermodynamics> comparison, idealize it as rigid and impermeable, excluding boundary <work> and matter transfer. A thin metal partition approximates this arrangement.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§4.1, printed p. 109: rigid, particle-impermeable diathermal walls and the thin-metal example]{ref}

= Adiabatic wall
{parent=Thermal contact}

An adiabatic wall prevents <heat transfer>. Two bodies at $300\,\mathrm K$ and $350\,\mathrm K$ can remain separated by a rigid adiabatic wall with no <heat transfer>, although they are not in <thermal equilibrium> with each other. An adiabatic boundary alone need not make a system an <isolated system>: a moving insulated piston can still transmit <work>. Isolation additionally excludes matter transfer and every <energy> exchange.

= Empirical temperature
{parent=Zeroth law of thermodynamics}
{title2=$\theta$}

An empirical temperature labels classes of <thermal equilibrium> using a reproducible <thermometer> response. Equal labels express the <zeroth law of thermodynamics> comparison. A suitable continuous response and calibration let ordinary laboratory states be represented on a numerical scale; the abstract <equivalence relation> alone does not guarantee a useful real-valued coordinate. An <injective function> of the label preserves equality, so the <zeroth law of thermodynamics> alone fixes neither unit nor spacing. <Thermometer calibration> supplies a chosen response relation; <thermodynamic temperature> supplies the additional absolute-scale structure.

= Zeroth-law thermometer comparison
{parent=Zeroth law of thermodynamics}

Put a contact <thermometer> $C$ in <thermal contact> with $A$, wait for <thermal equilibrium>, and record response $X_A$. Repeat with $B$ under the same operating constraints. If $X_A=X_B$, and the calibrated response is an <injective function> of <temperature> on this range, $C$ occupies the same thermal reference state in both comparisons. The <zeroth law of thermodynamics> then predicts $A\sim B$. Equality of raw numbers from different uncalibrated <thermometers> supplies no such premise.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others>[§1.1, Figure 1.2 and following paragraph: a thermometer measures its own temperature and transfers the equilibrium comparison]{ref}

Use a probe whose participating <heat capacity> is small relative to each sample, or use maintained <heat baths>. Otherwise the matching readings describe the disturbed final states. Also hold the probe's immersion and mechanical conditions fixed: its response can depend on more than <temperature>. Matching readouts only tests agreement within resolution and repeatability, rather than proving exact equality of <temperatures>.

Agreement within a tolerance is not itself an <equivalence relation>. With a $0.10\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ tolerance, readings $25.00$ and $25.16\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ each agree with $25.08\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, yet disagree with each other. This is a limitation of the measurement rule, not a failure of the exact <transitivity> asserted by the <zeroth law of thermodynamics>.

= Two-point thermometer calibration example
{parent=Zeroth-law thermometer comparison}

Consider an original worked <thermometer calibration>, using the height $X$ of a <liquid> column responding through <thermal expansion>. Stipulate two equilibrated reference baths whose corrected <thermometer> readings are $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and $40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, in <degree Celsius> units. Suppose the corresponding column heights are $12\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $52\,\mathrm{mm}$. These are constructed inputs, not observed measurements. Assume the response is linear over this interval, with the same immersion and reading procedure throughout. Applying <thermometer calibration> gives
$$
\frac{t_C(X)}{{}^\circ\mathrm C}
=40\frac{X-12\,\mathrm{mm}}{52\,\mathrm{mm}-12\,\mathrm{mm}}.
$$
For $X=37\,\mathrm{mm}$, first subtract the zero-reference height, then divide by the full reference span: $(37-12)/(52-12)=25/40$. Multiplying by the $40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ span gives $t_C=25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, or $T=298.15\,\mathrm K$ in <kelvin>. Thus equal $37\,\mathrm{mm}$ readings in separately equilibrated samples test equal <temperatures>, regardless of how much <internal energy> those samples contain. The Celsius offset and modern absolute-scale definition are described in <degree Celsius> and <kelvin>.

\Image[zeroth-law-thermometer-bath-calibration.png]
{id=zeroth-law-thermometer-bath-calibration}
{title=A reference-bath <thermometer> comparison and a worked two-point <thermometer calibration>}
{description=Left: two probes sample gently stirred <water>, with sensing tips immersed away from the walls and bottom; the heater is off for the ambient comparison. Right: an assumed linear column response maps $12\,\mathrm{mm}$ to $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, $52\,\mathrm{mm}$ to $40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $37\,\mathrm{mm}$ to $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. The schematic and all plotted values are illustrative, not experimental data.}

The two endpoints determine the assumed line, but do not establish linearity. An independent middle reference and a return to the initial bath can test curvature and drift. If the height has an error of $0.2\,\mathrm{mm}$ while reference values and endpoints are held exact in this calculation, the inferred <temperature> error is $0.2\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$; that is only the height contribution, not a full measurement error budget. A boiling-water reference needs a measured <atmospheric pressure> correction, rather than an automatic $100\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ assignment. The modern <kelvin> is not defined by either bath.

= Reference-bath thermometer comparison
{parent=Zeroth-law thermometer comparison}

A motivating real procedure is NIST's ambient <thermometer> verification. Its apparatus is a deep beaker of <water>, a calibrated reference <thermometer>, a test <thermometer>, and a magnetic stirrer with any heater switched off. Let the <water> approach room <temperature>, stir gently, immerse both sensing tips well below the surface, and wait before reading. NIST specifies about $20\,\mathrm{cm}$ of <water>, $10$–$15\,\mathrm{cm}$ immersion where possible, and an initial five-minute wait. Record test then reference, repeat in reverse order, and apply the reference's certificate correction. Subtract corrected reference from test: the difference estimates the test instrument's error at this bath <temperature>. Agreement between the two reading orders checks repeatability. <Mercury Thermometer Alternatives: Verification Methods for Alternative Thermometers by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[“Comparison with another calibrated thermometer at ambient temperature”, apparatus and steps 1–10; “Measurement at the steam point”, pressure and elevation correction]{ref}

For example, suppose a test reads $24.7\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, a reference reads $24.4\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and its certificate says to add $0.1\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. The corrected reference is $24.5\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, so the test error is $+0.2\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$: it reads high. These are illustrative readings. This verification tests a previously calibrated instrument at one point; it does not establish the two-point response used in <two-point thermometer calibration example>.

Additional controls follow from the <thermal equilibrium> premise. Keep tips away from walls and bottom, interchange probe positions to test a <temperature gradient>, and check that longer waits and modest immersion changes leave agreement unchanged within the required tolerance. A flat trace alone can be a <steady state> maintained by competing <heat transfer> paths. Five minutes is a procedural starting point, not a universal equilibration time. Excessive stirring can supply <work> and warm the bath, while probe stems can exchange <heat> with the room. These controls test whether both probes sample the same state; neither one-point agreement nor this experiment proves the <zeroth law of thermodynamics> for every material and condition.

= Compressibility
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$\kappa_X=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_{X,N}$}
{wiki}

Compressibility measures how readily a sample's <volume (thermodynamics)> decreases when its <pressure> increases. For a <simple compressible system> with fixed particle number $N$ and composition, specify the constraint $X$:
$$
\kappa_X=-\frac1V\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial P}\right)_{X,N},\qquad
\frac{dV}{V}=-\kappa_X\,dP\quad(X,N\text{ fixed}).
$$
Here $P$ denotes <pressure>, also written $p$ elsewhere. The minus sign makes <compressibility> positive on a mechanically stable equilibrium branch. Its units are inverse <pressure>, $\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$; its reciprocal $K_X=1/\kappa_X$ is the constrained <bulk modulus>, measured in <pascal (unit)> units. Holding <temperature> fixed gives <isothermal compressibility>; holding <entropy> fixed gives <isentropic compressibility> $\kappa_S$ along an <isentropic process>. The latter describes a reversible <adiabatic compression>, since an irreversible <adiabatic process> can increase <entropy>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-20-materials-at-equilibrium-sma-5111-fall-2003/e402e3f0f555e15ff3af29b5b44b4e57_notes1.pdf[G. Ceder, MIT thermodynamics notes, section 1.14: compressibility]{ref}

For an <ideal gas>, the <ideal gas law> $PV=Nk_BT$ and the <reversible ideal-gas adiabat> give, at the same equilibrium state,
$$
\kappa_T=\frac1P,\qquad
\kappa_S=\frac1{\gamma P},\qquad
\gamma=\frac{C_P}{C_V}>1.
$$
Here $C_P$ and $C_V$ are the whole sample's <heat capacity at constant pressure> and <heat capacity at constant volume>, and $\gamma$ is the <heat capacity ratio>. Constant $\gamma$ is required for the finite-path formula $PV^\gamma=\mathrm{constant}$. Reversible <adiabatic compression> raises the <temperature>, making the <pressure> rise more steeply than in an <isothermal compression>. Thus keeping <entropy> fixed makes the <gas> harder to compress locally. For a classical <monatomic gas> at $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$, $\gamma=5/3$, so $\kappa_T=10^{-5}\,\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$ and $\kappa_S=6\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{Pa^{-1}}$; the corresponding values of the <bulk modulus> are $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ and $166.67\,\mathrm{kPa}$.

For a smooth, stable single <thermodynamic phase> at positive <temperature>, <Maxwell relations> also imply
$$
\frac{\kappa_T}{\kappa_S}=\frac{C_P}{C_V},\qquad
\kappa_T-\kappa_S=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{C_P},
$$
where $\alpha$ is the <coefficient of thermal expansion>. The <ideal gas> result is one example of this broader link between mechanical and thermal responses. The equality $\kappa_T=\kappa_S$ is possible when $\alpha=0$ and the other responses remain finite. These formulas compare equilibrium <partial derivatives>, rather than arbitrary fast or dissipative compression histories. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-43-advanced-thermodynamics-spring-2024/1Q7kBt43LW4oPrh3pxYjs2xxe7HWsC1oR_transcript.pdf[G. P. Beretta, MIT 2.43, Lecture 7: response-function identities]{ref}

= Isothermal compressibility
{parent=Compressibility}
{title2=$\kappa_T=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_{T,N}$}

Isothermal compressibility is the <compressibility> measured at fixed <temperature>, particle number and composition. A slowly compressed sealed piston in contact with a <thermal reservoir> can approach this constraint. For an <ideal gas>, differentiating $V=Nk_BT/P$ at fixed <temperature> gives $(\partial V/\partial P)_{T,N}=-V/P$, hence $\kappa_T=1/P$. A small <pressure> increase $\Delta P$ therefore gives $\Delta V/V\simeq-\Delta P/P$. For a finite change, use the whole <isothermal process> curve $V_2/V_1=P_1/P_2$ instead.

Take one <mole> of classical <monatomic gas>, with stipulated <molar gas constant> $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, initially at $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$ and $P_0=100\,\mathrm{kPa}$. The initial <volume (thermodynamics)> is $V_0=nRT_0/P_0=0.024942\,\mathrm{m^3}$. Compressing to $V_0/2$ gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{Isothermal:}\quad &P_2=2P_0=200\,\mathrm{kPa},\quad T_2=300\,\mathrm K,\\
\text{Reversible adiabatic:}\quad &P_2=2^{5/3}P_0\simeq317.48\,\mathrm{kPa},\quad
T_2=2^{2/3}T_0\simeq476.22\,\mathrm K.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <isothermal compression> releases <heat> to the <thermal reservoir>; the reversible <adiabatic compression> retains the input <work> as <internal energy>. Reversing either curve gives the corresponding expansion, with <adiabatic expansion> cooling the <gas>.

\Image[gas-isothermal-adiabatic-compression.png]
{title=An <ideal gas> resists reversible <adiabatic compression> more strongly than <isothermal compression>}
{description=Left: two <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> curves through the same initial state; arrows point toward compression. Right: the corresponding <temperature> changes, including cooling during reversible <adiabatic expansion>. The dotted line marks half the initial <volume (thermodynamics)>. One <mole> of classical <monatomic gas>, with $\gamma=5/3$ and the stipulated $R$ above.}

The equilibrium curvature of <Gibbs free energy> provides another measurement of <isothermal compressibility>:
$$
\kappa_T=-\frac1V\left(\frac{\partial^2G}{\partial P^2}\right)_{T,N}.
$$
This follows from $V=(\partial G/\partial P)_{T,N}$, with $G$ denoting <Gibbs free energy>. Within a stable single <thermodynamic phase>, increasing <pressure> reduces <volume (thermodynamics)> and makes this curvature negative. At <phase coexistence>, changing the <phase fraction> can change the <volume (thermodynamics)> at constant <temperature> and <pressure>; the usual finite single-phase <isothermal compressibility> then need not exist. Near a <critical point>, it can diverge. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_45.html#Ch45-S3[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, section 45-3: compression and phase coexistence]{ref}

= Isentropic compressibility
{parent=Compressibility}
{title2=$\kappa_S=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_{S,N}$}

= Adiabatic compressibility
{synonym}

Isentropic compressibility is the <compressibility> at fixed <entropy>, particle number and composition. The conventional name adiabatic compressibility refers to a reversible <adiabatic process>; an irreversible <adiabatic compression> need not keep <entropy> fixed. For an <ideal gas>, it is $\kappa_S=1/(\gamma P)$, where $\gamma=C_P/C_V$ is the <heat capacity ratio>. It describes a steeper local pressure response than <isothermal compressibility> when $\gamma>1$.

= Maxwell relations
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{c}
{title2=$(\partial S/\partial V)_{T,N}=(\partial P/\partial T)_{V,N}$}
{wiki}

= Maxwell relation
{c}
{synonym}

Maxwell relations turn <pressure>, <volume (thermodynamics)> and <temperature> measurements into information about <entropy>. For example, the <pressure> rise when heating a rigid sealed vessel determines the <entropy> change when expanding the same sample isothermally:
$$
\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_{T,N}
=\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}.
$$
The quantities on the two sides describe different experiments, yet both are <mixed partial derivatives> of one <Helmholtz free energy>. This is the useful surprise: a mechanical measurement can reveal a thermal property. Similarly, the <coefficient of thermal expansion> gives $(\partial S/\partial P)_{T,N}=-\alpha V$. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/7bc33304ab1db561a64b4a6b12e25476_5_60_lecture13.pdf[MIT 5.60, Lecture 13: entropy derivatives from equations of state]{ref}

For a <simple compressible system>, hold particle number $N$ and composition fixed, and allow only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>. Write $U$ for <internal energy>, $H=U+PV$ for <enthalpy>, $F=U-TS$ for <Helmholtz free energy>, and $G=H-TS$ for <Gibbs free energy>. Their natural-variable differentials are
$$
\begin{aligned}
dU(S,V,N)&=T\,dS-P\,dV, &dH(S,P,N)&=T\,dS+V\,dP,\\
dF(T,V,N)&=-S\,dT-P\,dV, &dG(T,P,N)&=-S\,dT+V\,dP.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the first <partial derivatives>, with the subscript recording the variables kept fixed, are
$$
\begin{aligned}
U_S\big|_{V,N}=T,&\quad U_V\big|_{S,N}=-P, &H_S\big|_{P,N}=T,&\quad H_P\big|_{S,N}=V,\\
F_T\big|_{V,N}=-S,&\quad F_V\big|_{T,N}=-P, &G_T\big|_{P,N}=-S,&\quad G_P\big|_{T,N}=V.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <symmetry of second derivatives> produces all four standard <Maxwell relations>, including their signs:
$$
\begin{aligned}
U:\quad\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial V}\right)_{S,N}
&=-\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial S}\right)_{V,N},\\
H:\quad\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial P}\right)_{S,N}
&=\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial S}\right)_{P,N},\\
F:\quad\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_{T,N}
&=\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N},\\
G:\quad\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial P}\right)_{T,N}
&=-\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_{P,N}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For a brief derivation of the $F$ relation, differentiate $F_T=-S$ with respect to $V$ and $F_V=-P$ with respect to $T$: $-S_V=F_{TV}=F_{VT}=-P_T$. Each other row follows in the same way. Assume continuous second <partial derivatives> of the relevant <thermodynamic potential> in the region considered. At a <phase transition>, the equilibrium potential may lose this smoothness; apply the identities within each smooth <thermodynamic phase>, and handle <latent heat> and <phase coexistence> separately. If particle numbers vary, the potential differentials acquire <chemical potential> terms, so the fixed-$N$ constraints must remain explicit. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS2[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.4.2: the four Maxwell relations]{ref}

For an <ideal gas> with fixed $N$, the <ideal gas law> gives $P=Nk_BT/V$. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, heating changes the <pressure> at rate $Nk_B/V$. Consequently an <isothermal expansion> at positive <temperature> has
$$
\Delta S=\int_{V_1}^{V_2}\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}\,dV
=Nk_B\ln\frac{V_2}{V_1}.
$$
For one <mole> with $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $V_2=2V_1$, this is $\Delta S=R\ln2\simeq5.763\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. At $300\,\mathrm K$, a reversible <isothermal expansion> absorbs $Q=T\Delta S\simeq1728.85\,\mathrm J$ and performs equal <work>, since its <internal energy> is unchanged. The <entropy> difference is a <state function> difference: the same equilibrium endpoints give the same $\Delta S$ in an insulated <free expansion>, although there $Q=0$ and the process is irreversible.

\Image[maxwell-entropy-pressure-derivatives.png]
{title=<Ideal gas> <entropy> slopes equal measured <pressure> responses to heating}
{description=Left: <entropy> relative to one common reference state $(T_0,V_0)$ for a classical <monatomic gas>, $[S(T,V)-S(T_0,V_0)]/(Nk_B)=\frac32\ln(T/T_0)+\ln(V/V_0)$, with $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$. Each fixed-<temperature> curve gains $\ln2$ on doubling the <volume (thermodynamics)>. Right: both <partial derivatives>, multiplied by $V_0/(Nk_B)$, equal $V_0/V$; circles are independent finite differences of <entropy>, while the line is the <pressure> response.}

The same <Maxwell relations> let thermal measurements determine the <difference between constant-pressure and constant-volume heat capacities>. In a smooth single <thermodynamic phase>,
$$
dS=\frac{C_V}{T}\,dT+\frac{\alpha}{\kappa_T}\,dV
=\frac{C_P}{T}\,dT-\alpha V\,dP,
$$
using <heat capacity at constant volume>, <heat capacity at constant pressure>, <coefficient of thermal expansion> and <isothermal compressibility>. Along fixed <pressure>, $dV=\alpha V\,dT$, so
$$
C_P-C_V=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{\kappa_T}.
$$
Thus measured expansion and compression responses determine a <heat capacity> difference without measuring both <heat capacities> separately. They do not determine the absolute <heat capacities> or absolute <entropy> by themselves; an independent thermal measurement supplies that missing information. For an <ideal gas>, $\alpha=1/T$ and $\kappa_T=1/P$ reduce the result to $C_P-C_V=PV/T=Nk_B$, which is <Mayer's relation>.

When the necessary higher <partial derivatives> exist and commute, differentiating the <Maxwell relations> once more also gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial C_V}{\partial V}\right)_{T,N}
=T\left(\frac{\partial^2P}{\partial T^2}\right)_{V,N},\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial C_P}{\partial P}\right)_{T,N}
=-T\left(\frac{\partial^2V}{\partial T^2}\right)_{P,N}.
$$
Integrating these slopes determines how a <heat capacity> changes with <volume (thermodynamics)> or <pressure> at fixed <temperature>. For an <ideal gas>, both right-hand sides vanish because the <ideal gas law> is linear in <temperature> at either fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> or fixed <pressure>. Consequently $C_V$ and $C_P$ depend only on <temperature> at fixed $N$, although they need not be constant in <temperature>. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS2[Tong, section 4.4.2: heat-capacity changes from thermal pressure and volume curves]{ref}

Historically, <James Clerk Maxwell>'s \i[Theory of Heat], first published in 1871, presents these connections using the geometry of <isothermal processes> and reversible <adiabatic processes>. His fourth edition, page 167, explicitly relates expansion on heating at fixed <pressure> to <heat> emitted during an <isothermal compression>: the modern $G$ relation gives $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=T\,dS=-T\alpha V\,dP$. This explains why a substance with positive <coefficient of thermal expansion> emits <heat> when compressed isothermally, whereas one with negative coefficient absorbs it. https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/theory-of-heat/7052E2DAA4CD862126D5105ABBFFAD0F/listing[Maxwell, Theory of Heat, publication record and chapter IX]{ref} https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Page:Theory_of_Heat,_James_Clerk_Maxwell,_Fourth_Edition.djvu/183[Maxwell, Theory of Heat, fourth edition, page 167]{ref}

= Diffusion
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$\mathbf j=-D\nabla C$}

Diffusion is net transport associated with random microscopic motion. A localized patch of a dilute <solute> can spread through a stationary <solvent>: individual <molecules> move in both directions, but more cross from the crowded side of an imaginary surface than from the less crowded side. This imbalance gives a net <diffusive flux> down the <concentration> <gradient>; the <molecules> do not need to sense which direction has lower <concentration>. At uniform <concentration>, microscopic exchanges continue even though the net <diffusive flux> vanishes. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/1-061-transport-processes-in-the-environment-fall-2008/8260e3e1577cd739ce85143510d9c165_lec_01.pdf[Heidi Nepf, MIT: random motion and Fick's law]{ref}

The distinction from <advection> is easiest to see in a dye patch. Uniform bulk motion carries its centre downstream; <diffusion> broadens it relative to that centre. Both can happen together. A moving or spreading patch by itself does not identify the mechanism: a nonuniform <velocity field> can also stretch a patch. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/1-061-transport-processes-in-the-environment-fall-2008/c96f9a75576238ac2c4e0e73b84e8072_lec_05.pdf[Nepf, MIT: advection and diffusion of a release]{ref}

\Image[molecular-diffusion-profiles.png]
{title=<Diffusion> broadens a patch; uniform <advection> translates it}
{description=Original model curves, not measured data. Each panel starts with the same <Gaussian distribution> of dye, with <standard deviation> $\sigma_0=0.50\,\mathrm{mm}$. The chosen <diffusion coefficient> is $D=0.20\,\mathrm{mm^2\,h^{-1}}$ where enabled; the chosen bulk speed is $u=0.60\,\mathrm{mm\,h^{-1}}$ where enabled. The vertical axis is <concentration> divided by its spatial integral, so every curve has area one. Pure uniform <advection> preserves width and peak height; <diffusion> lowers the peak while preserving the total dye.}

For a dilute nonreacting <solute> in a uniform medium at fixed <temperature>, <Fick's first law> models the relative <diffusive flux> as $\mathbf j=-D\nabla C$, with scalar <diffusion coefficient> $D>0$ of units $\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$. Here $C$ is the <amount of substance> per unit <volume (thermodynamics)>, so $\mathbf j$ has units $\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$. With carrier <velocity field> $\mathbf u$, the total <flux> is $C\mathbf u+\mathbf j$. The <continuity equation>, with no sources or <chemical reactions>, gives
$$
\frac{\partial C}{\partial t}+\nabla\cdot(C\mathbf u)=\nabla\cdot(D\nabla C).
$$
For constant $D$ and <incompressible flow>, this reduces to $C_t+\mathbf u\cdot\nabla C=D\nabla^2C$, where $\nabla^2$ is the <Laplacian>. In a stationary carrier it is the <diffusion equation>. These assumptions matter: variable $D$ must remain inside the <divergence>, and changing sources require extra terms. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/1-061-transport-processes-in-the-environment-fall-2008/5536a0bf7f3bd0b21fde2169b93a4b5a_lec_02.pdf[Nepf, MIT: conservation with advective and diffusive fluxes]{ref}

The useful surprise is the common mathematics: <thermal conduction> smooths <temperature> through the analogous <heat equation>, while <molecular diffusion> smooths composition. This analogy already motivated the original formulation of <Fick's laws>. https://doi.org/10.1080/14786445508641925[Fick (1855), On liquid diffusion: the analogy with heat conduction]{ref} However, the <thermal diffusivity> and the <solutal diffusivity> need not be equal. A <diffusive time scale> $t_D\sim L^2/D$ means that crossing ten times the distance requires roughly one hundred times the time. This is why <molecular diffusion> is especially effective across thin layers, and why <chaotic advection> can assist mixing by making those layers thinner. Physical <diffusion> is a transport mechanism, distinct from the broader probabilistic class of <Markov diffusion>.

= Molecular diffusion
{parent=Diffusion}

Molecular diffusion is the component of material transport caused by the random thermal motion of <molecules>, measured relative to bulk motion of their surroundings. A <diffusion coefficient> is a property of a particular substance in a particular medium under specified conditions, not a universal speed of spreading. Its dependence on the <solute>, <solvent> and <temperature> is precisely what makes a spreading experiment informative. A <random walk> idealizes this motion; the <Brownian motion> description provides a continuous approximation over sufficiently long times. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/1-061-transport-processes-in-the-environment-fall-2008/8260e3e1577cd739ce85143510d9c165_lec_01.pdf[Nepf, MIT: molecular motion and diffusivity]{ref}

\b[A motivating experiment: watch dye enter a gel.] Samprovalaki, Robbins and Fryer studied rhodamine 6G and methylene blue entering agar gel from two side reservoirs. A covered slab, $10\times5\times1\,\mathrm{mm}$, sat on a controlled thermal stage; the dye was brought to the same <temperature>. They photographed the gel every ten minutes, averaged image intensity across a strip, and fitted the changing <concentration> to a <diffusion equation> model. The cover limited evaporation and stopped dye travelling over the gel surface. Runs at $30$, $50$ and $70\,\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}$ were repeated in triplicate. The measured spatial colour change therefore supplied a quantitative transport test, rather than merely showing that dye eventually spreads. Changing lighting also changes image intensity, so the optical response had to be checked. Importantly for interpretation, their constant-<diffusion coefficient> fit was imperfect and the gel coefficient is an effective transport coefficient, not automatically the value in free <water>. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfoodeng.2012.03.024[Samprovalaki, Robbins and Fryer (2012), Investigation of the diffusion of dyes in agar gels, sections 2-4]{ref}

\b[A finite-pulse version to test width growth.] The following is a proposed teaching variant, not that paper's apparatus or a report of measurements. Make a long, narrow, transparent gel channel of uniform cross-section. Join a thin dyed gel band between two undyed lengths of the same gel, with good contact and no air gaps; the dye should initially fill the cross-section so the dominant <concentration> variation is along the channel coordinate $x$. After assembly, keep the covered channel horizontal at uniform <temperature>, away from vibration. The dye is a finite initial pulse: no reservoir supplies more dye. Choose a dye whose image response can be calibrated and whose interaction with the gel is small enough for an approximately constant <diffusion coefficient> to be plausible.

Photograph the whole pulse at recorded times with fixed lighting, focus and exposure, including a length scale. Use uniformly dyed standards of the same gel thickness and a blank gel to convert image intensity to <concentration> $C(x,t)$; do not assume raw brightness is proportional to <concentration>. Average across the channel width and examine several parallel strips to check the one-dimensional approximation. Measure the initial profile instead of treating the band as infinitely thin. For a finite pulse with negligible end effects, define its spatial integral $Q$, centre $\bar x$ and width $\sigma$ by
$$
Q=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}C(x,t)\,dx,\qquad
\bar x(t)=\frac1Q\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}xC(x,t)\,dx,\qquad
\sigma^2(t)=\frac1Q\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(x-\bar x(t))^2C(x,t)\,dx.
$$
Here $\sigma^2$ is the <variance> of the normalized profile $C/Q$, and $\sigma$ is its <standard deviation>. If $C$ is measured in $\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$, then $Q$ has units $\mathrm{mol\,m^{-2}}$; multiplying by the channel cross-section gives the total <amount of substance>. For the unbounded one-dimensional <diffusion equation> with constant $D$, conserved $Q$, finite <variance> and sufficiently decaying tails,
$$
\sigma^2(t)=\sigma_0^2+2Dt,\qquad
D=\frac12\frac{d\sigma^2}{dt}.
$$
Thus plot width \i[squared] against time: its slope determines the <diffusion coefficient>, while its intercept includes the finite starting width. This moment law holds even for a non-<Gaussian distribution> initially; it follows by applying <integration by parts> twice to the <diffusion equation>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/1-061-transport-processes-in-the-environment-fall-2008/68b0a4a664246168ee639298b39384b1_lec_03.pdf[Nepf, MIT: profile moments and estimating diffusivity from variance growth]{ref}

\Image[molecular-diffusion-width-experiment.png]
{title=Measuring <molecular diffusion> from the growth of width squared}
{description=Original schematic of the proposed finite-pulse gel experiment, with an illustrative calculation rather than measured data. A camera records the broadening band. The plotted points are exact samples of $\sigma^2=0.25+0.40t$ with $\sigma^2$ in $\mathrm{mm^2}$ and $t$ in hours; the slope gives the chosen $D=0.20\,\mathrm{mm^2\,h^{-1}}$. The schematic is not to scale and is not a reproduction of the published apparatus.}

For the particularly readable case of an initially <Gaussian distribution> centred at zero, uniform <advection> at speed $u$ and constant <diffusion coefficient> $D$ give the one-dimensional <concentration> profile
$$
C(x,t)=\frac{Q}{\sqrt{2\pi(\sigma_0^2+2Dt)}}\exp\!\left[-\frac{(x-ut)^2}{2(\sigma_0^2+2Dt)}\right].
$$
Its centre moves as $\bar x=ut$ and its <variance> grows as $\sigma_0^2+2Dt$. Our illustrative choice $\sigma_0=0.50\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $D=0.20\,\mathrm{mm^2\,h^{-1}}$ gives widths $0.50$, $0.81$ and $1.36\,\mathrm{mm}$ at zero, one and four hours. These are calculated values, not a measured dye coefficient. Once $2Dt$ dominates the initial <variance>, width grows as $\sqrt{2Dt}$: four times the elapsed time gives approximately twice the width. A rectangular starting band will not immediately have a <Gaussian distribution>, although the same width-squared law still applies under the stated assumptions.

\b[Controls and limits of the interpretation.] In the proposed experiment, check that $Q$ stays constant and that the centre remains stationary while the width increases. Include a blank and a uniformly dyed gel photographed over the same period: an apparent signal change there exposes lighting drift, dye fading or evaporation. Repeat with a lower starting dye <concentration> and a second initial width; agreement of the slopes is a check on constant $D$ and on preparation effects. Keep the pulse far from the channel ends and record its faint tails: truncation and background errors can strongly bias a <variance>. Poor contact, dye binding, <chemical reactions> or transport in a liquid film can invalidate the simple model. A gel suppresses bulk liquid motion, but its inferred <diffusivity> need not equal that in the pure <solvent>. A stationary centre alone does not exclude symmetric flows, and linear width-squared growth alone does not prove a molecular mechanism: <turbulence> can also produce an effective diffusive description. These are reasons to combine the profile test with the controlled setup.

= Rotational diffusion
{parent=Diffusion}
{wiki}
{title2=$D_r$}

Rotational diffusion randomizes orientation. For a unit orientation vector on a sphere, isotropic rotational diffusion contributes $D_r\nabla_p^2f$ to the <Fokker-Planck equation>, where $\nabla_p^2$ is the <Laplace-Beltrami operator>. Its coefficient has units inverse time. It differs from the translational <diffusion coefficient>, whose units are length squared per time.

= Thermal expansion
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Thermal expansion is a change in a body's dimensions as its <temperature> changes under a specified mechanical constraint. A heated <gas> under a freely moving loaded piston usually increases its <volume (thermodynamics)> at fixed <pressure>; heating the same <gas> in a rigid vessel instead raises its <pressure>. The <coefficient of thermal expansion> quantifies the first response. Expansion can be negative, so the name does not assert that every substance grows on heating.

For an <ideal gas> at fixed <pressure> and particle number, $V=Nk_BT/P$ gives $V_2/V_1=T_2/T_1$. Heating from $300\,\mathrm K$ to $330\,\mathrm K$ therefore increases the <volume (thermodynamics)> by exactly $10\%$ within the <ideal gas> model. For a <solid>, specify whether the measurement concerns length or <volume (thermodynamics)>: for uniform expansion in all three directions, the volumetric coefficient is three times the <linear thermal expansion coefficient>, because $V\propto L^3$. Direction-dependent expansion requires the separate dimensional responses. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-20-materials-at-equilibrium-sma-5111-fall-2003/e402e3f0f555e15ff3af29b5b44b4e57_notes1.pdf[G. Ceder, MIT thermodynamics notes, section 1.14: linear and volumetric expansion]{ref}

= Coefficient of thermal expansion
{parent=Thermal expansion}
{title2=$\alpha=V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial T)_{P,N}$}

= Thermal expansion coefficient
{synonym}

The volumetric coefficient of <thermal expansion> is the fractional <volume (thermodynamics)> increase per <temperature> increase at fixed <pressure> and composition:
$$
\alpha=\frac1V\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_{P,N}
=-\frac1\rho\left(\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial T}\right)_{P,N},\qquad
\rho=\frac mV.
$$
The <mass density> form requires fixed <mass> $m$. Its units are $\mathrm{K^{-1}}$; $\alpha_T$ is another notation for this coefficient. For small <temperature> changes $\theta$ at fixed <pressure>,
$$
V(T_0+\theta)\simeq V(T_0)(1+\alpha(T_0)\theta),\qquad
\rho(T_0+\theta)\simeq\rho(T_0)(1-\alpha(T_0)\theta).
$$
For a finite change in a single <thermodynamic phase>, integrate the possibly varying coefficient: $\ln(V_2/V_1)=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\alpha(T,P,N)\,dT$. A constant-coefficient approximation gives an exponential, while the displayed linear formula additionally requires $|\alpha\theta|\ll1$.

For an <ideal gas> at fixed particle number,
$$
\alpha=\frac1T,\qquad
\alpha(300\,\mathrm K)=\frac1{300}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}\simeq3.333\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}.
$$
Integrating $1/T$ recovers the exact fixed-<pressure> <thermal expansion> ratio $V_2/V_1=T_2/T_1$. Combining expansion with <isothermal compressibility> gives the local response of any smooth <simple compressible system> of fixed composition,
$$
\frac{dV}{V}=\alpha\,dT-\kappa_T\,dP,\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}=\frac\alpha{\kappa_T}.
$$
The ratio has units $\mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}$. For the one-<mole> <ideal gas> state in <isothermal compressibility>, it is $P_0/T_0=333.33\,\mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}$: a $1\,\mathrm K$ increase at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> raises the <pressure> by about $333.33\,\mathrm{Pa}$. <Maxwell relations> identify this same ratio as the <entropy> slope with respect to <volume (thermodynamics)> and give $C_P-C_V=TV\alpha^2/\kappa_T$.

Under the <Boussinesq approximation>, the small fractional <mass density> change produces <buoyancy> while <mass density> is otherwise treated as constant. A positive <temperature> excess $\theta$ gives <reduced gravity> $g'=g\alpha_T\theta$ in the convention used in <natural ventilation>. The coefficient therefore converts a <heat> budget into a buoyancy budget. The same measurable expansion response supplies <entropy> changes and <heat capacity> differences through <Maxwell relations>, connecting fluid motion to equilibrium <thermodynamics>.

= Linear thermal expansion coefficient
{parent=Thermal expansion}
{title2=$\alpha_L=L^{-1}(\partial L/\partial T)_P$}

The linear thermal expansion coefficient is the fractional change in a specified <length> per change in <temperature>, at fixed external <pressure> for a sample free to expand. For small changes, $\Delta L/L\simeq\alpha_L\Delta T$. An isotropic sample with the same response in all directions has volumetric <coefficient of thermal expansion> $\alpha=3\alpha_L$, since differentiating $V\propto L^3$ gives $dV/V=3\,dL/L$. Direction-dependent materials require a specified measurement direction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-3-thermal-expansion[OpenStax University Physics 2, §1.3: linear and volumetric expansion]{ref}.

= Stochastic thermodynamics
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Stochastic thermodynamics assigns work, heat, and entropy production to individual fluctuating trajectories while recovering ordinary thermodynamics after ensemble averaging.

= Local detailed balance
{parent=Stochastic thermodynamics}

Local detailed balance states that the logarithm of the probability ratio between a trajectory and its time reverse equals the entropy delivered to the environment in units of $k_B$. For one heat bath, $\log(P_F/P_B)=\Delta Q/(k_BT)$ when positive $\Delta Q$ flows into the bath.

= Microscopic reversibility
{parent=Local detailed balance}
{wiki}

For an equilibrium process, a path and its physically time-reversed path have equal probability after including their equilibrium starting weights. Conditional on their endpoints, this means
$$
\frac{P_F}{P_B}=\exp[-(F_2-F_1)/(k_BT)].
$$
This is the path version of <detailed balance>. A time-even <order parameter> is read backward without changing sign, while a transport flux changes sign. The <Onsager--Machlup path probability> implements this reversal in a coarse-grained stochastic description.

= Entropy production
{title2=$\Delta S_{\rm tot}$}
{parent=Stochastic thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Entropy production is the sum of the entropy changes of a system and its environment. Under local detailed balance it is represented by a forward-to-reversed path-probability logarithm and has nonnegative expectation.

= Thermodynamic entropy flux
{parent=Entropy production}
{title2=$j_\eta=q/\theta$}

Local equilibrium associates the outgoing <heat flux> $q$ with outgoing <entropy> flux $q/\theta$ at positive <temperature> $\theta$. Its <divergence> appears in the <Lagrangian entropy inequality>, with the appropriate nominal flux in reference coordinates.

= Fluctuation theorem
{parent=Entropy production}
{wiki}

A fluctuation theorem compares the <probability> of a fluctuating <sample trajectory> with that of its reverse. Positive mean <entropy production> permits negative individual realizations: what is constrained is their relative <probability>, not a prohibition on every reversal. The finite statement below compares specified forward and reverse <probability laws>; replacing the reverse law by the forward law requires an additional symmetry. The <biased-step entropy production model> exhibits that symmetry exactly, while the <Crooks fluctuation theorem> compares two different driving protocols.

= Finite trajectory entropy production
{parent=Fluctuation theorem}
{title2=$\Sigma=\Delta S_{\rm tot}/k_B$}

Consider a finite-duration <Markov process> with discrete, time-even states coupled to one <thermal reservoir> at positive <temperature> $T$. Let $P_F[\gamma]$ include the initial <probability> $\rho_0(x_0)$ of the <sample trajectory> $\gamma$. Define $P_R$ by reversing the control protocol and initializing it with the actual forward final <probability distribution> $\rho_\tau$. Write $\Theta\gamma$ for the same states visited in reverse time order. Require normalized path laws with matching <probability support>: a path has positive forward weight exactly when its reverse has positive reverse weight. This excludes irreversible forbidden transitions and missing initial states.

Under <local detailed balance>, with positive <heat> $Q_{\rm bath}$ delivered to the <thermal reservoir>, the full path-weight <logarithm> is
$$
\Sigma_F[\gamma]=\ln\frac{P_F[\gamma]}{P_R[\Theta\gamma]}
=\frac{Q_{\rm bath}[\gamma]}{k_BT}+\ln\frac{\rho_0(x_0)}{\rho_\tau(x_\tau)}
=\frac{\Delta S_{\rm tot}[\gamma]}{k_B}.
$$
The endpoint term is the change in stochastic system <entropy>; the conditional path ratio alone accounts for the <thermal reservoir>. Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>. Both terms matter even for a short observation. <Entropy production along a stochastic trajectory and an integral fluctuation theorem by Udo Seifert>[Seifert, 2005, Eqs. (14)–(18) and (30): conditional and full path weights, endpoint distributions, total entropy]{ref}

Define the conjugate reverse functional by $\Sigma_R[\eta]=\ln(P_R[\eta]/P_F[\Theta\eta])$, so $\Sigma_R[\Theta\gamma]=-\Sigma_F[\gamma]$. Summing the path identity over paths with a given value gives the exact finite <fluctuation theorem>
$$
\frac{p_F(s)}{p_R(-s)}=e^s,
$$
for nonzero denominator, using <probability masses> or corresponding <probability density functions>. This $\Sigma_R$ is defined by the paired laws: it need not be the total <entropy production> obtained by independently restarting the thermodynamic construction for the reverse experiment. A generic driven experiment therefore does not imply $p_F(s)/p_F(-s)=e^s$.

= Integral fluctuation theorem
{parent=Finite trajectory entropy production}
{title2=$\langle e^{-\Sigma_F}\rangle_F=1$}

Normalization of the reverse law gives an exact finite identity:
$$
\left\langle e^{-\Sigma_F}\right\rangle_F
=\sum_\gamma P_F[\gamma]\frac{P_R[\Theta\gamma]}{P_F[\gamma]}=1.
$$
The sum notation describes discrete paths; the corresponding path <integral> has the same change-of-variable argument. <Jensen's inequality> for the <exponential function> then gives $e^{-\langle\Sigma_F\rangle_F}\le1$, hence $\langle\Sigma_F\rangle_F\ge0$. Equivalently this mean is the <Kullback-Leibler divergence> between the forward path law and the reverse path law pulled back by $\Theta$. Equality requires those two laws to agree. <Entropy production along a stochastic trajectory and an integral fluctuation theorem by Udo Seifert>[Seifert, 2005, Eqs. (17)–(18): integral relation and its mean inequality]{ref}

Rare negative <entropy production> contributes disproportionately to this <expectation> through $e^{-\Sigma_F}$. Missing such events in a finite sample can spoil an estimate of the identity. Matching <probability support> is also essential: reverse paths absent from the forward law cannot be recovered by cancelling forward weights. This <second law of thermodynamics> statement concerns an <ensemble average>; an individual negative value is compatible with it.

= Biased-step entropy production model
{parent=Fluctuation theorem}
{title2=$S_n=a(2K-n),\ a=\ln(p/q)$}

Take $n$ <independent> steps, each $+1$ with <probability> $p>1/2$ and $-1$ with <probability> $q=1-p>0$. Reverse a step sequence by reading it backward and changing each sign; use the same biased law for both experiments. With $K$ positive steps, the forward sequence weight is $p^Kq^{n-K}$ and its reversed weight is $p^{n-K}q^K$. Their <logarithm> ratio is
$$
S_n=(2K-n)a,\qquad a=\ln(p/q)>0,\qquad
K\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p).
$$
Here $S_n$ is dimensionless <entropy production>, not an <entropy> in joules per kelvin. A <Markov chain> on a three-state ring gives a state-space realization: start uniformly, take a clockwise step with <probability> $p$ and a counterclockwise step with <probability> $q$. The uniform <stationary distribution> makes endpoint weights cancel. A physical interpretation as exchanged <heat> requires <local detailed balance> with bath <entropy> $\pm k_Ba$ per directed step; the abstract model alone specifies path <probabilities>.

For an allowed value $s=a(2k-n)$, the <binomial distribution> gives
$$
p_n(s)=\binom nkp^kq^{n-k},\qquad
\frac{p_n(s)}{p_n(-s)}
=\frac{\binom nkp^kq^{n-k}}{\binom n{n-k}p^{n-k}q^k}
=\left(\frac pq\right)^{2k-n}=e^s.
$$
This is an exact same-law finite <fluctuation theorem>, with no long-time approximation. For $p=3/4$, $n=9$ and $k=6$, $s=3\ln3$ and $p_n(s)/p_n(-s)=27$. The positive mean is $\langle S_n\rangle=na(p-q)$, although the all-negative path has nonzero <probability> $q^n$. Directly, $\langle e^{-S_n}\rangle=(pe^{-a}+qe^a)^n=(q+p)^n=1$.

\Image[biased-step-entropy-probabilities.png]
{title=Exact biased-step model: discrete entropy-production probabilities and their log ratio at nine steps}

The left panel shows exact <probability masses> for $p=3/4$, with negative values highlighted; the right panel tests the <logarithm> ratio against $s$. These are model values, without experimental measurements or simulated measurement noise.

= Negative entropy production over finite durations
{parent=Biased-step entropy production model}
{title2=$\Pr(S_n<0)$}

A single step has negative <entropy production> with <probability> $q$, independent of how long an observation lasts. A whole window is negative only if negative steps outnumber positive ones:
$$
\Pr(S_n<0)=\sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor(n-1)/2\rfloor}\binom nkp^kq^{n-k}.
$$
For even $n$, the tie $K=n/2$ has zero <entropy production> and is excluded. At $p=3/4$, the negative-window <probabilities> for $n=1,9,21$ are approximately $0.25$, $0.04893$, and $0.00642$. Longer windows still contain negative increments: their expected number is $nq$. Their total is usually positive because the expected number of positive increments is $np$.

\Image[negative-entropy-window-duration.png]
{title=Exact biased-step model: negative totals become rare with duration while negative increments remain frequent}

Only odd window lengths are plotted to avoid alternating tie effects. The left panel gives the exact negative-window <probability> and the upper bound $(2\sqrt{pq})^n$; the right gives expected counts $np$ and $nq$. For steps of fixed duration $\delta t$, physical duration is $n\delta t$. These are counts and <probabilities> predicted by the model, not bead traces. The bound follows from $\Pr(S_n<0)\le\langle e^{-S_n/2}\rangle=(2\sqrt{pq})^n$. Since $2\sqrt{pq}<1$ for $p\ne q$, negative totals are exponentially suppressed even though the negative-step fraction stays $q$.

= Steady-state asymptotic fluctuation theorem
{parent=Fluctuation theorem}
{title2=$I(-s)-I(s)=s$}

A <steady state> does not by itself give every fluctuating observable an <entropy production> symmetry. If the dimensionless total <entropy production> $\Sigma_t$ has the same-law finite relation and a <large deviation principle> with <rate function> $I$ for $\Sigma_t/t$, then, on its common domain,
$$
p_t(\Sigma_t/t\approx s)\asymp e^{-tI(s)},\qquad
I(-s)-I(s)=s.
$$
The first expression denotes leading exponential scaling; the second follows by taking the scaled <logarithm> of the probability ratio. A finite-state, autonomous <Markov process> with time-even states, positive bidirectional rates and a positive <stationary distribution> supplies a standard setting for a finite total-<entropy production> relation. The stochastic system <entropy> is then bounded, so its endpoint contribution does not change the long-time <rate function> of bath <entropy production>. <Stochastic thermodynamics, fluctuation theorems, and molecular machines by Udo Seifert>[Seifert, 2012, §3.3.2, p. 22, Eq. (65); §4.4.3, p. 28, Eqs. (93)–(95): finite stationary total entropy and endpoint-conditioned medium entropy]{ref}

Unbounded state spaces can have endpoint fluctuations on the scale relevant to a <large deviation principle>. Dropping system <entropy>, changing the measured observable to <heat>, or changing the reverse preparation therefore requires a fresh argument. The exact finite <integral fluctuation theorem>, a same-law finite relation, and an asymptotic <rate function> symmetry are distinct claims.

= Crooks fluctuation theorem
{c}
{parent=Fluctuation theorem}
{title2=$P_F(W)/P_R(-W)=e^{\beta(W-\Delta F)}$}

For a system coupled to a <thermal reservoir> at fixed <temperature> $T$, prepare the forward process in the initial <canonical ensemble> and the reverse process in the final control parameter's <canonical ensemble>. Reverse the protocol, assume <microscopic reversibility> and matching path <probability support>, and count $W$ as <work> done on the system. With <Helmholtz free energy> difference $\Delta F=F_{\rm final}-F_{\rm initial}$ and $\beta=1/(k_BT)$,
$$
\frac{P_F(W)}{P_R(-W)}=e^{\beta(W-\Delta F)}.
$$
The final forward distribution need not already be in <thermal equilibrium>. Its reverse preparation is prescribed equilibrium preparation, rather than the actual forward final distribution used in <finite trajectory entropy production>. Thus the paired path <logarithm> $\beta(W-\Delta F)$ is dissipated <work> in thermal units, not automatically the instantaneous total stochastic <entropy production> at the end of an arbitrary driven protocol. <The Entropy Production Fluctuation Theorem and the Nonequilibrium Work Relation for Free Energy Differences by Gavin E. Crooks>[Crooks, 1999, Eqs. (5)–(7), (9)–(11): reversal, equilibrium weights and work relation]{ref} <Entropy production along a stochastic trajectory and an integral fluctuation theorem by Udo Seifert>[Seifert, 2005, Eq. (19) and following paragraph: actual final distribution versus final equilibrium distribution]{ref}

= Jarzynski equality
{c}
{parent=Crooks fluctuation theorem}
{title2=$\langle e^{-\beta W}\rangle_F=e^{-\beta\Delta F}$}

Integrating the <Crooks fluctuation theorem> over <work> and using normalization of the reverse <probability distribution> gives
$$
\langle e^{-\beta W}\rangle_F=e^{-\beta\Delta F}.
$$
Consequently <Jensen's inequality> gives $\langle W\rangle_F\ge\Delta F$. Some realizations may nevertheless have $W<\Delta F$. This is a <work> relation with equilibrium endpoint preparations, while the <integral fluctuation theorem> for total <entropy production> uses the actual final forward distribution. <The Entropy Production Fluctuation Theorem and the Nonequilibrium Work Relation for Free Energy Differences by Gavin E. Crooks>[Crooks, 1999, Eq. (3) and derivation from Eqs. (10)–(11)]{ref}

= Dragged-bead fluctuation theorem experiment
{parent=Fluctuation theorem}

The 2002 experiment trapped a $6.3\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ bead in <water> with a $980\,\mathrm{nm}$ <laser>, a $100\times$, <numerical aperture> $1.3$ objective and a piezo-driven microscope stage. A quadrant <photodiode> tracked bead position at $1\,\mathrm{kHz}$ with approximately $500\,\mathrm{Hz}$ bandwidth and reported $15\,\mathrm{nm}$ resolution. Stage translation drove water past the fixed <optical trap> at $1.25\,\mathrm{\mu m/s}$. Weak trapping prolonged the transient; stationary recording before translation established the position reference. <Stokes drag law> calibration was checked against the bead's measured <diffusion coefficient>. <Experimental Demonstration of Violations of the Second Law of Thermodynamics for Small Systems and Short Time Scales by G. M. Wang and others>[Wang and colleagues, 2002, p. 050601-2: apparatus, calibration and trajectory protocol]{ref}

From calibrated <displacement> and calibrated <harmonic optical trap> <force>, the experimenters evaluated the dimensionless driven-bead dissipation functional
$$
\Sigma_t^{\rm drive}=\frac1{k_BT}\int_0^t\mathbf v_{\rm opt}\cdot\mathbf F_{\rm opt}(u)\,du.
$$
Among 540 trajectories, negative values became less frequent with duration. Figure 2 compared the negative/positive count ratio with $\langle e^{-\Sigma_t^{\rm drive}}\rangle_{\Sigma_t^{\rm drive}>0}$; its agreement tested the paper's integrated transient relation. The detailed log-ratio plot in Figure 4 used simulated particle <sample trajectories>, not bead measurements. Near-zero sign discrimination and stage-start transients limited the earliest points. <Experimental Demonstration of Violations of the Second Law of Thermodynamics for Small Systems and Short Time Scales by G. M. Wang and others>[Wang and colleagues, 2002, Eq. (2), Eq. (3), pp. 050601-2–4 and Figs. 1, 2 and 4]{ref}

For any continuous <probability distribution> satisfying the same-law relation, integration over $s>0$ yields
$$
\frac{\Pr(\Sigma<0)}{\Pr(\Sigma>0)}
=\left\langle e^{-\Sigma}\right\rangle_{\Sigma>0}.
$$
This conditional relation explains the comparison without equating it to the unconditional <integral fluctuation theorem>. The experiment supports a specified driven-system relation; it does not establish a universal same-law symmetry for any definition of <heat> or <entropy production>. In particular, a transient work-like functional should not silently acquire the actual-distribution endpoint term in <finite trajectory entropy production>. Negative realizations are compatible with positive mean dissipation and the <second law of thermodynamics>; they do not provide a cyclic machine with reliable <work> output from a single <thermal reservoir>.

= Fluctuation-dissipation relation for a Langevin particle
{title2=$\sigma^2=2\zeta k_BT$}
{parent=Stochastic thermodynamics}

For a Langevin particle with drag coefficient $\zeta$ and white-force covariance amplitude $\sigma^2$, detailed balance at temperature $T$ requires $\sigma^2=2\zeta k_BT$.

= Vector noise normalization in inertial Langevin dynamics
{parent=Fluctuation-dissipation relation for a Langevin particle}
{title2=$\langle\mathbf A(t)\cdot\mathbf A(s)\rangle=(6\gamma k_BT/m^2)\delta(t-s)$}

Divide the inertial <Langevin equation> by mass and let $\zeta=\gamma/m$. An isotropic three-dimensional equilibrium velocity has $\langle|\mathbf u|^2\rangle=3k_BT/m$ by the <equipartition theorem>. Its white acceleration dot-covariance amplitude is therefore $6\zeta k_BT/m$, while the amplitude per Cartesian component is $2\zeta k_BT/m$. A scalar moment formula must use the component variance rather than the trace.

= Boltzmann constant
{title2=$k_B$}
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The Boltzmann constant converts temperature into an energy scale.

= Boltzmann distribution
{title2=$P_i=Z^{-1}e^{-\beta E_i}$}
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The <Boltzmann distribution> assigns <probabilities> to individual <microstates> in a <canonical ensemble>, a <statistical ensemble> for thermal contact. The system exchanges <energy> with a <thermal reservoir> at fixed positive absolute <temperature> $T$, while its particle number $N$ and external parameters, such as <volume>, stay fixed. The <thermal reservoir> is large enough that these exchanges barely change its <temperature>; the coupling permits <thermal equilibrium> but contributes negligible interaction <energy>. All the <microstates> considered have the same $N$. No <chemical potential> term is needed in their weights; fixed $N$ does not imply that the <chemical potential> is zero.

For a finite discrete set of <microstates> with <energies> $E_i$, write
$$
p_i=\frac{e^{-\beta E_i}}{Z},\qquad
\beta=\frac1{k_BT},\qquad
Z=\sum_i e^{-\beta E_i},\qquad
\sum_i p_i=1.
$$
Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $\beta$ is the <inverse temperature>, $e^{-\beta E_i}$ is a <Boltzmann factor>, and $Z$ is the <partition function>. The <Boltzmann factor> is a weight; dividing by the <partition function> makes it a <probability>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-62-physical-chemistry-ii-spring-2008/ec4f40d5a0fa41df3692f076e850543d_02_562ln08.pdf[MIT 5.62 Physical Chemistry II, spring 2008, lecture 2, pages 4–7: fixed N, V, T and canonical probabilities]{ref}

The useful surprise is that a fixed <temperature> still permits <thermal fluctuations> in the system's <energy>. The <Boltzmann distribution> predicts their <probabilities> and <expected values> without tracking every microscopic exchange. Its <exponential function> also makes rare excited <microstates> sensitive to <temperature>: an <energy> gap much larger than $k_BT$ gives strong <Boltzmann suppression>, while warming increases their relative populations. A population ratio can therefore reveal a gap or serve as a <thermometer>, provided <thermal equilibrium> and the relevant <state degeneracies> are known.

Historically, <Josiah Willard Gibbs>'s \i[Elementary Principles in Statistical Mechanics] (1902) presents the <canonical ensemble> in Chapter IV. His equations 90–92 give an <exponential function> of <energy> and its normalization, in classical <phase space>. This classical formulation exhibits the same weighting principle. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics[Gibbs (1902), title page and preface]{ref} https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics/Chapter_IV[Gibbs (1902), Chapter IV, equations 90–92]{ref}

= Boltzmann distribution ratios and energy reference
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$p_i/p_j=e^{-\beta(E_i-E_j)}$}

For two individual <microstates>, the <partition function> cancels:
$$
\frac{p_i}{p_j}=e^{-\beta(E_i-E_j)}.
$$
At positive <temperature>, a higher-<energy> individual <microstate> is less probable, and equal-<energy> individual <microstates> are equally probable. For example, a gap $E_i-E_j=k_BT\ln2$, using the <natural logarithm>, gives $p_i/p_j=1/2$. This comparison concerns individual <microstates>; a whole <macrostate> can contain several of them.

Changing the reference for <energy> leaves every <probability> unchanged. If all <energies> become $E_i'=E_i+C$ for the same constant $C$, then
$$
Z'=e^{-\beta C}Z,\qquad
p_i'=\frac{e^{-\beta(E_i+C)}}{e^{-\beta C}Z}=p_i.
$$
The <partition function> itself changes, but its common factor cancels in the <Boltzmann distribution>. Thus choosing a <ground state> <energy> of zero simplifies calculations without changing populations. The <expected value> of <energy> shifts by $C$, as it must when its reference changes.

= Boltzmann distribution and state degeneracy
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$P(E)=g(E)e^{-\beta E}/Z$}

Group <microstates> by their <energy> $E$. The <state degeneracy> $g(E)$ counts how many <microstates> have that <energy>; their aggregate <probability> is
$$
P(E)=\sum_{i:E_i=E}p_i=\frac{g(E)e^{-\beta E}}{Z},\qquad
Z=\sum_{\text{distinct }E}g(E)e^{-\beta E}.
$$
Here $P(E)$ is the <probability> of the <macrostate> retaining only <energy>, while $p_i$ is the <probability> of one individual <microstate>. For two such <macrostates>,
$$
\frac{P(E_a)}{P(E_b)}=\frac{g(E_a)}{g(E_b)}e^{-\beta(E_a-E_b)}.
$$
Counting more <microstates> can compensate for a smaller <Boltzmann factor>. Omitting the <state degeneracy> confuses a sum over <microstates> with a sum over distinct <energies>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-62-physical-chemistry-ii-spring-2008/bda82786b6014d3f468798339f658629_03_562ln08.pdf[MIT 5.62, spring 2008, lecture 3, page 4: degeneracy and the aggregate energy probability]{ref}

= Boltzmann distribution in a four-state system
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$Z=(1+q)^2$}

Consider two distinguishable, noninteracting <two-level systems>, each with <energies> $0$ and $\Delta>0$. Their four <microstates> $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)$ have total <energies> $0,\Delta,\Delta,2\Delta$. Thus the three <energy> <macrostates> have <state degeneracies> $1,2,1$. This is a stipulated finite model, not a measured material.

Set $q=e^{-\beta\Delta}$. The four individual <Boltzmann factors> are $1,q,q,q^2$, so the <partition function> and aggregate <probabilities> are
$$
Z=1+2q+q^2=(1+q)^2,\qquad
\bigl(P(0),P(\Delta),P(2\Delta)\bigr)=\frac{(1,2q,q^2)}{(1+q)^2}.
$$
Each of the two middle <microstates> has <probability> $q/Z$, while their shared <energy> <macrostate> has <probability> $2q/Z$.

At the <temperature> satisfying $\Delta=k_BT\ln2$, the <Boltzmann factor> is $q=1/2$ and the <partition function> is $Z=9/4$. Therefore
$$
\bigl(p_{00},p_{01},p_{10},p_{11}\bigr)=\left(\frac49,\frac29,\frac29,\frac19\right),\qquad
\bigl(P(0),P(\Delta),P(2\Delta)\bigr)=\left(\frac49,\frac49,\frac19\right).
$$
The middle <energy> is as likely as the <ground state> <energy> because its <state degeneracy> is twice as large. Yet each individual middle <microstate> is only half as likely as the <ground state> <microstate>. The diagram places the four <energies> beside their individual <probabilities>, then shows the sums within each <macrostate>.

\Image[boltzmann-microstates-and-degeneracy.png]
{title=Four <microstates> and three <energy> <macrostates> at a gap of $k_BT\ln2$}

The <expected value> of <energy> follows directly by weighting the three possible <energies>:
$$
\langle E\rangle=0\,P(0)+\Delta P(\Delta)+2\Delta P(2\Delta)
=\frac{2\Delta q+2\Delta q^2}{(1+q)^2}
=\frac{2\Delta q}{1+q}.
$$
At $q=1/2$ this is $2\Delta/3$. An individual <energy> measurement still returns $0$, $\Delta$ or $2\Delta$; the <expected value> describes the <ensemble average>, rather than a fourth possible measured <energy>.

= Boltzmann distribution temperature limits in a finite system
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann distribution}
{title2=$x=k_BT/\Delta$}

Keep the gap $\Delta$ fixed and vary positive <temperature>. With $x=k_BT/\Delta$, the <Boltzmann factor> is $q=e^{-1/x}$. In the four-<microstate> example, as $T\to0^+$, $q\to0$: the unique <ground state> has <probability> tending to one, and the other <microstates> have <probabilities> tending to zero. If several <microstates> instead shared the lowest <energy>, their <state degeneracy> would split that limiting population equally.

As $T\to\infty$ in this fixed finite model, all four individual <Boltzmann factors> tend to one, so all four individual <microstates> have <probability> tending to $1/4$. The three aggregate <probabilities> instead approach
$$
\bigl(P(0),P(\Delta),P(2\Delta)\bigr)\longrightarrow
\left(\frac14,\frac12,\frac14\right).
$$
The equal-population limit applies to this finite set of <microstates>. An infinite collection of <microstates> cannot all have the same positive normalized <probability>, so the statement cannot be extended to an unbounded <energy> spectrum. Real systems may also acquire additional accessible <microstates> as <temperature> rises.

\Image[boltzmann-populations-versus-temperature.png]
{title=Aggregate <Boltzmann distribution> populations versus positive <temperature> for <state degeneracies> 1, 2, 1}

The plotted crossing occurs at $x=1/\ln2$, where $P(0)=P(\Delta)=4/9$. For larger positive <temperatures>, the middle <macrostate> is more probable than the lowest-<energy> <macrostate>, although the lowest-<energy> individual <microstate> remains more probable than either individual middle <microstate>. The <expected value> of <energy> rises from zero toward $\Delta$ in this finite model.

= Statistical ensemble
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A <statistical ensemble> describes hypothetical copies of the same system under specified constraints, with a <probability distribution> over their <microstates>. An <ensemble average> is an <expected value> across those copies. The <canonical ensemble> fixes <temperature> and particle number while allowing <energy> exchange; the <microcanonical ensemble> fixes <energy>, and the <grand canonical ensemble> also allows particle exchange. These choices specify different physical preparations. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics[Gibbs (1902), preface: ensembles of systems with different microscopic conditions]{ref}

= Temperature
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Temperature labels <thermal equilibrium>: two internally equilibrated bodies have the same <temperature> when contact permitting only <heat> exchange produces no net <heat transfer>. The <zeroth law of thermodynamics> makes the comparison transitive. If bodies A and B separately reach <thermal equilibrium> with the same <thermometer> without appreciably disturbing either body, they agree with each other. This is what lets a <gas thermometer> compare a metal block with <water>, despite their different <internal energies> and <heat capacities>. Insulation alone makes no such comparison: it can prevent <heat transfer> between bodies at different <temperatures>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.1 Temperature and Thermal Equilibrium>[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, section 1.1: temperature and the zeroth law]{ref}

The <zeroth law of thermodynamics> establishes equality comparisons, but does not choose a numerical scale. <Thermodynamic temperature> supplies an absolute scale consistent with <Carnot's theorem> and <entropy>. For a <simple compressible system> at <thermodynamic equilibrium> with fixed particle number $N$ and composition,
$$
T=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial S}\right)_{V,N},\qquad
\frac1T=\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial U}\right)_{V,N}.
$$
Here $U$ is <internal energy>, $S$ is <entropy>, and $V$ is <volume (thermodynamics)>; the second equality assumes the derivative can be inverted. Temperature is a <thermodynamic conjugate variable> to <entropy> and belongs to the <intensive and extensive thermodynamic quantities> as an intensive quantity. Its <partial derivative> tells us how much <internal energy> must change per unit change of <entropy> under the stated constraints. More general systems require their other mechanical and material variables to be held fixed too. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1#S1.SS2.SSS2[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 1.2.2: temperature from the entropy derivative]{ref}

For an infinitesimal quantity $\delta Q>0$ of <heat> transferred from A to B, with no <work> or matter exchange, the equilibrium <entropy> changes give
$$
dS_{\rm total}=\delta Q\left(\frac1{T_B}-\frac1{T_A}\right).
$$
For ordinary positive <temperatures>, spontaneous <heat transfer> from $T_A>T_B$ increases total <entropy>; <thermal equilibrium> has $T_A=T_B$. This comparison uses changes in <entropy>, rather than the total <internal energy> contained in either body. A small hot object can have less <internal energy> than a large cool one. See <entropy maximization under thermal contact> for the equilibrium criterion and <negative temperature> for the special case of bounded <energy> spectra.

Express absolute <thermodynamic temperature> $T$ in <kelvin>, and a Celsius reading $t_C$ in <degree Celsius>:
$$
\frac{t_C}{{}^\circ\mathrm C}=\frac{T}{\mathrm K}-273.15,\qquad
\frac{\Delta t_C}{{}^\circ\mathrm C}=\frac{\Delta T}{\mathrm K}.
$$
The offset is exact: $300\,\mathrm K$ corresponds to $26.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $400\,\mathrm K$ to $126.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Their absolute <temperature> ratio is $4/3$; dividing their Celsius readings gives a different number. Thus <gas thermometer> pressure ratios and <Carnot's theorem> require <thermodynamic temperature>. The modern <SI> definition of the <kelvin> fixes the <Boltzmann constant> $k_B=1.380649\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$; a reference bath realizes or transfers the scale rather than defining that constant.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[section 2.3.1: kelvin and Celsius temperature]{ref}

Temperature is not universally the <mean> <kinetic energy> of a particle. In a classical, nonrelativistic <ideal gas> at <thermal equilibrium>, the <equipartition theorem> gives the mean translational <kinetic energy> relative to the gas's bulk motion:
$$
\left\langle\frac12m|\mathbf v-\mathbf u|^2\right\rangle=\frac32k_BT.
$$
Here $m$ is particle <mass>, $\mathbf v$ its <velocity>, and $\mathbf u$ the mean flow <velocity>. In this regime, different particle <masses> at the same <temperature> have equal mean translational <kinetic energies> but different characteristic speeds. A moving gas can also carry bulk <kinetic energy> without being hotter in its rest frame. Internal molecular modes and <quantum statistics> require further care; the <equipartition theorem> has classical assumptions, whereas the equilibrium <entropy> definition applies more widely. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S2#S2.SS2.SSS1[Tong, Statistical Physics, section 2.2.1: classical equipartition and its limits]{ref}

= Thermometer
{parent=Temperature}
{wiki}

A thermometer measures <temperature> through a physical response whose relation to <temperature> is known. Examples include the length change from <thermal expansion>, a change in <electrical resistance>, and the <pressure> of a <gas thermometer>. If the response <function> $X=f(T)$ is an <injective function> over the working range, its measured value determines $T=f^{-1}(X)$ through the <inverse function>. <Thermometer calibration> establishes that relation for the particular instrument and measurement conditions. Two matching readings are meaningful only after calibration to a common scale and with the probes sampling comparable equilibrium states. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-2-thermometers-and-temperature-scales[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, section 1.2: physical responses used in thermometers]{ref}

For a contact thermometer, the sensing part must reach <thermal equilibrium> with the region being measured. That region may differ from the rest of the object: an immersed probe near a heater can read a local <temperature> while the surrounding <water> remains cooler. A stable reading can also be a <steady state> set by competing <heat transfer> paths to the sample and the room. Good contact, adequate immersion, mixing where appropriate, and checks for spatial differences help establish which <temperature> the reading represents. These are measurement controls inferred from the <thermal equilibrium> requirement, not a guarantee that every flat trace is equilibrium.

A thermometer also changes the sample it touches. Consider an original toy calculation with an initially uniform sample at $T_o=350\,\mathrm K$, a probe at $T_p=300\,\mathrm K$, and constant whole-body <heat capacities> $C_o=100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$ and $C_p=1\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Suppose together they form an <isolated system>, with no <work> or <phase transition> and negligible contact <energy>. The <first law of thermodynamics> and <thermal equilibrium> give
$$
T_f=\frac{C_oT_o+C_pT_p}{C_o+C_p}=349.50495\ldots\,\mathrm K,\qquad
T_f-T_o=\frac{C_p}{C_o+C_p}(T_p-T_o).
$$
The final <thermometer> reading underestimates the sample's original <temperature> by about $0.495\,\mathrm K$, although it correctly measures their new common <temperature>. The disturbance becomes small when the participating probe <heat capacity> is small compared with the sample's. A large controlled <heat bath> is a different limit: it supplies or removes <heat> while approximately maintaining its own <temperature>.

Waiting time is a separate issue from disturbance. Under a toy <Newton's law of cooling> model for a uniform probe in a maintained <heat bath>, a constant contact conductance $G$ gives
$$
C_p\frac{dT_p}{dt}=G(T_b-T_p),\qquad
T_p(t)=T_b+[T_p(0)-T_b]e^{-t/\tau},\qquad \tau=\frac{C_p}{G}.
$$
The <time constant> $\tau$ controls this <exponential decay>. For stipulated values $T_b=350\,\mathrm K$, $T_p(0)=300\,\mathrm K$ and $\tau=5\,\mathrm s$, the probe reads about $331.61\,\mathrm K$ after one <time constant> and $347.51\,\mathrm K$ after three. These are calculated values, not an instrument specification or measured data. Real probes may have multiple relaxation times and additional <heat transfer> paths; choose an equilibration criterion from the required accuracy, rather than assuming that a fixed waiting time works universally.

= Resistance thermometer
{parent=Thermometer}
{wiki}

A <thermometer> that infers <temperature> from a calibrated <electrical resistance>. Measuring current can heat its sensing element; extrapolation toward zero current controls that bias in the <saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement>.

= Platinum resistance thermometer
{parent=Resistance thermometer}

A <resistance thermometer> using <platinum> as its sensing material. It provides the <temperature> readout in the <saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement>.

= Gas thermometer
{parent=Thermometer}
{title2=$P\propto T\quad(N,V\text{ fixed})$}

A gas thermometer infers <temperature> from the <equation of state> of a confined <gas>. The constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> version measures <pressure>; the constant-<pressure> version measures <volume (thermodynamics)>. For an <ideal gas>,
$$
PV=Nk_BT=nRT,\qquad
\frac{T}{T_0}=\frac{P}{P_0}\quad(N,V\text{ fixed}),\qquad
\frac{T}{T_0}=\frac{V}{V_0}\quad(N,P\text{ fixed}).
$$
Here $N$ is particle number, $n$ is <amount of substance>, $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, and $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. The reference and test states must be internally equilibrated, and $T_0$ must be known on the absolute <thermodynamic temperature> scale. A pressure ratio cancels the unknown gas inventory and bulb size in the ideal constant-volume limit. This cancellation is the method's appeal: a mechanical measurement transfers an absolute <temperature> scale between different materials. The <Maxwell speed distribution> and <ideal gas pressure from wall collisions> explain the microscopic origin of the response. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas[OpenStax, University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: the ideal gas law]{ref}

For an original ideal-model calculation, choose $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$, $P_0=120\,\mathrm{kPa}$ and $P=160\,\mathrm{kPa}$. Then $T=400\,\mathrm K$. These are stipulated values, not observations. Both <pressures> must be absolute, referred to vacuum. If the surrounding <atmospheric pressure> were $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$, the corresponding <gauge pressures> would be $20$ and $60\,\mathrm{kPa}$; their ratio is three, which would incorrectly predict $900\,\mathrm K$. A pressure difference from the atmosphere cannot be substituted into the <ideal gas law>.

\Image[temperature-thermometry-gas-apparatus.png]
{title=An original schematic of constant-volume <gas thermometer> measurements}
{description=Simplified measurement layout inspired by the NBS apparatus, not a replica or a report of measurements. A controlled bath thermalizes the bulb, separate calibrated <thermometers> check its surroundings, and a diaphragm transmits <pressure> to an absolute-pressure standard without mixing gases. The connecting tube contains gas at other <temperatures>; its inventory belongs in the <gas thermometer dead-space correction>.}

\b[A motivating calibration experiment.] Schooley's account of the NBS/NIST work describes a <helium>-filled metal bulb, a capillary, a <capacitance diaphragm gauge>, and a mercury pressure standard. Circulating bath liquid passed the bulb assembly and platinum resistance <thermometers>. The resistance probes checked the surrounding <temperature>; gas <pressure> supplied the independent thermodynamic comparison. In an early series, workers filled the cleaned bulb with purified <helium> at the highest test <temperature>, checked <pressure> over three hours, then measured at lower <temperatures> and near a reference at $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Six filling pressures allowed extrapolation toward zero pressure. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/095/jresv95n3p255_A1b.pdf[J. F. Schooley (1990), NBS/NIST Gas Thermometry From 0 to 660 °C, sections 4–5]{ref}

To turn this principle into a readable measurement protocol, use the following controls. These steps are a proposed teaching interpretation of the cited apparatus, rather than additional reported NBS observations:
* \b[Define the inventory.] Evacuate and clean the bulb and connecting tube, fill with a known pure <gas>, close the filling valve, and keep the same total particle number during each reference/test comparison. Monitor <pressure> at a maintained <temperature> before and after a run. A leak changes $N$; a slow drift can also reflect contamination, sorption, or imperfect equilibration, so pressure stability alone does not identify its cause.
* \b[Measure the reference.] Place the bulb in a controlled environment with independently assigned $T_0$. Record $P_0$ after thermalization, together with the independent <thermometer> readings and their spread. A reference near $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ has an absolute <temperature> near $273.15\,\mathrm K$, rather than zero <temperature>. Repeating reference readings tests whether the inventory or apparatus changed during the comparison.
* \b[Measure the test state.] Change the bath setting, allow the bulb and <gas> to settle, and record $P$ only when the surrounding <thermometers> and <pressure> are sufficiently stable for the intended accuracy. Compare readings at different positions and vary the settling time. Record the connecting tube's <temperature> profile too: uniform bath <temperature> does not make the entire connecting tube isothermal.
* \b[Measure the volume changes.] Determine the bulb's <thermal expansion> and its deformation from differences between inside and outside <pressure>. Measure the connecting tube's dimensions and the effective valve volume. “Constant volume” is the ideal model; a real vessel expands. These measurements supply corrections, even though the absolute bulb volume cancels in the simplest pressure ratio.
* \b[Measure pressure against a reference.] For a liquid-column pressure standard, measure the vertical column height, the liquid's <temperature>-dependent <mass density>, local gravitational acceleration, and the reference-side <pressure>. Include diaphragm offsets and the gas's <hydrostatic pressure> change between the sensor and bulb. The pressure needed in the <equation of state> is at the bulb, not automatically at the remote gauge.

The corresponding NBS apparatus used a protective case with an outer <helium> layer held close to the bulb <pressure>, reducing pressure deformation while aiding thermal contact. <Thermal expansion> was measured on bulb material. Gas in the capillary remained partly outside the hot region, requiring a measured tube <temperature> profile and geometry. Pure working <gas> also mattered: contaminants that sorb on surfaces can change the freely circulating inventory. These are physical effects to measure or bound, rather than scatter that disappears by averaging. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/IR/nbsir85-3133.pdf[J. F. Schooley, Thermometry, NBS IR 85-3133, chapter V, gas thermometry and the NBS experiment]{ref}

For illustration, neglect connecting-tube gas and describe a uniform bulb with a dilute <virial expansion> in particle <number density> $\rho_N=N/V$:
$$
PV=Nk_BT\left[1+B_2(T)\rho_N+O(\rho_N^2)\right],\qquad
\frac{T}{T_0}=\frac{P}{P_0}\frac{V(T)}{V(T_0)}\frac{Z(T_0)}{Z(T)}.
$$
Here $Z=PV/(Nk_BT)$ and $B_2$ is the <second virial coefficient> in the particle-density convention; a molar-density convention rescales the coefficient. The second relation follows exactly from the definition of $Z$ for the same $N$, and shows the signs of the corrections. If the bulb expands by $0.1\%$ and $Z=1$ at both states, the toy pressure ratio above gives $400.4\,\mathrm K$, rather than $400\,\mathrm K$. Improved expansion and <helium> nonideality models really changed the temperatures inferred from the 1989 NIST data: Harvey's 2023 reanalysis found expansion updates of roughly $1$–$3\,\mathrm{mK}$ and nonideality updates of roughly $-0.1$ to $-0.5\,\mathrm{mK}$. Those are reported corrections to historical results, not the much larger illustrative correction just calculated. https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=956523[Allan H. Harvey (2023), Small Corrections to 1989 NIST Constant-Volume Gas Thermometry Data, sections 2–3]{ref}

With equal corrected bulb <volumes>, negligible or corrected connecting-tube gas, fixed reference and test <temperatures>, and a dilute classical single gas phase,
$$
\frac{P}{P_0}=\frac{T}{T_0}\left[1+\bigl(B_2(T)-B_2(T_0)\bigr)\rho_N+O(\rho_N^2)\right],\qquad
\lim_{\rho_N\to0}\frac{P}{P_0}=\frac{T}{T_0}.
$$
Repeat the reference/test comparison for several sealed fills, reducing $N$ between comparisons but keeping it fixed within each pair. The extrapolation varies density at fixed endpoint <temperatures>; it does not cool one fill toward zero <temperature>. Real gases have different finite-density corrections but share the classical <ideal gas> intercept under these assumptions. The limit does not remove leaks, bulb expansion, gauge offsets, or the <gas thermometer dead-space correction>. At very low <pressures>, <thermal transpiration> can make remote sensor and bulb <pressures> differ through a nonisothermal capillary; this must be corrected too. The NBS work measured this pressure correction using a separate comparison of narrow and wider tubes. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/095/jresv95n3p255_A1b.pdf[Schooley (1990), sections 7.3–7.6: pressure, volume, dead space and gas nonideality]{ref}

\Image[temperature-thermometry-pressure-extrapolation.png]
{title=Ideal pressure ratios and a dilute-limit <gas thermometer> comparison in two toy gas models}
{description=Left: the stipulated ideal constant-volume calibration $P=0.4\,\mathrm{kPa\,K^{-1}}T$. Right: apparent <temperature> $T_{\rm app}=300\,\mathrm K\,P/P_0$ for invented dimensionless fill $x=\rho_N/\rho_*$, with $P/P_0=(4/3)(1+0.03x)/(1+0.01x)$ for model A and $(4/3)(1-0.02x)/(1+0.01x)$ for model B. Both approach the stipulated $400\,\mathrm K$ intercept; neither curve represents a named gas or measured data.}

At $x=1$, the toy models give $T_{\rm app}=407.92\,\mathrm K$ and $388.12\,\mathrm K$, respectively, although their imposed true test <temperature> is the same. Their zero-density intercept is the reusable quantity. Measuring several low-density points permits a fit and a check for curvature; asserting a straight line from one finite-density point supplies neither. A practical fit must balance nonideality against increasing relative pressure error and the capillary corrections, and keep its extrapolation uncertainty.

= Gas thermometer dead-space correction
{parent=Gas thermometer}

Gas outside the uniform-temperature bulb of a <gas thermometer> contributes to its conserved particle inventory. An idealized calculation divides the connecting tube into small regions with <volumes> $V_j$ and local <temperatures> $T_j$. Neglect <hydrostatic pressure> offsets and <thermal transpiration>, assume a common <pressure> $P$ and a local <ideal gas law> in each region, and let the bulb have <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_b$ and <temperature> $T_b$. Adding the particle inventories gives
$$
N=\frac{P}{k_B}\left(\frac{V_b}{T_b}+\sum_j\frac{V_j}{T_j}\right).
$$
Thus even an <ideal gas> in a perfectly rigid sealed apparatus need not obey $P\propto T_b$: some of its particles remain in regions with other <temperatures>. For a stipulated bulb of $100\,\mathrm{cm^3}$, a connecting volume of $1\,\mathrm{cm^3}$ maintained at $300\,\mathrm K$, and bulb reference and test <temperatures> of $300$ and $400\,\mathrm K$, conservation of $N$ gives
$$
\frac{P}{P_0}=\frac{100/300+1/300}{100/400+1/300}
=\frac{101}{76}\approx1.328947,\qquad
T_{\rm app}=300\,\mathrm K\frac{P}{P_0}\approx398.684\,\mathrm K.
$$
These are toy dimensions and a calculated bias, not measured data. Reducing $N$ reduces both <pressures> proportionally and leaves this ratio unchanged: extrapolating away gas nonideality does not eliminate the connecting-volume error. Actual apparatus also requires the tube's <thermal expansion>, a measured <temperature> profile, and the pressure corrections omitted in this model.

= Thermometer calibration
{parent=Thermometer}

<Thermometer> calibration compares an instrument's response with assigned reference <temperatures> under specified conditions. In a proposed two-reference procedure, thermalize the sensing part at $T_0$ and $T_1$, record responses $X_0$ and $X_1$, and, only if a linear response is justified over that interval, use
$$
T(X)=T_0+(T_1-T_0)\frac{X-X_0}{X_1-X_0}.
$$
Additional reference <temperatures> test curvature; returning to the first reference tests drift. A <gas thermometer> instead obtains its response from an <equation of state>, with a reference and corrections for its apparatus. In either case, uncertainty in the reference propagates to the inferred <temperature>. Calibration determines the response relation; it cannot by itself remove the finite-sample disturbance or incomplete <thermal equilibrium> described in <thermometer>. A reference established on a practical scale can also differ slightly from true <thermodynamic temperature>; the BIPM distinguishes the International Temperature Scale of 1990 readings from their thermodynamic counterparts.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[section 2.3.1: practical and thermodynamic temperature]{ref}

= Kelvin
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=$\mathrm K$}

The kelvin is the SI unit of <thermodynamic temperature>, with symbol $\mathrm K$. Its definition fixes the numerical value of the <Boltzmann constant>; it is written without a degree sign.https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/kelvin[BIPM, SI unit kelvin]{ref}

= Degree Celsius
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=${}^\circ\mathrm C$}

The degree Celsius has the same size as the <kelvin>, with a shifted temperature origin: $t/{}^\circ\mathrm C=T/\mathrm K-273.15$. Thus a change of one degree Celsius is a change of one <kelvin>, but temperature ratios in <Carnot's theorem> must use absolute <thermodynamic temperature>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>{ref}

= Inverse temperature
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=$\beta=1/(k_BT)$}

<Inverse temperature> is $\beta=1/(k_BT)$, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant> and $T$ is the absolute <temperature>. A <canonical ensemble> weights an energy $E$ by its <Boltzmann factor> $e^{-\beta E}$. Calling couplings dimensionless means absorbing this factor $\beta$ into their energy coefficients. The alternative convention $k_B=1$ makes <inverse temperature> $1/T$; physical and dimensionless couplings must not be mixed.

In a thermal <Euclidean worldline path integral> with $\hbar=1$, $\beta$ is also the circumference of the imaginary-time circle.

= Thermal equilibrium
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Two internally equilibrated systems are in thermal equilibrium if bringing them into contact that permits only <heat> exchange causes no net <heat transfer>. In ordinary nonrelativistic <thermodynamics> without appreciable gravitational temperature shifts, their <thermodynamic temperatures> are equal. Merely preventing <heat transfer> with insulation does not establish <thermal equilibrium>: two insulated bodies can have different <temperatures>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.1 Temperature and Thermal Equilibrium>[Samuel J. Ling, William Moebs and Jeff Sanny, OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 1.1]{ref}

A <thermometer> illustrates the definition. Its own <temperature> settles to the object's <temperature> after thermal contact, provided its effect on the object is negligible. The existing <zeroth law of thermodynamics> makes this comparison transitive: two objects separately in <thermal equilibrium> with the same <thermometer> are in <thermal equilibrium> with each other. This lets one calibrated scale compare different materials.

Equal <temperatures> do not imply equal <internal energies> or equal <heat capacities>. Nor does <thermal equilibrium> by itself imply <mechanical equilibrium> or <chemical equilibrium>; these require additional allowed exchanges. There is also a distinction between no net exchange in <thermal equilibrium> and a constant <temperature> maintained by equal incoming and outgoing <heat>. The latter can be a <steady state> with ongoing <entropy production>. Microscopic exchanges can continue at <thermal equilibrium>, but their opposing contributions balance on average.

Historically, OpenStax credits the name “zeroth law” to Ralph Fowler in the 1930s: the already numbered laws needed a logically earlier statement about <thermal equilibrium>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.1 Temperature and Thermal Equilibrium>[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, section 1.1: naming the zeroth law]{ref}

= Thermal equilibration of two finite bodies
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{title2=$T_f=(C_1T_{1,0}+C_2T_{2,0})/(C_1+C_2)$}

Consider two rigid bodies forming an <isolated system>, with fixed composition and constant whole-body <heat capacity at constant volume> values $C_1=1\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$ and $C_2=3\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$. Their initial <temperatures> are $T_{1,0}=300\,\mathrm K$ and $T_{2,0}=400\,\mathrm K$. Assume each body remains internally uniform, their contact stores negligible <energy>, no <phase transition> occurs and no <work> is done or extracted. These are stipulated model values, not material measurements.

Because $dU_i=C_i\,dT_i$, write $U_i=a_i+C_iT_i$ with arbitrary fixed reference constants $a_i$. The conserved relative <internal energy> is
$$
E=U_1+U_2-a_1-a_2=C_1T_1+C_2T_2=1500\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
This is an <energy> reference convention, not a claim about actual <heat capacities> down to zero <temperature>. At <thermal equilibrium> the two <temperatures> equal $T_f$, so the <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
T_f=\frac{C_1T_{1,0}+C_2T_{2,0}}{C_1+C_2}=375\,\mathrm K,\qquad
\Delta U_1=75\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad \Delta U_2=-75\,\mathrm{kJ}.
$$
The larger <heat capacity> pulls the common <temperature> closer to its initial value: body 1 warms by $75\,\mathrm K$, while body 2 cools by $25\,\mathrm K$.

\Image[thermal-equilibrium-two-bodies.png]
{title=Two finite bodies approach <thermal equilibrium> at $375\,\mathrm K$}
{description=Ideal model with $C_1=1$ and $C_2=3\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$ and contact conductance $G=0.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,s^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The weighted <temperature> stays at $375\,\mathrm K$ and the relative <internal energy> stays at $1500\,\mathrm{kJ}$; the plotted curves are model predictions.}

To predict a timescale, stipulate a constant contact conductance $G=1/R_{\rm th}=0.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,s^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, the reciprocal of the contact's <thermal resistance>. The rate of <heat transfer> into body 1 is $G(T_2-T_1)$, giving
$$
C_1\dot T_1=G(T_2-T_1),\qquad C_2\dot T_2=-G(T_2-T_1).
$$
Adding proves conservation of $E$. Subtracting gives <exponential decay> of the <temperature> difference, with <time constant>
$$
\tau=\frac{C_1C_2}{G(C_1+C_2)}=7.5\,\mathrm s,\qquad
T_2-T_1=100\,\mathrm K\,e^{-t/\tau},
$$
and therefore
$$
T_1(t)=375\,\mathrm K-75\,\mathrm K\,e^{-t/\tau},\qquad
T_2(t)=375\,\mathrm K+25\,\mathrm K\,e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
Exact equality is approached as $t\to\infty$ in this ideal model. Changing $G$ changes the rate but not $T_f$: equilibrium and the speed of reaching it are separate predictions. Real bodies can have internal <temperature gradients>, changing <heat capacities> and contact losses, so the plotted $7.5\,\mathrm s$ is an assumed model timescale.

= Tolman–Ehrenfest relation
{c}
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{title2=$T\sqrt{-k^2}=\mathrm{constant}$}

= Tolman temperature law
{c}
{synonym}

In static <thermal equilibrium>, the local temperature times the stationary lapse is constant. Energy measured locally redshifts to conserved Killing energy by that same lapse; requiring a single equilibrium Boltzmann exponent gives the relation. The <Killing vector field> normalization fixes the constant but not the ratio of temperatures at different locations.

= Boltzmann suppression
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{c}
{title2=$e^{-m/T}$}

<Boltzmann suppression> is the exponential reduction of an equilibrium population when creating it costs energy much greater than the temperature. For a nonrelativistic species with negligible <chemical potential>, the leading factor is $e^{-m/T}$, multiplied by its momentum phase-space volume. It concerns the equilibrium abundance; freeze-out can preserve a larger population than the instantaneous equilibrium value at later times.

= Boltzmann suppression of a nonrelativistic relic
{parent=Boltzmann suppression}
{c}
{title2=$n_{\rm eq}\simeq g(mT/2\pi)^{3/2}e^{-m/T}$}

A nonrelativistic species with $g$ states and negligible <chemical potential> has equilibrium density $g(mT/(2\pi))^{3/2}e^{-m/T}$. If particle-antiparticle annihilation maintains equilibrium after the species becomes massive, its eventual freeze-out population can be much smaller than a relativistically decoupled population. The nonrelativistic annihilation rate and any asymmetry determine the final abundance.

= Kinetic equilibrium
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}

Momentum-exchange collisions maintain a common <temperature> and an equilibrium-shaped momentum <probability distribution>. Particle abundances and <chemical potentials> need not have their <chemical equilibrium> values: elastic scattering alone conserves particle number. Thus kinetic equilibrium does not by itself imply either zero <chemical potential> or conserved comoving <entropy>.

= Heat bath
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{wiki}

= Thermal reservoir
{synonym}

A heat bath, or thermal reservoir, is an idealized system so large that exchanging a finite amount of <heat> does not appreciably change its <temperature>.

= Entropy maximization under thermal contact
{parent=Thermal equilibrium}
{title2=$\partial S_{\rm tot}/\partial E_1=1/T_1-1/T_2$}

At fixed <volumes> and material inventories, two internally equilibrated bodies that exchange only <heat> within an <isolated system> have fixed total <internal energy> $E=E_1+E_2$. Their total <entropy> is
$$
S_{\mathrm{tot}}(E_1)=S_1(E_1)+S_2(E-E_1).
$$
Using $1/T_i=(\partial S_i/\partial E_i)_{V_i,N_i}$, an interior stationary point obeys
$$
\frac{dS_{\rm tot}}{dE_1}=\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}=0.
$$
Thus <entropy> stationarity requires matching <thermodynamic temperatures>. Positive values of <heat capacity at constant volume> make this a strict local maximum because
$$
\frac{d^2S_{\rm tot}}{dE_1^2}=-\frac1{C_1T_1^2}-\frac1{C_2T_2^2}<0.
$$
For bodies with positive <heat capacities> throughout the accessible range, this <concavity> gives at most one interior maximum. This is the stability check behind the equal-<temperature> condition; see <entropy concavity and positive heat capacity>. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys1.pdf[David Tong, Statistical Physics, sections 1.2.1–1.2.2: entropy maximization, temperature and heat capacity]{ref}

For <thermal equilibration of two finite bodies>, <entropy from heat capacity> gives
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=C_1\ln\frac{T_f}{T_{1,0}}+C_2\ln\frac{T_f}{T_{2,0}}
=\left(\ln\frac{375}{300}+3\ln\frac{375}{400}\right)\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}
\simeq0.02952799\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}>0.
$$
Body 1 gains about $0.22314355\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$ of <entropy>, while body 2 loses about $0.19361556\,\mathrm{kJ\,K^{-1}}$. Total <energy> is conserved even though total <entropy> increases. The <entropy> calculation uses reversible comparison paths between the endpoint states; actual exchange across a finite <temperature> difference is irreversible.

\Image[thermal-equilibrium-entropy-maximum.png]
{title=At fixed total <energy>, matching <temperatures> maximize total <entropy>}
{description=The curve is total <entropy> relative to the initial $300\,\mathrm K$, $400\,\mathrm K$ state. Every point satisfies $C_1T_1+C_2T_2=1500\,\mathrm{kJ}$. Only $250\le T_1/\mathrm K\le500$ is shown; the mathematical model's positive-<temperature> domain is $0<T_1/\mathrm K<1500$.}

To construct the plot, vary $T_1$ while enforcing the <energy> constraint, rather than holding $T_2$ fixed. In numerical units of <kelvin>, $T_2/\mathrm K=(1500-T_1/\mathrm K)/3$, and the plotted function is
$$
\mathcal S(T_1)=C_1\ln\frac{T_1}{300\,\mathrm K}+C_2\ln\frac{T_2(T_1)}{400\,\mathrm K},\qquad
\frac{dT_2}{dT_1}=-\frac{C_1}{C_2}.
$$
Here $\mathcal S$ means $S_{\rm tot}-S_{\rm tot,initial}$, so its zero is a reference choice. The <chain rule> yields
$$
\mathcal S'(T_1)=\frac{C_1}{T_1}-\frac{C_1}{T_2},\qquad
\mathcal S''(T_1)=-\frac{C_1}{T_1^2}-\frac{C_1^2}{C_2T_2^2}<0.
$$
The unique maximum is at $T_1=T_2=375\,\mathrm K$. The physical trajectory starts at $T_1=300\,\mathrm K$ and moves toward that maximum; the rest of the curve compares other possible <energy> partitions, not later times in the trajectory.

The contact model also proves the direction of change directly:
$$
\dot S_{\rm tot}=G(T_2-T_1)\left(\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}\right)
=\frac{G(T_2-T_1)^2}{T_1T_2}\ge0.
$$
For $G>0$ and positive <temperatures>, equality holds exactly at <thermal equilibrium>. The appealing link is that a dynamical <heat transfer> law moves the system uphill on the constrained <entropy> curve, while conservation of <energy> keeps it on that curve. This predicts both why the endpoint is stable and why the reverse separation into hot and cold bodies cannot happen spontaneously in the <isolated system>.

= Entropy
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$S$}
{wiki}

Entropy is an <extensive quantity> and a <state function> of an equilibrium system, measured in $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Differences between equilibrium states are calculated along a <reversible thermodynamic process>:
$$
S_2-S_1=\int_1^2\frac{\delta Q_{\rm rev}}{T}.
$$
Here <heat> entering the system is positive and $T$ is its absolute <thermodynamic temperature>. The reversible path used for calculation need not be the path actually taken. Dividing the actual <heat> by a single <temperature> generally fails for an irreversible process.

For example, put two rigid bodies, each with constant <heat capacity> $C=100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, at $600\,\mathrm K$ and $300\,\mathrm K$ in an otherwise <isolated system>. Assume each body has a uniform internal <temperature>, no <phase transition> occurs, and no <work> is extracted. Their final <thermal equilibrium> is at $450\,\mathrm K$, because the <internal energy> lost by one equals that gained by the other. Using <entropy from heat capacity>,
$$
\Delta S_h=C\log(450/600)=-28.77\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta S_c=C\log(450/300)=40.55\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},
$$
so $\Delta S_{\rm tot}=C\log(9/8)=11.78\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. One body's <entropy> decreases while the total increases. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node41.html[MIT, Entropy changes and the hot-brick problem]{ref}

\Image[heat-exchange-entropy.png]
{title=Thermal equilibration of two finite bodies conserves energy and increases total entropy}

In <statistical mechanics>, <Boltzmann's entropy formula> counts equally likely <microstates> compatible with a <macrostate>; <Gibbs entropy> handles a specified <probability distribution> over <microstates>. If the number of equally likely <microstates> doubles, <entropy> increases by $k_B\log2$. The <logarithm> makes multiplicative counts additive for independent systems. These are precise statements about states and probabilities, not a universal measure of visible messiness. Calling <entropy> merely "disorder" obscures the thermal calculation above and hides which <microstates> are being counted. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node57.html[MIT, Statistical entropy and randomness]{ref}

The usefulness of <entropy> is that measurable <heat capacities> determine its changes, and the <second law of thermodynamics> turns those changes into constraints on processes. It also connects macroscopic measurements to microscopic descriptions without requiring us to track every particle.

= Specific entropy
{title2=$s=S/M$}
{parent=Entropy}

Specific entropy is <entropy> per unit mass. For a <perfect gas> with constant <specific heat capacity> $c_v$ at constant volume and <specific-heat ratio> $\gamma$, differences obey
$$
s_2-s_1=c_v\log\left[\frac{p_2}{p_1}\left(\frac{\rho_1}{\rho_2}\right)^\gamma\right],
$$
where $p$ is <pressure> and $\rho$ is <mass density>. Thus a <homentropic flow> has constant $p/\rho^\gamma$.

= Boltzmann's entropy formula
{title2=$S=k_B\log\Omega$}
{c}
{parent=Entropy}
{wiki}

= Boltzmann entropy
{c}
{synonym}

Why does a <gas> spread through an available container? One part of the answer is counting: a nearly uniform <macrostate> can be realized by vastly more <microstates> than a <macrostate> with every particle crowded into one corner. <Boltzmann's entropy formula> turns that multiplicity into <entropy>. The useful insight is that a large <entropy> measures how many microscopic realizations fit a specified macroscopic description, rather than how untidy a picture looks.

For a specified <macrostate> $M$ containing $\Omega(M)$ accessible, equally likely <microstates>, write
$$
S(M)=k_B\ln\Omega(M),\qquad p_i=\frac1{\Omega(M)}\quad(i\text{ compatible with }M).
$$
Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, and $\ln$ is the <natural logarithm>, also written $\log$ in this article's title. The count $\Omega(M)$ is dimensionless; <entropy> has units of <energy> divided by <temperature>. Always specify the <macrostate>, the constraints and the model's microscopic resolution before counting. A <microcanonical ensemble> provides a familiar equilibrium setting for the equal-<probability> assumption. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node57.html[MIT, §7.4: equiprobable microstates and S = k ln Ω]{ref}

The <natural logarithm> compresses an enormous count into a manageable number and makes products of counts into sums of <entropies>; see <additivity of Boltzmann entropy>. With this counting convention, one allowed <microstate> has zero <Boltzmann entropy>. This zero refers to the chosen model and resolution; it does not by itself assign an absolute <entropy> to a real material.

= Boltzmann entropy of a two-box occupancy
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}
{title2=$S(k)=k_B\ln\binom Nk$}

Take $N=20$ distinguishable, labeled particles and two boxes of equal <volume>. Retain only whether each particle is in the left or right box: use a <bit> $0$ for left and $1$ for right. Each length-20 string is one coarse <microstate> of this toy model. Assume the labels make <independent> side choices with equal <probability>, so the $2^{20}=1\,048\,576$ strings are equiprobable. Equal <volumes> motivate these weights for noninteracting particles with no preference for either box; equal <volumes> alone would not establish them for an arbitrary interacting <gas>.

Now retain only the number $k$ in the left box as the <macrostate>. Choosing which $k$ labels are left gives a <binomial coefficient>, so
$$
\Omega(k)=\binom{20}{k}=\frac{20!}{k!(20-k)!},\qquad
S(k)=k_B\ln\binom{20}{k},\qquad
P(k)=\frac{\binom{20}{k}}{2^{20}}.
$$
The <factorials> count assignments to labels; the <probability> of a <macrostate> sums the <probabilities> of its constituent <microstates>. This is the <binomial distribution> with parameters $20$ and $1/2$. The same <binomial coefficient> appears in the counting of distinguishable <two-level systems>, although here the <macrostate> records occupancy rather than <energy>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/0cb99318c2ed889316cdcff839759e8d_MIT8_333F13_Lec12.pdf[Mehran Kardar, MIT 8.333 (2013), lecture 12, §IV.C, equations IV.17–18: binomial microstate counts]{ref}

The largest <binomial coefficient> occurs at $k=10$, the balanced <macrostate>. All-left means $k=20$ and supplies just one string. Thus
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Omega(10)&=184\,756,& S(10)/k_B&=\ln184\,756\approx12.126791,\\
\Omega(20)&=1,& S(20)/k_B&=0,\\
P(10)&\approx0.176197,& P(20)&=2^{-20}\approx9.53674\times10^{-7}.
\end{aligned}
$$
There are $184\,756$ balanced strings for every all-left string, even though each individual string has the same <probability>. Exact balance has about $17.6\%$ of the total <probability>; equilibrium reasoning concerns the many <macrostates> near balance too, rather than certainty of exactly ten particles on each side. The diagram compares the counts with their <natural logarithms>.

\Image[boltzmann-entropy-binomial-macrostates.png]
{title=Counts and Boltzmann entropies of the occupancy macrostates of twenty labeled particles}

Four fair, <independent> coins give a smaller worked example with the same <binomial distribution>. If $k$ is the number of heads, the five <macrostates> $k=0,1,2,3,4$ contain respectively $1,4,6,4,1$ <microstates>. Their <probabilities> are respectively $1/16,4/16,6/16,4/16,1/16$. In particular, two heads is one <macrostate> containing six ordered coin sequences, with <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln6$ in this counting analogy. A prescribed ordered sequence has one realization. The next diagram enumerates the same sixteen strings as left/right assignments.

= Boltzmann entropy when a restriction is removed
{c}
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}
{title2=$\Delta S=k_B\ln(\Omega_f/\Omega_i)$}

In <Boltzmann entropy of a two-box occupancy>, impose an initial restriction that all labels are left. There is one allowed coarse <microstate>. Remove that restriction, allowing all occupancy values: the allowed set is now the union of every occupancy <macrostate>, containing $2^N$ strings. If the final description assigns equal <probability> to this whole set, its <Boltzmann entropy> change is
$$
\Omega_i=1,\qquad \Omega_f=\sum_{k=0}^N\binom Nk=2^N,\qquad
\Delta S=k_B\ln\frac{\Omega_f}{\Omega_i}=Nk_B\ln2.
$$
The counting identity is the <binomial theorem>. For twenty labels, $\Delta S=20k_B\ln2\approx13.862944k_B$. This final description fixes neither box occupancy; it is coarser than the particular <macrostate> $k=10$, whose <Boltzmann entropy> is $k_B\ln184\,756$. The two counts answer different questions, so substituting the balanced <macrostate> for the full allowed set would change the calculation.

For four labels, the initial all-left string is `0000`; removing the restriction makes all sixteen strings available. The $k=2$ column has only six strings. Accordingly, the whole allowed set has <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln16=4k_B\ln2$, while the balanced <macrostate> has <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln6$.

\Image[entropy-two-box-configuration-count.png]
{title=Sixteen configurations of four labeled particles grouped by left occupancy, compared with the all-left restriction}

Removing a barrier changes the allowed set immediately, but it does not instantaneously randomize the actual <probability distribution>. Motion and equilibration must make those configurations accessible. The counting result illustrates the configurational contribution to <free expansion>; it does not describe the relaxation time or every contribution to the <entropy> of a real <gas>.

= Additivity of Boltzmann entropy
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}
{title2=$\Omega_{AB}=\Omega_A\Omega_B,\quad S_{AB}=S_A+S_B$}

For two subsystems with independently specified <macrostates>, suppose every allowed <microstate> of $A$ can be paired with every allowed <microstate> of $B$. Their allowed set is a <Cartesian product>. With equal <probability> for each pair, <Boltzmann's entropy formula> gives
$$
\Omega_{AB}=\Omega_A\Omega_B,\qquad
S_{AB}=k_B\ln(\Omega_A\Omega_B)=S_A+S_B.
$$
For example, take two <independent> copies of the toy model, each with two labels choosing between its two boxes. Each copy has four configurations and <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln4$. Combining their <independent> labels gives sixteen configurations and <Boltzmann entropy> $k_B\ln16=2k_B\ln4$. This explains why <entropy> adds even though microscopic counts multiply. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node57.html[MIT, §7.4, equation 7.17: independent state counts multiply]{ref}

The product assumption matters. A constraint shared between the subsystems can forbid some pairs; for example, fixing only their combined <energy> generally requires summing over its possible divisions. See <entropy maximization under thermal contact> for how <thermal equilibrium> selects the dominant division. Statistical <independence> cannot be inferred merely from drawing a boundary between two parts.

= Scope of Boltzmann's entropy formula
{parent=Boltzmann's entropy formula}

The strings in <Boltzmann entropy of a two-box occupancy> are complete <microstates> only within the binary toy model. They retain a <coarse-grained variable> for each particle and omit its exact <position> within a box and its <momentum>. An actual classical <gas> instead occupies continuous <phase space>; obtaining a dimensionless count from a <phase space> volume requires a specified cell convention and the appropriate treatment of particle identity. A <quantum state> count must respect the allowed <quantum states> and <quantum statistics>. Thus $2^N$ is not a general count of <microstates> for a <gas> obeying <quantum ideal-gas statistics>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/0cdb670a825c6aaea7e229ac40776cbf_MIT8_333F13_Lec13.pdf[Kardar, MIT 8.333 (2013), lecture 13, §§IV.D–E: gas phase space and particle counting]{ref}

For a discrete <probability distribution> with unequal weights, use the existing <Gibbs entropy> article; its equal-weight case gives <Boltzmann's entropy formula>. <Shannon entropy> uses the corresponding information measure, with the logarithm base setting its units. A count alone cannot replace the specified <probability distribution>, as the <Boltzmann distribution> examples demonstrate. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node56.html[MIT, §7.3: statistical entropy for general microstate probabilities]{ref}

Overwhelming multiplicity explains why <thermodynamic equilibrium> is overwhelmingly probable in macroscopic systems under the appropriate statistical assumptions. It does not make low-<entropy> <thermal fluctuations> logically impossible. Nor does counting alone derive irreversible macroscopic evolution: one also needs dynamics, an initial preparation, and a reason the system samples the allowed <microstates> on the timescale of interest. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/0cb99318c2ed889316cdcff839759e8d_MIT8_333F13_Lec12.pdf[Kardar, lecture 12, §§IV.A–B: statistical equilibrium and the limits of counting arguments]{ref}

Historically, Boltzmann's 1877 paper counted microscopic arrangements, called complexions, and related the <natural logarithm> of their number to <entropy>. The Sharp and Matschinsky translation appeared in \i[Entropy] 17 (2015), pages 1971–2009, DOI 10.3390/e17041971. Its notation differs from ours: Boltzmann's permutability measure $\Omega$ is already logarithmic, whereas here $\Omega$ is the count. https://gilles.montambaux.com/files/histoire-physique/Boltzmann-1877-entropy.pdf[Boltzmann (1877), translated by Kim Sharp and Franz Matschinsky (2015), §III after equation 34 and §V]{ref} <Josiah Willard Gibbs>'s \i[Elementary Principles in Statistical Mechanics] (1902) developed the <statistical ensemble> framework; Chapter XIV compares several statistical definitions of <entropy> with <thermodynamics>. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics[Gibbs (1902), title page and preface]{ref} https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elementary_Principles_in_Statistical_Mechanics/Chapter_XIV[Gibbs (1902), Chapter XIV: discussion of thermodynamic analogies]{ref}

= Entropy density
{title2=$s$}
{parent=Entropy}
{wiki}

For a homogeneous equilibrium system with <energy density> $\rho$, <pressure> $P$, <chemical potential> $\mu$, and <number density> $n$, the entropy density is
$$
s=\frac{\rho+P-\mu n}{T}.
$$

= Comoving entropy
{parent=Entropy density}
{title2=$S_c=a^3s$}

Comoving entropy is the <entropy density> multiplied by the physical volume factor $a^3$ for a fixed comoving region in an expanding homogeneous universe. In adiabatic expansion it is conserved. Separate decoupled radiation sectors conserve their own comoving entropy, so disappearance of massive species can heat the still-equilibrated sector relative to a decoupled species.

= Entropy density at zero chemical potential
{parent=Entropy density}
{title2=$s=(\rho+P)/T$}

For an extensive equilibrium system, the <thermodynamic Euler relation> gives $E=TS-PV+\mu N$. At zero <chemical potential>, divide by $V$ to obtain $s=(\rho+P)/T$. Equivalently, $dP=(\rho+P)dT/T$ makes
$$
d\left(\frac{(\rho+P)V}{T}\right)=\frac{d(\rho V)+P\,dV}{T}.
$$
The <first law of thermodynamics> identifies this as $dS$; the <thermodynamic Euler relation> fixes the entropy normalization. For adiabatic expansion satisfying the <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation>, the numerator on the right vanishes, proving <cosmological entropy conservation>.

= Nonrelativistic entropy at zero chemical potential
{parent=Entropy density at zero chemical potential}
{title2=$s\sim g m^3(2\pi)^{-3/2}x^{-1/2}e^{-x}$}

For a dilute nonrelativistic gas with $x=m/T\gg1$ and zero <chemical potential>, the <Bose-Einstein distribution> approaches the <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>. Expanding $E=m+p^2/(2m)+\cdots$ in the <number density> integral gives $n\sim g(mT/(2\pi))^{3/2}e^{-x}$. Since $\rho=mn+3nT/2+\cdots$ and $P=nT$, the <entropy density at zero chemical potential> gives $s=n(x+5/2+\cdots)$ and hence the displayed leading expression. This includes the internal multiplicity $g$; a single real <scalar field> has $g=1$.

= Second law of thermodynamics
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

For an <isolated system>, the second law requires $\Delta S\ge0$. More generally, include all affected surroundings to obtain
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=\Delta S_{\rm system}+\Delta S_{\rm surroundings}\ge0.
$$
Equality describes the reversible limit; irreversible macroscopic processes produce <entropy>. A <closed system> can lose <entropy> by exporting <heat>, so nondecrease does not apply to that system alone. The <Clausius statement of the second law> forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is transferring <heat> from cold to hot. The <Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law> forbids a cyclic device whose sole effect is converting <heat> from one <thermal reservoir> completely into <work>. "Sole effect" matters: a <refrigerator> moves <heat> from cold to hot by consuming <work>. <Thermodynamics and Propulsion/5.1 Concept and Statements of the Second Law (Why do we need a second law?)>{ref}

For direct transfer of $Q=1000\,\mathrm J$ from a <thermal reservoir> at $600\,\mathrm K$ to one at $300\,\mathrm K$, with no <work> extracted,
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=-\frac{1000}{600}+\frac{1000}{300}
=\frac53\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}>0.
$$
The reverse transfer alone would have negative total <entropy> change. Both directions conserve <energy>, but only the hot-to-cold direction is spontaneous. The finite-body example under <entropy> replaces these constant <temperatures> by changing ones.

<Friction>, <free expansion> and <heat transfer> across a finite <temperature> difference all introduce irreversibility. Moving arbitrarily slowly is insufficient if <friction> remains. A <reversible thermodynamic process> must permit restoration of both the system and surroundings. This gives engineers a useful ideal benchmark: irreversibility spends an opportunity to obtain <work>, even though <energy> itself is conserved. https://www.ocw.mit.edu/ans7870/16/16.unified/thermoF03/chapter_7.htm[MIT, Irreversibility and lost opportunities for work]{ref}

= Reversible thermodynamic process
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}
{title2=$dS=\delta Q_{\rm rev}/T$}

A thermodynamically reversible process can be reversed without a net change of the system and surroundings. Its entropy change equals reversible heat transfer divided by temperature. A reversible <adiabatic process> conserves <entropy>; adiabaticity alone does not forbid internal entropy production in an irreversible evolution.

= Clausius statement of the second law
{c}
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}

No cyclic device can have as its sole effect the transfer of <heat> from a colder <thermal reservoir> to a hotter <thermal reservoir>. The word “sole” includes the surroundings: the device must return to its starting state, and there must be no net supplied <work>, depletion of a stored resource, or accompanying change elsewhere. The statement excludes a self-running <refrigerator>, while allowing an ordinary <refrigerator> or <heat pump> supplied with <work>.<Thermodynamics and Propulsion/5.1 Concept and Statements of the Second Law (Why do we need a second law?)>[§5.1, statement 2 and Figure 5.2: sole-result restriction]{ref}

For a proposed transfer $Q>0$ from a fixed cold <thermal reservoir> at $T_c$ to a fixed hot <thermal reservoir> at $T_h>T_c>0$, with no other effect, the total <entropy> change would be
$$
\Delta S_{\rm tot}=\frac{Q}{T_h}-\frac{Q}{T_c}<0.
$$
The <first law of thermodynamics> permits this <energy> balance; the <second law of thermodynamics> forbids the negative total <entropy> change. For $Q=600\,\mathrm J$, $T_c=300\,\mathrm K$ and $T_h=600\,\mathrm K$, it is $-1\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Direct <heat transfer> in the opposite direction has positive total <entropy> change. A powered <heat pump> has a different budget: it supplies <work> and delivers more <heat> to the hot side than it extracts from the cold side.

A short equivalence argument links this restriction to the <Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law>. Couple a hypothetical device that moves $600\,\mathrm J$ from cold to hot with no other effect to the <heat engine> illustrated above, which absorbs $1000\,\mathrm J$, rejects $600\,\mathrm J$, and delivers $400\,\mathrm J$. The cold-side transfers cancel; the combined cyclic device takes $400\,\mathrm J$ from the hot <thermal reservoir> and delivers exactly $400\,\mathrm J$ of <work>, violating the <Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law>. Matching transfers is an ideal composition argument, not a claim that such a device exists.<8.333 Statistical Mechanics I: Statistical Mechanics of Particles/Statistical Mechanics I: Lecture 2>[§I.D, printed pp. 7–8: equivalence by coupling devices]{ref}

= Kelvin-Planck statement of the second law
{c}
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}

No cyclic device can have as its sole effect the extraction of <heat> from one <thermal reservoir> and its complete conversion into net <work>. A putative device absorbing $Q_h>0$ and returning to its starting state with $W=Q_h$ and no other transfer would conserve <energy>, yet violate the <second law of thermodynamics>. Rejecting <heat> to colder surroundings is an essential part of the ordinary two-<thermal reservoir> <heat engine> budget.<Thermodynamics and Propulsion/5.1 Concept and Statements of the Second Law (Why do we need a second law?)>[§5.1, statement 1 and Figure 5.1: complete conversion as the sole result]{ref}

The cyclic and sole-effect qualifications matter. A single <isothermal expansion> of an <ideal gas> can absorb <heat> and deliver equal <work> while leaving its <volume (thermodynamics)> changed; restoring the initial state must also be counted. Likewise, a device discharging stored <internal energy> or consuming fuel has another effect. Converting supplied electrical <work> completely to <heat> through <Joule heating> reverses the proposed conversion and is allowed. The statement also allows cyclic devices that exchange <heat> with one <thermal reservoir> while consuming net <work>; it forbids positive net <work> output with that single <heat> source as the only external change.

Conversely, a hypothetical complete-conversion device could take $100\,\mathrm J$ from the hot <thermal reservoir> and supply $100\,\mathrm J$ of <work> to an ideal <refrigerator> that extracts $100\,\mathrm J$ from the cold <thermal reservoir> and delivers $200\,\mathrm J$ to the hot one. At $600\,\mathrm K$ and $300\,\mathrm K$, that <refrigerator> is itself admissible: its reservoir <entropy> changes cancel. The composite would consume no external <work> and move a net $100\,\mathrm J$ from cold to hot, violating the <Clausius statement of the second law>. Together with the construction there, this proves the equivalence of the two impossibility statements.<8.333 Statistical Mechanics I: Statistical Mechanics of Particles/Statistical Mechanics I: Lecture 2>[§I.D, printed p. 8: engine driving a refrigerator]{ref}

For the two fixed positive-<temperature> <thermal reservoir> hypotheses, <Carnot's theorem> gives the sharper restriction $\eta\le1-T_c/T_h<1$. A <reversible thermodynamic process> reaches that bound in the ideal <Carnot cycle>; an ordinary device's smaller <thermal efficiency> reflects its <entropy production> and measurement boundary. An inefficient observed engine is compatible with the <second law of thermodynamics>, rather than evidence that the bound has been experimentally determined.

= Carnot's theorem
{c}
{parent=Second law of thermodynamics}
{title2=$\eta\le1-T_c/T_h$}
{wiki=Carnot's_theorem_(thermodynamics)}

= Carnot theorem
{c}
{synonym}

No cyclic <heat engine> exchanging <heat> only with two fixed <thermal reservoirs> at positive absolute <temperatures> $T_h>T_c$ can exceed the <thermal efficiency> of an engine whose cycle is a <reversible thermodynamic process> between them. All such reversible engines have the same <thermal efficiency>, regardless of their working substance. Let $Q_h>0$ be <heat> absorbed from the hot <thermal reservoir> and $Q_c>0$ the magnitude rejected to the cold one. Then
$$
W=Q_h-Q_c,\qquad
\eta=\frac W{Q_h}\le\eta_C=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}.
$$
The <work> convention is the one in the <first law of thermodynamics>: $W>0$ is net output by the engine. Use absolute <thermodynamic temperatures>, such as values in <kelvin>; ratios of readings in <degree Celsius> are invalid because their zero is shifted.

For $T_h=600\,\mathrm K$, $T_c=300\,\mathrm K$ and $Q_h=1000\,\mathrm J$, the maximum <thermal efficiency> is $50\%$. A reversible engine delivers $W=500\,\mathrm J$ and rejects $Q_c=500\,\mathrm J$. The <thermal reservoirs> have <entropy> changes $-1000/600$ and $+500/300\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, which cancel. The engine's own <entropy> change is zero over its <thermodynamic cycle>.

\Image[carnot-engine-efficiency.png]
{title=Carnot engine energy transfers and the reversible cycle between 600 K and 300 K}

A short derivation now becomes transparent: the <second law of thermodynamics> gives $-Q_h/T_h+Q_c/T_c\ge0$, and the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $W=Q_h-Q_c$. Together they yield the bound and, more precisely,
$$
W=Q_h\left(1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}\right)-T_c\Delta S_{\rm tot}.
$$
At fixed $Q_h,T_h,T_c$, every extra unit of total <entropy production> reduces output by $T_c$ units of <energy>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node44.html[MIT, Maximum heat-engine work]{ref}

Fixed <thermal reservoirs> are essential. Finite bodies cool and warm, so their initial <temperature> ratio cannot be used throughout an entire extraction process. As a computed contrast, the bodies with equal constant <heat capacity> in the <entropy> example would reach $T_f=\sqrt{600\times300}=424.26\,\mathrm K$ if a reversible engine extracted <work> until their <temperatures> matched. Zero total <entropy> change gives this geometric mean; the <first law of thermodynamics> then gives $W_{\max}=C(600+300-2T_f)=5147.19\,\mathrm J$. Direct contact reaches $450\,\mathrm K$ and extracts no <work>.

Carnot's 1824 book asked whether the useful output of machines driven by <heat> was fundamentally limited and whether changing the working substance could improve that limit. Its reversible comparisons are the historical setting of <Carnot's theorem>. Carnot used the then-current caloric picture, treating <heat> as conserved in its passage through an engine; the modern balance $Q_h-Q_c=W$ should not be read back into that text.<Réflexions sur la puissance motrice du feu et sur les machines propres à développer cette puissance by Sadi Carnot>[pp. 6–7 and 20–23]{ref}

= Thermodynamic temperature
{parent=Carnot's theorem}
{wiki}

Absolute thermodynamic temperature can be defined so that a reversible engine exchanging heats $Q_h$ and $Q_c$ with reservoirs at $T_h$ and $T_c$ satisfies
$$
\frac{Q_c}{Q_h}=\frac{T_c}{T_h},
\qquad
\eta=1-\frac{T_c}{T_h}.
$$
A choice of one reference temperature fixes the scale.

= Carnot cycle
{c}
{parent=Carnot's theorem}
{title2=$W=(T_h-T_c)\Delta S$}
{wiki}

A Carnot cycle realizes the reversible bound in <Carnot's theorem>. Its four steps for an <ideal gas> working substance are:

* Reversible <isothermal expansion> at $T_h$, absorbing $Q_h$ and increasing <entropy> by $\Delta S$.
* Reversible <adiabatic expansion>, doing <work> while the <temperature> falls from $T_h$ to $T_c$ and <entropy> stays fixed.
* Reversible <isothermal compression> at $T_c$, rejecting $Q_c$ and decreasing <entropy> by $\Delta S$.
* Reversible <adiabatic compression>, requiring <work> to restore $T_h$ at fixed <entropy>.

The <temperature> versus <entropy> plot in the <Carnot's theorem> figure is therefore a rectangle traversed clockwise. Because $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=T\,dS$, its width is $\Delta S=Q_h/T_h$. For the illustrated engine, $\Delta S=5/3\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$: the upper step absorbs $600\times5/3=1000\,\mathrm J$, the lower step rejects $300\times5/3=500\,\mathrm J$, and the enclosed area is the $500\,\mathrm J$ net <work>. Geometry displays the <thermal efficiency> without solving the gas motion. The two vertical steps are <isentropic processes>, not constant-<temperature> steps. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node24.html[MIT, Four steps of a Carnot cycle]{ref} https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node46.html[MIT, Temperature-entropy diagrams]{ref}

Reversible <heat transfer> is a limit of vanishing <temperature> differences; ordinary finite-rate transfer introduces <entropy production>. The <Carnot cycle> is a performance benchmark rather than a guarantee of useful <power>. Running it backward gives the ideal operation of a <refrigerator> or <heat pump>, with <work> supplied to move <heat> toward the hotter <thermal reservoir>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node25.html[MIT, Reversed cycles and refrigeration]{ref}

= Isothermal process
{parent=Carnot cycle}
{wiki}

An <isothermal process> changes a <thermodynamic system> while keeping its <temperature> constant. It need not keep its <pressure>, <volume (thermodynamics)> or <internal energy> constant, and it does not generally have zero <heat> transfer. An <adiabatic process> specifies zero <heat> transfer instead. For example, a slowly expanding <ideal gas> can absorb <heat> from a <heat bath> and deliver the same amount of <work> while its <temperature> stays fixed.

For a fixed <amount of substance> and composition, <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>. With $Q>0$ for <heat> entering and $W_{\rm by}>0$ for <work> delivered to the surroundings, the <first law of thermodynamics> therefore gives
$$
\Delta U=Q-W_{\rm by}=0,\qquad Q=W_{\rm by}.
$$
This equality does not determine either transfer until the path is specified. <Isothermal ideal-gas work> gives the reversible value; an insulated <free expansion> has a different value even when its equilibrium endpoints have the same <temperature>. The <closed system> convention here excludes changes in bulk <kinetic energy> and external <potential energy>.

A conducting cylinder in a large, stirred <heat bath> approximates an <isothermal process> when the piston moves slowly enough for <thermal conduction> to keep up. A <quasistatic process> keeps the <gas> close to internal <thermodynamic equilibrium>; a <reversible thermodynamic process> additionally requires negligible <friction> and vanishing driving differences in <pressure> and <temperature>. Exact equality of the <gas> and <heat bath> <temperatures> gives no finite <heat transfer> rate through a finite <thermal resistance>. The reversible description is a limit of arbitrarily small differences and arbitrarily slow motion, illustrated by the <finite-rate isothermal piston model>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.4 Thermodynamic Processes>[§3.4, “Isothermal Processes”, Figure 3.9 and the discussion of removing piston weights in infinitesimal steps]{ref}

For a <real gas>, <internal energy at constant temperature> can change with <volume (thermodynamics)> because molecular interaction <potential energy> changes. In one smooth <thermodynamic phase> of a fixed-composition <simple compressible system>,
$$
\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V}\right)_{T,n}
=T\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_{V,n}-p.
$$
Thus an <isothermal process> generally has $Q=\Delta U+W_{\rm by}$, with no reason to set $\Delta U=0$. The right-hand side vanishes for the <ideal gas law>, but not generally for another <equation of state>.<Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Free Expansion of Gas>[Equations 6.151–6.154: the volume contribution to internal-energy changes]{ref}

= Isothermal ideal-gas work
{parent=Isothermal process}
{title2=$W_{\rm by,rev}=nRT\ln(V_f/V_i)$}

For a <reversible thermodynamic process> that changes the <volume (thermodynamics)> of a fixed amount of <ideal gas> at absolute <temperature> $T$, the <work> done by the <gas> is
$$
W_{\rm by,rev}=\int_{V_i}^{V_f}p\,dV
=nRT\int_{V_i}^{V_f}\frac{dV}{V}
=nRT\ln\frac{V_f}{V_i}
=nRT\ln\frac{p_i}{p_f}.
$$
Here $n$ is the <amount of substance>, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, $p$ the <pressure>, and $\ln$ the <natural logarithm>. The <ideal gas law> supplies $p=nRT/V$; the <integral> is the signed <area> under the <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> curve. <Isothermal expansion> gives positive <work>; <isothermal compression> gives negative <work>. A convention counting <work> done on the <gas> instead has $W_{\rm on}=-W_{\rm by}$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.2 Work, Heat, and Internal Energy>[§3.2, equations 3.3–3.4, Figure 3.5 and the isothermal integral following Figure 3.6]{ref}

For a calculated example, let $n=1\,\mathrm{mol}$, $T=300\,\mathrm K$ and $V_i=10\,\mathrm L$, $V_f=20\,\mathrm L$. The <pressure> falls from approximately $249.4$ to $124.7\,\mathrm{kPa}$, and
$$
W_{\rm by,rev}=Q=nRT\ln2\approx1.729\,\mathrm{kJ},\qquad \Delta U=0.
$$
Traversing the same <isothermal process> backward changes both transfer signs: $W_{\rm by,rev}=Q\approx-1.729\,\mathrm{kJ}$. Neither direction changes <internal energy of an ideal gas>. These stipulated values illustrate the <energy> balance; they are not experimental measurements.

\Image[isothermal-ideal-gas-signed-work-area.png]
{title=Signed work along an ideal-gas isotherm: expansion and compression}

The shaded geometric <area> is positive in both panels; the direction of the <integral> supplies the sign of <work>. Knowing only the two equilibrium endpoints does not justify shading this curve for an irreversible process. For an idealized piston moving against a specified external resisting <pressure>, boundary <work> is $W_{\rm by}=\int p_{\rm ext}\,dV$. Substitution of the equilibrium <gas> <pressure> requires the appropriate reversible limit. In <free expansion> into <vacuum>, $p_{\rm ext}=0$ and this boundary <work> is zero.

= Finite-rate isothermal piston model
{parent=Isothermal process}

Why must a conducting piston move slowly? A simple model couples the <first law of thermodynamics> to <Newton's law of cooling>. Assume a fixed amount of <ideal gas> with uniform <temperature> $T(t)$ and constant whole-sample <heat capacity at constant volume> $C_V$, a <heat bath> maintained at $T_b$, negligible <friction>, and mechanically slow motion. Let $G>0$ be the effective thermal conductance, the reciprocal of the <thermal resistance>, in $\mathrm{W\,K^{-1}}$. Then
$$
\dot Q=G(T_b-T),\qquad p=\frac{nRT}{V},\qquad
C_V\dot T=G(T_b-T)-p\dot V.
$$
Positive <heat transfer> must offset expansion <work> to keep $T$ near $T_b$. A finite $G$ and nonzero piston speed therefore require a <temperature> difference. This uniform-<temperature> model neglects spatial <temperature gradients>, sensor response, cylinder <heat capacity> and any change of $G$ with piston position; it is an illustrative calculation, not a fit to the <isothermal gas syringe experiment>.

\Image[isothermal-piston-thermal-bath-temperature-lag.png]
{title=Conducting piston in a thermal bath and modeled temperature lag during expansion}

For a prescribed exponential <volume (thermodynamics)> change $V=V_i e^{\lambda t}$, define $\tau=C_V/G$, $s=t/\tau$ and $\epsilon=nR\lambda/G$. The dimensionless <temperature> $\theta=T/T_b$ obeys a <linear ordinary differential equation> with initial value $\theta(0)=1$:
$$
\frac{d\theta}{ds}=1-(1+\epsilon)\theta,\qquad
\theta(s)=\frac{1}{1+\epsilon}
+\frac{\epsilon}{1+\epsilon}e^{-(1+\epsilon)s}.
$$
The plotted expansion uses stipulated $C_V=(5/2)nR$ and stops at $V_f=2V_i$, at $s_f=\ln2/[(5/2)\epsilon]$. During expansion, the larger $\epsilon$ produces the larger <temperature> deficit; once the piston is held fixed, the deficit undergoes <exponential decay> as $e^{-(s-s_f)}$. Reducing $|\epsilon|$ makes the path more nearly an <isothermal process>, while increasing its duration. Reversing the piston with $\lambda<0$ produces a <temperature> excess and outward <heat transfer>; the small-$|\epsilon|$ regime is the intended near-isothermal approximation. Mechanical slowness alone does not ensure sufficient <heat transfer>.

= Boyle's law
{c}
{parent=Isothermal process}
{title2=$pV=\mathrm{constant}$}
{wiki}

= Boyle law
{c}
{synonym}

For a fixed amount of an ideal gas at constant temperature, pressure times volume is constant. Equivalently its density is proportional to pressure. In a dilute bubbly flow, this allows bubble volume and its contribution to <buoyancy> to increase as hydrostatic pressure decreases, provided gas mass is retained and dissolution is negligible.

= Isothermal compression
{parent=Isothermal process}

An <isothermal compression> reduces the <volume (thermodynamics)> of a <thermodynamic system> at fixed <temperature>. For a fixed-composition <ideal gas> compressed reversibly from $V_i$ to $V_f<V_i$, <isothermal ideal-gas work> gives
$$
W_{\rm by,rev}=nRT\ln(V_f/V_i)<0,\qquad
Q=W_{\rm by,rev}<0,\qquad \Delta U=0.
$$
The surroundings supply positive <work> $W_{\rm on}$, and the <gas> rejects the same amount of <heat> to the <heat bath>. Compressing $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ at $300\,\mathrm K$ from $20$ to $10\,\mathrm L$ requires approximately $1.729\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <work> input and rejects $1.729\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat>. If that <heat transfer> cannot keep up, the <temperature> rises: the process departs from <isothermal compression> toward <adiabatic compression>. The <finite-rate isothermal piston model> explains the required small <temperature> excess.

= Isothermal gas syringe experiment
{parent=Isothermal compression}

A <gas syringe> makes the distinction between a constant-<temperature> path and equal-<temperature> endpoints observable. The <Ideal Gas Law Apparatus (TD-8596A), Instruction Manual 012-08420B by PASCO scientific> specifies a TD-8596A <gas syringe> with a mechanical stop, an internal thermistor, an attached <pressure> sensor and data acquisition. Record the stop <volume (thermodynamics)> with the coupling disconnected, then set $40\,\mathrm{mL}$ and seal the coupling. Record <pressure> and <temperature>, compress quickly to the stop near $20\,\mathrm{mL}$, and hold until both readouts settle. The manual gives roughly $30\,\mathrm s$ for settling and about $0.5\,\mathrm s$ for the thermistor response. Releasing the plunger gives a second transient and a final <volume (thermodynamics)> to record.<Ideal Gas Law Apparatus (TD-8596A), Instruction Manual 012-08420B by PASCO scientific>[pp. 2–4: Introduction, Equipment Setup and Experiment 1 procedure]{ref}

The manual describes a <pressure> overshoot and <temperature> rise during rapid compression, followed by cooling at the held <volume (thermodynamics)>; the settled <pressure> remains higher than initially. This illustrates approximately <adiabatic compression> followed by <heat> rejection, rather than an <isothermal compression> throughout. Use the settled initial and compressed readings to test <Boyle's law>, correcting both syringe readings by the unmarked tubing <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_d$:
$$
p_i(V_{\rm read,i}+V_d)=p_f(V_{\rm read,f}+V_d).
$$
Use <absolute pressure>, not <gauge pressure>. A transient comparison of $pV/T$ must pair simultaneous <pressure> and <temperature> readings: the sensor lag can separate their peaks.<Ideal Gas Law Apparatus (TD-8596A), Instruction Manual 012-08420B by PASCO scientific>[pp. 4–5: Constant Temperature and Varying Temperature analyses; p. 8: answers 1–3]{ref}

For an additional test of the path, repeat with small compression steps, wait for stable <temperature> and <pressure> at every step, and repeat in reverse. Keep the sealed <amount of substance>, connecting tubing and ambient <temperature> fixed; prevent hand warming of the barrel and check for leaks by watching for continued <pressure> drift at held <volume (thermodynamics)> after <thermal equilibrium>. These are proposed controls, rather than reported measurements. Agreement of equilibrated points with <Boyle's law> supports the fixed-<temperature> <ideal gas law> approximation, but does not prove that the rapid segments between them were <isothermal processes>. A single local <temperature> sensor can miss <temperature gradients>; plunger <friction> can produce direction-dependent readings. The <pressure> and <volume (thermodynamics)> data estimate reversible <work> only insofar as the path approaches a <reversible thermodynamic process>; they do not directly measure <heat>. After motion stops, cooling at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> has zero boundary <work> and $Q=\Delta U<0$, even though the complete equal-endpoint-<temperature> <ideal gas> run has $\Delta U=0$.

= Gas syringe
{parent=Isothermal gas syringe experiment}

A <gas syringe> traps a <gas> in a graduated barrel with a movable plunger, allowing its <volume (thermodynamics)> to be varied and read. In an <isothermal gas syringe experiment>, the relevant total <volume (thermodynamics)> also includes any connected tubing and sensor cavity containing the same <gas>.

= Isothermal expansion
{parent=Isothermal process}

An <isothermal expansion> increases the <volume (thermodynamics)> of a <thermodynamic system> at fixed <temperature>. For a reversible <ideal gas> path, the <gas> delivers positive <work> and absorbs positive <heat>, with $Q=W_{\rm by,rev}=nRT\ln(V_f/V_i)$ and $\Delta U=0$. The <heat bath> supplies the expansion <energy>; without sufficient <heat transfer>, doing <work> instead lowers the <temperature>. This transfer is the hot step of a <Carnot cycle>, and it is why an expanding <gas> need not cool when its surroundings continuously replenish its <internal energy>.

An insulated <free expansion> into an evacuated compartment is different. In the idealization with no <heat> exchange, no external <work> and no container <energy> change, $Q=W_{\rm by}=0$. Since <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>, its final equilibrium <temperature> equals its initial value. The transient need not have a uniform <temperature> or <pressure>, so equal endpoint <temperatures> do not make it a reversible isotherm. Do not apply the logarithmic <isothermal ideal-gas work> formula to this irreversible process. Its <entropy> still increases by $\Delta S=nR\ln(V_f/V_i)$; <entropy change in free expansion> evaluates this <state function> difference using a hypothetical reversible path.<Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Free Expansion of Gas>[Equations 6.144–6.148 and the negligible-container-heat-capacity assumption]{ref}

For a <real gas>, even equal-<temperature> equilibrium endpoints need not have equal <internal energy>. An insulated <free expansion> can therefore change the <temperature>, while a controlled <isothermal expansion> must provide the <heat> needed for both its <internal energy> change and its delivered <work>. The <internal energy at constant temperature> article gives the <Van der Waals equation> example.<Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Van der Waals Gas>[Equations 6.163–6.166: attractive interactions and free-expansion cooling]{ref}

= Heat
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$Q$}
{wiki}

Heat is <energy> transferred across a <thermodynamic system>'s boundary because of a <temperature> difference. It is measured in <joules>, like <work> and <internal energy>. A hot cup transfers <heat> to cooler surroundings; its <temperature> describes its state, while the transferred <heat> describes a process. The cup stores <internal energy>, rather than a substance called <heat>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, University Physics Volume 2, section 1.4: heat and internal energy]{ref}

For a <closed system> with unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, choose $Q>0$ for <heat> entering and $W>0$ for <work> done by the system. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
\Delta U=Q-W,\qquad dU=\delta Q-\delta W.
$$
Here $U$ is the stored <internal energy>. For example, a sealed piston cylinder receiving $20\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat> and delivering $5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <work> gains $15\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <internal energy>. A <closed system> retains its matter while exchanging <energy>; it need not be an <isolated system>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-3-first-law-of-thermodynamics[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, First law of thermodynamics and sign conventions]{ref}

\Image[heat-work-internal-energy-balance.png]
{title=<Heat> input and <work> output determine the <internal energy> increase}
{description=The dashed boundary encloses a <closed system> in a sealed piston cylinder. The incoming arrow carries $Q=20\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat>; the outgoing arrow carries $W=5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <work> done by the system. With unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, $\Delta U=15\,\mathrm{kJ}$. No matter crosses the boundary.}

Unlike <internal energy>, <heat> is not a <state function>: different paths between the same equilibrium states can transfer different $Q$, with compensating differences in <work>. The symbol $\delta Q$ records this dependence on the path; there is no stored quantity whose difference is always $Q$. The two paths in <first law of thermodynamics> illustrate this directly. Supplied <heat> can raise <temperature> as <sensible heat>, change a <phase fraction> through <latent heat>, or help supply expansion <work>. Measuring <temperature> alone therefore does not determine transferred <heat>.

For a <reversible thermodynamic process>, $\delta Q_{\rm rev}=T\,dS$, where $T$ is absolute <temperature> and $S$ is <entropy>. This identification requires reversibility: an irreversible <adiabatic process> has $Q=0$ and can nevertheless increase <entropy>. Likewise $Q=\Delta H$, with <enthalpy> $H$, requires the particular fixed-<pressure> conditions in <enthalpy>. These distinctions make <heat> useful as a precise boundary-transfer quantity in <heat engines>, <refrigerators> and heating calculations.

Historically, James Prescott Joule's paddle experiments showed that stirring <water> with input <work> could raise its <temperature>, helping establish <conservation of energy>. The lesson is still useful: a <temperature> rise can result from <work> even without incoming <heat>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, OpenStax, section 1.4: Joule's paddle experiment]{ref}

= Sensible heat
{parent=Heat}
{title2=$Q_{\rm sens}$}
{wiki}

Sensible heat is transferred <heat> associated with a <temperature> change within a single <thermodynamic phase>. By contrast, <latent heat> can change the <phase fraction> while the <temperature> stays fixed during equilibrium <phase coexistence>. Neither name denotes an additional stored substance: both describe how transferred <heat> participates in a process.

For fixed <mass> $m$ and composition, use the <heat capacity> appropriate to the heating constraint. Under the <quasistatic process> assumptions in <heat capacity>, with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> and unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, fixed-<pressure> heating gives
$$
Q_{\rm sens}=m\int_{T_1}^{T_2}c_p(T)\,dT,
\qquad Q_{\rm sens}=mc_p(T_2-T_1)\quad\text{if }c_p\text{ is constant}.
$$
Here $c_p$ is <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>; fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> heating instead uses <specific heat capacity at constant volume> $c_V$. A cooling step has negative $Q_{\rm sens}$ with the sign convention in <heat>. A <temperature> difference has the same numerical value in units of the <kelvin> or <degree Celsius>. The <integral> follows by adding the small transfers $\delta Q=mc_p(T)\,dT$, rather than assuming a constant <heat capacity>.

As a stylized <ice>-to-<water> example for a fixed-composition <closed system> at fixed <pressure> $1\,\mathrm{atm}$, take $m=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$, an initial <temperature> of $-10\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, <melting> at $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and a final <temperature> of $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Assume constant <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> values $c_{\rm ice}=2.1\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $c_{\rm water}=4.18\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, and <latent heat of fusion> $L_f=334\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$. These are stipulated, rounded model values; ignore the container and <heat> losses, retain all the material, and assume only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> under the <quasistatic process> conditions above. The three steps are
$$
\begin{aligned}
Q_{\rm ice}&=0.5\times2.1\times10=10.5\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{\rm melt}&=0.5\times334=167\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{\rm water}&=0.5\times4.18\times20=41.8\,\mathrm{kJ},\\
Q_{\rm total}&=10.5+167+41.8=219.3\,\mathrm{kJ}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Only the first and third terms are <sensible heat>; the middle term is <latent heat>. The plateau begins after $10.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and ends after $177.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of total supplied <heat>. At $94\,\mathrm{kJ}$ total input, half the <mass> is <liquid> <water> and the <temperature> remains $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. The equilibrium <phase transition> <temperature> depends on <pressure>; this example stipulates a single fixed <pressure>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.5 Phase Changes>{ref}

\Image[sensible-and-latent-heating-curve.png]
{title=Heating $0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ of <ice> to <water>: <sensible heat> and <latent heat>}
{description=Illustrative fixed-<pressure> <heating curve> using the constant parameters above. The sloping portions raise <temperature>; the long $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ plateau changes the <phase fraction>. Total supplied <heat> is measured from the initial <ice> state and equals the <enthalpy> change under the stated assumptions.}

This split makes heating budgets easy to interpret: <melting> consumes $167/219.3\approx76.2\%$ of the supplied <heat> in this model despite causing no <temperature> rise. Within either single <thermodynamic phase>, the <heating curve> slope is $dT/dQ=1/(mc_p)$; a larger <heat capacity> makes the slope shallower. A flat interval represents changing <phase fraction>, so it cannot be modeled by applying $Q=mc_p\Delta T$ across the <phase transition>. The complementary constructed example in <latent heat> tracks that <phase fraction> explicitly.

= Heat transfer
{parent=Heat}
{wiki}

<Heat transfer> asks how quickly <heat> crosses a boundary and how the <temperature> varies along its path. Its principal mechanisms are <thermal conduction>, <convection> and <thermal radiation>. A warm wall can conduct <heat> through its interior while exchanging <heat> with moving air and emitting <thermal radiation> at its surfaces. Identifying these paths lets us estimate heating requirements and cooling times.

Distinguish the accumulated <heat> $Q$ from its rate $\dot Q=dQ/dt$, a <power> measured in $\mathrm W$. The <heat flux> $q$ is the rate per unit <area>, measured in $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$: a uniform normal <heat flux> across <area> $A$ gives $\dot Q=Aq$. The steady bar in <thermal conduction> carries $q=400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ through $A=0.01\,\mathrm{m^2}$, hence $\dot Q=4\,\mathrm W$; maintained for $60\,\mathrm s$, it transfers $Q=240\,\mathrm J$. This distinction makes a surface's intensity and a whole object's heating load easy to compare.

= Nusselt number
{c}
{parent=Heat transfer}
{title2=$\mathrm{Nu}$}
{wiki}

The <Nusselt number> is the ratio of total <heat transfer> to conductive <heat transfer> across the same length scale: $\mathrm{Nu}=qh/(k_T\Delta T)$, where $q$ is heat flux and $k_T$ is <thermal conductivity>.

= Thermal inertia
{parent=Heat transfer}
{title2=$\Gamma=\sqrt{k\rho c_p}$}

For a homogeneous material, thermal inertia is $\Gamma=\sqrt{k\rho c_p}$, where $k$ is <thermal conductivity>, $\rho$ is density and $c_p$ is specific <heat capacity>. It measures the resistance of the surface temperature to periodic heating and controls the phase lag relevant to the <Yarkovsky effect>.

= Radiative cooling
{parent=Heat transfer}
{wiki}

A body loses <internal energy> by emitting <thermal radiation> faster than it absorbs radiation or receives other heating. In a gravitating gas this can remove pressure support and allow further contraction.

= Compton cooling by the cosmic microwave background
{parent=Radiative cooling}
{title2=$t_C\propto(1+z)^{-4}/x_e$}

Hot electrons can transfer energy to <cosmic microwave background> photons through <Compton scattering>. For a fixed electron fraction $x_e$ and electron temperature well above the radiation temperature, the <optically thin gas cooling time> scales approximately as $(1+z)^{-4}/x_e$. Its volume loss scales with electron density, not with $n_H^2$, because the radiation density is independent of local gas density. The process tends toward the radiation temperature; it heats rather than cools electrons colder than that radiation.

= Dust thermal emission
{parent=Radiative cooling}

Dust grains emit thermal radiation after absorbing energy from radiation or collisions. Collisions can transfer thermal energy from dense gas to grains, whose radiation then cools the gas. This channel requires dust and does not operate in strictly pristine hydrogen-helium material without prior enrichment.

= Inverse Compton cooling
{parent=Radiative cooling}
{title2=$P_e=4\sigma_TcU_\gamma k_B(T_e-T_\gamma)/(m_ec^2)$}

Thermal electrons can transfer energy to lower-energy radiation photons through <Compton scattering>. In the nonrelativistic Thomson regime, the net cooling power per electron is $4\sigma_TcU_\gamma k_B(T_e-T_\gamma)/(m_ec^2)$ for a blackbody photon bath. A sufficiently hot electron population therefore loses energy; a colder population is heated instead. The <cosmic microwave background> radiation energy density grows as $(1+z)^4$, making this channel more important for high-redshift diffuse ionized gas.

= Astrophysical cooling function
{parent=Radiative cooling}
{title2=$\mathcal C=n_H^2\Lambda(T)$}

= Hydrogen-density-squared cooling function
{synonym}

An <astrophysical cooling function> packages radiative losses of a plasma into a temperature-dependent coefficient. In the hydrogen-density-squared convention, the volume energy loss is $n_H^2\Lambda(T)$, so the coefficient has units $\mathrm{erg\,cm^3\,s^{-1}}$. Its dependence on <ionization> state, chemical abundances and radiation field must be specified; a low-density collisional-equilibrium curve is not universal.

= Molecular line cooling
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}

Radiation from rotational and vibrational transitions of molecules removes gas thermal energy. In primordial gas below the atomic threshold, <molecular hydrogen> is a principal coolant; <hydrogen deuteride> can matter in suitable chemical conditions. Molecular formation, photodissociation, optical depth and density-dependent level populations control the rate. This allows some small systems below the atomic <virial temperature> threshold to form stars.

= Cooling suppression by photoionization
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}

<Photoionization> changes the ion population, often removing bound-state coolants at temperatures where collisional equilibrium would retain them. It can therefore suppress primordial line-cooling peaks. It also supplies <photoionization heating>. The net loss is cooling minus heating and depends on radiation spectrum, intensity, shielding and gas density. A universal density-independent function of temperature alone is not generally sufficient for irradiated gas.

= Optically thin gas cooling time
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}
{title2=$t_{\rm cool}=3\chi k_BT/(2n_H\Lambda)$}

= Radiative gas cooling time
{synonym}

For a nonrelativistic ideal gas with $n_{\rm tot}$ thermal particles per volume, divide its thermal energy $3n_{\rm tot}k_BT/2$ by the optically thin volume radiation loss. In the $n_H^2$ cooling convention this becomes $3\chi k_BT/(2n_H\Lambda)$, where $\chi=n_{\rm tot}/n_H$. Density scaling and ionization-dependent particle count must be kept consistent.

= Cooling-to-free-fall equality curve
{parent=Optically thin gas cooling time}
{title2=$n_{H,\rm crit}=\frac{32Gm_p}{3\pi Xf_g}\left(\frac{3\chi k_BT}{2\Lambda}\right)^2$}

Equating the optically thin cooling time to the uniform-sphere free-fall time gives a critical gas density proportional to $T^2/\Lambda^2$. With <hydrogen mass fraction> $X$, particle ratio $\chi$ and gravitating gas fraction $f_g$, it is $[32Gm_p/(3\pi Xf_g)][3\chi k_BT/(2\Lambda)]^2$. At fixed temperature, gas above this density cools faster than free fall. A smaller gravitating gas fraction moves the boundary upward because gravity is faster at a fixed gas density.

= Primordial atomic cooling curve
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}
{title2=$\Lambda_{\rm H,He}(T)$}

The <primordial atomic cooling curve> describes hydrogen-helium gas without heavy elements. Near the <hydrogen> excitation threshold it rises sharply, then shows <hydrogen> and <helium> excitation/<ionization> features. Bound-state losses weaken when both elements become highly ionized. At sufficiently high temperatures <thermal bremsstrahlung> gives a slowly rising tail. Composition, density convention and <ionization> assumptions fix the numerical normalization.

= Atomic line cooling
{parent=Astrophysical cooling function}
{title2=$\mathcal C_{\rm line}=\sum n_en_iq_{ij}(T)\,E_{ij}$}

<Atomic line cooling> removes gas kinetic energy by <collisional excitation> followed by photon escape. It is strongest where atoms or ions have bound <electrons> and thermally accessible transitions. If photons are trapped, or if the relevant atoms are fully ionized, the simple optically thin line-cooling rate no longer gives the same loss.

= Metal-line cooling
{parent=Atomic line cooling}
{title2=$\Lambda(T,Z)$}

Heavy elements provide many ionic transitions at temperatures where <hydrogen> and <helium> have lost their most effective line emitters. <Metal-line cooling> can therefore increase the plasma cooling function substantially and broaden its temperature features. Low-energy fine-structure transitions also permit cooling below the <hydrogen> atomic threshold when the relevant ions and <electrons> are present.

= Heat flux density
{title2=$\mathbf q$}
{parent=Heat}

= Heat flux
{synonym}

<Heat flux density> is a <vector> $\mathbf q$ describing the rate of <heat transfer> per unit <area>, measured in $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. Through a planar face of <area> $A$ with uniform normal component $q$, the signed rate is $\dot Q=Aq$ in $\mathrm W$; positive $q$ means <heat> travels in the chosen positive normal direction. <Fourier's law> determines the conductive contribution. In a bar with a constant cross-sectional <area>, steady <thermal conduction> without internal sources or side losses makes both $q$ and $\dot Q$ constant along the bar. Constant $\dot Q$ need not mean constant $q$ when the cross-sectional <area> varies.

= Thermal conduction
{parent=Heat}
{wiki}

= Heat conduction
{synonym}

<Thermal conduction> transfers <heat> through microscopic interactions without bulk transport of matter. To predict the heating load through a bar, combine <Fourier's law> with <conservation of energy> and the imposed end <temperatures>. In the <isotropic> model with positive <thermal conductivity> $k$, the conductive <heat flux> points from high to low <temperature>, opposite to the <temperature gradient>. A spoon warming along its handle and <heat> escaping through a wall are familiar examples.

Consider a bar of <length> $L=0.10\,\mathrm m$, constant cross-sectional <area> $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and constant <thermal conductivity> $k=2\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Maintain its ends at $T_h=40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and $T_c=20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, with $x$ increasing toward the colder end. Assume a <steady state>, one-dimensional <thermal conduction>, no internal sources and no <heat transfer> through the sides. The <temperature> falls by $20\,\mathrm K$ over $0.10\,\mathrm m$, giving
$$
\frac{dT}{dx}=-200\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}},\qquad
q=-k\frac{dT}{dx}=400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}},\qquad
\dot Q=Aq=4\,\mathrm W.
$$
The difference between the two readings in <degree Celsius> units is the same $20$-<kelvin> difference. The positive <heat flux> carries <heat> toward increasing $x$.

\Image[fourier-conduction-steady-bar.png]
{title=Steady <thermal conduction> through a bar: declining <temperature>, constant <heat flux>}
{description=An <isotropic> bar with stipulated constant <thermal conductivity> $k=2\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, <length> $0.10\,\mathrm m$ and cross-sectional <area> $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Its ends remain at $40$ and $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and its sides exchange no <heat>. The lower panel distinguishes $q=400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ from $\dot Q=4\,\mathrm W$.}

The general solution under these assumptions is
$$
T(x)=T_h-\frac{T_h-T_c}{L}x,\qquad
q=\frac{k(T_h-T_c)}{L},\qquad
\dot Q=\frac{kA(T_h-T_c)}{L}.
$$
A proof outline is enough to explain the straight line: <conservation of energy> in any small slice, with no accumulation, sources or side losses, gives $d\dot Q/dx=0$. Constant cross-sectional <area> gives $dq/dx=0$; constant <thermal conductivity> and <Fourier's law> then give $d^2T/dx^2=0$. Two <integrations> and the end <boundary conditions> determine the displayed <affine function>. This is why a visually simple <temperature> profile can encode a continuous flow of <energy>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node117.html[E. M. Greitzer, Z. S. Spakovszky and I. A. Waitz, MIT Thermodynamics and Propulsion, section 16.3: steady one-dimensional conduction]{ref}

Doubling the <length> halves the rate, while doubling the cross-sectional <area> doubles it, at fixed end <temperatures> and <thermal conductivity>. This explains why a long narrow support can limit unwanted <heat transfer>, while a short broad path can help remove <heat>. Joining materials leads to <thermal resistance> in series. During initial warming, <internal energy> can accumulate and the <temperature> profile need not be a straight line; the time dependence is described by the <heat equation> and <thermal diffusivity>.

Now join two layers, each of <length> $L_1=L_2=0.10\,\mathrm m$, with constant cross-sectional <area> $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and stipulated constant <thermal conductivities> $k_1=1$ and $k_2=4\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Keep the outer ends at $40$ and $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Assume a <steady state>, one-dimensional <thermal conduction>, no internal sources or side losses, and perfect contact: the <temperature> is continuous at the interface, with no extra contact drop. For each layer, use the area-specific <thermal resistance> $r_i=L_i/k_i$ and $R_i=r_i/A$. The series calculation gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
r_1+r_2=\frac{L_1}{k_1}+\frac{L_2}{k_2}
=0.100+0.025=0.125\,\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}},\\
R_1+R_2=\frac{r_1+r_2}{A}=12.5\,\mathrm{K\,W^{-1}},\\
q=\frac{20\,\mathrm K}{0.125\,\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}}}
=160\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}},\qquad \dot Q=Aq=1.6\,\mathrm W,\\
\Delta T_1=qr_1=16\,\mathrm K,\qquad
\Delta T_2=qr_2=4\,\mathrm K,\qquad
T_{\rm interface}=40\,{}^\circ\mathrm C-16\,\mathrm K=24\,{}^\circ\mathrm C.
\end{aligned}
$$

\Image[layered-thermal-conduction.png]
{title=Two layers in series: continuous <heat flux>, different <temperature gradients>}
{description=Each layer is $0.10\,\mathrm m$ long with the same cross-sectional <area> $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ and perfect contact. Stipulated constant <thermal conductivities> are $1$ and $4\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The <temperature> drops by $16\,\mathrm K$ and $4\,\mathrm K$, reaching $24\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ at the interface, while $q=160\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ and $\dot Q=1.6\,\mathrm W$ throughout.}

The <heat flux> is continuous: otherwise <energy> would accumulate at the interface. Yet <Fourier's law> gives different <temperature gradients>, $dT/dx=-q/k_1=-160\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$ and $dT/dx=-q/k_2=-40\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$. Lower <thermal conductivity> requires a steeper <temperature gradient> to carry the same <heat flux>; equal <lengths> therefore give drops in the ratio $4:1$. A kink in the <temperature> profile records the change of material while perfect contact keeps the <temperature> itself continuous.

= Thermal resistance
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{title2=$R_{\rm th}=\Delta T/\dot Q$}

<Thermal resistance> measures the <temperature> drop required to drive a given rate of <heat transfer>. For the steady one-dimensional bar model in <thermal conduction>,
$$
R_{\rm th}=\frac{T_h-T_c}{\dot Q}=\frac{L}{kA}
\quad[\mathrm{K\,W^{-1}}],\qquad
r=AR_{\rm th}=\frac{L}{k}
\quad[\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}}],\qquad
q=\frac{T_h-T_c}{r}.
$$
Here $r$ relates the <temperature> drop to <heat flux>, while $R_{\rm th}$ relates it to the rate of <heat transfer>. The single bar above has $r=0.050\,\mathrm{m^2\,K\,W^{-1}}$ and $R_{\rm th}=5\,\mathrm{K\,W^{-1}}$, so its $20\,\mathrm K$ drop drives $4\,\mathrm W$.

To see why series <thermal resistances> add, apply <Fourier's law> in each layer to obtain $\Delta T_i=\dot Q R_i$. With no accumulation or side losses the rate of <heat transfer> is the same through both, so adding the drops gives $T_h-T_c=\dot Q(R_1+R_2)$. This lets us size layered walls without solving a new <differential equation> for every layer. The structure matches <Ohm's law>: <temperature> difference plays the role of <electric potential difference>, the rate of <heat transfer> plays the role of <electric current>, and <thermal resistance> plays the role of <electrical resistance>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node118.html[E. M. Greitzer, Z. S. Spakovszky and I. A. Waitz, MIT Thermodynamics and Propulsion, section 16.4: series thermal resistances and the electrical analogy]{ref}

= Temperature dipole around an insulating sphere
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{title2=$T=T_0+(\mathbf H\cdot\mathbf r)(1+a^3/(2r^3))$}

In the absence of heat advection, an insulating <sphere> of radius $a$ in a uniform remote <temperature> gradient $\mathbf H$ has the displayed exterior <harmonic function> as its temperature field. The reflected dipole term makes $\partial_rT=0$ at $r=a$. The surface temperature is $T_0+(3/2)a\mathbf H\cdot\mathbf n$, so the tangential gradient is amplified by $3/2$. If the sphere has <thermal conductivity> ratio $K$ relative to its surroundings, temperature and heat-flux continuity instead give surface gradient factor $3/(2+K)$.

= Isobaric conductive cooling in mass coordinates
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{title2=$T_\tau+\Lambda(T)/T-\lambda_0\partial_m(T^{\alpha-1}T_m)=0$}

With fixed <pressure> $p$, let $A=\mu p/\mathcal R$, so $\rho=A/T$. A material mass coordinate has $\partial_x=\rho\partial_m$ and the material time derivative becomes $\partial_t|_m$. The energy and continuity equations then give the displayed equation with $\tau=Ct$ and $C=(\gamma-1)(\mu/\mathcal R)^2p/\gamma$. A mass integral from a fixed origin is material only when the <mass flux> there vanishes; otherwise subtract its time-integrated flux to label parcels. Uniform <pressure> is a low-Mach approximation when sound crossing is faster than thermal evolution.

= Error-function isobaric cooling front
{parent=Isobaric conductive cooling in mass coordinates}
{title2=$T=T_0\operatorname{erf}\!\left(m/(2\sqrt{\lambda_0\tau})\right)$}

For conductivity $\lambda=\lambda_0T$, no cooling in the hot phase and a zero-<temperature> boundary at $m=0$, the mass-coordinate equation is linear diffusion. Its similarity profile solves $f''+\xi f'/2=0$, $f(0)=0$, $f(\infty)=1$, giving the displayed <error function>. The thermal layer broadens as the square root of time. Conducted energy enters the cold radiating reservoir at rate per area $L=\lambda_0A T_m(0)=A T_0\sqrt{\lambda_0}/\sqrt{\pi\tau}$, hence $L\propto t^{-1/2}$.

= Temperature gradient
{title2=$\nabla T$}
{parent=Thermal conduction}

The temperature gradient is the <gradient> $\nabla T$ of the <temperature> field. It points in the direction of fastest temperature increase.

= Fourier's law
{title2=$\mathbf q=-k\nabla T$}
{c}
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{wiki}

<Fourier's law> predicts the local conductive <heat flux> from the local <temperature gradient>. In a <continuum approximation> with <isotropic> <thermal conductivity> $k>0$, its <vector> form and one-dimensional form are
$$
\mathbf q=-k\nabla T,\qquad
q=-k\frac{dT}{dx},\qquad
\dot Q=Aq\quad\text{for uniform normal flux over area }A.
$$
The <heat flux> $q$ has units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, the <temperature gradient> has units $\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$ and the <thermal conductivity> has units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The rate of <heat transfer> $\dot Q$ has units $\mathrm W$. These units distinguish a local material response from the total <power> crossing a surface.

The minus sign makes <heat> flow toward lower <temperature>: for $k>0$, $\mathbf q\cdot\nabla T=-k|\nabla T|^2\leq0$. In the worked bar in <thermal conduction>, $dT/dx=-200\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}}$ therefore gives $q=+400\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. Reversing the end <temperatures> reverses the <heat flux>. A zero <temperature gradient> gives zero conductive <heat flux> in this model.

<Fourier's law> is a constitutive relation expressing measured material behavior; it is not a consequence of <conservation of energy> alone. It applies locally and does not require a <steady state>. The straight-line bar solution additionally assumes constant <thermal conductivity>, constant cross-sectional <area>, no sources or side losses, and fixed end <temperatures>. <Anisotropic> materials require the directional response described by the <thermal conductivity tensor>. The <scalar> $k$ used in the examples expresses the stated <isotropy> assumption. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node116.html[E. M. Greitzer, Z. S. Spakovszky and I. A. Waitz, MIT Thermodynamics and Propulsion, section 16.2: Fourier's law and thermal conductivity]{ref}

Historically, <Joseph Fourier>'s \i[Théorie analytique de la chaleur] was published in 1822. In Chapter II, article 112, he writes the <heat> crossing a spherical surface as the product of <thermal conductivity>, elapsed time, surface <area> and the negative radial <temperature gradient>; article 113 balances the incoming and outgoing <heat> to obtain the <heat equation> for a <sphere>. The enduring idea is this connection between a local flow rule and a global prediction of changing <temperature>. https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Théorie_analytique_de_la_chaleur[Joseph Fourier, Théorie analytique de la chaleur, Firmin Didot, 1822]{ref} https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Théorie_analytique_de_la_chaleur/Chapitre_2[Fourier, 1822, Chapter II, articles 112–113]{ref}

= Thermal conductivity
{title2=$k$}
{parent=Fourier's law}
{wiki}

<Thermal conductivity> is the material response coefficient in <Fourier's law>. For an <isotropic> material it is a <scalar> $k$, with units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$; larger positive $k$ means a larger conductive <heat flux> for the same <temperature gradient>. It can depend on <temperature> and composition. Direction-dependent response in an <anisotropic> material is described by a <thermal conductivity tensor>. The two worked examples use stipulated positive constants, rather than measured values for named materials.

<Thermal conductivity> describes material response, whereas <thermal resistance> also includes geometry: for the steady bar, $R_{\rm th}=L/(kA)$. Doubling $k$ at fixed <length>, cross-sectional <area> and end <temperatures> halves <thermal resistance> and doubles the rate of <heat transfer>. Doubling <length> changes <thermal resistance> without changing the material's <thermal conductivity>. This distinction lets a designer improve cooling either by choosing a higher <thermal conductivity> or by shortening and widening the path. It also explains why the less conductive layer in <thermal resistance> takes the greater <temperature> drop.

<Thermal diffusivity> answers a different question: how rapidly a <temperature> disturbance spreads. Its usual model $\kappa=k/(\rho c_p)$ also involves <mass density> $\rho$ and <specific heat capacity> $c_p$, so equal <thermal conductivities> need not imply equal rates of warming.

= Thermal conductivity tensor
{parent=Thermal conductivity}
{title2=$\mathbf q=-a\nabla T$}

The thermal conductivity tensor maps a temperature gradient to the negative heat-flux density. In passive reciprocal conduction it is symmetric positive definite. This permits direction-dependent transport while ensuring positive dissipated energy.

= Thermal diffusivity
{title2=$\kappa$}
{parent=Thermal conduction}
{wiki}

<Thermal diffusivity> measures how rapidly <thermal conduction> smooths a <temperature> difference inside a material. <Thermal conductivity> measures the <heat flux> driven by a specified <temperature gradient>; <thermal diffusivity> also accounts for the <heat capacity> that must be warmed. A material can carry substantial <heat> yet change <temperature> slowly because it stores substantial <internal energy> per <kelvin>.

Write $\alpha=\kappa$ for <thermal diffusivity>, reserving $k$ for <thermal conductivity>. In a homogeneous <isotropic> material,
$$
\alpha=\frac{k}{\rho c}=\frac{k}{C_{\mathrm{vol}}},\qquad
[\alpha]=\frac{\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}}{\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}\,K^{-1}}}
=\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}.
$$
Here $\rho$ is <mass density>, $c$ is <specific heat capacity> for the relevant mechanical constraint, and $C_{\mathrm{vol}}=\rho c$ is <volumetric heat capacity>. The familiar $k/(\rho c_p)$ uses <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>. For ordinary <solids> with negligible thermal expansion, the difference between the two constrained <specific heat capacities> is often neglected. A rigid fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> model instead uses <specific heat capacity at constant volume>; a compressible moving <fluid> requires the accompanying <work> and transport terms. Thus the constraint is part of the approximation, not a choice made solely from the units. <Light and Laser Flash Analyzers by NETZSCH Analyzing and Testing>[NETZSCH, Principle of the LFA Method: conductivity equals diffusivity times specific heat capacity times density]{ref}

Assume a stationary material in the <continuum approximation>, local thermal equilibration on times shorter than those resolved, no internal heating, no <phase transition>, and negligible <convection> and mechanical <work>. Combining <conservation of energy> with <Fourier's law> gives the one-dimensional balance
$$
\rho c\frac{\partial T}{\partial t}=-\frac{\partial q}{\partial x}
=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(k\frac{\partial T}{\partial x}\right).
$$
If $k,\rho,c$ are constant over the region and <temperature> excursion, this becomes the <heat equation> $\partial_tT=\alpha\partial_x^2T$. If $k$ varies, retain the differentiated product; replacing it with $k\partial_x^2T$ loses a term. <Anisotropic> materials need a <thermal conductivity tensor>. The scalar constant-$\alpha$ examples below deliberately exclude these complications. <Rear-surface integral method for calculating thermal diffusivity from laser flash experiments by Elliot J. Carr>[Carr, section 1: homogeneous isotropic constant-property assumptions; section 2, equations (1)–(3): insulated one-dimensional heat equation]{ref}

For an original comparison, stipulate two model materials with equal <thermal conductivity> $k=2\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and equal <mass density> $\rho=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, but <specific heat capacities> $c_A=1000$ and $c_B=4000\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Then
$$
\alpha_A=2\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\alpha_B=5\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}.
$$
At the same <temperature gradient>, both have the same instantaneous <heat flux> by <Fourier's law>. At the same initial <temperature> profile and <boundary conditions>, material B takes four times as long to smooth a given spatial pattern. With fixed end <temperatures>, identical geometry and no sources, both eventually have the same steady profile and <heat flux>. The larger <heat capacity> changes the transient, not this <steady state>. These are stipulated parameters, not measurements of named substances.

= Prandtl number
{c}
{parent=Thermal diffusivity}
{title2=$\operatorname{Pr}=\nu/\kappa_T$}
{wiki}

The <Prandtl number> is the ratio of <kinematic viscosity> to <thermal diffusivity>. It compares the rates at which momentum and <temperature> variations diffuse. In thermal-diffusion units the <Boussinesq equations> have momentum inertia multiplied by $\operatorname{Pr}^{-1}$; large, rather than small, <Prandtl number> supplies the usual instantaneous <Stokes flow> limit.

= Thermal diffusion time
{title2=$L^2/\kappa$}
{parent=Thermal diffusivity}

The <thermal diffusion time> $t_D=L^2/\alpha$, with $\alpha=\kappa$ the <thermal diffusivity>, is a characteristic scale for smoothing a <temperature> variation over <length> $L$. It is the thermal instance of the <diffusive time scale>. Rescaling $x=L\widehat x$ and $t=(L^2/\alpha)\widehat t$ removes $L$ and $\alpha$ from the constant-coefficient <heat equation>. Doubling <length> therefore multiplies comparable internal equilibration times by four; increasing <thermal diffusivity> by four divides them by four.

This scale is not a universal time of complete <thermal equilibrium>. A nonzero <Fourier mode> decays exponentially and never becomes exactly zero at finite time. The geometry, <boundary conditions>, initial pattern and accepted residual determine the numerical factor. For example, an insulated bar's slowest nonuniform <Fourier mode> has decay time $L^2/(\pi^2\alpha)$, and reducing its amplitude to a fraction $\varepsilon$ requires
$$
t_\varepsilon=\frac{L^2}{\pi^2\alpha}\log\frac1\varepsilon,
\qquad 0<\varepsilon<1.
$$
External <heat transfer> can impose a longer cooling time even when internal <temperature> differences smooth quickly. <Thermal diffusivity> alone does not determine cooling to the room's <temperature>.

= Thermal diffusion rate of a Fourier mode
{parent=Thermal diffusion time}
{title2=$\beta=\xi k^2$}

With <thermal diffusivity> $\xi$, a <Fourier mode> of angular <wavenumber> $k$ decays at rate $\beta=\xi k^2$. To avoid confusing this $k$ with <thermal conductivity>, use $\alpha=\xi$ and angular <wavenumber> $K$ below: the same rate is $\beta=\alpha K^2$, with units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$.

Consider an insulated bar $0\leq x\leq L$ with constant <thermal diffusivity>, no sources and no <heat flux> through either end or the sides. An original initial <temperature> profile and its exact evolution are
$$
T(x,0)=\overline T+A_0\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L},\qquad
T(x,t)=\overline T+A_0e^{-\alpha K_n^2t}\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L},\qquad
K_n=\frac{n\pi}{L},\quad n=1,2,\ldots.
$$
The <cosine> has zero <temperature gradient> at both ends, satisfying the insulated <Neumann boundary conditions>. Its spatial mean is zero, so <conservation of energy> leaves the mean <temperature> $\overline T$ unchanged while the nonuniform part disappears. A short derivation substitutes $T=\overline T+A(t)\cos(K_nx)$ into the <heat equation>: the second spatial <derivative> supplies $-K_n^2$, hence $A'=-\alpha K_n^2A$. The <exponential decay> time is $\tau_n=1/(\alpha K_n^2)$; doubling angular <wavenumber> quarters this time. If cycles-per-<length> <spatial frequency> $f_x$ is used instead, $K=2\pi f_x$ and the rate is $4\pi^2\alpha f_x^2$.

\Image[thermal-diffusivity-insulated-fourier-mode-decay.png]
{title=Insulated-bar <Fourier mode> decay: equal <thermal conductivity>, different <heat capacities>}
{description=Original constant-property model with $L=0.010\,\mathrm m$ and $n=1$. The normalized initial <temperature> profile is identical for both stipulated materials in <thermal diffusivity>. The larger <volumetric heat capacity> gives a smaller <thermal diffusivity> and four times the decay time. Profiles at $t=10\,\mathrm s$ and amplitude histories are analytical predictions, not measured records.}

For the two model materials in <thermal diffusivity> and $L=0.010\,\mathrm m$, $\tau_{1,A}\approx5.07\,\mathrm s$ and $\tau_{1,B}\approx20.3\,\mathrm s$. After $10\,\mathrm s$, the remaining amplitude fractions are approximately $0.139$ and $0.610$. A narrow hot-and-cold pattern disappears faster than a broad one because its <temperature> curvature, and hence the imbalance in conductive <heat flux>, is larger.

= Thermal diffusion length
{parent=Thermal diffusion time}
{title2=$\ell(t)=\sqrt{\alpha t}$}

The <thermal diffusion length> $\ell(t)=\sqrt{\alpha t}$ is the characteristic <length> over which <thermal conduction> changes a <temperature> distribution during time $t$ in the constant-<thermal-diffusivity> model. It is a scale, not a sharp front or distance travelled at a constant speed: the <heat equation> has <infinite propagation speed> mathematically. Different definitions of penetration depth introduce numerical factors. In particular, the normalized one-dimensional <heat kernel> has spatial <variance> $2\alpha t$, so its root-mean-square width is $\sqrt{2\alpha t}$.

\Image[thermal-diffusivity-length-time-scaling.png]
{title=<Thermal diffusion length> grows as the square root of time; <thermal diffusion time> grows as <length> squared}
{description=Original predictions for the two stipulated materials in <thermal diffusivity>. Left: $\ell=\sqrt{\alpha t}$, with four times the time needed to double <length>. Right: $t_D=L^2/\alpha$ on logarithmic axes. Equal <thermal conductivity> with four times the <volumetric heat capacity> halves the reached <length> at a given time and quadruples the time at a given <length>. The scale $t_D$ differs from the insulated-bar decay time by the factor $\pi^2$.}

At $t=10\,\mathrm s$, the model materials have <thermal diffusion lengths> $4.47$ and $2.24\,\mathrm{mm}$. For $L=10\,\mathrm{mm}$ their <thermal diffusion times> are $50$ and $200\,\mathrm s$. These values describe the same <heat equation> as the <thermal diffusion rate of a Fourier mode>; the different numerical factors come from the definition of the scale.

= Laser flash analysis
{parent=Thermal diffusivity}
{wiki}

<Laser flash analysis> measures <thermal diffusivity> by heating one face of a specimen briefly and recording the opposite face's <temperature> rise. The original 1961 experiment used a flash tube rather than a <laser>: a blackened thin metal specimen sat in an opaque ceramic holder, with <thermocouple> wires pressed against its rear face. An amplifier, oscilloscope and camera recorded the signal. Uniform front illumination and the black coating promoted uniform absorption; the opaque low-<thermal-conductivity> holder blocked direct rear illumination and reduced edge losses. The delayed rear response reveals internal <thermal conduction> rather than direct heating of the sensor. <Flash Method of Determining Thermal Diffusivity, Heat Capacity, and Thermal Conductivity by W. J. Parker and others>[Parker, Jenkins, Butler and Abbott, pp. 1682–1683, Experimental Procedure and figure 3: holder, flash, thermocouple and recording chain]{ref}

A modern version uses a <laser> or flash lamp, a specimen of measured thickness $L$, controlled initial <temperature>, and a rear-facing <infrared> detector with a synchronized recorder. The readout is a <temperature> rise versus time, starting from a pre-pulse baseline. <Thermal conductivity> additionally requires <mass density> and <specific heat capacity>; it is not obtained from timing alone. <Light and Laser Flash Analyzers by NETZSCH Analyzing and Testing>[NETZSCH, Principle of the LFA Method: opposite-face infrared readout and conductivity product]{ref}

Under ideal one-dimensional, insulated, constant-property assumptions, instantaneous uniform absorption in a very thin front layer gives the normalized rear rise
$$
R(t)=\frac{T(L,t)-T_0}{T_\infty-T_0}
=1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^n
\exp\left(-\frac{n^2\pi^2\alpha t}{L^2}\right),\qquad t>0.
$$
Here $T_0$ is the initial uniform <temperature> and $T_\infty$ is the final uniform <temperature> in the insulated model. Fitting this curve estimates <thermal diffusivity>; each term has the same $\alpha K_n^2$ decay as the insulated-bar <Fourier modes>. Equivalently, the half-rise time $t_{1/2}$ solves $R(t_{1/2})=1/2$. Writing $s=\alpha t/L^2$, the series gives $s_{1/2}\approx0.13879$, hence
$$
\alpha\approx0.13879\frac{L^2}{t_{1/2}}.
$$
This coefficient comes from the stated ideal <heat equation> solution, not from an empirical sensor calibration. It has been independently checked by solving the series equation. Absolute absorbed pulse <energy> cancels from the normalized curve; determining <heat capacity> from its absolute height needs additional information. <Rear-surface integral method for calculating thermal diffusivity from laser flash experiments by Elliot J. Carr>[Carr, section 2, equations (4)–(7): rear-face series, half-rise time and corrected dimensionless constant 1.370]{ref}

Parker and colleagues identify finite flash duration, <heat> loss, nonuniform illumination, recording bandwidth and electrical interference as practical limits. A specimen too thin responds during the pulse; one too thick allows losses before the rear rise finishes. They shielded the electrical chain and recorded a baseline; their reported tests were preliminary metal measurements near ambient and moderately elevated <temperatures>. <Flash Method of Determining Thermal Diffusivity, Heat Capacity, and Thermal Conductivity by W. J. Parker and others>[Parker and colleagues, pp. 1682–1684: signal distortion, shielding, thickness tradeoff and Conclusions]{ref}

Useful additional controls are to measure thickness at several points, verify the recorder's time scale and detector response independently, record a no-pulse baseline, and repeat at different pulse <energies> while avoiding <phase transitions>. Compare different thicknesses of the same homogeneous material: ideal half-rise times scale as $L^2$. Failure of these checks can expose finite-pulse effects, sensor delay, optical transmission, coating storage or edge losses; a thickness error is especially consequential because the inferred <thermal diffusivity> depends on $L^2$. These are proposed diagnostic controls, not invented measurements. When the pulse is not short or the sample exchanges appreciable <heat> with its surroundings, fit a model that includes those effects instead of applying the ideal half-rise coefficient unchanged.

= Stefan–Boltzmann law
{title2=$F=\epsilon\sigma T^4$}
{c}
{parent=Heat}
{wiki}

The Stefan–Boltzmann law gives the thermal-radiation flux emitted by a surface as $F=\epsilon\sigma T^4$, where $\epsilon$ is its <emissivity> and $\sigma$ is the Stefan–Boltzmann constant.

= Stefan-Boltzmann constant
{parent=Stefan–Boltzmann law}
{c}
{title2=$\sigma$}

The <Stefan-Boltzmann constant> $\sigma$ is the proportionality constant in the blackbody flux $F=\sigma T^4$. The blackbody <radiation pressure> is $p_{\rm rad}=4\sigma T^4/(3c)$.

= Emissivity
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Stefan–Boltzmann law}
{wiki}

Emissivity is the ratio of the thermal radiation emitted by a surface to that emitted by an ideal black body at the same <temperature>.

= Radiative equilibrium
{parent=Stefan–Boltzmann law}
{wiki}

A body is in radiative equilibrium when absorbed radiative power equals emitted radiative power. A surface absorbing flux $F$ and emitting as a grey body has equilibrium temperature $T=(F/(\epsilon\sigma))^{1/4}$.

= Heat capacity
{parent=Heat}
{title2=$C_X=(\delta Q/dT)_X$}
{wiki}

Heat capacity measures the <heat> supplied per small <temperature> increase along a specified process:
$$
C_X=\left(\frac{\delta Q}{dT}\right)_X.
$$
The subscript $X$ records the constraint, such as fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> or fixed <pressure>. The units are $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. The symbol $\delta Q$ emphasizes that <heat> is a transfer along a path, rather than a <state function> with a differential $dQ$. Consequently, an unspecified <heat capacity> does not determine the required <heat>.

For a <closed system> of fixed composition undergoing a <quasistatic process>, assume that only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> occurs, with no <friction> and no change in bulk <kinetic energy> or <potential energy>. The <first law of thermodynamics> and $H=U+pV$, where $U$ is <internal energy> and $H$ is <enthalpy>, then give
$$
\delta Q=dU+p\,dV=dH-V\,dp,\qquad
C_V=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}\right)_V,\qquad
C_p=\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial T}\right)_p.
$$
These <partial derivatives> define <heat capacity at constant volume> and <heat capacity at constant pressure>, with the amount and composition also held fixed. Integrating along the selected constraint gives $Q=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_X(T)\,dT$ within a single <thermodynamic phase>; $Q=C_X\Delta T$ requires constant $C_X$. <Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>{ref}

For a computed example, take a <monatomic gas> in the classical <ideal gas> regime, with <amount of substance> $n=1\,\mathrm{mol}$ and stipulated <molar gas constant> $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. The <equipartition theorem> gives $U=3nRT/2$, and the <ideal gas law> gives $pV=nRT$. Thus
$$
C_V=\frac32nR=12.471\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},\qquad
C_p=\frac52nR=20.785\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}.
$$
For the same <temperature> increase $\Delta T=10\,\mathrm K$, the two processes require
$$
Q_V=124.71\,\mathrm J,\qquad Q_p=207.85\,\mathrm J,\qquad
\Delta U_V=\Delta U_p=124.71\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_V=0,\quad W_p=p\Delta V=nR\Delta T=83.14\,\mathrm J.
$$
Here <work> done by the <gas> is positive. At fixed <pressure>, the extra <heat> supplies expansion <work>; the <internal energy> increase is the same because <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>. Their final <volume (thermodynamics)> values differ. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-5-heat-capacities-of-an-ideal-gas[OpenStax, Heat capacities of an ideal gas]{ref}

\Image[heat-capacity-constraints.png]
{title=Required <heat> depends on the constraint and the amount of material}
{description=Left: one <mole> of classical <monatomic gas> with the stipulated <molar gas constant> above; the shaded difference is expansion <work> at fixed <pressure>. Right: the illustrative fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> body from <specific heat capacity>, with <mass> $2\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $c_V=400\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Each curve starts at zero accumulated <heat>, rather than zero absolute <internal energy>.}

The practical value of <heat capacity> is that a measured <heat> input and <temperature> response can determine it. Once its constraint and <temperature> dependence are known, it predicts heating requirements and supplies <entropy> changes through <entropy from heat capacity>. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1[Tong, Statistical Physics, heat capacity as a measurable response]{ref}

= Molar heat capacity
{parent=Heat capacity}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm m}=C/n$}

Molar heat capacity is <heat capacity> divided by <amount of substance> $n$, with units $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Its constraint must still be specified: $C_{V,\mathrm m}=C_V/n$ and $C_{p,\mathrm m}=C_p/n$. For the classical <monatomic gas> example in <heat capacity>, these are $3R/2$ and $5R/2$, where $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. Some textbooks use $C_V$ and $C_p$ for these per-<mole> quantities; the convention here reserves them for the whole sample.

= Heating curve
{parent=Heat capacity}

A heating curve records <temperature> against accumulated supplied <heat> under a specified constraint. Within one <thermodynamic phase>, its slope is $dT/dQ=1/C_X$, where $C_X$ is the constrained <heat capacity>. A <latent heat> plateau instead records changing <phase fraction> at the <phase coexistence> <temperature>. The <sensible heat> example combines two sloping intervals and a <melting> plateau in one heating budget.

= Entropy from heat capacity
{parent=Heat capacity}

Along a reversible path at fixed volume, $dS=C_V(T)dT/T$. Hence entropy differences can be measured macroscopically from
$$
S(T_2)-S(T_1)=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\frac{C_V(T)}T\,dT,
$$
with an additional $L/T_c$ across a first-order transition of <latent heat> $L$ at $T_c$.

= Entropy concavity and positive heat capacity
{parent=Heat capacity}

For a thermodynamically stable system, the <entropy> is concave in energy, so
$$
\frac{\partial^2S}{\partial E^2}
=-\frac1{T^2}\frac{\partial T}{\partial E}<0.
$$
Consequently $\partial E/\partial T>0$: energy increases with <temperature>, and the corresponding <heat capacity> is positive.

= Specific heat capacity
{title2=$c=C/m$}
{parent=Heat capacity}
{wiki}

Specific heat capacity is <heat capacity> per unit <mass>:
$$
c=\frac Cm,\qquad c_V=\frac{C_V}{m},\qquad c_p=\frac{C_p}{m}.
$$
Its units are $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, and the constraint remains part of the definition. In particular, <specific heat capacity at constant volume> and <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> are different responses. For homogeneous material, doubling the <mass> doubles its <heat capacity> while leaving its <specific heat capacity> unchanged. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry[OpenStax, Specific heat and heating calculations]{ref}

As an illustrative material model, give a fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> body <mass> $m=2\,\mathrm{kg}$ and constant <specific heat capacity at constant volume> $c_V=400\,\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. With no <phase transition> and no other <work>, increasing its uniform <temperature> by $10\,\mathrm K$ requires
$$
C_V=mc_V=800\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}},\qquad
Q=mc_V\Delta T=8000\,\mathrm J=\Delta U.
$$
These stipulated values describe no measured substance. The right panel of the diagram in <heat capacity> shows the straight line $Q=mc_V\Delta T$: a <temperature> increase of $5\,\mathrm K$ takes $4000\,\mathrm J$ of <heat>. If the <specific heat capacity at constant volume> depends on <temperature>, use $Q=m\int_{T_1}^{T_2}c_V(T)\,dT$ instead. The corresponding fixed-<pressure> calculation uses <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> and gives $Q=\Delta H$ under the assumptions in <heat capacity>.

Choosing between <specific heat capacity>, <molar heat capacity> and <volumetric heat capacity> is choosing whether to compare equal <mass>, equal <amount of substance> or equal <volume (thermodynamics)>. A small sample with large <specific heat capacity> can still have less total <heat capacity> than a much larger sample.

= Volumetric heat capacity
{parent=Specific heat capacity}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm{vol}}=C/V=\rho c$}
{wiki}

Volumetric heat capacity is <heat capacity> per unit <volume (thermodynamics)>, with units $\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}\,K^{-1}}$. For homogeneous material of <mass density> $\rho=m/V$, it equals $\rho c$, using <specific heat capacity> $c$ for the same constraint. It measures the <heat> required per small <temperature> increase for a chosen occupied <volume (thermodynamics)>. In a material model using <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>, <thermal diffusivity> is $\alpha=k/(\rho c_p)$, where $k$ is <thermal conductivity>.

= Specific heat capacity at constant volume
{parent=Specific heat capacity}
{title2=$c_V=(\partial u/\partial T)_\rho$}

The specific heat capacity at constant volume is the temperature derivative of <specific internal energy> at fixed density and composition: $c_V=(\partial u/\partial T)_\rho$. It is the corresponding extensive <heat capacity at constant volume> divided by material mass.

= Specific heat capacity at constant pressure
{title2=$c_P$}
{parent=Specific heat capacity}

The <specific heat capacity> at fixed <pressure> and composition is $c_P=(\partial h/\partial T)_P$, where $h$ is <specific enthalpy>. Equivalently, it is <heat capacity at constant pressure> divided by <mass>. Under the process assumptions in <heat capacity>, it includes expansion <work> as well as the <internal energy> change. The <sensible heat> example uses this response separately for <ice> and <water>.

= Pure-radiation constant-pressure heat-capacity singularity
{parent=Specific heat capacity at constant pressure}

For pure <blackbody radiation>, $P=aT^4/3$ depends only on <temperature>. Constant pressure therefore fixes temperature, so a usual finite constant-pressure temperature derivative is unavailable. In the material gas-radiation limit the <specific-heat ratio> diverges, although all three <stellar adiabatic exponents> approach $4/3$. The radiation index $4/3$ is an adiabatic pressure-volume response, not a finite heat-capacity ratio.

= Heat capacity at constant volume
{title2=$C_V$}
{parent=Heat capacity}

= Constant-volume heat capacity
{synonym}

For a fixed-composition <closed system>, the <heat capacity> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> is $C_V=(\partial U/\partial T)_V$, with <internal energy> $U$. Under the <quasistatic process> assumptions in <heat capacity>, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> is permitted, so $dV=0$ gives $\delta Q=dU=C_V\,dT$. A rigid vessel therefore needs $Q=\Delta U$, or $Q=C_V\Delta T$ when $C_V$ is constant. Its per-unit-<mass> counterpart is <specific heat capacity at constant volume>.

= Dulong-Petit law
{c}
{parent=Heat capacity at constant volume}
{wiki=Dulong–Petit_law}

In the classical high-<temperature> limit, each independent <harmonic oscillator> contributes $k_B$ to the <heat capacity at constant volume>, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>. A three-dimensional crystalline <solid> with $N$ <chemical atoms> has $3N$ vibrational <normal modes> in the bulk harmonic approximation. Its vibrational <internal energy> and <heat capacity at constant volume> therefore obey
$$
U_{\rm vib}=U_0+3Nk_BT,\qquad
C_V=\left(\frac{\partial U_{\rm vib}}{\partial T}\right)_{V,N}=3Nk_B.
$$
Here $U_0$ is independent of <temperature> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>. Thus the <Dulong-Petit law> predicts $C_V\simeq3Nk_B$ for a real <solid> in this regime. Per <mole> of <chemical atoms>, rather than per <mole> of a compound's formula units,
$$
C_{V,\rm mol}\longrightarrow3R\simeq24.9434\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}},\qquad R=N_Ak_B,
$$
with <molar gas constant> $R$ and <Avogadro constant> $N_A$. A formula unit containing $r$ <chemical atoms> consequently has a classical vibrational prediction of $3rR$ per <mole> of formula units.

For example, in this ideal classical model, one <mole> of <chemical atoms> heated at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> from $300$ to $600\,\mathrm K$ gains vibrational <internal energy>
$$
\Delta U_{\rm vib}=3R(600-300)\,\mathrm K\simeq7483.02\,\mathrm J.
$$
The <heat capacity at constant volume> stays the same even though the thermal part of the <internal energy> doubles. These <temperatures> illustrate the stipulated classical model; they do not assert that every real <solid> is classical at $300\,\mathrm K$.

\Image[dulong-petit-classical-energy.png]
{title=Classical <equipartition theorem>: one <harmonic oscillator> stores $k_BT$, one <chemical atom> stores $3k_BT$}
{description=Mean <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> at $300$ and $600\,\mathrm K$, in units of $k_B(300\,\mathrm K)$. Each independent quadratic term contributes $k_BT/2$. The three-direction model has three kinetic and three potential terms per <chemical atom>; doubling <temperature> doubles both contributions.}

The proof outline is the <equipartition theorem>: one classical <harmonic oscillator> has a quadratic <kinetic energy> and a quadratic <potential energy>, each with mean $k_BT/2$. Their sum is $k_BT$, whose <derivative> with respect to <temperature> is $k_B$. Summing over $3N$ <normal modes> gives $3Nk_B$. The classical result does not require equal <angular frequencies>: each harmonic <normal mode> contributes the same amount once it is classical. Overall rigid motions give only a finite-size correction to the bulk count. This independence from the individual <angular frequencies> makes the <Dulong-Petit law> useful as a quick estimate of a <solid>'s heating requirement.

Experiments on freely expanding <solids> often measure the <heat capacity at constant pressure> $C_P$, whereas the harmonic prediction is for the <heat capacity at constant volume> $C_V$. In a smooth single <thermodynamic phase> of a simple compressible material, the <difference between constant-pressure and constant-volume heat capacities> is
$$
C_P-C_V=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{\kappa_T},
$$
where $\alpha=V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial T)_P$ is the volumetric <coefficient of thermal expansion> and $\kappa_T=-V^{-1}(\partial V/\partial P)_T$ is the <isothermal compressibility>. Using measured $C_P$ as an approximation to $C_V$ requires this correction to be small compared with $C_V$. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS2[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.4.2: relation between the two heat capacities]{ref}

High <temperature> here means $k_BT\gg\hbar\omega$ for the relevant vibrational <normal modes>, with reduced <Planck constant> $\hbar$ and mode <angular frequency> $\omega$. At low <temperature>, excitation across a quantum <energy> spacing is suppressed: modes with $\hbar\omega\gg k_BT$ freeze out thermally, and the classical <equipartition theorem> fails. The <Einstein model of a solid> makes this loss of vibrational <heat capacity> explicit. The <Dulong-Petit law> is therefore an ideal high-<temperature> vibrational limit, not a statement that all <solids> have the same <heat capacity> at every <temperature>. Other contributions and departures from the harmonic approximation can matter even at high <temperature>. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S3#S3.SS3[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 3.3: solid vibrations, the classical limit and quantum freeze-out]{ref}

Historically, Petit and Dulong's 1819 experiments compared the <specific heat capacities> of several elemental <solids> and found an approximately constant product with their relative atomic weights. Their original empirical regularity came before the classical harmonic explanation; it suggested that counting <chemical atoms> reveals a simpler pattern than comparing equal <masses>. https://webserver.lemoyne.edu/giunta/PETIT.html[Petit and Dulong, Recherches sur quelques points importants de la Théorie de la Chaleur, Annales de Chimie et de Physique 10, 395–413 (1819), contemporary English translation hosted by Carmen Giunta]{ref}

= Einstein solid
{c}
{parent=Dulong-Petit law}
{title2=$C_V/(3Nk_B)=x^2e^x/(e^x-1)^2$}
{wiki}

= Einstein model of a solid
{c}
{synonym}

The <Einstein model of a solid> represents the vibrations of $N$ <chemical atoms> by $3N$ independent one-dimensional <quantum harmonic oscillators>, all with the same positive <angular frequency> $\omega$. Equivalently, each <chemical atom> has three independent oscillator directions. The common <angular frequency> is a simplifying model assumption, not a claim about every vibration of a real <solid>. Hold the <volume (thermodynamics)>, $N$ and $\omega$ fixed. Define the characteristic <temperature> $\theta_E=\hbar\omega/k_B$ and the dimensionless ratio $x=\theta_E/T$. The vibrational <internal energy> is
$$
U=3N\hbar\omega\left[\frac12+\frac{1}{e^x-1}\right],\qquad
x=\frac{\hbar\omega}{k_BT}=\frac{\theta_E}{T}.
$$
The first term is the <zero-point energy> $3N\hbar\omega/2$. It is constant at fixed $\omega$, so it contributes no <heat capacity at constant volume>. The <temperature>-dependent term gives
$$
\frac{C_V}{3Nk_B}=\frac{x^2e^x}{(e^x-1)^2}
=\frac{x^2e^{-x}}{(1-e^{-x})^2}.
$$
This is the vibrational <heat capacity at constant volume> of the model, with no expansion or other contributions included. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-012-fundamentals-of-materials-science-fall-2005/d3fa883afa8df7cf7aa0432fcaadde8f_lec22t.pdf[MIT 3.012, Lecture 22, pages 9–13: Einstein-solid partition function, energy and heat capacity]{ref}

For a worked comparison, choose the model parameter $\theta_E=300\,\mathrm K$. The normalized <heat capacity at constant volume> depends only on $T/\theta_E$:

|| $T/\theta_E$
|| <Temperature> $T$ in $\mathrm K$
|| $C_V/(3Nk_B)$

| $0.1$
| $30$
| $0.00454$

| $0.5$
| $150$
| $0.72406$

| $1$
| $300$
| $0.92067$

| $2$
| $600$
| $0.97942$

At $T=\theta_E$, one <mole> of <chemical atoms> therefore has vibrational <heat capacity at constant volume> $3R(0.92067)\simeq22.965\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, using the unrounded model value. Even at twice the characteristic <temperature>, the model remains about $2.06\%$ below the <Dulong-Petit law> limit. These are calculations for the ideal <Einstein solid>, not material measurements.

\Image[einstein-solid-heat-capacity.png]
{title=<Einstein solid> vibrational <heat capacity> approaches the <Dulong-Petit law> limit}
{description=The model ratio $C_V/(3Nk_B)$ over $0.02\leq T/\theta_E\leq3$, with the four computed cases marked. The dashed classical line is $1$; quantum freeze-out suppresses the low-<temperature> response. All <quantum harmonic oscillators> have the same stipulated <angular frequency>.}

A short derivation uses the <thermal partition function of a quantum harmonic oscillator>. The <energy eigenvalues> $E_n=\hbar\omega(n+1/2)$ and their <Boltzmann factors> form a <geometric series>, giving the one-oscillator <canonical partition function> $z=e^{-x/2}/(1-e^{-x})$. The mean occupation is $1/(e^x-1)$, hence the displayed $U$ after summing over $3N$ independent <quantum harmonic oscillators>. Taking its <derivative> with $dx/dT=-x/T$ gives the displayed <heat capacity at constant volume>. https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/omg1.pdf[David Tong, Statistical Physics, Example Sheet 1 (January 2012), problem 5: oscillator energy, the Einstein model and its limits]{ref}

The two limits explain the shape. At high <temperature>, a <Taylor series> in small $x$ gives
$$
\frac{C_V}{3Nk_B}=1-\frac{x^2}{12}+O(x^4)\longrightarrow1
\qquad(T\gg\theta_E).
$$
At low <temperature>, $1-e^{-x}\to1$, so
$$
\frac{C_V}{3Nk_B}\sim x^2e^{-x}\longrightarrow0
\qquad(T\ll\theta_E).
$$
The <zero-point energy> remains even when the thermal response is tiny: freeze-out means that changing <temperature> scarcely changes the occupations, not that the <chemical atoms> have zero <energy>. This is the useful lesson of the <Einstein model of a solid>: the classical counting rule reappears when many levels are accessible, while discrete excitation spacings explain its failure upon cooling.

The exponential low-<temperature> decay is also the model's limitation. Real three-dimensional crystalline <solids> have <acoustic phonon branches> with arbitrarily small <angular frequencies> in the bulk limit. The <Debye model> includes these collective <normal modes> and predicts a low-<temperature> vibrational contribution $C_V\propto T^3$, which the single-frequency <Einstein solid> cannot reproduce. This qualification concerns the vibrational contribution; additional excitations can change a material's total <heat capacity>. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S3#S3.SS3[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 3.3: acoustic phonons and the Debye low-temperature law]{ref}

Einstein introduced the quantum treatment of <solid> vibrations in his 1907 paper \i[Die Plancksche Theorie der Strahlung und die Theorie der spezifischen Wärme]. Its historical importance is that quantum excitation energies explained why the classical <Dulong-Petit law> could fail; the modern <quantum harmonic oscillator> formulation above includes the <zero-point energy> explicitly. https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/andp.19063270110[A. Einstein, Annalen der Physik 327, 180–190 (1907), original-paper publication metadata]{ref}

= Heat capacity at constant area
{title2=$C_A$}
{parent=Heat capacity}

For a two-dimensional system of fixed area $A$, the heat capacity at constant area is $C_A=(\partial E/\partial T)_{N,A}$ when the particle number and other conserved quantities are also fixed. It is the two-dimensional analogue of <heat capacity at constant volume>.

= Third law of thermodynamics
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

The third law implies that the entropy of a system with a nondegenerate ground state approaches zero as its temperature approaches absolute zero. In particular, ordinary equilibrium heat capacities approach zero.

= Pressure
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$P=F_\perp/A$}
{wiki}

Pressure measures the compressive <normal force> per unit <surface area>. For a uniformly loaded surface of <surface area> $A$, the magnitude of the perpendicular <force> is $F_\perp=PA$; locally, $P=dF_\perp/dA$. Its <SI> unit is the pascal, $1\,\mathrm{Pa}=1\,\mathrm{N\,m^{-2}}$. A <force> parallel to the surface contributes shear <stress>, rather than <pressure>. In a <gas>, the steady macroscopic <force> comes from many microscopic transfers of <momentum>; <ideal gas pressure from wall collisions> makes this connection explicit. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.2: microscopic origin of pressure]{ref}

For a computed example, a uniform <pressure> of $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ on a piston of <surface area> $10\,\mathrm{cm^2}=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^2}$ produces a perpendicular <force> of $100\,\mathrm N$. If the opposite face is exposed to $80\,\mathrm{kPa}$, the net <force> due to the two <pressures> is $(100-80)\times10^3\times10^{-3}=20\,\mathrm N$, directed toward the lower-<pressure> side. The <pressure> on either face and their difference answer different questions; <gauge pressure> records a difference from a specified reference, often <atmospheric pressure>.

A scalar <pressure> describes a <gas> in local <thermodynamic equilibrium> with <isotropic> random particle <velocities>: every wall orientation has the same normal <force> per unit <surface area>. Without this <isotropy>, normal <stresses> can depend on direction and a <pressure tensor> is needed. Thus random motion alone is insufficient; a preferred direction matters.

In <thermodynamics>, <pressure> and <volume (thermodynamics)> are <thermodynamic conjugate variables>. For a <simple compressible system>, reversible expansion delivers <work> $\delta W=P\,dV$, and at fixed <entropy> $S$ and particle number $N$,
$$
P=-\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V}\right)_{S,N},
$$
where $U$ is <internal energy>. A large <pressure> therefore means both a large wall <force> per unit <surface area> and a large amount of expansion <work> per small increase in <volume (thermodynamics)>.

= Atmospheric pressure
{parent=Pressure}
{wiki}
{title2=$p_{\rm atm}$}

<Atmospheric pressure> often provides the reference for a <gauge pressure> measurement: $p_g=p_{\rm abs}-p_{\rm atm}$. The absolute <pressure> is measured relative to an ideal <vacuum>, whereas zero <gauge pressure> means equality to the chosen reference. The actual <atmospheric pressure> can vary; it must be specified when converting between these descriptions. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-2-measuring-pressure[OpenStax: atmospheric, gauge and absolute pressure]{ref}.

= Kinematic pressure
{parent=Pressure}
{title2=$p/\rho_0$}

Dividing a <pressure> disturbance by the constant reference <mass density> $\rho_0$ gives a quantity whose <gradient> enters the <Boussinesq equations> directly as an <acceleration>. Its units are squared <velocity>. Distinguish this convention from physical <pressure> when calculating <energy flux> or comparing <wave polarization> formulae.

= Pressure tensor
{parent=Pressure}
{title2=$P_{ij}$}

In the local rest frame of a gas, the <pressure tensor> is the directional <momentum flux> $P_{ij}=\int p_iv_jf(\mathbf p)\,d^3p$, where $f$ is the <phase-space distribution function> per unit <momentum> volume and $\mathbf v$ is particle velocity. For an isotropic distribution, $P_{ij}=P\delta_{ij}$, recovering the <kinetic pressure of an isotropic gas>. An anisotropic distribution can exert different normal <pressures> along different directions.

= Gas pressure
{title2=$p_{\rm gas}$}
{parent=Pressure}

Gas pressure is the mechanical pressure contributed by material particles. For a classical ideal gas, $p_{\rm gas}=nk_BT$.

= Radiation pressure
{title2=$p_{\rm rad}$}
{parent=Pressure}
{wiki}

Radiation pressure is momentum flux carried by electromagnetic radiation. Isotropic blackbody radiation has $p_{\rm rad}=a_{\rm rad}T^4/3$.

= Equation of state
{parent=Pressure}
{wiki}

= Equations of state
{synonym}

An equation of state is a relation among thermodynamic state variables such as pressure, volume, temperature, energy, and particle number.

= Barotropic stellar equation of state
{parent=Equation of state}
{title2=$p=p(\rho)$}

= Stellar barotropic equation of state
{synonym}

A <perfect fluid in general relativity> stellar model in which pressure depends only on energy density, $p=p(\rho)$. A positive derivative makes this relation locally invertible and turns outward-decreasing pressure into outward-decreasing density. This general relation need not have the constant ratio $p/\rho$ used for a constant cosmological fluid parameter.

= Clausius gas
{parent=Equation of state}
{c}

A Clausius gas with covolume parameter $b$ obeys $P(V-Nb)=Nk_BT$. It is the repulsive-volume part of a <Van der Waals equation> without the attractive term.

= Van der Waals equation
{c}
{parent=Equation of state}
{wiki}

The <Van der Waals equation> is a simple <real gas> <equation of state> that can also describe a <liquid-gas critical point>. With particle count $N$ fixed, write the <volume per particle> as $v=V/N$:
$$
p=\frac{k_BT}{v-b}-\frac{a}{v^2},\qquad v>b,\quad a>0,\quad b>0,
$$
or equivalently $(p+aN^2/V^2)(V-Nb)=Nk_BT$. Here $p$ is <pressure>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>. In <SI>, $v$ and $b$ have units $\mathrm{m^3}$ per particle, $a$ has units $\mathrm{Pa\,m^6}=\mathrm{J\,m^3}$, and $k_B$ has units $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$; the particle count is dimensionless. The pole at $v=b$ excludes smaller <volume (thermodynamics)> values from this model.

The $b$ correction represents <excluded volume>; the attraction term lowers <pressure> at fixed <temperature> and particle <number density> $1/v$. This is a <mean-field theory>, replacing detailed intermolecular structure by two parameters. At $v\gg b$ with $a/v^2\ll k_BT/v$, it approaches the <ideal gas law> $pv=k_BT$. It supplies a useful qualitative route from a dilute <gas> to <phase coexistence>, without a claim of quantitative accuracy for every <fluid>.<Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Van der Waals Gas>[opening explanation and equation 6.156: excluded volume and attractive pressure correction; Fitzpatrick uses molar volume]{ref}

Do not mix this particle convention with the <molar volume> convention elsewhere in this article. With <amount of substance> $n_{\rm mol}=N/N_A$, <Avogadro constant> $N_A$, and <molar gas constant> $R=N_Ak_B$, one has $v_m=N_Av$, $b_m=N_Ab$, and $a_m=N_A^2a$, so $p=RT/(v_m-b_m)-a_m/v_m^2$. The molar parameters have units $\mathrm{m^3\,mol^{-1}}$ and $\mathrm{Pa\,m^6\,mol^{-2}}$, respectively.

<Johannes Diderik van der Waals> introduced this <equation of state> in 1873, connecting molecular attraction with departures from the dilute <ideal gas law>. Its historical significance is the unified treatment of <gases> and <liquids>, rather than a universally exact fit.<Johannes Diderik van der Waals, Facts by Nobel Prize Outreach>[Nobel Prize Outreach, Johannes Diderik van der Waals, Work: equation introduced in 1873 and molecular attraction]{ref}

= Van der Waals law of corresponding states
{c}
{parent=Van der Waals equation}

The <Van der Waals equation> becomes independent of its material parameters when <pressure>, <temperature> and molecular <volume> are divided by their critical values: $\bar p=8\bar T/(3\bar v-1)-3/\bar v^2$. This explains the common reduced isotherms within this model, without claiming exact agreement for all real fluids.

= Van der Waals isotherm
{c}
{parent=Van der Waals equation}
{title2=$p(v,T)$}

A <Van der Waals isotherm> is the <pressure>–particle <volume (thermodynamics)> curve at a fixed <temperature>. Put $x=v/v_c$, $t=T/T_c$, and $q=p/p_c$, using the <Van der Waals critical point> values below. Then the existing <Van der Waals law of corresponding states> becomes
$$
q(x,t)=\frac{8t}{3x-1}-\frac{3}{x^2},\qquad x>\frac13.
$$
All curves in the figure are calculations in this reduced model.

\Image[van-der-waals-reduced-isotherms.png]
{id=van-der-waals-reduced-isotherms}
{title=<Van der Waals isotherms> below, at and above the <Van der Waals critical point>}
{description=The dashed subcritical homogeneous continuation contains an unstable red segment. The green <Maxwell construction> plateau describes equilibrium <phase coexistence>; the two shaded areas are equal. Curves are model calculations, not <carbon dioxide> measurements.}

For a uniform <thermodynamic phase>, the <isothermal compressibility> is
$$
\kappa_T=-\frac1v\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial p}\right)_T
=-\frac{1}{v(\partial p/\partial v)_T}.
$$
Thus the upward-sloping portion of a subcritical <Van der Waals isotherm> has $\kappa_T<0$: compression would reduce <pressure>, allowing a density disturbance to grow. It is mechanically unstable. The <spinodal points> bound that portion; setting the <derivative> $\partial q/\partial x$ to zero gives their curve $t=(3x-1)^2/(4x^3)$. Negative slope alone guarantees only local mechanical stability: portions between the <spinodal> and the <phase coexistence> endpoints have <metastability>. Equilibrium replaces the entire interval between those endpoints, including these metastable portions, by a <Maxwell construction> plateau.<Statistical Physics by David Tong>[§5.1, equation 5.3 and §5.1.1, Maxwell Construction and Meta-Stable States: unstable middle branch and coexistence versus spinodal]{ref}

= Van der Waals critical point
{c}
{parent=Van der Waals equation}
{title2=$(v_c,T_c,p_c)$}

The <liquid-gas critical point> of the <Van der Waals equation> occurs where its two subcritical stationary points merge into a horizontal <inflection point>. The conditions are $(\partial p/\partial v)_T=(\partial^2p/\partial v^2)_T=0$. The short calculation is
$$
-\frac{k_BT_c}{(v_c-b)^2}+\frac{2a}{v_c^3}=0,\qquad
\frac{2k_BT_c}{(v_c-b)^3}-\frac{6a}{v_c^4}=0.
$$
Dividing these relations gives $2/(v_c-b)=3/v_c$, hence
$$
v_c=3b,\qquad T_c=\frac{8a}{27bk_B},\qquad p_c=\frac{a}{27b^2}.
$$
Substitution gives the <Van der Waals law of corresponding states> and the parameter-independent ratio $p_cv_c/(k_BT_c)=3/8$. That ratio belongs to this <equation of state>; reducing a real <fluid>'s variables does not enforce it.<Statistical Physics by David Tong>[§5.1.3, equations 5.7–5.8: critical values, reduced equation and compressibility ratio]{ref}

An exact rearrangement of the reduced critical <Van der Waals isotherm> makes its flatness visible:
$$
q(x,1)-1=-\frac{3(x-1)^3}{x^2(3x-1)}.
$$
The first nonzero departure near $x=1$ is $-3(x-1)^3/2$. On the critical particle <volume (thermodynamics)> approached from $t>1$, $(\partial q/\partial x)_{x=1}=-6(t-1)$ and
$$
\kappa_T=\frac{1}{6p_c(t-1)}.
$$
The divergent <isothermal compressibility> and cubic flattening explain why the critical region is mechanically soft. These are <mean-field theory> predictions; <thermal fluctuations> limit their use for real <fluids> very close to a <thermodynamic critical point>.

= Van der Waals liquid-gas coexistence
{c}
{parent=Van der Waals equation}

Below $T_c$, the equilibrium <liquid> and <gas> have the same <temperature>, <pressure> $p_*$, and per-particle <chemical potential>. For macroscopic bulk <thermodynamic phases>, neglect interface contributions. The <Maxwell equal-area construction> determines $v_l$ and $v_g$ by
$$
p(v_l,T)=p(v_g,T)=p_*,\qquad
\int_{v_l}^{v_g}[p(v,T)-p_*]\,dv=0.
$$
It expresses equality of <chemical potentials>, since $d\mu=v\,dp$ at fixed <temperature>. Equivalently, the equilibrium <Helmholtz free energy> per particle follows a common <tangent line> between the two endpoint states, lying below the homogeneous continuation between them. The construction uses that mathematical continuation to compare endpoints; it is not a physical compression path through negative <isothermal compressibility> states.<Statistical Physics by David Tong>[§5.1.1, equation 5.5, Maxwell Construction and figures 36–37: chemical-potential equality and flat coexistence intervals]{ref}

Here is a reproducible calculated example at $t=0.9$. With $q_*=p_*/p_c$, the homogeneous intersections solve the <cubic equation>
$$
3q_*x^3-(q_*+8t)x^2+9x-3=0.
$$
The <Maxwell construction> gives
$$
q_*\simeq0.646998352,\qquad
(x_l,x_m,x_g)\simeq(0.603401903,\ 1.090526633,\ 2.348842376).
$$
All three <roots of a polynomial> satisfy $x>1/3$. Only the smallest and largest are the <phase coexistence> endpoints; the middle one has negative <isothermal compressibility>. Equal <pressures> at three admissible values of <volume (thermodynamics)> therefore do not imply three stable <thermodynamic phases>. An analytic area check is
$$
\frac{8t}{3}\ln\frac{3x_g-1}{3x_l-1}
+3\left(\frac1{x_g}-\frac1{x_l}\right)-q_*(x_g-x_l)=0.
$$
At full numerical precision, the residual is below $10^{-11}$ in reduced units; independent numerical integration gives the same cancellation. At $t=0.9$, the <spinodal points> are instead $x\simeq0.718597189$ and $1.528504964$.

\Image[van-der-waals-critical-coexistence.png]
{id=van-der-waals-critical-coexistence}
{title=<Phase coexistence> and <spinodal> curves meet at the <Van der Waals critical point>}
{description=The horizontal coordinate is reduced <number density> $n/n_c=1/x$, not <molar volume>. The green region is equilibrium <liquid>–<gas> coexistence, bounded by the <binodal>. Hatching marks unstable homogeneous states, and the strips between solid and dashed boundaries mark homogeneous <metastability>. This is a model <phase diagram>, not measured data.}

The equilibrium plateau changes the <phase fraction> rather than the endpoint densities. For a gas particle fraction $f_g=N_g/N$ and mean particle <volume (thermodynamics)> $\bar v=V/N$, the <lever rule> reads
$$
\bar v=(1-f_g)v_l+f_gv_g,\qquad
f_g=\frac{\bar v-v_l}{v_g-v_l}.
$$
Thus $\bar v=v_c$ at $t=0.9$ gives $f_g\simeq0.227219$: this is a particle fraction, not the <volume fraction> occupied by <gas>. Differentiating equality of <chemical potentials> along this model's <phase coexistence curve> yields the same <Clapeyron equation> explained below, $dp_*/dT=\ell/[T(v_g-v_l)]$, with <latent heat> $\ell$ per particle. Both endpoint differences vanish as the <Van der Waals critical point> is approached; the equation's critical limit must be taken as a ratio.

= Andrews's carbon dioxide experiment
{c}
{parent=Van der Waals equation}

The 1869 <The Bakerian Lecture: On the Continuity of the Gaseous and Liquid States of Matter by Thomas Andrews> describes dried <carbon dioxide> trapped above <mercury (element)> in a sealed <capillary tube>. A steel screw pressurized <water> in a strong <copper> tube; movable <mercury (element)> transmitted the load. A second <capillary tube> held air as a <pressure> gauge. Flowing <water> around the visible <capillary tubes> controlled <temperature>; a <thermometer> read the bath. A <cathetometer> read column positions, converted to <volume (thermodynamics)> readings using weighed-<mercury (element)> calibration.<The Bakerian Lecture: On the Continuity of the Gaseous and Liquid States of Matter by Thomas Andrews>[Andrews, pp. 576–578 and Plate LXIII, figures 3 and 5–7: sample preparation, screw, coupled capillaries, water jackets and optical readout]{ref}

Below the critical <temperature>, compression produced a visible <liquid>–<gas> boundary; above it, large density changes occurred without that boundary. Three trials gave $30.92\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ as his critical <temperature>. Purity checks detected residual air; <mercury (element)> height corrections accounted for unequal <hydrostatic pressures>. He explicitly left air nonideality and pressure-induced tube-capacity corrections unresolved, so his air-gauge <pressures> were approximate. These readouts support a <liquid-gas critical point>; they do not measure a homogeneous unstable loop or establish universal quantitative accuracy of the later <Van der Waals equation>.<The Bakerian Lecture: On the Continuity of the Gaseous and Liquid States of Matter by Thomas Andrews>[Andrews, pp. 576, 578–580 and 583: residual air, approximate air-gauge pressure, hydrostatic corrections and three critical-temperature trials]{ref}

= Equation of state of an ideal ultrarelativistic gas
{parent=Equation of state}

An isotropic ideal gas whose particles obey the ultrarelativistic dispersion $\varepsilon=pc$ satisfies
$$
pV=\frac E3.
$$

= Adiabatic equation of state
{parent=Equation of state}

An adiabatic equation of state relates pressure and volume along a process with no heat transfer. It commonly has the form $pV^\gamma=\text{constant}$.

= Volume
{disambiguate=thermodynamics}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

Thermodynamic volume is the <volume> occupied by a thermodynamic system, treated as an <extensive variable>.

= Molar volume
{parent=Volume (thermodynamics)}
{title2=$v_m=V/n$}
{wiki}

The <volume (thermodynamics)> per <amount of substance>, with $n$ measured in <moles>. For a uniform pure substance, $v_m=M/\rho$, where $M$ is its <molar mass> and $\rho$ its <mass density>. The <Clapeyron equation> pairs a difference of <molar volumes> with <latent heat> per <mole>.

= Volume per particle
{parent=Volume (thermodynamics)}
{title2=$v=V/N$}

The <volume (thermodynamics)> divided by the positive number of particles $N$. The <Van der Waals equation> uses this convention for $v$, whereas <specific volume> divides by <mass> and <molar volume> divides by the <amount of substance>. With the <Avogadro constant> $N_A$, the <molar volume> is $v_m=N_Av$.

= Specific volume
{parent=Volume (thermodynamics)}
{title2=$v=V/m=1/\rho$}
{wiki}

The <volume (thermodynamics)> per unit <mass>; for a uniform substance it is the reciprocal of <mass density>. The <Clapeyron equation> pairs a difference of <specific volumes> with <latent heat> per unit <mass>.

= Internal energy
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}
{title2=$U$}

Internal energy $U$ is the <energy> stored in the microscopic <degrees of freedom> of a <thermodynamic system>: random molecular <kinetic energy>, internal molecular excitations, and interaction <potential energy>. Its value requires a specified system and an <energy> reference. In <thermodynamic equilibrium>, <internal energy> is a <state function>; it records the present state, without remembering whether a previous change came from stirring, compression or <heat> transfer. This makes <internal energy> the common accounting quantity connecting mechanical experiments to thermal ones.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.2 Work, Heat, and Internal Energy>[§3.2, “Internal Energy”, equation 3.5 and the discussion of molecular motion]{ref}

<Heat> and <work> describe <energy> crossing the system boundary during a process. They are not separately stored contents of the system. For a <closed system> whose bulk <kinetic energy> and external <potential energy> have unchanged endpoint values, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
\Delta U=Q_{\rm in}+W_{\rm on}=Q_{\rm in}-W_{\rm out}.
$$
Here $Q_{\rm in}$ is positive for incoming <heat>, and $W_{\rm on}=-W_{\rm out}$ is positive for net <work> done on the system. Two routes between the same equilibrium states have the same $\Delta U$, although their <heat> and <work> can differ. The infinitesimal notation $dU=\delta Q_{\rm in}+\delta W_{\rm on}$ distinguishes a <state function> change from path-dependent transfers.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§4.2, equation 4.156 and “Quasi-Static Processes”: state energy versus heat and work; Tong uses work positive inward]{ref}

For a calculated example, take one <mole> of a classical <monatomic gas> in the <ideal gas> regime, at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, from $300$ to $310\,\mathrm K$. With stipulated <molar gas constant> $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, its <internal energy> change is $3nR\Delta T/2=124.71\,\mathrm J$. A purely thermal route supplies $Q_{\rm in}=124.71\,\mathrm J$ with $W_{\rm on}=0$. An ideal insulated stirring route supplies $W_{\rm on}=124.71\,\mathrm J$ with $Q_{\rm in}=0$, measured between equilibrium endpoints after the motion dies away. The same stored increment has two different transfer histories; neither history defines a separate kind of stored <energy>.

\Image[internal-energy-storage-and-transfers.png]
{id=internal-energy-storage-and-transfers}
{title=<Internal energy> is stored; <heat> and <work> are ways to change it}
{description=Original schematic and calculated <ideal gas> example with the stipulated parameters above; no experimental data. The two routes end at the same <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>. Microscopic <kinetic energy> and interaction <potential energy> belong inside $U$; whole-body motion and external elevation are accounted for separately under the stated convention.}

The familiar <internal energy of an ideal gas> is a useful special case. Neglect intermolecular interactions, hold the <amount of substance> $n$ and composition fixed, and suppose that $f$ independent quadratic terms per particle obey the classical <equipartition theorem>. Choosing the constant reference contribution as zero gives
$$
U=\frac f2nRT,\qquad C_V=\frac f2nR.
$$
Here $T$ is absolute <temperature>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, and $C_V$ is the whole sample's <heat capacity at constant volume>. A <monatomic gas> has $f=3$ translational terms. A rigid diatomic <molecule> has $f=5$ when its two rotational terms are active and vibration is frozen; activating one <harmonic oscillator> vibration adds two terms, one <kinetic energy> and one <potential energy>, giving $f=7$. The counting is of quadratic contributions, so a vibrational coordinate is not just one contribution.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/2 Classical Gases>[§§2.2.1 and 2.4, equations 2.57–2.58 and the rotation/vibration energy counting]{ref}

This constant-$f$ formula applies only over a regime with those same active terms, classical translational statistics, nonrelativistic motion and unchanged composition. Quantum freezing, <quantum degeneracy>, <chemical reactions> or appreciable intermolecular interactions invalidate that simple counting. More generally, at fixed amount and composition an <ideal gas> can still have $U=U(T)$ with varying <heat capacity at constant volume>, so use $\Delta U=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_V(T)\,dT$. A nonzero constant reference adds $U_*$ to $fnRT/2$ without altering $\Delta U$.

= Internal energy and system boundaries
{parent=Internal energy}

For an ordinary stationary laboratory sample, a useful convention is
$$
E_{\rm total}=U+K_{\rm bulk}+\Phi_{\rm ext},\qquad
\Delta U+\Delta K_{\rm bulk}+\Delta\Phi_{\rm ext}
=Q_{\rm in}+W_{\rm on}.
$$
The bulk <kinetic energy> $K_{\rm bulk}$ includes organized whole-body motion; for pure translation it is $Mv_{\rm cm}^2/2$, with total <mass> $M$ and <center of mass> speed $v_{\rm cm}$. The external <potential energy> $\Phi_{\rm ext}$ includes elevation in an imposed <gravitational field>, approximately $Mgz$ in a uniform field. Accelerating a sealed bottle or lifting it without otherwise changing its state changes these mechanical terms, rather than its <internal energy>. Molecular <kinetic energy> is measured relative to the chosen bulk motion.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/3.2 Work, Heat, and Internal Energy>[§3.2, “Internal Energy”: molecular speeds relative to the center of mass; moving-train and roof examples]{ref}

State the convention rather than applying this separation blindly. If an experiment separately tracks a rotating apparatus or an organized flow, its mechanical <kinetic energy> must not also be counted in $U$. In <Joule's paddle-wheel experiment>, waiting for the apparatus and fluid to come to rest removes such an endpoint ambiguity. Including the vessel and paddle changes whose <internal energy> is measured; including the falling weights additionally puts their changing <gravitational potential energy> inside the enlarged system's total <energy> balance.

= Internal energy at constant temperature
{parent=Internal energy}

Constant <temperature> does not generally mean constant <internal energy>. At fixed amount and composition, within one smooth <thermodynamic phase> of a <simple compressible system>, an equilibrium identity is
$$
dU=C_V\,dT+\left[T\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V-p\right]dV.
$$
The bracket vanishes for the <ideal gas law> $pV=nRT$. It need not vanish for a <real gas>: changing the <volume (thermodynamics)> changes the average interaction <potential energy>, even when the random-motion contribution stays at the same <temperature>. The identity follows by writing $dU=T\,dS-p\,dV$ and using a <Maxwell relation>; it does not require the actual process to be reversible. <Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Free Expansion of Gas>{ref}

For a <Van der Waals equation> model with <volume (thermodynamics)> per <mole> $v=V/n$, $p=RT/(v-b)-a/v^2$ gives $(\partial U/\partial V)_{T,n}=a/v^2$. Thus an <isothermal expansion> from $v_1$ to $v_2>v_1$, at fixed <amount of substance> $n$, increases <internal energy> by
$$
\Delta U=na\left(\frac1{v_1}-\frac1{v_2}\right)>0.
$$
Separating attractive particles makes their interaction <potential energy> less negative. For stipulated $n=1\,\mathrm{mol}$, $a=0.10\,\mathrm{Pa\,m^6\,mol^{-2}}$, $b=10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^3\,mol^{-1}}$, $T=300\,\mathrm K$, $v_1=0.010$ and $v_2=0.020\,\mathrm{m^3\,mol^{-1}}$, the calculated increase for this one-<mole> sample is $5\,\mathrm J$, despite $\Delta T=0$. This dilute stable-branch model example is not a measurement of a named <gas>. <Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics by Richard Fitzpatrick/Van der Waals Gas>{ref}

A <phase transition> gives another reason. During equilibrium vaporization of a pure substance at fixed <pressure>, the <temperature> can stay at its <phase coexistence> value while the <phase fraction> changes. Molecular separation changes interaction <potential energy>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.5 Phase Changes>[§1.5, figure 1.18 and equations 1.7–1.8: molecular attractions and constant-temperature phase conversion]{ref} If the <closed system> does only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, its <latent heat> input obeys
$$
Q_{\rm in}=\Delta H,\qquad
\Delta U=\Delta H-p\Delta V.
$$
Here $H=U+pV$ is <enthalpy>. A vaporization <latent heat> therefore is not automatically the <internal energy> increment: some input supplies expansion <work>. A fixed <temperature> reading alone cannot tell how much <internal energy> changed.

= Joule's paddle-wheel experiment
{c}
{parent=Internal energy}

Joule's paper <On the Mechanical Equivalent of Heat> (1850) describes a <copper> vessel holding <water> and a brass paddle with stationary vanes. Descending <lead> weights drove its shaft through strings and pulleys; the vanes opposed simple co-rotation. A boxwood shaft segment reduced <thermal conduction>. He measured fall distances and initial/final <temperatures>, repeating twenty descents per run and disconnecting the drive for rewinding. Calibrated <thermometers> read the <water> and room.<On the Mechanical Equivalent of Heat by James Prescott Joule>[pp. 64–66 and Plate VII: thermometer calibration, water apparatus and operating sequence]{ref}

\Image[joule-paddle-wheel-calorimeter.png]
{id=joule-paddle-wheel-calorimeter}
{title=<Joule's paddle-wheel experiment>: mechanical input and thermal readout}
{description=Original explanatory schematic, not a scale reconstruction or a plot of measurements. The outlined system includes <water>, vessel and immersed paddle, but excludes the weights and external drive. The symbolic balance separates mechanical losses outside this boundary from <heat> exchange with the room. Rewinding occurs with the drive disconnected.}

His matched resting trials retained the <water> amount, duration, surroundings and reading procedure, omitting paddle motion to estimate environmental <heat> exchange. A screen reduced <thermal radiation> from his body. His calculation included the vessel and paddle <heat capacities>, external pulley/string losses, descent <kinetic energy> and string elasticity; it acknowledged residual vibration and sound.<On the Mechanical Equivalent of Heat by James Prescott Joule>[pp. 66, 69–70 and 82: matched controls, apparatus water-equivalent capacity, mechanical corrections and residual losses]{ref}

The measured observable is a corrected <temperature> rise, interpreted through the participating <heat capacity>; it is not a direct view of molecular motion. The experiment tests whether measured mechanical input accounts quantitatively for thermal storage. Frictional warming by itself would establish neither the conversion factor nor the absence of <heat> exchange. A modern authored demonstration at UCSB instead measures crank turns and a cylinder's <temperature> with an electrical <thermometer>: the apparatus differs, but again both mechanical input and thermal response must be measured. <UCSB Physics Lecture Demonstrations/52.33 -- Mechanical equivalent of heat>{ref}

= Paddle-wheel calorimetry energy balance
{parent=Joule's paddle-wheel experiment}

Choose the <water>, vessel and immersed paddle as the <closed system>, with fixed outer <volume (thermodynamics)> and negligible endpoint changes in bulk mechanical <energy>. Between common equilibrium <temperatures> $T_1$ and $T_2$,
$$
\Delta U_{\rm assembly}=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_{\rm eff}(T)\,dT
=W_{\rm shaft,on}+Q_{\rm room,in}.
$$
The effective <heat capacity> $C_{\rm eff}$ includes every component that participates in the common <temperature> change. For nearly constant $C_{\rm eff}$, the readout is $C_{\rm eff}\Delta T$. <Heat> transferred between <water> and paddle stays inside this boundary; it must not be added again as external input. The drive supplies shaft <work> even though the vessel has no expansion <work>.

For a simplified modern model, let $M$ be the total descending <mass>, let each of $N$ descents cover height $h$, and let $W_{\rm loss,out}$ be <energy> diverted by the external drive. With negligible remaining drive <kinetic energy> and elastic storage,
$$
W_{\rm shaft,on}=NMgh-W_{\rm loss,out},\qquad
NMgh=C_{\rm eff}\Delta T+W_{\rm loss,out}+Q_{\rm room,out}.
$$
The signed $Q_{\rm room,out}=-Q_{\rm room,in}$ may be negative if the room warms the assembly. This model omits the small endpoint and elastic corrections explicitly considered in the historical analysis. It does not equate all of the weights' lost <gravitational potential energy> with the <water>'s gained <internal energy>.

As a proposed modern protocol, measure the falling <masses> and distances, calibrate the <thermometer> and $C_{\rm eff}$ independently, record room <temperature> and resting drift before and after each run, and wait for fluid motion to decay before the final equilibrium reading. Repeat with different fall budgets and run durations. A stable ratio of corrected input to corrected thermal storage supports the <first law of thermodynamics> over the tested range. Resting controls only estimate leakage if their thermal conditions represent the driven run; sensor lag, nonuniform <temperature>, unmeasured drive losses, evaporation or changing <heat capacity> can spoil the inference. Those are limits of the measurement, rather than evidence that <heat> was stored as a separate substance.

= Specific internal energy
{parent=Internal energy}
{title2=$e$}

Specific <internal energy> is <internal energy> per unit mass. For a <perfect gas> with constant <specific-heat ratio>, $e=p/[(\gamma-1)\rho]$. Its material balance is $\rho De/Dt=-p\nabla\cdot\mathbf u+Q$, where $Q$ is <heat> supplied per unit volume per unit time.

= Thermodynamic potential
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A <state function> used to describe a <thermodynamic system> with a chosen set of natural variables. <Internal energy>, <enthalpy>, <Helmholtz free energy>, and <Gibbs free energy> use different variables and serve different environmental constraints.

= Thermodynamic free energy
{parent=Thermodynamic potential}
{wiki}

= Free energy
{synonym}

A <free energy> is a <thermodynamic potential> that compares possible states under specified environmental constraints. For a <closed system> of fixed composition, treated as a <simple compressible system> with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> minimizes <Helmholtz free energy> at fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>, or <Gibbs free energy> at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>. The choice follows the experiment: a rigid sealed vessel and a sealed cylinder with a freely moving loaded piston impose different constraints. Both may exchange <heat> with a <thermal reservoir>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-00-thermodynamics-of-materials-fall-2002/14c95abd6fac023d84bd33c5d74d787d_lecture_02_oneside.PDF[W. C. Carter, MIT thermodynamics notes, sections 2-1.14 and 2-1.19].

\Image[thermodynamic-potential-constraints.png]
{title=Choose <Helmholtz free energy> for fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>, or <Gibbs free energy> for fixed <temperature> and <pressure>}
{description=Original schematic of two closed vessels in contact with a <thermal reservoir>. Composition is fixed, and only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> is allowed. The right piston permits the <volume (thermodynamics)> to change.}

The useful insight is that <internal energy> alone cannot rank these alternatives: exchanging <heat> also changes the surroundings' <entropy>. Subtracting $TS$ packages that accounting into a property of the chosen system. Adding $pV$ additionally accounts for displacing the surroundings at fixed <pressure>. A <free energy> is a <state function>; supplied <heat> describes a transfer during a process. Under the stated constraints, supplied <heat> is $Q=\Delta U$ at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> or $Q=\Delta H$ at fixed <pressure>, whereas neither $\Delta F$ nor $\Delta G$ generally equals $Q$.

A lower <free energy> identifies a thermodynamic preference, without specifying a timescale. A barrier can trap a system in a <local minimum>, producing <metastability> although a lower <global minimum> exists. The <enzyme> example illustrates the corresponding distinction for <chemical reactions>: changing the route can change the <reaction rate> while preserving the endpoint <Gibbs free energy> difference and <chemical equilibrium>.

= Free-energy functional
{parent=Thermodynamic free energy}
{title2=$\mathcal A[m]$}

A <free-energy functional> assigns a <free energy> to an entire spatial <order parameter> configuration rather than only to one uniform value. In <Landau-Ginzburg theory>, a <local derivative expansion> gives $\mathcal A[m]=\int d^Dx\{\kappa(\nabla m)^2/2+V(m)\}$, with appropriate boundary terms and regular backgrounds. The <Landau approximation> minimizes this functional; a fluctuating <statistical field theory> instead integrates its <Boltzmann weight> over configurations. These procedures need not give identical <critical exponents>.

= Helmholtz free energy
{title2=$F$}
{parent=Thermodynamic free energy}
{c}
{wiki}

= Helmholtz energy
{c}
{synonym}

The <Helmholtz free energy> is
$$
F=U-TS,
$$
where $U$ is <internal energy>, also denoted $E$ elsewhere here, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $S$ is <entropy>. For the <closed system> of fixed composition and only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> described above, spontaneous relaxation at fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)> cannot increase $F$. Stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> is a <global minimum> over the permitted alternatives, including different <thermodynamic phases>. A rigid vessel fixes the total <volume (thermodynamics)>; it does not require each coexisting <thermodynamic phase> to retain its own <volume (thermodynamics)>.

For a constructed comparison at $300\,\mathrm K$, suppose one candidate has $1.5\,\mathrm{kJ}$ more <internal energy> but $6\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$ more <entropy> than another at the same <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>. Its difference is
$$
\Delta F=1500-300(6)=-300\,\mathrm J.
$$
Thus the candidate with higher <internal energy> has lower <Helmholtz free energy>. This is the practical reason for retaining the <entropy> term when selecting a state.

For equilibrium states within a smooth branch of fixed composition, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $dU=T\,dS-p\,dV$, hence
$$
dF=-S\,dT-p\,dV,\qquad
S=-\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial T}\right)_V,\qquad
p=-\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial V}\right)_T.
$$
These <derivatives> extract <entropy> and <pressure>; the differential is not an evolution equation for relaxation. If <chemical species> amounts $n_i$ vary, add $\sum_i\mu_i\,dn_i$, with molar <chemical potentials> $\mu_i$. A <closed system> can still change <chemical species> amounts through <chemical reactions>; the fixed-composition simplification then needs the conserved-inventory constraints of <chemical equilibrium>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/e763a06aa3b5de09cd6bfea6280f219c_5_60_lecture14.pdf[MIT 5.60, Lecture 14, fundamental equations and chemical potentials].

The minimum principle has a short explanation. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>, no boundary <work> occurs, so $Q=\Delta U$. A <thermal reservoir> at positive <temperature> $T$ loses <entropy> $Q/T$. Consequently
$$
\Delta S_{\rm total}=\Delta S-\frac{\Delta U}{T}=-\frac{\Delta F}{T}\geq0,
$$
by the <second law of thermodynamics>. Here both endpoint states share the imposed <temperature>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/64efc6f359b20c43dab61fc77791d744_5_60_lecture12.pdf[MIT 5.60, Lecture 12, criteria for spontaneous change].

The microscopic counterpart is $F=-k_BT\ln Z$ in the <canonical ensemble>, with <Boltzmann constant> $k_B$ and <partition function> $Z$. The <partition function> examples compute the balance between <internal energy> and <entropy>; the <chemical equilibrium> example already displays an ideal-mixture <Helmholtz free energy> minimum.

= Gibbs free energy
{parent=Thermodynamic free energy}
{c}
{title2=$G$}
{wiki}

= Gibbs energy
{c}
{synonym}

The <Gibbs free energy> is
$$
G=U-TS+pV=H-TS,\qquad H=U+pV,
$$
where $H$ is <enthalpy>. For a <closed system> of fixed composition, with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, spontaneous relaxation at fixed <temperature> and <pressure> cannot increase $G$; stable <thermodynamic equilibrium> minimizes it over the allowed states. A loaded piston can move while maintaining the imposed <pressure>, so the <enthalpy> term includes the displacement cost. Along a smooth equilibrium branch of fixed composition,
$$
dG=-S\,dT+V\,dp.
$$
Thus its <temperature> slope at fixed <pressure> is $-S$, while its <pressure> slope at fixed <temperature> is $V$. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/7bc33304ab1db561a64b4a6b12e25476_5_60_lecture13.pdf[MIT 5.60, Lecture 13, Gibbs free energy and its derivatives].

Consider two illustrative <thermodynamic phases>, A and B, of the same substance at one fixed <pressure>. Write B minus A, using quantities per <mole>:
$$
\Delta H=12\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta S=40\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}=0.040\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}.
$$
Treat these differences as approximately constant over a limited <temperature> window. Then
$$
\frac{\Delta G(T)}{\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}}=12-0.040\frac{T}{\mathrm K},\qquad
T_* =\frac{\Delta H}{\Delta S}=300\,\mathrm K.
$$
At $280\,\mathrm K$, $\Delta G=+0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, so A is preferred. At $320\,\mathrm K$, $\Delta G=-0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, so B is preferred. At $300\,\mathrm K$, both have equal molar <Gibbs free energy>, permitting <phase coexistence> in this bulk model. B's greater <entropy> outweighs its greater <enthalpy> above the crossing.

\Image[free-energy-temperature-crossing.png]
{title=Illustrative molar <Gibbs free energy> difference crosses zero at $300\,\mathrm K$}
{description=Constructed B-minus-A values: $+0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at $280\,\mathrm K$ and $-0.8\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at $320\,\mathrm K$. The constants approximate a limited <temperature> window and represent no measured substance.}

Each plotted point compares alternatives at the same <temperature>; moving along the curve changes the imposed <temperature>. The model predicts preference, not instantaneous conversion: <nucleation> barriers can sustain <metastability>. Real <enthalpy> and <entropy> differences generally vary with <temperature> through <heat capacities>, bending this line outside the approximation.

At fixed <pressure>, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives $Q=\Delta H$ under the stated <work> restriction, so the same <thermal reservoir> accounting yields $\Delta S_{\rm total}=-\Delta G/T\geq0$. Variable composition restores $\sum_i\mu_i\,dn_i$ to $dG$; <constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium> handles those permitted changes.

The historical foundation is Gibbs's https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scientific_Papers_of_Josiah_Willard_Gibbs,_Volume_1/Chapter_IIIa[On the Equilibrium of Heterogeneous Substances, first part completed in 1876, continued in 1878]{ref}. Its equations (87) and (91) introduce the combinations now called <Helmholtz free energy> and <Gibbs free energy>, connecting <entropy> criteria, <chemical potentials> and <phase coexistence> within one framework.

= Gibbs free energy of an ideal-gas mixture
{c}
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

For a single-phase <ideal gas> mixture with molar amounts $N_i$, common <temperature> $T$, and <pressure> $P$, extensivity and ideal mixing give
$$
G=\sum_iN_i\mu_i=N\sum_i x_i\left[\mu_i^\circ(T)+\mathcal RT\log\left(\frac{x_iP}{P^\circ}\right)\right].
$$
Here $N=\sum_iN_i$, $x_i$ is the <mole fraction>, $\mathcal R$ is the molar gas constant, and $P^\circ$ is the standard <pressure>. Integrating $\partial\mu_i/\partial P=\mathcal RT/P$ and including the ideal mixing <entropy> proves $\mu_i=\mu_i^\circ+\mathcal RT\log(x_iP/P^\circ)$. Reactions can change $N$, so fractions alone do not set the extensive <Gibbs free energy>. Its composition Hessian is $\mathcal RT(\delta_{ij}/N_i-1/N)$; it is positive semidefinite by the weighted <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>.

= Gibbs free-energy Maxwell relation
{c}
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

Equality of mixed derivatives of $dG=-S\,dT+V\,dP$ gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial P}\right)_T
=-\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_P.
$$

= Chemical-potential balance for a reaction
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

For a reaction with stoichiometric changes $\nu_i$, varying the reaction extent at fixed temperature and pressure gives
$$
dG=\left(\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i\right)d\xi.
$$
Equilibrium requires $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i=0$.

= Thermochemical equilibrium
{parent=Chemical-potential balance for a reaction}

Thermochemical equilibrium requires every chemical reaction to have zero Gibbs free-energy change. For ideal reactants, the equilibrium constant is $K=\exp[-\Delta_rG^\circ/(RT)]$ after standard-state activity factors are included.

= Constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium
{parent=Thermochemical equilibrium}

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, a closed reacting system reaches <thermochemical equilibrium> by minimizing <Gibbs free energy> over nonnegative species amounts subject to conserved elemental abundances. For an allowed <stoichiometric vector> $\nu$, an interior minimum has $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i=0$. More generally the <chemical potential> vector lies in the row span of the elemental conservation matrix at positive concentrations, with the appropriate one-sided inequalities for absent species. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives the thermodynamic differential, while the <second law of thermodynamics> supplies the minimization direction.

= Reverse reaction rate from equilibrium thermodynamics
{parent=Thermochemical equilibrium}

For an elementary reversible reaction, detailed balance gives $k_f\prod_r n_r^{\nu_r}=k_r\prod_p n_p^{\nu_p}$ at equilibrium. Hence the reverse rate coefficient is the forward coefficient divided by the equilibrium constant, with units fixed by the reaction stoichiometry.

= Phase coexistence curve
{parent=Gibbs free energy}

Two phases coexist where their molar Gibbs free energies agree. Differentiating that equality along the coexistence curve relates its slope to the entropy and volume jumps.

= Triple point
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}

= Vapor pressure
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}
{title2=$p_{\rm sat}(T)$}
{wiki}

= Saturation pressure
{synonym}

The equilibrium <pressure> of a vapor in contact with its <liquid> or <solid> at a specified <temperature>. For pure <liquid>–<gas> <phase coexistence> with negligible interface effects, it is the <pressure> on their <phase coexistence curve>. In a <gas> <mixture>, distinguish the component's <partial pressure> from the total <pressure>; the pure-substance <integrated Clausius-Clapeyron relation> does not describe the total <pressure> of arbitrary humid air.

= Maxwell construction
{c}
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}

= Maxwell equal-area construction
{c}
{synonym}

At fixed <temperature>, choose a coexistence <pressure> $p_*$ such that the gas and liquid have equal <chemical potentials>. Because $d\mu=v\,dp$, this gives $\int_{v_l}^{v_g}v\,dp=0$, or equivalently $\int_{v_l}^{v_g}[p(v,T)-p_*]\,dv=0$. The equal areas replace the unstable part of an isotherm by a horizontal coexistence segment.

= Phase diagram
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}
{wiki}

A phase diagram partitions a space of thermodynamic variables into regions occupied by different equilibrium phases and records the coexistence curves between them.

= Solidus
{parent=Phase diagram}
{title2=$T_S(C)$}

The solidus bounds the entirely solid equilibrium region of a <phase diagram>. For a linear <liquidus> $T_L(C_l)=-mC_l$ and a constant <segregation coefficient> $C_s=k_D C_l$, the corresponding solidus is $T_S(C_s)=-mC_s/k_D$. The <concentration> argument is the solid <concentration>, not the neighboring liquid <concentration>.

= Lever rule
{parent=Phase diagram}
{title2=$\phi=(\overline C-C_s)/(C_l-C_s)$}
{wiki}

The lever rule finds phase fractions from a horizontal tie line on a <phase diagram>. For solid and liquid <concentrations> $C_s,C_l$ and bulk <concentration> $\overline C$, conservation gives $\overline C=(1-\phi)C_s+\phi C_l$. Thus $\phi=(\overline C-C_s)/(C_l-C_s)$ is the <liquid fraction>, provided $C_s\le\overline C\le C_l$. For pure solid, $\phi=\overline C/C_l$.

= Bicritical point
{parent=Phase diagram}

A bicritical point is where two continuous transition lines and a first-order line meet, typically separating a disordered phase from two competing ordered phases.

= Liquidus
{parent=Phase diagram}
{wiki}

The liquidus is the locus in a <phase diagram> above which the equilibrium state is entirely liquid. In a dilute binary solution it is often approximated locally by a linear relation such as $T_L=T_0-mC$.

= Freezing-point depression
{parent=Liquidus}
{wiki}

Freezing-point depression is the lowering of a solvent's equilibrium freezing temperature by dissolved solute. In the linear dilute approximation, $T_L=T_0-mC$ with $m>0$.

= Eutectic system
{parent=Phase diagram}
{wiki}

An eutectic system has a composition and temperature at which a liquid is in equilibrium with two distinct solid phases. Cooling liquid of that composition through the eutectic temperature produces both solid phases together.

= Eutectic composition
{parent=Eutectic system}
{title2=$C_E$}

The eutectic composition is the liquid <concentration> at the <eutectic temperature>, where liquid and two solid phases coexist. It is a composition coordinate on a <phase diagram>, not the overall <concentration> of every sample in an <eutectic system>.

= Eutectic temperature
{title2=$T_E$}
{parent=Eutectic system}

The eutectic temperature is the temperature of simultaneous liquid–two-solid <phase coexistence> in an <eutectic system>. In directional freezing of a solution away from the eutectic composition, a <mushy layer> can end at this temperature, where its remaining liquid solidifies.

= First-order phase transition
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}

At a first-order phase transition, the Gibbs free energy is continuous while at least one first derivative, such as entropy or volume, jumps. A nonzero entropy jump produces latent heat $L=T\Delta S$.

= Two-phase scalar coexistence endpoint conditions
{parent=First-order phase transition}
{title2=$V'=V''=V^{(3)}=0,\quad V^{(4)}>0$}

A smooth scalar <Landau free energy> can end a two-phase <phase coexistence curve> by merging two minima and their intervening maximum. At a generic stable endpoint, its first three derivatives vanish and its fourth derivative is positive. A field shift and two mixed control parameters reduce the local form to a quartic <Landau free energy>; the order-parameter jump then vanishes and the <magnetic susceptibility> diverges. This conclusion assumes there is no competing third phase and that the endpoint lies inside the control-parameter domain. It is not a general theorem about every first-order line: a sextic potential with fixed negative quartic coefficient has a symmetry-related ordered coexistence line that ends at a triple point with a finite jump.

= Spin-flop transition
{parent=First-order phase transition}

= Classical nucleation theory
{parent=First-order phase transition}
{wiki}

Classical nucleation theory balances the positive surface free energy of a droplet against its negative bulk free-energy gain. In three dimensions,
$$
F(R)=4\pi\sigma R^2-\frac{4\pi}{3}\Delta R^3
$$
has critical radius $R^*=2\sigma/\Delta$ and barrier $F^*=16\pi\sigma^3/(3\Delta^2)$.

= Critical nucleus
{parent=Classical nucleation theory}

A critical nucleus is the unstable droplet at the maximum of the nucleation free energy. Smaller droplets shrink, while larger droplets lower their free energy by growing.

= Arrhenius nucleation time
{parent=Classical nucleation theory}

Thermal crossing of a nucleation barrier $F^*$ has rate proportional to $e^{-F^*/(k_BT)}$ and mean waiting time proportional to $e^{F^*/(k_BT)}$, up to a kinetic prefactor.

= Latent heat
{parent=Phase coexistence curve}
{title2=$L=T\Delta S=\Delta H$}
{wiki}

Latent heat is the <heat> absorbed or released in a <first-order phase transition> through its <entropy> jump. For an equilibrium conversion from <thermodynamic phase> $\alpha$ to <thermodynamic phase> $\beta$ at fixed <pressure>, evaluate both <thermodynamic phases> at the same <temperature> and <pressure> on their <phase coexistence curve>:
$$
L=T_{\rm tr}(S_\beta-S_\alpha)=H_\beta-H_\alpha.
$$
Use one normalization throughout: $L$, $S$ and $H$ may all be per <mole>, giving $L$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$, or all per unit <mass>, giving $L$ in $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}$. Here $S$ is <entropy> and $H$ is <enthalpy> on that basis. Equal <Gibbs free energies> of the coexisting <thermodynamic phases> give $\Delta H=T_{\rm tr}\Delta S$. Identifying this <enthalpy> difference with supplied <heat> assumes a <closed system> and an equilibrium conversion with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>. <Statistical Physics by David Tong>[Tong, Statistical Physics, phase coexistence and latent heat]{ref}

For a pure substance with positive <latent heat> per unit <mass> $L$, let $x$ be the <phase fraction> of the higher-<enthalpy> <thermodynamic phase>, measured by <mass>. While both <thermodynamic phases> coexist at the fixed <pressure>,
$$
Q=mL(x_2-x_1),\qquad T=T_{\rm tr}.
$$
Adding <heat> changes $x$ rather than the <temperature>; this creates the horizontal portion of a <heating curve>. Once $x=1$, additional <heat> raises the <temperature> according to <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>. Reversing the conversion releases <heat>. The plateau represents changing <enthalpy>, rather than a finite single-<thermodynamic phase> <heat capacity> satisfying $Q=C_p\Delta T$. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.5 Phase Changes>{ref}

For a constructed fixed-<pressure> model, take $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, initial <temperature> $280\,\mathrm K$, transition <temperature> $T_{\rm tr}=300\,\mathrm K$, <solid> <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_s=2\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, <latent heat> $L=100\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$, and <liquid> <specific heat capacity at constant pressure> $c_l=3\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. These are stipulated model parameters, not empirical properties of a named material. Heating the <solid> to $300\,\mathrm K$ takes $40\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <sensible heat>; converting it fully to <liquid> takes another $100\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of <heat>; heating the <liquid> to $320\,\mathrm K$ takes $60\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The total is $200\,\mathrm{kJ}$. After a total <heat> input of $90\,\mathrm{kJ}$, $x=(90-40)/100=1/2$ and the <temperature> is still $300\,\mathrm K$. The corresponding per-unit-<mass> <entropy> jump is $L/T_{\rm tr}=\frac13\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.

\Image[sensible-and-latent-heat.png]
{title=<Sensible heat> raises <temperature>; <latent heat> changes <phase fraction>}
{description=Constructed $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ fixed-<pressure> model with total supplied <heat> starting at zero at $280\,\mathrm K$. The plateau runs from $40$ to $140\,\mathrm{kJ}$, while the <liquid> <phase fraction> by <mass> rises from zero to one. The horizontal axis measures $Q=\Delta H$, rather than absolute <internal energy>.}

A <continuous phase transition> has no <latent heat>. Its <heat capacity> can have a jump or a singularity without a finite interval in which added <heat> converts coexisting <thermodynamic phases> at a constant <temperature>. A heating plateau therefore requires the stated <phase coexistence> conditions; it is not a feature of every <phase transition>. <Statistical Physics by David Tong>[Tong, Statistical Physics, first-order and continuous transitions]{ref}

= Stefan number
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ste}=c_p\Delta T/L$}
{parent=Latent heat}
{wiki}

The Stefan number compares sensible heat $c_p\Delta T$ with <latent heat> $L$ per unit mass. Some solidification literature uses its reciprocal as the Stefan parameter, so the defining formula must accompany the convention.

= Latent-to-sensible heat ratio
{title2=$S=L/(c_p\Delta T)$}
{parent=Stefan number}

= Inverse Stefan number
{synonym}

The latent-to-sensible heat ratio is the reciprocal of the <Stefan number> defined by $\mathrm{Ste}=c_p\Delta T/L$. Large $S$ means that phase change uses much more energy than changing temperature by $\Delta T$.

= Clausius-Clapeyron relation
{parent=Latent heat}
{c}
{wiki=Clausius–Clapeyron_relation}
{title2=$dp/dT=L/(T\Delta v)$}

= Clapeyron equation
{c}
{synonym}

= Clausius-Clapeyron equation
{c}
{synonym}

The <Clapeyron equation> connects the slope of a <phase coexistence curve> to the <latent heat> and the change in <volume (thermodynamics)> during a <first-order phase transition>. A steep boundary can indicate a large <latent heat>, a small change in <volume (thermodynamics)>, or both. This lets a measured boundary constrain caloric properties and predicts how changing <pressure> shifts a <melting> <temperature>.

For a pure substance in bulk <phase coexistence>, label the initial and final <thermodynamic phases> $\alpha$ and $\beta$. Both have the same absolute <temperature> $T$, <pressure> $p$, and <chemical potential>. With $s$, $h$, and $v$ denoting <entropy>, <enthalpy>, and <volume (thermodynamics)> on one common basis, define $\Delta s=s_\beta-s_\alpha$, $\Delta h=h_\beta-h_\alpha$, and $\Delta v=v_\beta-v_\alpha$. Then
$$
\boxed{\frac{dp}{dT}=\frac{\Delta s}{\Delta v}=\frac{L}{T\Delta v}},\qquad L=\Delta h=T\Delta s.
$$
The <derivative> follows the <phase coexistence curve>; it is not a <partial derivative> within one <thermodynamic phase>. Use <molar volume> $v_m$ with molar <latent heat> $L_m$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$, or <specific volume> $v$ with <latent heat> $L$ in $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}$. Both give $dp/dT$ in $\mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}$. For <molar mass> $M$, $L_m=ML$ and $\Delta v_m=M\Delta v$, so the predicted slope is independent of the chosen basis. This formulation assumes a <simple compressible system>, fixed composition, and negligible interface contributions; a curved interface can instead require the <Young–Laplace equation>.

For positive <latent heat> in the stated conversion direction, the sign of the slope is the sign of $\Delta v$. Converting <liquid> to <gas> normally increases <volume (thermodynamics)> and gives a positive slope. The <negative melting-point slope of water> illustrates the opposite sign. At a <thermodynamic critical point>, the coexisting <gas> and <liquid> merge: both $L$ and $\Delta v$ vanish, so their ratio needs a limiting analysis rather than substitution of zero into the <Clapeyron equation>.<Statistical Physics by David Tong>[§5.1.2, equation (5.185) and the discussion of the critical endpoint]{ref}

\Image[clapeyron-coexistence-slopes-and-volume-jump.png]
{title=<Phase coexistence curve> tangents: positive vaporization slope and negative <water> <melting> slope}

A short derivation uses the molar <Gibbs free energy> $g_m$, equal to the <chemical potential> for a pure substance. Equality persists along the boundary, so $dg_{m,\alpha}=dg_{m,\beta}$ with $dg_m=-s_m\,dT+v_m\,dp$. Therefore $-\Delta s_m\,dT+\Delta v_m\,dp=0$. Equality of $g_m=h_m-Ts_m$ also gives $\Delta h_m=T\Delta s_m$. Eliminating $\Delta s_m$ yields the <Clapeyron equation>.

= Ideal-vapor Clausius-Clapeyron approximation
{c}
{parent=Clausius-Clapeyron relation}
{title2=$d\ln p/dT\simeq L_m/(RT^2)$}

For <liquid>–<gas> <phase coexistence>, make two distinct approximations: the <gas> obeys the <ideal gas law>, $v_{m,g}=RT/p$, and its <molar volume> greatly exceeds the <liquid> <molar volume>, $v_{m,g}\gg v_{m,l}$. Here $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $p$ is the <saturation pressure> of the pure substance. Substitution into the <Clapeyron equation> gives
$$
\frac{dp}{dT}\simeq\frac{L_m(T)p}{RT^2},\qquad \frac{d\ln(p/p_*)}{dT}\simeq\frac{L_m(T)}{RT^2},
$$
where $p_*>0$ is any fixed reference <pressure>, making the <logarithm> dimensionless. Constant <latent heat> is a further approximation, needed for the elementary <integrated Clausius-Clapeyron relation> below.<Statistical Physics by David Tong>[§5.1.2, “An Approximate Solution to the Clausius-Clapeyron Equation”: the three assumptions and the exponential solution]{ref}

The neglected <liquid> <molar volume> has a transparent error estimate. If the <gas> is ideal but $\epsilon=v_{m,l}/v_{m,g}$ is retained, then
$$
\left(\frac{dp}{dT}\right)_{\rm full}=\frac{L_m p}{RT^2(1-\epsilon)},\qquad \frac{(dp/dT)_{\rm approx}}{(dp/dT)_{\rm full}}=1-\epsilon.
$$
Thus ignoring the <liquid> <molar volume> underestimates this slope by a relative amount $\epsilon$. This estimate does not bound errors from treating a <real gas> as ideal or from freezing $L_m(T)$. Near a <thermodynamic critical point>, the <gas> and <liquid> <molar volumes> approach each other and these dilute-vapor assumptions fail.

= Integrated Clausius-Clapeyron relation
{c}
{parent=Ideal-vapor Clausius-Clapeyron approximation}
{title2=$\ln(p_2/p_1)\simeq-(L_m/R)(1/T_2-1/T_1)$}

If the molar <latent heat> $L_m$ is approximately constant on the chosen <temperature> interval, the <ideal-vapor Clausius-Clapeyron approximation> integrates to
$$
\ln\frac{p_2}{p_1}\simeq-\frac{L_m}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_2}-\frac{1}{T_1}\right),\qquad p(T)\simeq p_1\exp\left[\frac{L_m}{R}\left(\frac{1}{T_1}-\frac{1}{T}\right)\right].
$$
For $L_m>0$, higher absolute <temperature> gives higher <saturation pressure>. A plot of $\ln(p/p_1)$ against <inverse temperature> $1/T$ is a straight line of slope $-L_m/R$. One reference pair $(T_1,p_1)$ fixes its intercept. On a per-unit-<mass> basis replace $L_m/R$ by $L/R_s$, with $R_s=R/M$ and $M$ the <molar mass>; using $L$ in $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}$ with the molar $R$ would give incompatible units.

If only the constant-<latent heat> approximation is relaxed, the same ideal-vapor model instead gives
$$
\ln\frac{p_2}{p_1}\simeq\frac{1}{R}\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\frac{L_m(T)}{T^2}\,dT.
$$
Consequently an apparent straight-line fit across a broad <temperature> range need not represent a single material constant. The simple exponential also approaches a finite value as $T\to\infty$; this is a property of the stipulated formula, not a prediction that <liquid>–<gas> <phase coexistence> extends beyond a <thermodynamic critical point>.

= Stipulated vapor-pressure calculation
{parent=Integrated Clausius-Clapeyron relation}

Consider an unnamed model substance with molar <latent heat> $L_m=40.0\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and <saturation pressure> $p_1=10.0\,\mathrm{kPa}$ at $T_1=300\,\mathrm K$. Stipulate the <ideal-vapor Clausius-Clapeyron approximation> throughout $280\leq T\leq340\,\mathrm K$, constant $L_m$, and $R=8.314\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. These parameters define an illustrative model, not a measured dataset or a fit to <water>. At $T_2=330\,\mathrm K$,
$$
\ln\frac{p_2}{p_1}=\frac{40000}{8.314}\left(\frac{1}{300}-\frac{1}{330}\right)\simeq1.458,\qquad p_2\simeq42.97\,\mathrm{kPa}.
$$
At the reference state the <gas> <molar volume> is $RT_1/p_1=0.24942\,\mathrm{m^3\,mol^{-1}}$, and the local slope is $dp/dT\simeq0.5346\,\mathrm{kPa\,K^{-1}}$. The tangent estimate $p_2\approx10.0+30(0.5346)=26.04\,\mathrm{kPa}$ is much lower than the integrated result: a local <derivative> should not be extrapolated over this whole interval. By contrast, over a small <temperature> increment, the fractional sensitivity is $d\ln(p/p_1)/dT=L_m/(RT_1^2)\simeq0.05346\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$.

\Image[ideal-vapor-pressure-and-inverse-temperature.png]
{title=Stipulated <saturation pressure> versus <temperature> and its linear <logarithm> versus <inverse temperature>}

= Latent heat from a vapor-pressure slope
{parent=Integrated Clausius-Clapeyron relation}
{title2=$L_m\simeq-R\,d\ln(p/p_*)/d(1/T)$}

Within the <ideal-vapor Clausius-Clapeyron approximation>, a local slope of the <natural logarithm> of <saturation pressure> against <inverse temperature> estimates the molar <latent heat>:
$$
L_m(T)\simeq-R\frac{d\ln(p/p_*)}{d(1/T)}.
$$
In the <stipulated vapor-pressure calculation>, the slope is $-4811.16\,\mathrm K$, which recovers $40.0\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. For variable $L_m(T)$, a two-point estimate gives an average weighted by $T^{-2}$, rather than the <latent heat> at an arbitrary midpoint. Differentiating noisy <pressure> measurements amplifies their errors. Outside the ideal-vapor regime, use independently determined <molar volumes> in $L_m=T\Delta v_m\,dp/dT$; a straight-looking graph alone cannot establish that the <gas> is ideal.

= Saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement
{parent=Clausius-Clapeyron relation}

A concrete experiment turns the <phase coexistence curve> into pairs of <temperature> and <saturation pressure>. A 1969 paper reports <water> measurements made in 1942 and 1947–1949 using a boiler and precision <mercury (element)> <manometer>. <Platinum resistance thermometers> read the boiler <temperature> through a <resistance bridge>. The <pressure>-transfer line passed through an ice-water condenser and a dry-ice trap; <helium> filled connecting sections. Corrected <mercury (element)> column height gave the <pressure> at the thermometer coils, including <hydrostatic pressure> contributions from <water> vapor, <helium>, <mercury (element)>, and <mercury (element)> vapor.<Some Precise Measurements of the Vapor Pressure of Water in the Range From 25 to 100 °C by H. F. Stimson>[Stimson, §§1–2, pp. 493–495: boiler, bridge thermometry, and fluid-head corrections]{ref}

Controls included normal/reversed-lead readings, readings at two thermometer <electric currents> extrapolated to zero current to remove self-heating, repeated bridge calibration, and daily <triple point> <thermometer calibration> checks. Cellar <temperature> measurements corrected fluid-column and gauge-block <thermal expansion>. One measurement series was excluded because a trapped gas bubble was suspected of corrupting the <mercury (element)> head. The paper adjusted measurements to round <temperatures> using previously tabulated slopes; those adjusted values therefore are not independent measurements of those slopes.<Some Precise Measurements of the Vapor Pressure of Water in the Range From 25 to 100 °C by H. F. Stimson>[Stimson, §2, pp. 494–495, and §3, p. 495: corrections and suspected gas-bubble bias]{ref}

The thermodynamic inference requires <liquid> and <gas> in <phase coexistence>, a well-defined common <temperature>, and a <pressure> corrected to that same location. Leaks, contamination, or a <temperature gradient> can spoil that interpretation. A separate <calorimetry> measurement of <latent heat>, together with the two <specific volumes>, allows a test of the full <Clapeyron equation>. Converting a measured <phase coexistence curve> slope directly into <latent heat> instead assumes that equation; it is not an independent test.

= Common-pressure melting-point slope
{parent=Clausius-Clapeyron relation}
{title2=$dT_m/dp=-T_m(1/\rho_s-1/\rho_l)/L$}

When the solid and liquid have the same <pressure>, equality of <chemical potentials> implies $-(s_l-s_s)dT+(1/\rho_l-1/\rho_s)dp=0$. With <latent heat of fusion> $L=T_m(s_l-s_s)$, this gives the displayed slope. If solid is less dense than liquid, raising the common pressure depresses the <melting> temperature. $T_m$ is absolute <temperature>. This coexistence-curve derivative differs from a thermomolecular pressure difference between equal-density phases.

= Negative melting-point slope of water
{parent=Common-pressure melting-point slope}

For a local illustrative <ice>–<water> <melting> calculation, stipulate $T_m=273.15\,\mathrm K$, <latent heat of fusion> $L=334\,\mathrm{kJ\,kg^{-1}}$, <solid> <mass density> $\rho_s=917\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, and <liquid> <mass density> $\rho_l=1000\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$. These are explicitly stipulated rounded inputs, not measurements made here. The <specific volume> change on <melting> is
$$
\Delta v=\frac{1}{\rho_l}-\frac{1}{\rho_s}=-9.0513\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}}.
$$
The <common-pressure melting-point slope> therefore gives
$$
\frac{dT_m}{dp}=\frac{T_m\Delta v}{L}=-7.4022\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{K\,Pa^{-1}}\simeq-0.07402\,\mathrm{K\,MPa^{-1}}.
$$
A small $1\,\mathrm{MPa}$ increase in the common <pressure> thus lowers the <melting> <temperature> by about $0.074\,\mathrm K$ in this local model. Positive <latent heat> does not force a positive <phase coexistence curve> slope: <ice> contracts on conversion to <liquid> <water>. Increasing <pressure> favors the smaller <specific volume>. This calculation compares equilibrium states of the same two <thermodynamic phases>; extending its tangent to large <pressures> would require updated <mass densities>, <latent heat of fusion>, and the relevant <ice> <phase diagram>.

= Chemical potential
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mu$}

The <chemical potential> measures how a <thermodynamic potential> changes when matter is added under specified constraints. It plays for matter exchange the role that <temperature> plays for <heat> exchange: a difference supplies a direction, and equality characterizes <thermodynamic equilibrium> when the corresponding exchange is allowed. This connects <diffusion>, <phase coexistence> and <chemical equilibrium> through one quantity.

For a single component, the particle convention is
$$
\mu_{\rm particle}=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial N}\right)_{S,V},
$$
where $U$ is <internal energy>, $N$ counts particles, $S$ is <entropy> and $V$ is <volume (thermodynamics)>. This <partial derivative> holds <entropy> and <volume (thermodynamics)> fixed; it measures an incremental change rather than the average <energy> per particle. See https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys1.pdf[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 1.4].

In <chemistry> the molar convention instead uses <amount of substance> $n$, measured in <moles>:
$$
\mu_{\rm m}=\left(\frac{\partial G}{\partial n}\right)_{T,p},
\qquad n=\frac{N}{N_{\rm A}},
\qquad \mu_{\rm m}=N_{\rm A}\mu_{\rm particle}.
$$
Here $G$ is <Gibbs free energy>, $T$ is <temperature>, $p$ is <pressure> and $N_{\rm A}$ is the <Avogadro constant>. The units are $\mathrm J$ per particle versus $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$. For several <chemical species>, define each <chemical potential> by varying only its own particle count or <amount of substance>, with all other components' counts or amounts fixed. Many texts use $\mu$ for either convention, so the units and the variable being differentiated must identify it.

For a <simple compressible system> with variable component inventories, the fundamental differentials are
$$
dU=T\,dS-p\,dV+\sum_i\mu_{{\rm particle},i}\,dN_i,
\qquad
dG=-S\,dT+V\,dp+\sum_i\mu_{{\rm m},i}\,dn_i.
$$
They describe changes between states in <thermodynamic equilibrium> with only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>. If every component inventory is fixed, the sums vanish. The <Gibbs free energy> form follows by differentiating $G=U-TS+pV$ and using $dN_i=N_{\rm A}\,dn_i$; this is why the two definitions describe the same <chemical potential> in different units.

A <chemical potential> can be negative. Holding <entropy> fixed while adding matter need not increase <internal energy>; Tong explains this explicitly for a classical <ideal gas> in https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys2.pdf[Statistical Physics, section 2.2.4]. Its absolute sign also depends on the reference <energy>: replacing $U$ by $U+\epsilon_0N$ shifts $\mu_{\rm particle}$ by $\epsilon_0$. A common reference shift cancels from differences for the same <chemical species>, which are what determine exchange. Thus “energy cost” means a signed increment, not an always positive amount that must be supplied.

= Chemical potential equality for particle exchange
{parent=Chemical potential}
{title2=$\mu_{{\rm m},A}=\mu_{{\rm m},B}$}

Consider two internally equilibrated reservoirs A and B at the same imposed <temperature> and <pressure>. They may have different compositions or represent different phases. Transfer a small positive <amount of substance> $\delta n$ of the same <chemical species> from A to B, holding the other inventories fixed. To first order, their total <Gibbs free energy> changes by
$$
\delta G_{\rm total}
=-\mu_{{\rm m},A}\delta n+\mu_{{\rm m},B}\delta n
=(\mu_{{\rm m},B}-\mu_{{\rm m},A})\delta n.
$$
Hence exchange toward lower <chemical potential> lowers <Gibbs free energy>. If transfer is allowed in both directions and the two reservoirs contain the exchangeable component, <thermodynamic equilibrium> requires equal <chemical potentials> for that component. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/e763a06aa3b5de09cd6bfea6280f219c_5_60_lecture14.pdf[MIT 5.60, Lecture 14, pages 1–2].

An impermeable partition can maintain unequal <chemical potentials>; equality is a condition on an allowed exchange. Two pure samples of the same <ideal gas> already have equal <chemical potentials> at the same <temperature> and <pressure>. Different compositions at a common <pressure> can instead give different <chemical potentials> for one component through its <thermodynamic activity>.

Equality concerns the same exchangeable component in different places, not all different <chemical species>. For a permitted <chemical reaction>, the corresponding interior <chemical equilibrium> criterion is $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_{{\rm m},i}=0$, with signed <stoichiometric vector> entries $\nu_i$. The existing <chemical equilibrium> and <constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium> sections develop those composition constraints.

= Chemical potential and Helmholtz free energy
{parent=Chemical potential equality for particle exchange}
{title2=$\mu_{\rm m}=(\partial F/\partial n)_{T,V}$}

Rigid compartments impose a different constraint: fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> rather than fixed <pressure>. With <Helmholtz free energy> $F=U-TS$,
$$
dF=-S\,dT-p\,dV+\sum_i\mu_{{\rm m},i}\,dn_i,
\qquad
\mu_{{\rm m},i}=\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial n_i}\right)_{T,V,n_{j\ne i}}.
$$
This follows immediately by differentiating $F=U-TS$ in the <chemical potential> differential above. It expresses the same <chemical potential> through the <thermodynamic potential> appropriate to fixed <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)>.

Consider a <closed system> consisting of two rigid compartments of equal <volume (thermodynamics)> $V$, maintained at a common fixed <temperature> $T$. A valve permits exchange of the same <ideal gas>; the partition stays in place. Initially $n_A=9\,\mathrm{mol}$ and $n_B=3\,\mathrm{mol}$, with conserved total <amount of substance> $n_{\rm tot}=12\,\mathrm{mol}$. These are macroscopic <moles>, modeled continuously, not twelve individual particles. By the <ideal gas law>, $p_B/p_A=n_B/n_A=1/3$, so initially
$$
\mu_{{\rm m},B}-\mu_{{\rm m},A}=-RT\ln3.
$$
At $300\,\mathrm K$ with the stipulated $R$, this is approximately $-2740.311\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$, favoring transfer from A to B.

At fixed $T$ and equal $V$, integrating the ideal-gas <chemical potential> gives the total <Helmholtz free energy>, up to a term independent of the allocation,
$$
F_{\rm total}
=RT\left[n_A\ln\frac{n_A}{n_{\rm ref}}+n_B\ln\frac{n_B}{n_{\rm ref}}-n_{\rm tot}\right]+C.
$$
Here $n_{\rm ref}$ is a positive reference <amount of substance>; any omitted common term linear in $n_A+n_B$ is constant along this exchange. Let $x=n_A/n_{\rm tot}$ be the <dimensionless> fraction in A. The relative <Helmholtz free energy> then takes the particularly simple form
$$
\frac{F_{\rm total}(x)-F_{\rm total}(1/2)}{n_{\rm tot}RT}
=x\ln x+(1-x)\ln(1-x)+\ln2.
$$
Its <derivative> along the allowed transfer and its second <derivative> are
$$
\frac{dF_{\rm total}}{dn_A}
=\mu_{{\rm m},A}-\mu_{{\rm m},B}
=RT\ln\frac{x}{1-x},
\qquad
\frac{d^2F_{\rm total}}{dn_A^2}
=RT\left(\frac{1}{n_A}+\frac{1}{n_B}\right)>0.
$$
Thus the <Helmholtz free energy> is a strictly <convex function> of the allocation for positive amounts. Its unique minimum has $x=1/2$, or $n_A=n_B=6\,\mathrm{mol}$, and equal <chemical potentials>. Initially its excess above this minimum is $n_{\rm tot}RT[\tfrac34\ln(3/2)+\tfrac14\ln(1/2)]\simeq3915.474\,\mathrm J$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Relaxation decreases the total <Helmholtz free energy> by this amount in the model; the final <pressures> are also equal because both compartments have the same <volume (thermodynamics)>.

\Image[chemical-potential-particle-transfer.png]
{title=<Chemical potential> differences are slopes of the total <Helmholtz free energy>}
{description=Two equal rigid compartments at fixed <temperature> start with $9\,\mathrm{mol}$ and $3\,\mathrm{mol}$ of the same <ideal gas>, and reach $6\,\mathrm{mol}$ each after exchange. Lower left: the <dimensionless> excess <Helmholtz free energy>. Lower right: its <derivative> with respect to the allocation, divided by $RT$, equals the <chemical potential> difference divided by $RT$. The horizontal variable is $x=n_A/n_{\rm tot}$.}

The beautiful connection is that the direction of matter exchange and the slope of a <free energy> curve are the same calculation. The <entropy> gained by redistributing the <ideal gas> makes equal allocation favorable here. As with <chemical equilibrium>, a <free energy> minimum identifies the favored state, while transport properties determine how quickly it is reached. The fixed-<temperature>, fixed-<pressure> <Gibbs free energy> criterion is summarized in <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/16.4 Free Energy>; the rigid experiment instead uses <Helmholtz free energy>.

= Chemical potential of an ideal gas
{parent=Chemical potential}
{title2=$\mu_{\rm m}=\mu_{\rm m}^\circ+RT\ln(p/p^\circ)$}

For a pure <ideal gas> at <temperature> $T$ and <pressure> $p$,
$$
\mu_{\rm m}(T,p)=\mu_{\rm m}^\circ(T)+RT\ln\frac{p}{p^\circ},
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $p^\circ$ specifies the reference <pressure> of the <standard state>. The ratio inside the <natural logarithm> is <dimensionless>. The <chemical potential> difference therefore depends on a ratio of <pressures>, not an absolute difference. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/e763a06aa3b5de09cd6bfea6280f219c_5_60_lecture14.pdf[MIT 5.60, Lecture 14, page 4].

For a <chemical species> in an ideal <gas> <mixture>, replace $p$ by its <partial pressure>. More generally write $\mu_{{\rm m},i}=\mu_{{\rm m},i}^\circ+RT\ln a_i$ using its <thermodynamic activity> $a_i$ and a consistent <standard state>. For an ideal dilute <chemical solution>, $a_i=c_i/c^\circ$ with reference <concentration> $c^\circ$; the <thermodynamic activity> section describes departures from this idealization.

As a computed comparison, take identical <chemical species> at $T=300\,\mathrm K$, with $p_A=2p^\circ$ and $p_B=p^\circ$. Stipulate $R=8.314462618\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Then
$$
\mu_{{\rm m},B}-\mu_{{\rm m},A}
=RT\ln\frac{p_B}{p_A}
=-RT\ln2
\simeq-1728.944\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Halving the <pressure> gives the same decrement whether starting from $2p^\circ$ or $20p^\circ$, provided the <ideal gas> approximation remains valid. This comparison uses different <pressures>; it is not the common-<pressure> reservoir setup of <chemical potential equality for particle exchange>. The fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> example in <chemical potential and Helmholtz free energy> supplies an exchange experiment with unequal initial <pressures>.

\Image[chemical-potential-and-pressure.png]
{title=<Ideal gas> <chemical potential> difference versus <pressure> ratio}
{description=At a common <temperature>, the <chemical potential> difference is $RT\ln(p_B/p_A)$. The arrows give the initial transfer direction through an open route between rigid compartments; the decreasing <thermodynamic potential> there is <Helmholtz free energy>. The marked comparison uses $300\,\mathrm K$ and the stipulated <molar gas constant>.}

= Electrochemical potential
{parent=Chemical potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$\widetilde\mu_{\rm m}=\mu_{\rm m}+zF_{\rm Far}\psi$}

The molar <electrochemical potential> combines the molar <chemical potential> $\mu_{\rm m}$ with the electrical contribution $zF_{\rm Far}\psi$, where $z$ is the signed charge number of an <ion>, $F_{\rm Far}=N_{\rm A}e$ is the <Faraday constant> and $\psi$ is <electric potential>. Both terms have units $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$. In the particle convention the corresponding expression is $\widetilde\mu_{\rm particle}=\mu_{\rm particle}+ze\psi$, with <elementary charge> $e$; multiplying it by the <Avogadro constant> gives the molar form. Transfer between locations at the same <temperature> and <pressure> is driven by differences in <electrochemical potential>. The <membrane transport> examples use it to distinguish neutral <solute> transfer from <ion> transfer.

= Electrochemical gradient
{parent=Electrochemical potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nabla\widetilde\mu_{\rm m}$}

An <electrochemical gradient> combines spatial changes in <chemical potential> and <electric potential> for a charged species. <Passive transport> proceeds downhill in <electrochemical potential> when an appropriate route is available; a <concentration> difference alone does not determine the direction for an <ion>.

= Chemical potential of a composition field
{parent=Chemical potential}
{title2=$\mu=\delta F/\delta\phi$}

For a composition <free energy> $F=\int[f(\phi)+\kappa|\nabla\phi|^2/2]$, the local chemical potential is the <functional derivative>
$$
\mu=f'(\phi)-\kappa\nabla^2\phi.
$$
At <phase coexistence>, exchanges between the bulk phases require equal <chemical potentials>. Mechanical equilibrium additionally requires equal <pressure> for a flat interface, or a <Young–Laplace equation> pressure jump for a curved interface.

= Chemical equilibrium
{parent=Chemical potential}
{wiki}

At <chemical equilibrium>, a reversible <chemical reaction> has equal forward and reverse <reaction rates>, so its macroscopic composition stays constant. Individual <molecules> can keep changing identity: the reaction has not stopped. This dynamic balance is the definition described by the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01023[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical equilibrium]{ref}. Equal <reaction rates> also need not mean equal <concentrations>. A small population converting rapidly can balance a large population converting slowly.

This distinction gives two useful questions. Which composition is favored at the specified <temperature> and other constraints? <Thermodynamics> answers through <chemical potentials> and an <equilibrium constant>. How quickly does a prepared mixture approach that composition? <Chemical kinetics> answers through <reaction rates>. Knowing the destination does not determine the journey's duration. A composition that barely changes during observation may reflect extremely slow <chemical kinetics>, so constancy alone does not establish <chemical equilibrium>.

Consider the idealized elementary <chemical reaction>
$$
\mathrm A\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B,
\qquad k_f=0.3\,\mathrm{s}^{-1},\qquad k_r=0.1\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}.
$$
Assume a well-mixed <closed system> at fixed <temperature> and <volume>, with first-order <law of mass action> rates in both directions and no competing <chemical reactions>. Write $a=[\mathrm A]$ and $b=[\mathrm B]$ for <concentrations> in $\mathrm M=\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm L$. The total $a+b=C=1\,\mathrm M$ is conserved because each conversion replaces one <molecule> by one <molecule>. This conservation is special to the chosen <stoichiometry>.

The forward and reverse <reaction rates>, measured as conversions per unit <volume> per unit time, are $J_f=k_fa$ and $J_r=k_rb$. Hence
$$
\frac{da}{dt}=-k_fa+k_rb=-(k_f+k_r)a+k_rC.
$$
Setting this <derivative> to zero gives
$$
a_{\rm eq}=\frac{k_rC}{k_f+k_r}=0.25\,\mathrm M,
\qquad b_{\rm eq}=0.75\,\mathrm M.
$$
With $a(0)=1\,\mathrm M$ and $b(0)=0$, solving the <ordinary differential equation> yields
$$
a(t)=\bigl(0.25+0.75e^{-0.4t/\mathrm s}\bigr)\,\mathrm M,
\qquad b(t)=\bigl(0.75-0.75e^{-0.4t/\mathrm s}\bigr)\,\mathrm M.
$$
At $t=5\,\mathrm s$, these are approximately $0.3515\,\mathrm M$ and $0.6485\,\mathrm M$. Initially $J_f=0.3\,\mathrm{M/s}$ and $J_r=0$. At <chemical equilibrium>, both are $0.075\,\mathrm{M/s}$, while the net <reaction rate> $J_f-J_r$ is zero. The persistent exchange is visible in the lower panel even after the upper panel has nearly flattened.

\Image[reversible-reaction-equilibrium.png]
{title=Constant equilibrium composition with continuing molecular conversion}
{description=The idealized <chemical reaction> starts entirely in A. Upper panel: <concentrations>. Lower panel: forward, reverse and net <reaction rates>. The equilibrium rates coincide at a positive value.}

Why does <chemical equilibrium> also involve a <free energy> minimum? For a <closed system> at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, without imposed non-expansion <work>, stable equilibrium minimizes <Gibbs free energy> over compositions allowed by conserved elemental inventories. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-00-thermodynamics-of-materials-fall-2002/14c95abd6fac023d84bd33c5d74d787d_lecture_02_oneside.PDF[W. C. Carter, MIT thermodynamics notes, sections 2-1.14 and 2-1.19]. This is <constrained Gibbs minimization for chemical equilibrium>: freely minimizing over unrelated species amounts would change the inventory. At fixed <temperature> and <volume>, the appropriate potential is <Helmholtz free energy>. In either setting, varying an allowed <extent of reaction> gives the corresponding <free energy> slope $\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i$, with molar <chemical potentials> and signed <stoichiometric vector> entries. An interior equilibrium has zero slope; stability requires a minimum. For A converting to B, this says $\mu_{\mathrm B}=\mu_{\mathrm A}$.

The beauty of this connection is that molecular exchange, a macroscopic composition, and a geometric minimum describe the same balance. In the ideal concentration model, let $x=b/C$ and choose the standard molar <chemical potential> difference consistent with $K=3$. Up to a composition-independent constant, the fixed-volume <Helmholtz free energy> then obeys
$$
\frac{F}{nRT}=(1-x)\ln(1-x)+x\ln x-x\ln3,
$$
where $n$ is the conserved total <amount of substance> and $R$ the <molar gas constant>. Its <derivative> is $\ln[x/(1-x)]-\ln3$, vanishing at $x=3/4$. Its second <derivative> is $1/x+1/(1-x)>0$ inside $0<x<1$, so this balance is the unique minimum. The mixing <entropy> terms explain why the favored composition still contains A.

= Chemical disequilibrium
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

A chemical system is out of local <chemical equilibrium> when reaction adjustment cannot keep pace with transport, imposed changes or external driving. Comparing the <chemical relaxation time> with transport and irradiation times identifies plausible departures. <Disequilibrium chemistry in an exoplanet atmosphere> combines this principle with mixing, horizontal advection and <atmospheric photochemistry>.

= Zero chemical potential from number-changing reactions
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}
{title2=$\mu_\phi=0$}

For one particle species in <chemical equilibrium> under $2\phi\leftrightarrow3\phi$, chemical balance gives $2\mu_\phi=3\mu_\phi$, hence $\mu_\phi=0$. Elastic $2\phi\leftrightarrow2\phi$ imposes no such constraint. In an expanding universe the number-changing reaction must remain fast compared with the <Hubble parameter>; after chemical freeze-out a nonzero <chemical potential> can develop even if elastic collisions maintain <kinetic equilibrium>.

= Chemical relaxation time
{title2=$\tau_{\rm chem}$}
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

The chemical relaxation time estimates how rapidly a perturbed abundance returns toward <chemical equilibrium>. Comparing it with the <eddy mixing time> identifies a <chemical quench level>.

For the A-to-B model in <chemical equilibrium>, deviations decay by <exponential decay> with $\tau_{\rm chem}=1/(k_f+k_r)=2.5\,\mathrm s$. After one such interval, $1/e$ of the initial deviation remains; reaching one percent takes $\ln(100)\tau_{\rm chem}\simeq11.51\,\mathrm s$. Multiplying both <rate constants> by two preserves their ratio and the equilibrium composition, but halves $\tau_{\rm chem}$ to $1.25\,\mathrm s$. The equilibrium exchange rates double to $0.15\,\mathrm{M/s}$. Faster exchange therefore coexists with the same final composition and the same <free energy> minimum.

\Image[equilibrium-vs-rate.png]
{title=Doubling both rates changes the approach time but preserves equilibrium}
{description=Left: B <concentration> for two pairs of <rate constants> with the same ratio. Right: the shared ideal-mixture <Helmholtz free energy>, shifted to zero at its constrained minimum.}

= Equilibrium constant
{title2=$K$}
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}
{wiki}

For a specified <chemical reaction> and <standard state> convention, the dimensionless <equilibrium constant> is the equilibrium value of the <reaction quotient> $Q=\prod_i a_i^{\nu_i}$, where $a_i$ denotes <thermodynamic activity> and $\nu_i$ a signed <stoichiometric vector> entry. <Thermodynamic activities> incorporate the reference state and departures from ideal behavior. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02177[IUPAC Gold Book, equilibrium constant].

In the ideal concentration model for A converting to B, use $a_{\mathrm A}=[\mathrm A]/c^\circ$ and $a_{\mathrm B}=[\mathrm B]/c^\circ$ with the same reference $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm M$. Their ratio gives $Q=[\mathrm B]/[\mathrm A]$ and $K=0.75/0.25=3$. Equality $K=k_f/k_r$ applies to this elementary first-order pair; it is not a universal ratio of two measured <rate constants> for an arbitrary multistep <chemical reaction>.

At a specified constant <temperature>, with consistent <standard states>, the standard molar reaction <Gibbs free energy> is
$$
\Delta_rG^\circ=\sum_i\nu_i\mu_i^\circ=-RT\ln K,
\qquad \Delta_rG=\Delta_rG^\circ+RT\ln Q=RT\ln(Q/K).
$$
Here $\Delta_rG$ is the local slope per <mole> of <extent of reaction> at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, rather than the finite total <Gibbs free energy> change between two mixtures. At equilibrium $\Delta_rG=0$, while $\Delta_rG^\circ$ generally remains nonzero. For $K=3$ at $298\,\mathrm K$, it is about $-2.72\,\mathrm{kJ/mol}$. For positive activities, $Q<K$ favors forward conversion and $Q>K$ favors reverse conversion; neither sign supplies a <reaction rate>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/ec12f084d867a5e5532b12ee99bdc767_MIT5_111F14_Lec18.pdf[MIT 5.111, Lecture 18, reaction free energy and equilibrium].

= Dissociation constant
{parent=Equilibrium constant}
{wiki}
{title2=$K_d$}

= Standard state
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

A standard state is a specified reference state used to define a species' standard <chemical potential> $\mu_i^\circ$. The reference convention, including <temperature>, must be consistent when calculating <thermodynamic activities>, <reaction quotients>, and <equilibrium constants>.

= Thermodynamic activity
{parent=Standard state}
{title2=$a_i=\exp[(\mu_i-\mu_i^\circ)/(RT)]$}

The thermodynamic activity is the dimensionless quantity defined by $\mu_i=\mu_i^\circ+RT\ln a_i$, where $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. For an ideal dilute solution with a specified <concentration> reference, $a_i=c_i/c^\circ$; departures from ideality are represented by an <activity coefficient> multiplying this ratio.

= Activity coefficient
{parent=Thermodynamic activity}
{title2=$\gamma_i$}

= Reaction quotient
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}
{title2=$Q=\prod_i a_i^{\nu_i}$}

For a <chemical reaction> with signed <stoichiometric vector> entries $\nu_i$, positive for products and negative for reactants, the reaction quotient is the product of the species' <thermodynamic activities> raised to these powers. It equals the <equilibrium constant> at <chemical equilibrium>, with all quantities using the same <standard state> convention.

= Photon chemical potential
{parent=Chemical equilibrium}

The chemical potential of photons in thermal equilibrium is zero because their number is not conserved.

= Ideal gas
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

= Perfect gas
{synonym}

An ideal gas is a model of a dilute <gas> whose particle centres move according to <classical mechanics> and occupy negligible <volume (thermodynamics)> compared with their container. Treating the particles as <point masses> removes the molecular-size correction; neglecting interactions between separated particles removes the intermolecular contribution to <potential energy>. The usual <kinetic theory> model retains brief <elastic collisions> with the walls and, optionally, with other particles: these transfer <momentum> while conserving the total <kinetic energy> of the colliding bodies. There is no appreciable interaction <energy> stored between <collisions>. These assumptions lead to the <ideal gas law>, $PV=Nk_BT$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: molecular model and ideal gas law]{ref} https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.2: kinetic-model assumptions]{ref}

The model's attraction is its universality: at the same <temperature> and <number density>, different classical <ideal gases> have the same <pressure>, even when their particle <masses> differ. In <ideal gas pressure from wall collisions>, heavier particles move more slowly at a given <temperature>, and the two effects cancel. For example, one <mole> of any <ideal gas> at $300\,\mathrm K$ in $0.025\,\mathrm{m^3}$ has <pressure> approximately $99.774\,\mathrm{kPa}$, as computed under the <ideal gas law>.

The <ideal gas law> describes the translational contribution to <pressure>; it does not specify all of the <internal energy> or the <heat capacity>. A <monatomic gas> has three classical translational <degrees of freedom>, whereas a <gas> of <molecules> can also store <rotational kinetic energy> and <energy> in vibrational <normal modes>. An <equation of state> alone therefore does not determine how much <heat> a chosen <temperature> increase requires. It also does not select a process: <isothermal expansion> and <adiabatic expansion> need different additional constraints. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-3-heat-capacity-and-equipartition-of-energy[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.3: internal degrees of freedom and heat capacity]{ref}

A <real gas> departs from this approximation when particle size or interactions become important, especially at high <number density> or low <temperature> near a <phase transition>. The <Van der Waals equation> adds such corrections. Independently, at sufficiently low <temperature> or high <number density>, <quantum statistics> can become essential: even particles with negligible interactions need not obey the classical <ideal gas law>. The classical statistical assumption is the <nondegenerate gas> regime, which is distinct from neglecting interactions. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: departures from ideal-gas behavior]{ref} <Statistical Physics by David Tong/3 Quantum Gases>{ref}

= Partial pressure
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}
{title2=$p_i$}

The <partial pressure> of a component of a <gas> <mixture> is the <pressure> it would exert alone at the same <temperature> and total <volume (thermodynamics)>. For an ideal <mixture>, $p_i=n_iRT/V$ and $p=\sum_i p_i$. The <chemical potential of an ideal gas> uses $p_i$ for that component rather than the total <pressure> $p$.

= Molar gas constant
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$R=N_Ak_B$}

The molar gas constant is the product of the <Avogadro constant> and the <Boltzmann constant>. It converts the particle-number form of the <ideal gas law>, $pV=Nk_BT$, into the amount-of-substance form $pV=nRT$, using $N=nN_A$ with $n$ measured in <moles>.

= Ideal gas law
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$PV=Nk_BT=n_{\rm mol}RT$}
{wiki}

The <ideal gas law> is the <equation of state> relating the absolute <pressure> $P$, <volume (thermodynamics)> $V$, particle count $N$, and absolute <thermodynamic temperature> $T$ of a classical <ideal gas>:
$$
PV=Nk_BT=n_{\rm mol}RT,\qquad
N=n_{\rm mol}N_{\mathrm A},\qquad R=N_{\mathrm A}k_B.
$$
Here $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $N_{\mathrm A}$ is the <Avogadro constant>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, and $n_{\rm mol}$ is the <amount of substance> in <moles>. The distinct symbol $n_{\rm mol}$ avoids confusing <amount of substance> with the <number density> $n=N/V$, measured in $\mathrm{m^{-3}}$: the same law can be written $P=nk_BT$. Use absolute <pressure>, relative to a <vacuum>, rather than an unconverted <gauge pressure>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: particle-count and molar forms]{ref}

The exact <SI> definitions of the <Avogadro constant> and <Boltzmann constant> give
$$
R=(6.02214076\times10^{23})(1.380649\times10^{-23})
\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}
=8.31446261815324\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}.
$$
This numerical value is exact; the applicability of the <ideal gas> model to a <real gas> is an approximation. https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole[BIPM, exact Avogadro constant in the mole definition]{ref} https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/kelvin[BIPM, exact Boltzmann constant in the kelvin definition]{ref}

For $n_{\rm mol}=1\,\mathrm{mol}$, $T=300\,\mathrm K$, and $V=0.025\,\mathrm{m^3}$, direct substitution gives
$$
RT=2494.338785445972\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}},\qquad
P=\frac{n_{\rm mol}RT}{V}
=99773.55141783888\,\mathrm{Pa}
\approx99.774\,\mathrm{kPa}.
$$
At fixed <temperature> and particle count, doubling the <volume (thermodynamics)> to $0.050\,\mathrm{m^3}$ halves the <pressure> to approximately $49.887\,\mathrm{kPa}$: this is <Boyle's law>. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> and particle count, doubling the <temperature> from $300\,\mathrm K$ to $600\,\mathrm K$ doubles the <pressure> to approximately $199.547\,\mathrm{kPa}$. These ratios use <kelvins>, not readings in the <degree Celsius> scale: $300\,\mathrm K$ is $26.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $600\,\mathrm K$ is $326.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$.

\Image[ideal-gas-state-slices.png]
{title=Ideal gas law: pressure versus volume at fixed temperatures, and pressure versus absolute temperature at fixed volume}

The curved slices show <Boyle's law> at three <temperatures>; the straight slice shows proportionality to absolute <temperature> at one fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>. They are relations among equilibrium states, rather than a prescribed process history. Compression could hold the <temperature> fixed through <heat transfer>, or raise it during <adiabatic compression>. To calculate a process's <heat> or <work>, supplement the <ideal gas law> with the process constraint, the <first law of thermodynamics>, and the relevant <internal energy> or <heat capacity> information. The graph's extrapolation toward $T=0$ is mathematical; it does not assert that a <real gas> remains classical or gaseous there.

For a stellar <mixture> with fixed <mean molecular weight>, the <ideal gas law> can be written $P=\mathcal R\rho T/\mu$, where $\rho$ is <mass density> and $\mathcal R$ is the <stellar gas constant>. It assumes negligible interaction corrections and a <nondegenerate gas>; excitation or <ionization> can change the particle count and thermodynamic response.

In his 1834 memoir on the motive power of <heat>, Émile Clapeyron combined gas laws into a relation between <pressure>, <volume (thermodynamics)>, and <temperature> while analysing <heat engines> (printed page 164). The equation above uses modern <SI> notation and absolute <thermodynamic temperature>; it is not a transcription of his historical temperature convention. https://www3.nd.edu/~powers/ame.20231/clapeyron1834.pdf[Clapeyron, Mémoire sur la puissance motrice de la chaleur (1834), page 164]{ref}

= Ideal gas pressure from wall collisions
{parent=Ideal gas law}
{title2=$P=Nm\langle v_x^2\rangle/V$}

Consider a particle of <mass> $m$ in a rigid rectangular box of length $L$ along $x$ and wall <surface area> $A$, so $V=AL$ is the <volume (thermodynamics)>. Choose $+x$ toward the right wall. For an incoming normal <velocity> $v_x=v>0$, an <elastic collision> reverses the normal <momentum>:
$$
p_{x,\rm in}=mv,\qquad p_{x,\rm out}=-mv,\qquad
\Delta p_{x,\rm particle}=-2mv,\qquad
J_{x,\rm wall}=+2mv.
$$
The <impulse> delivered to the wall points outward, toward $+x$, by <Newton's third law>. The particle's opposite <momentum> change is not the wall's <impulse>. A tangential <velocity> component is unchanged by the ideal smooth-wall <collision>.

\Image[ideal-gas-wall-momentum.png]
{title=Schematic ideal gas model: an elastic bounce transfers outward momentum to a wall; this is not a real experimental setup}

Ignoring intervening particle-particle <collisions> in this counting model, the particle travels a normal distance $2L$ before hitting the same wall again. Its hit rate is $|v_x|/(2L)$; multiplying by the <impulse> per hit gives the time-averaged normal <force> and its contribution to <pressure>:
$$
\overline F_{\perp,1}=(2m|v_x|)\frac{|v_x|}{2L}
=\frac{mv_x^2}{L},\qquad
P_1=\frac{\overline F_{\perp,1}}{A}=\frac{mv_x^2}{V}.
$$
For example, stipulate $m=4\times10^{-26}\,\mathrm{kg}$, $|v_x|=500\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, $L=0.10\,\mathrm m$, and $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$. Then $V=0.0010\,\mathrm{m^3}$, the <impulse> per hit is $4\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{N\,s}$, the hit rate is $2500\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and $P_1=10^{-17}\,\mathrm{Pa}$. This is one particle's tiny time-averaged contribution to one wall's <pressure>, not an <isotropic> equilibrium <gas> by itself.

For $N$ particles of equal <mass>, add their <force> contributions and define $\langle v_x^2\rangle=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^Nv_{x,i}^2$. In the <gas>'s rest frame, <isotropy> gives $\langle v_x^2\rangle=\langle v_y^2\rangle=\langle v_z^2\rangle=\langle v^2\rangle/3$, where $v^2=v_x^2+v_y^2+v_z^2$. Hence
$$
P=\frac{Nm\langle v_x^2\rangle}{V}
=\frac{Nm\langle v^2\rangle}{3V},\qquad
\frac12m\langle v^2\rangle=\frac32k_BT
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad PV=Nk_BT.
$$
The final equality uses the classical <equipartition theorem>. The <kinetic pressure of an isotropic gas> therefore recovers the <ideal gas law> from <momentum> transfers. Doubling the normal speed would double both the <impulse> per hit and the hit rate, giving four times the <pressure> contribution; <pressure> measures a mean squared speed. Interparticle <elastic collisions> redistribute <velocities> and help establish <isotropy>; the individual round-trip times need not remain $2L/|v_x|$, but the equilibrium <momentum flux> result survives. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-2-pressure-temperature-and-rms-speed[OpenStax University Physics Volume 2, section 2.2: wall-collision pressure derivation]{ref}

= Monatomic gas
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$\gamma=5/3$}

A monatomic gas consists of individual atoms or ions rather than molecules. In the classical dilute ideal-gas regime, its three translational degrees of freedom give $U=3Nk_BT/2$ by <equipartition theorem>. Thus $C_V=3Nk_B/2$, while $C_P-C_V=Nk_B$ follows by differentiating the ideal-gas enthalpy $H=U+PV=5Nk_BT/2$. Consequently the <adiabatic index> is $\gamma=C_P/C_V=5/3$ and the <specific internal energy> is $e=3P/(2\rho)$. The same translational result applies to a fully ionized mixture when the particle numbers are fixed. This excludes additional excitation, ionization and radiation contributions to the heat capacity.

= Mean free path
{parent=Ideal gas}
{title2=$\ell$}

The mean free path is the average distance travelled between <collisions>. For dilute targets with <number density> $n$ and effective <collision cross-section> $\sigma_c$, $\ell\sim1/(n\sigma_c)$; identical-particle conventions introduce factors such as $\sqrt2$.

= Mean free path through randomly oriented disc absorbers
{parent=Mean free path}
{title2=$\ell^{-1}=n_{\rm com}(1+z)^3\pi R_d^2/2$}

For an unclustered population of fully covering circular disc absorbers, the inverse <mean free path> is proper number density times mean projected area. A comoving number density must first be multiplied by $(1+z)^3$. The incidence then constrains the proper radius; covering factor and multiplicity of detectable lines can change the inference.

= Mean molecular weight
{title2=$\mu$}
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}

The mean molecular weight is the average mass per freely moving gas particle in units of the atomic mass unit. A fully ionized hydrogen-helium gas with mass fractions $X,Y$ has $\mu^{-1}=2X+3Y/4$.

= Stellar gas constant
{parent=Mean molecular weight}
{title2=$\mathcal R=k_B/m_u$}

In stellar <ideal gas> equations, the <stellar gas constant> $\mathcal R=k_B/m_u$ converts a dimensionless <mean molecular weight> $\mu$ into the specific thermal relation $P=\mathcal R\rho T/\mu$. Here $m_u$ is the reference atomic mass unit, commonly approximated by the proton mass in simplified hydrogen-helium models. It has units of energy per mass per temperature. It is the molar gas constant divided by the molar mass corresponding to $m_u$, rather than the molar gas constant used with a dimensional molar molecular weight.

= Mean molecular weight per ion
{parent=Mean molecular weight}
{title2=$\mu_i^{-1}=\sum_jX_j/A_j$}

= Ion mean molecular weight
{synonym}

The <mean molecular weight> per ion counts nuclei rather than nuclei plus free electrons: $\rho/(\mu_i m_u)$ is the ion number density. For species with mass fractions $X_j$ and mass numbers $A_j$, $\mu_i^{-1}=\sum_jX_j/A_j$. It is useful when ions provide a thermal <ideal gas> pressure but <electron degeneracy pressure> supplies a separate contribution.

= Mean molecular weight per electron
{title2=$\mu_e=\rho/(m_un_e)$}
{parent=Mean molecular weight}

The mean molecular weight per electron is the mass carried per free electron in units of atomic mass $m_u$. Fully ionized hydrogen has $\mu_e=1$, while helium, carbon, and oxygen have approximately $\mu_e=2$. It converts <electron number density> to bulk <mass density> in the <equation of state of a cold electron gas>.

= Heat capacity ratio
{title2=$\gamma$}
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}

= Specific-heat ratio
{synonym}

= Adiabatic exponent
{synonym}

= Adiabatic index
{synonym}

The heat capacity ratio compares <heat capacity at constant pressure> $C_p$ with <heat capacity at constant volume> $C_V$ at the same equilibrium state and fixed composition:
$$
\gamma=\frac{C_p}{C_V}
=\frac{C_{p,\mathrm m}}{C_{V,\mathrm m}}
=\frac{c_p}{c_V}.
$$
Here $C_p,C_V$ refer to the whole sample, in $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$; <molar heat capacities> $C_{p,\mathrm m}=C_p/n$ and $C_{V,\mathrm m}=C_V/n$ are per <mole>; <specific heat capacities> $c_p=C_p/m$ and $c_V=C_V/m$ are per unit <mass>. The <amount of substance> $n$ or <mass> $m$ cancels only when both capacities use the same normalization. Thus <heat capacity ratio> is dimensionless and independent of sample size. For a fixed-composition <ideal gas> with positive finite $C_V$, <Mayer's relation> gives
$$
\gamma(T)=1+\frac{nR}{C_V(T)}=1+\frac{R}{C_{V,\mathrm m}(T)}>1,
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. More ways to store <internal energy> make a given <temperature> increase require more <heat>, bringing <heat capacity ratio> closer to unity. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node18.html[MIT Unified Engineering, Specific heats: definitions and ideal-gas relations]{ref}

The <equipartition theorem> predicts $C_{V,\mathrm m}=fR/2$ when $f$ independent quadratic terms in molecular <energy> are thermally active and their number stays fixed; consequently $\gamma=1+2/f$. A classical <monatomic gas> with no appreciable electronic excitation has $f=3$, giving $C_{V,\mathrm m}=3R/2$ and $\gamma=5/3$. For an <ideal gas> of diatomic <molecules> with three translational and two rotational terms active, but its vibrational <normal mode> effectively frozen, $f=5$ gives $C_{V,\mathrm m}=5R/2$ and $\gamma=7/5$. A fully classical harmonic vibration adds both <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, giving $f=7$ and $\gamma=9/7$. These are model regimes, not universal constants of every <gas>: rotational freezing, electronic excitation, and changing composition invalidate the simple count.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/2 Classical Gases>[§§2.2.1 and 2.4: equipartition and diatomic gases]{ref}

The mechanical significance of <heat capacity ratio> follows from the <first law of thermodynamics>. For a fixed amount of <ideal gas> in a reversible <adiabatic process>, $C_V(T)\,dT=-p\,dV$. Combining this with the <ideal gas law> gives the local relation
$$
\left(\frac{\partial\ln p}{\partial\ln V}\right)_S=-\gamma(T),\qquad
\int_{T_1}^{T_2}\frac{C_V(T)}{T}\,dT+nR\ln\frac{V_2}{V_1}=0.
$$
The <partial derivative> holds at fixed <entropy> and composition even when <heat capacity ratio> varies with <temperature>. The finite-path formulas $pV^\gamma=\mathrm{constant}$ and $TV^{\gamma-1}=\mathrm{constant}$ require the additional approximation of constant <heat capacity ratio>. A rapid compression also needs internal equilibration to approximate this path; rapid motion alone does not ensure reversibility. The same local response gives <isentropic compressibility> $1/(\gamma p)$ and, for small sound disturbances in this <ideal gas> regime, <speed of sound> $a=\sqrt{\gamma p/\rho}$, where $\rho$ is <mass density>. Thus thermal storage controls mechanical stiffness, sound propagation and the measurements in <Rüchardt experiment> and <Clément-Desormes experiment>.

= Rüchardt experiment
{c}
{parent=Heat capacity ratio}

The Rüchardt experiment infers <heat capacity ratio> from the <period of an oscillation> of a piston supported by trapped <gas>. Use a vertical precision cylinder with a close-fitting movable piston, known moving <mass> $M$, face <area> $A$, trapped equilibrium <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_0$, and local atmospheric <pressure> $p_{\rm atm}$. With negligible static <friction>, <force balance> gives the absolute gas <pressure> $p_0=p_{\rm atm}+Mg/A$, where $g$ is gravitational acceleration. Displace the piston slightly and release it: compression raises <pressure>, and expansion lowers it, producing a restoring <force>. The <oscillation> is a mechanical probe of thermal response, without separately measuring two <heat> inputs. https://groups.ist.utl.pt/wwwelab/wiki/index.php?title=Determination_of_the_Adiabatic_Constant[Instituto Superior Técnico e-lab, Determination of the Adiabatic Constant: piston apparatus and period model]{ref}

Let $x$ be upward <displacement> from equilibrium, so $V=V_0+Ax$. For constant <heat capacity ratio> and a reversible <adiabatic process>, the net upward <force>, including the ambient load and weight, is
$$
F(x)=A p_0\left[\left(1+\frac{Ax}{V_0}\right)^{-\gamma}-1\right]
\simeq-\frac{\gamma p_0A^2}{V_0}x.
$$
The <linear approximation> requires $|Ax|/V_0\ll1$. Applying <Newton's second law> gives <simple harmonic motion> with
$$
\omega_0^2=\frac{\gamma p_0A^2}{MV_0},\qquad
\gamma=\frac{4\pi^2MV_0}{p_0A^2\mathcal T^2},\qquad
\mathcal T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_0}.
$$
Here $\omega_0$ is the undamped <angular frequency> and $\mathcal T$ the undamped <oscillation period>, rather than <temperature>. Record repeated cycles, measure $M,A,V_0,p_{\rm atm}$ independently, and include all connected trapped <volume (thermodynamics)>. An illustrative model with $M=0.020\,\mathrm{kg}$, $A=2.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $V_0=0.010\,\mathrm{m^3}$, $p_{\rm atm}=100000\,\mathrm{Pa}$, $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ and $\gamma=1.4$ gives $p_0=100981\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $\mathcal T\simeq1.182\,\mathrm s$. These stipulated values are not measurements.

\Image[heat-capacity-ratio-gas-spring.png]
{title=<Rüchardt experiment>: <heat capacity ratio> sets the restoring stiffness of trapped <gas>}
{description=Original schematic and analytical <ideal gas> curves. Upward piston <displacement> increases <volume (thermodynamics)>; the blue curve uses constant $\gamma=1.4$, its dashed tangent gives the small-<amplitude> model, and the orange curve is the <isothermal process> limit. The shaded central interval illustrates a small fractional <volume (thermodynamics)> change, without specifying an experimental acceptance threshold. No measured waveform is shown.}

In Shearwood and Sloan's implementation, piston-mounted magnets induce an <electromotive force> in a stationary coil through <Faraday's law of induction>; an oscilloscope records the signal. They observed faster <oscillations> at smaller trapped <volume (thermodynamics)> values and stronger <damping> at small cylinder <volume (thermodynamics)> values. The signal tracks <velocity> locally, so its <amplitude> is not directly piston <displacement>. Calibration also identified inlet dead space beyond the graduated cylinder volume. https://arxiv.org/html/2209.03323v1[Chris Shearwood and Peter A. Sloan, The Rüchardt experiment revisited, §§3–4: signal, dead volume and observations]{ref}

Repeat at smaller initial <amplitudes> to test the <linear approximation>, and compare several $V_0$ values. <Heat transfer> weakens the restoring <force> toward the <isothermal process> limit, biasing the inference toward $1$. Check leakage through resting-piston drift and before-and-after trapped <volume (thermodynamics)> readings. For viscous <damping> with envelope $e^{-t/\tau}$, the measured <angular frequency> obeys $\omega_0^2=\omega_d^2+\tau^{-2}$; this correction does not remove thermal leakage. Internal equilibration must be faster than an <oscillation period>, with <heat transfer> and leakage slow over a run. Large <amplitude> or sticking <friction> require a richer model. https://arxiv.org/html/2209.03323v1[Shearwood and Sloan, §§2.1, 2.4 and 3.2: damping, heat exchange and leakage limitations]{ref}

= Clément-Desormes experiment
{c}
{parent=Heat capacity ratio}

The Clément-Desormes experiment infers <heat capacity ratio> from a rapid <pressure> reduction followed by reheating. Use a rigid bottle, pump or bellows, a quick release valve, and a U-tube <pressure> gauge connected to the bottle and atmosphere. First pump to a small excess <pressure>, close the inlet, and wait for <thermal equilibrium> with the room at $T_0$. Measure $p_1>p_a$, where $p_a$ is local absolute atmospheric <pressure>. Briefly open the release valve until the bottle reaches $p_a$, then close it promptly. The remaining <gas> is cooler than the room. As it warms back to $T_0$ at fixed bottle <volume (thermodynamics)>, its <pressure> rises to $p_3$, with $p_a<p_3<p_1$ in the ideal procedure. The rebound reveals cooling during release. https://labs.physik.kit.edu/prakt-nebenfach-manuals/Experiments/Adiabatenexponent_Luft/[KIT Faculty of Physics, Clément-Desormes laboratory protocol: release and pressure rebound]{ref}

The bottle loses <molecules> while the valve is open: it is not a <closed system> whose geometrical <volume (thermodynamics)> expands. In the well-mixed, negligible-<heat-transfer> discharge model with constant <heat capacity ratio>, the remaining <gas> obeys $T_2/T_0=(p_a/p_1)^{(\gamma-1)/\gamma}$. Subsequent heating at fixed <amount of substance> and bottle <volume (thermodynamics)> gives $p_3/p_a=T_0/T_2$ by the <ideal gas law>. Eliminating the unmeasured $T_2$ yields
$$
\gamma=\frac{\ln(p_1/p_a)}{\ln(p_1/p_3)}
\simeq\frac{\Delta p_1}{\Delta p_1-\Delta p_3}
=\frac{h_1}{h_1-h_3},\qquad
\Delta p_i=p_i-p_a=\rho_{\rm liquid}gh_i.
$$
The <logarithms> use absolute <pressure> ratios. The approximation requires small <gauge pressures> $\Delta p_i\ll p_a$; $h_i$ are signed height differences between the two liquid surfaces, and the final equality uses the same liquid in both readings with <hydrostatic equilibrium>. It is not an instruction to insert gauge readings into the logarithmic formula. For synthetic states generated with $p_a=100000\,\mathrm{Pa}$, $p_1=102000\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $\gamma=1.4$, the model gives $p_3\simeq100567.39\,\mathrm{Pa}$. The logarithmic inference recovers $1.4$; the small-<pressure> approximation gives approximately $1.3961$.

Wait for a stable initial <pressure> after pumping and for a stable final <pressure> after closing. Vary the valve-open interval and initial excess <pressure> across repeated trials: closing too early leaves residual excess <pressure>, while leaving the valve open during warming lets more <gas> escape and suppresses the rebound. Test the seal for leaks, and read both liquid levels consistently. In a variant with incomplete release, measure the actual immediate post-release absolute <pressure> $p_2$ and replace the numerator by $\ln(p_1/p_2)$. The Zurich laboratory manual explains that liquid-column <oscillations> can delay this reading until appreciable reheating has occurred; its proposed backward extrapolation assumes an exponential thermal relaxation and must be checked against the recorded readings. These controls separate a thermal signal from valve timing, instrumental lag and leakage. https://www.physik.uzh.ch/~matthias/espace-assistant/manuals/en/anleitung-cd_e.pdf[University of Zurich, PHY112/122 laboratory manual, §1.3 and Appendix 1.4(a): pressure readings and reheating correction]{ref}

= Nondegenerate gas
{parent=Ideal gas}

A gas is nondegenerate when its occupation numbers are low enough that <quantum statistics> reduce to the <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution>. A common criterion is $n\lambda_{\rm th}^3\ll1$, where $\lambda_{\rm th}$ is the thermal de Broglie wavelength.

For an <ideal gas>, this is the classical statistical regime; nondegeneracy alone does not assert the absence of interactions.

= Internal energy of an ideal gas
{parent=Ideal gas}

For a fixed <amount of substance> and composition of classical <ideal gas>, <internal energy> depends only on <temperature>: $U=U(T,n)$, with no independent <volume (thermodynamics)> dependence. This does not require constant <heat capacity at constant volume>. Between equilibrium endpoints,
$$
dU=C_V(T)\,dT=nC_{V,\mathrm m}(T)\,dT,
\qquad U(T_2)-U(T_1)=n\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_{V,\mathrm m}(T)\,dT.
$$
Here $C_V$ is total <heat capacity at constant volume>, $C_{V,\mathrm m}$ is <molar heat capacity>, and $U/m$ for sample <mass> $m$ is <specific internal energy>, whose change is $\int_{T_1}^{T_2}c_V(T)\,dT$. These endpoint relations apply even when the intervening process is not at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)>; they do not say that transferred <heat> always equals $\Delta U$. With constant $C_V$, the safe statement is $U(T)=U(T_*)+C_V(T-T_*)$. Writing $U=C_VT$ additionally chooses the reference <energy>; it does not prove that a physical <gas> remains classical and ideal down to zero <temperature>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_45.html#Ch45-S1[Richard Feynman, Robert Leighton and Matthew Sands, §45-1: internal-energy derivatives and heat capacity]{ref}

A thermodynamic proof outline uses the <first law of thermodynamics> in equilibrium form, $dU=T\,dS-p\,dV$, and the <Maxwell relation> $(\partial S/\partial V)_T=(\partial p/\partial T)_V$:
$$
\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V}\right)_T
=T\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V-p
=T\frac{nR}{V}-\frac{nRT}{V}=0.
$$
The last step uses the <ideal gas law> and the <molar gas constant> $R$, with fixed composition and <amount of substance>. Microscopically, separated <ideal gas> particles store no intermolecular interaction <energy>; their translational <kinetic energy> and any internal molecular <energy> are determined by <temperature>. Negligible intermolecular interactions still allow a <molecule> to have internal vibrational <potential energy>.

An original analytical model makes the distinction between an <ideal gas> and constant <heat capacity> concrete. Assume classical translation, two fully active rotational terms, and one independent <quantum harmonic oscillator> per diatomic <molecule>, with fixed <angular frequency> $\omega$. Define the vibrational <temperature> scale $\Theta=\hbar\omega/k_B$, using the reduced <Planck constant> $\hbar=h/(2\pi)$ and <Boltzmann constant> $k_B$. Summing its thermal occupations gives
$$
\frac{U(T)-U_*}{nR}=\frac52T+\frac{\Theta}{e^{\Theta/T}-1},\qquad
\frac{C_{V,\mathrm m}(T)}{R}=\frac52+\frac{x^2e^{-x}}{(1-e^{-x})^2},\qquad x=\frac{\Theta}{T}.
$$
The constant $U_*$ includes the chosen reference and <zero-point energy>, whose <temperature> derivative vanishes. When $T\ll\Theta$ the vibrational contribution to <heat capacity> is exponentially small; when $T\gg\Theta$ it tends to $R$ per <mole>. This model assumes rotations remain active throughout the interval and excludes dissociation, electronic excitation and <real gas> corrections. Its low-$T/\Theta$ edge is not a model of rotational freezing or of an actual substance at absolute zero.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/3 Quantum Gases>[§3.4: quantum vibrational spectrum, thermal occupations and zero-point energy]{ref}

\Image[heat-capacity-ratio-vibrational-model.png]
{title=Temperature-dependent <internal energy of an ideal gas>, <molar heat capacities> and <heat capacity ratio> in a vibrational model}
{description=Analytical illustration with active rotations and one <quantum harmonic oscillator> per <molecule>. Left: <Mayer's relation> keeps the capacity difference equal to $R$. Middle: <heat capacity ratio> falls from $7/5$ toward $9/7$. Right: the <internal energy> change from $T=0.2\Theta$ is an <integral> of varying <heat capacity at constant volume>; the dashed line suppresses the vibrational contribution. No measured gas curve or material-specific vibrational frequency is implied.}

For this stipulated model, heating from $T_1=0.2\Theta$ to $T_2=\Theta$ gives $\Delta U/(nR\Theta)\simeq2.57519$. Holding the frozen-vibration value $C_{V,\mathrm m}=5R/2$ throughout would give $2$, underestimating the required <internal energy> increase by about $22.3\%$ of the model's full value. The <integral> measures all thermal storage activated along the interval. At fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> with only <heat> input, this is also the supplied <heat>; at fixed <pressure>, expansion <work> must be added through <Mayer's relation>.

<Joule's free-expansion experiment> tests the independence from <volume (thermodynamics)> in a different way: an insulated rigid vessel allows <gas> into an evacuated compartment, so no external <work> is delivered and ideally no <heat> is exchanged. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives $\Delta U=0$; positive $C_V$ then predicts equal initial and final equilibrium <temperatures> for the <ideal gas>. The apparatus, thermal-capacity controls and limitations of this inference are described in <Joule's free-expansion experiment> and <temperature sensitivity of a free-expansion experiment>. This endpoint test differs from the reversible <adiabatic expansion> of a loaded piston, which delivers <work> and lowers <internal energy>.

= Mayer's relation
{c}
{parent=Ideal gas}
{wiki}

= Mayer relation
{c}
{synonym}

Mayer's relation states that the difference between <heat capacity at constant pressure> and <heat capacity at constant volume> for a fixed-composition <ideal gas> is
$$
C_p(T)-C_V(T)=nR=Nk_B.
$$
Here both capacities are total <heat capacities> in $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$, $n$ is <amount of substance>, $N$ is the number of particles, $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>. In other normalizations the same statement is
$$
C_{p,\mathrm m}(T)-C_{V,\mathrm m}(T)=R,\qquad
c_p(T)-c_V(T)=\frac{nR}{m}.
$$
The first is a difference of <molar heat capacities>, in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$; the second uses <specific heat capacities>, in $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$. Writing $C_p-C_V=R$ with total capacities would omit the sample's <amount of substance>. The relation holds pointwise even when the capacities depend on <temperature>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-5-heat-capacities-of-an-ideal-gas[Samuel J. Ling, William Moebs and Jeff Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §3.5: ideal-gas capacity difference]{ref}

The proof outline is short: <enthalpy> is $H=U+pV=U+nRT$, and <internal energy of an ideal gas> is independent of <volume (thermodynamics)> at fixed <temperature>. Differentiating at fixed composition and <amount of substance> therefore gives $C_p=dH/dT=dU/dT+nR=C_V+nR$. It uses the <ideal gas law>, rather than assuming any particular number of molecular <degrees of freedom>. At fixed <pressure>, the additional <heat> supplies expansion <work>, $p\,dV=nR\,dT$. The two processes can have the same <temperature> endpoints and equal $\Delta U$ while requiring different <heat> inputs.

For one <mole> of the rigid-rotation diatomic <ideal gas> model, $C_V=5R/2$ and $C_p=7R/2$. Heating by $10\,\mathrm K$ with $R\simeq8.31446\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ gives $Q_V\simeq207.86\,\mathrm J$ in a rigid sealed vessel and $Q_p\simeq291.01\,\mathrm J$ under a frictionless piston at fixed <pressure>. Both gain $207.86\,\mathrm J$ of <internal energy>, while the piston delivers approximately $83.14\,\mathrm J$ of <work>. This is a calculated model budget. If the capacities vary with <temperature>, the corresponding finite statement is still
$$
Q_p-Q_V=nR(T_2-T_1),\qquad
Q_V=n\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_{V,\mathrm m}(T)\,dT,
$$
under the fixed-<pressure> and fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> heating assumptions in <heat capacity>. Their final <volume (thermodynamics)> values generally differ. Combining <Mayer's relation> with <heat capacity ratio> also gives $C_V=nR/(\gamma-1)$ and $C_p=\gamma nR/(\gamma-1)$ locally; constant values cannot be extrapolated across intervals in which $\gamma(T)$ changes.

For a smooth stable single <thermodynamic phase> of a general <simple compressible system>, the broader <Maxwell relation> identity is
$$
C_p-C_V=T\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_p
=\frac{TV\alpha^2}{\kappa_T},
$$
where $\alpha$ is the <coefficient of thermal expansion> and $\kappa_T$ the <isothermal compressibility>, with composition fixed throughout. Substituting the <ideal gas law> gives $nR$; a <real gas> need not do so. This distinction matters near a <phase transition> or when interactions appreciably change the <equation of state>. It explains why <Mayer's relation> is useful: thermal measurements under different constraints become linked to a mechanical response, while the difference retains a particularly simple value for an <ideal gas>.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§4.4.2: Maxwell relations and the general heat-capacity difference]{ref}

= Real gas
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A <real gas> departs from the <ideal gas> law because interactions and molecular volume matter. Nonideal <equations of state>, including the <Dieterici equation>, model such departures.

= Dieterici equation
{parent=Real gas}
{c}

The Dieterici equation of state
$$
p=\frac{k_BT}{v-b}\exp\left(-\frac{a}{k_BTv}\right)
$$
models excluded volume through $b$ and attraction through $a$. Its critical point has $v_c=2b$ and $T_c=a/(4bk_B)$.

= Intensive and extensive thermodynamic quantities
{parent=Thermodynamics}

An extensive quantity scales in proportion to the amount of material, while an intensive quantity is unchanged when the system is replicated. Energy, entropy, volume, and particle number are extensive; temperature, pressure, and chemical potential are intensive.

= Extensive quantity
{parent=Intensive and extensive thermodynamic quantities}

= Extensive variable
{synonym}

An extensive quantity scales in proportion to the size of a thermodynamic system: combining $n$ independent identical copies multiplies it by $n$. <Energy>, <entropy> and <volume> are examples.

= Euler relation in thermodynamics
{c}
{parent=Extensive quantity}
{title2=$E=TS-PV+\mu N$}

= Thermodynamic Euler relation
{synonym}

For a single-component equilibrium system with extensive <internal energy> $E(S,V,N)$, scaling its <entropy>, <volume> and particle number together gives $E(\lambda S,\lambda V,\lambda N)=\lambda E(S,V,N)$. The <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> therefore gives $E=S\partial_SE+V\partial_VE+N\partial_NE$. The <first law of thermodynamics> identifies these derivatives as $T,-P,\mu$, respectively, proving $E=TS-PV+\mu N$. Dividing by volume yields the <entropy density> formula $s=(\rho+P-\mu n)/T$. This step fixes the entropy normalization rather than merely identifying its differential. Differentiating the Euler relation and subtracting $dE=T\,dS-P\,dV+\mu\,dN$ gives the <Gibbs-Duhem equation> $S\,dT-V\,dP+N\,d\mu=0$. The argument requires extensivity; it is not an assumption about arbitrary nonadditive systems.

= Gibbs-Duhem equation
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki=Gibbs–Duhem_equation}

The <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> applied to an <extensive quantity>, together with $dE=T\,dS-p\,dV+\mu\,dN$ give
$$
E=TS-pV+\mu N,
\qquad
S\,dT-V\,dp+N\,d\mu=0.
$$

= Conjugate variables
{disambiguate=thermodynamics}
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

= Thermodynamic conjugate variable
{synonym}

Two thermodynamic variables are conjugate when their product has units of energy and they occur as a paired term in a thermodynamic differential, such as pressure and volume or tension and length.

= First law of thermodynamics
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$dU=\delta Q-\delta W$}
{wiki}

The first law applies <conservation of energy> to a thermodynamic system. For a <closed system> with unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, write
$$
dU=\delta Q-\delta W,\qquad \Delta U=Q-W.
$$
Here $U$ is <internal energy>, $Q>0$ is <heat> entering the system and $W>0$ is <work> done by the system on its surroundings. Compression therefore gives negative $W$. Some references define <work> positive when done on the system; their plus sign represents the same balance with a different convention. If bulk <kinetic energy> or <potential energy> changes, include it in the total <energy> balance. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node15.html[MIT, First law and work sign convention]{ref}

Supplying $1000\,\mathrm J$ of <heat> while a gas does $300\,\mathrm J$ of <work> increases its <internal energy> by $700\,\mathrm J$. Compressing it with $200\,\mathrm J$ of input <work> in an <adiabatic process> instead gives $Q=0$, $W=-200\,\mathrm J$ and $\Delta U=200\,\mathrm J$.

The distinction between $d$ and $\delta$ records state versus path: <internal energy> is a <state function>, whereas <heat> and <work> depend on the process. For an <ideal gas> expanding reversibly at constant <temperature>, $W=Nk_BT\log(V_2/V_1)$ and $Q=W$, since <internal energy of an ideal gas> depends only on <temperature>. Choose $Nk_BT=1000\,\mathrm J$ and $V_2=2V_1$: then $Q=W=693.15\,\mathrm J$. An insulated <free expansion> into vacuum between the same equilibrium endpoints has $Q=W=0$. Both have $\Delta U=0$, yet their transfers differ. Their common <entropy> increase is $Nk_B\log2$. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node40.html[MIT, Ideal-gas entropy differences]{ref}

For a simple compressible equilibrium system of fixed composition, combining the laws gives
$$
dU=T\,dS-p\,dV.
$$
Here $p$ is <pressure> and $V$ is <volume>. This identity relates neighboring equilibrium states. Identifying $T\,dS$ with actual <heat> and $p\,dV$ with actual <work> requires a suitable <reversible thermodynamic process>; an irreversible piston calculation uses the external resisting <pressure> for boundary <work>. This distinction prevents applying a <reversible ideal-gas adiabat> to an <irreversible adiabatic piston compression>.

= Adiabatic process
{parent=First law of thermodynamics}
{wiki}

An <adiabatic process> has no <heat transfer> across the chosen boundary throughout the process: $\delta Q=0$, rather than merely zero net <heat> after incoming and outgoing transfers cancel. It is a condition on <energy> transfer, whereas an <isothermal process> holds <temperature> constant. A sealed cylinder can approach an <adiabatic process> through insulation or through motion short compared with the time needed for appreciable <heat transfer>. Its <temperature> can nevertheless change because <work> crosses the boundary.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§§4.2–4.3: work, heat, and adiabatic legs of a cycle]{ref}

Take the trapped <gas> as a <closed system>, with fixed <amount of substance> and composition and no net change in bulk <kinetic energy> or <potential energy> between the endpoints. Define $Q_{\rm in}>0$ as <heat> entering and $W_{\rm out}>0$ as <work> delivered to the surroundings. The <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
\Delta U=Q_{\rm in}-W_{\rm out},\qquad
Q_{\rm in}=0\ \Longrightarrow\ \Delta U=-W_{\rm out}.
$$
Here $U$ is <internal energy>. An <adiabatic compression> normally supplies <work>, so $W_{\rm out}<0$; an <adiabatic expansion> against a resisting load delivers <work>, so $W_{\rm out}>0$. An <adiabatic process> need not preserve <internal energy> and need not make the <closed system> an <isolated system>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node19.html[MIT Unified Engineering, §2.5.2: heat inward and work outward in the first-law balance]{ref}

For a <reversible thermodynamic process>, $dS=\delta Q_{\rm rev}/T$ at positive absolute <temperature> $T$. A reversible <adiabatic process> therefore has $dS=0$: it is an <isentropic process>. Irreversible <adiabatic processes> can instead generate <entropy>; <free expansion> is the existing counterexample, with its calculation in <entropy change in free expansion>. Conversely, constant <entropy> alone does not certify an <adiabatic process> or reversibility: outward <heat> can export the <entropy> generated internally. For a <closed system> the macroscopic <entropy> balance is
$$
\Delta S=\int\frac{\delta Q_{\rm in}}{T_b}+S_{\rm gen},\qquad S_{\rm gen}\geq0,
$$
where $T_b$ is the absolute boundary <temperature> where each <heat> transfer occurs and $S_{\rm gen}$ is internal <entropy production>. With no <heat transfer>, this reduces to $\Delta S=S_{\rm gen}$; reversibility makes $S_{\rm gen}=0$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/4-6-entropy[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §4.6: reversible heat and irreversible entropy increase]{ref}

A motivating experiment measures the <gas> <temperature> directly during compression and expansion, instead of inferring it from how warm a cylinder feels. PASCO's authored TD-8565 manual describes a sealed piston cylinder, a fine nickel-wire <thermometer>, an absolute <pressure> sensor and a position readout for <volume (thermodynamics)>. A computer records all three channels together. The wire's <electrical resistance> changes with <temperature>; its small size permits a rapid response. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Adiabatic-Gas-Law-Apparatus-TD-8565-Manual.pdf[PASCO scientific, Adiabatic Gas Law Apparatus, guide 012-05110D, pp. 1–2: apparatus and readouts]{ref}

Use ordinary trapped air and the apparatus's designed travel stops; mount the base securely, close the valve during a stroke, and record the actual <volume (thermodynamics)> limits. John Hart's university demonstration uses a large transparent cylinder with a low-<heat capacity> <thermocouple>, a <pressure> sensor, a travel gauge and a safety stop. It explicitly keeps the release valve closed during the demonstration. The basic observable is a rise in <temperature> on compression and a fall on loaded expansion, alongside the corresponding <pressure> and <volume (thermodynamics)> changes. Hart discusses wall <thermal conduction>, sensor response and leakage as reasons real records differ from the reversible model. https://atoc.colorado.edu/TeachingandLearning/Demonstrations/AdiabaticExpansion/Adiabatic_expansion.htm[John Hart, University of Colorado (2000), Adiabatic Expansion and Gas Law Physics: sensor demonstration and limitations]{ref}

For a proposed rapid-versus-slow comparison, let the air and walls return to the same initial <thermal equilibrium> before each run, then compare the same starting and finishing <volume (thermodynamics)> values with different stroke durations. Read <temperature>, absolute <pressure> and position continuously, including the hold after motion stops. A rapid compression gives a temporary <temperature> rise; holding the smaller <volume (thermodynamics)> lets <heat> leave and the <temperature> approach the surroundings again. Slow compression in good thermal contact allows that <heat transfer> during the stroke and approaches an <isothermal compression>. Loaded expansion reverses the signs: the rapid stroke cools, while <heat> entering during slow motion reduces the cooling. PASCO explicitly supports rapid nearly <adiabatic processes> and slow <isothermal processes>; the matched-run controls here are a proposed extension, not a reported set of measurements. https://www.pasco.com/products/lab-apparatus/thermodynamics/gas-laws/td-8565[PASCO scientific, TD-8565 product documentation: simultaneous readouts and adiabatic/isothermal comparisons]{ref}

Insulation suppresses <thermal conduction>; it does not prove that <heat transfer> vanishes. As proposed controls, repeat the matched strokes with and without a removable insulating jacket, record room and wall <temperatures>, measure fixed-position drift, and check valve closure and seal leakage. Calibrate the <thermometer> and position readout independently, including any connected dead <volume (thermodynamics)>; account for the <heat capacity> of the probe rather than treating it as a passive observer. Hart explicitly identifies the small air sample relative to the probe and walls, sensor inertia and seal leakage as limitations. A fast display cannot eliminate those physical effects. https://atoc.colorado.edu/TeachingandLearning/Demonstrations/AdiabaticExpansion/Adiabatic_expansion.htm[John Hart, Adiabatic Expansion and Gas Law Physics: wall, probe and seal limitations]{ref}

There are two distinct timing requirements. Let $\tau_{\rm eq}$ describe internal equilibration, $\tau_{\rm probe}$ the <thermometer> response, $t_{\rm move}$ the stroke duration and $\tau_{\rm heat}$ the effective <heat transfer> <time constant>. A useful sufficient window for a resolved, nearly reversible <adiabatic process> is
$$
\tau_{\rm eq},\tau_{\rm probe}\ll t_{\rm move}\ll\tau_{\rm heat},
$$
with negligible <friction>. The left inequality permits a <quasistatic process> and a usable readout; the right limits <heat> leakage. A violent stroke may satisfy only the right inequality. PASCO reports a roughly $30$–$50\,\mathrm{ms}$ lag of its <temperature> channel behind the other channels, so apparent simultaneous samples require care. If no such window exists, analyze equilibrium endpoints with the <first law of thermodynamics>, and regard the path as approximate. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Adiabatic-Gas-Law-Apparatus-TD-8565-Manual.pdf[PASCO scientific, guide 012-05110D, p. 4: temperature-sensor lag]{ref}

\Image[adiabatic-processes-sensor-and-timescales.png]
{title=Direct <temperature> readout and a <heat transfer> <time constant> in an <adiabatic process> experiment}
{description=Original apparatus schematic and analytical fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> relaxation model, not measured records. After a stroke and internal equilibration, the stipulated <Newton's law of cooling> model gives $[T(t)-T_b]/|T(0)-T_b|=\pm e^{-t/\tau_{\rm heat}}$. The positive and negative curves illustrate the holds after compression and loaded expansion. The drawing is a generic sensor arrangement, not a reproduction of a commercial device; it distinguishes the small probe from the walls and their possible <heat transfer>.}

The diagram's model is deliberately a hold after the stroke, when boundary <work> is zero. For constant whole-sample <heat capacity at constant volume> $C_V$ and effective thermal conductance $K>0$, it assumes
$$
C_V\frac{dT}{dt}=-K(T-T_b),\qquad
\tau_{\rm heat}=\frac{C_V}{K},\qquad
T(t)-T_b=[T(0)-T_b]e^{-t/\tau_{\rm heat}}.
$$
This is <Newton's law of cooling> with one uniform <gas> <temperature> and a fixed surrounding <temperature>; actual wall and probe dynamics can require more than one <time constant>. At $t/\tau_{\rm heat}=0.1$, the fraction of the initial <temperature> anomaly erased is $1-e^{-0.1}\simeq0.0952$; at one <time constant> it is about $0.6321$. These are analytical fractions, not experimental tolerances or evidence that the preceding stroke was reversible. Measuring the hold helps estimate <heat> leakage independently of the motion.

The usefulness of an <adiabatic process> is that it separates mechanical <work> from <heat transfer>. Its reversible limit connects <temperature> changes, mechanical stiffness and <entropy> in one model: <Rüchardt experiment> and <Clément-Desormes experiment> already use that connection to measure <heat capacity ratio>. The contrast with <free expansion> explains the deeper point: suppressing <heat> does not determine how much <work> is exchanged or how much <entropy> is produced.

= Adiabatic compression
{parent=Adiabatic process}

<Adiabatic compression> decreases the <volume (thermodynamics)> of a sample without <heat transfer> across its boundary. For the fixed-composition <ideal gas> and endpoint assumptions in <adiabatic process>, compression <work> increases <internal energy>; positive <heat capacity at constant volume> then means a higher final <temperature>. The effect comes from supplied <work>, even though no <heat> enters. The amount of <temperature> increase depends on the <work> actually delivered to the <gas>, not on the <adiabatic process> condition alone.

The <reversible ideal-gas adiabat> supplies a particularly useful quantitative benchmark. Assume a fixed <amount of substance> $n$, a <quasistatic process>, negligible <friction>, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, and constant <heat capacity ratio> $\gamma=C_p/C_V>1$. Here $C_p$ and $C_V$ are the whole sample's <heat capacity at constant pressure> and <heat capacity at constant volume>. With $R$ the <molar gas constant>, <Mayer's relation> gives $C_V=nR/(\gamma-1)$. The proof outline is $C_V\,dT=-p\,dV$ from the <first law of thermodynamics>, followed by $p=nRT/V$ from the <ideal gas law> and integration. The result is
$$
pV^\gamma=\text{constant},\qquad TV^{\gamma-1}=\text{constant}.
$$
These finite power laws belong to this reversible, constant-$\gamma$ <ideal gas> model. If <heat capacity ratio> varies, use the integral relation in <heat capacity ratio>; if the compression is irreversible, use the actual <work> balance, as in <irreversible adiabatic piston compression>. A short stroke by itself justifies neither power law. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/3-6-adiabatic-processes-for-an-ideal-gas[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, §3.6: quasi-static ideal-gas adiabat]{ref}

Writing $r=V_1/V_2>1$ for the compression ratio, the reversible model predicts
$$
\frac{T_2}{T_1}=r^{\gamma-1},\qquad
\frac{p_2}{p_1}=r^\gamma,\qquad
W_{\rm in}=-W_{\rm out}=C_V(T_2-T_1)
=\frac{p_1V_1}{\gamma-1}\left(r^{\gamma-1}-1\right).
$$
All <temperatures> in these ratios are absolute. The larger <pressure> rise than in an <isothermal compression>, where $p_2/p_1=r$, explains the greater local stiffness described by <isentropic compressibility>.

For an original stipulated example, set $\gamma=7/5$, $p_1V_1=nRT_1=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $r=2$. Then $T_2/T_1=2^{0.4}\simeq1.31951$ and $p_2/p_1=2^{1.4}\simeq2.63902$. The input <work> and <internal energy> increase are both $0.79877\,\mathrm{kJ}$, while $Q_{\rm in}=0$. No initial <temperature> or apparatus performance is implied by the chosen <energy> scale. Independent integration gives the signed outward <work>
$$
W_{\rm out}=\int_{V_1}^{V_2}p\,dV
=\frac{p_2V_2-p_1V_1}{1-\gamma},
$$
which is negative during compression and agrees with $-\Delta U$. This <integral> uses equilibrium gas <pressure> only because the path is reversible.

\Image[adiabatic-processes-work-and-temperature.png]
{title=Reversible <adiabatic compression> and <adiabatic expansion>: <temperature> changes balance signed <work>}
{description=Original analytical <ideal gas> model with constant $\gamma=1.4$ and common initial state $p_1V_1=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The two runs go from $V_1$ separately to $V_1/2$ and $2V_1$; they are not a compression followed by its reversal. Shaded <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> areas give <work> magnitudes, with signs supplied by the direction of motion. The dashed <isothermal process> lines are comparisons, not additional <adiabatic processes>. The right panel displays $\Delta U=-W_{\rm out}$ for each run. No curves are measured data.}

In the sensor experiment of <adiabatic process>, compare the <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <integral> with $C_V\Delta T$ as an <energy> check only when internal <pressure> is sufficiently uniform and piston dynamics permit that boundary-<work> estimate. Account for <friction> separately before comparing it with hand input <work>. PASCO's manual supplies a compression procedure and an illustrative logarithmic-slope result $1.39$, compared with the model value $1.40$ for a diatomic <gas>; that is its sample result, not new measurements here. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Adiabatic-Gas-Law-Apparatus-TD-8565-Manual.pdf[PASCO scientific, guide 012-05110D, pp. 5–6: work comparison and sample fitted exponent]{ref}

= Adiabatic expansion
{parent=Adiabatic process}

<Adiabatic expansion> increases the accessible <volume (thermodynamics)> without <heat transfer>. In a loaded piston expansion of the fixed-composition <ideal gas> considered in <adiabatic process>, positive outward <work> comes from <internal energy>, so the final <temperature> decreases. This provides a cooling mechanism without removing <heat> during the stroke. In the <Carnot cycle>, reversible <adiabatic expansion> connects two different <temperatures> between its <isothermal processes>.<Statistical Physics by David Tong/4 Classical Thermodynamics>[§4.3.1: adiabatic expansion between the isothermal legs]{ref}

For the same constant-$\gamma$ <reversible ideal-gas adiabat>, put $b=V_2/V_1>1$. The scaling and <first law of thermodynamics> give
$$
\frac{T_2}{T_1}=b^{1-\gamma},\qquad
\frac{p_2}{p_1}=b^{-\gamma},\qquad
W_{\rm out}=C_V(T_1-T_2)
=\frac{p_1V_1}{\gamma-1}\left(1-b^{1-\gamma}\right)>0.
$$
With the figure's stipulated $\gamma=1.4$, $p_1V_1=1\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $b=2$, the final ratios are $T_2/T_1\simeq0.75786$ and $p_2/p_1\simeq0.37893$. Outward <work> is $0.60535\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $\Delta U=-0.60535\,\mathrm{kJ}$. Reversing this particular path from its cooled endpoint would require the same <work> input; the figure's separate compression starts at the original state, so its input is different. Endpoint <entropy> supplies another independent check:
$$
\Delta S=C_V\ln\frac{T_2}{T_1}+nR\ln\frac{V_2}{V_1}=0.
$$
The two <logarithm> terms cancel using $C_V(\gamma-1)=nR$, confirming the reversible path is an <isentropic process>. https://web.mit.edu/16.unified/www/FALL/thermodynamics/notes/node40.html[MIT Unified Engineering, §5.4: ideal-gas endpoint entropy and constant-entropy scaling]{ref}

PASCO's expansion demonstration first compresses trapped air and waits for <thermal equilibrium> before the rapid expansion. Its manual warns that <friction>-warmed cylinder regions contact the <gas> during expansion, compromising quantitative comparison. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Adiabatic-Gas-Law-Apparatus-TD-8565-Manual.pdf[PASCO scientific, guide 012-05110D, p. 3: expansion preparation and frictional wall-heating limitation]{ref} The hold separates cooling caused by expansion from simply undoing the earlier hot compression. As proposed controls, observe the <temperature> dip and subsequent warming, compare rapid and slow strokes from matched initial states, and assess wall <heat transfer> and probe lag before fitting a reversible model.

Cooling is not a universal consequence of <adiabatic expansion>: <free expansion> of an <ideal gas> has no outward <work> and returns to the same equilibrium <temperature>. Its <entropy> increases, so the reversible power laws do not describe it. Reuse <free expansion>, <Joule's free-expansion experiment> and <temperature sensitivity of a free-expansion experiment> for that apparatus, its <energy> budget and the real-gas qualifications. The useful distinction is the load: expansion cools this <ideal gas> when it must spend <internal energy> doing <work>.

= Isentropic process
{parent=Adiabatic process}
{wiki}

An isentropic process keeps the <entropy> constant. Every reversible <adiabatic process> is isentropic, while an irreversible adiabatic process can increase entropy.

= Reversible ideal-gas adiabat
{parent=Adiabatic process}

For a fixed amount of ideal gas with constant heat capacities, the first law and $\delta Q=0$ give
$$
TV^{\gamma-1}=\text{constant},
\qquad
pV^\gamma=\text{constant},
$$
where $\gamma=C_p/C_V$.

= Enthalpy
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{title2=$H=U+pV$}
{wiki}

Enthalpy is the <state function> obtained by adding <pressure> times <volume (thermodynamics)> to <internal energy>:
$$
H=U+pV.
$$
Here $U$ denotes <internal energy>, also written $E$ in some references. Like <internal energy>, <enthalpy> is measured in <joules> and is an <extensive variable>. Its definition packages the <internal energy> change and expansion <work> into one useful quantity for fixed-<pressure> calculations. It does not define a body's stored <heat>. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02141/plain[IUPAC Gold Book, enthalpy]{ref}

For a fixed amount and composition of a <simple compressible system>, the relation between neighboring equilibrium states is
$$
dH=T\,dS+V\,dp.
$$
Here $T$ is absolute <temperature> and $S$ is <entropy>. Thus <entropy> and <pressure> are the natural variables of this <thermodynamic potential>, with <partial derivatives> $T=(\partial H/\partial S)_p$ and $V=(\partial H/\partial p)_S$. Fixed composition matters: changing composition adds further terms. The formula follows in one line from the equilibrium <first law of thermodynamics> identity:
$$
dH=dU+p\,dV+V\,dp=(T\,dS-p\,dV)+p\,dV+V\,dp=T\,dS+V\,dp.
$$
The cancellation of $p\,dV$ is the reason <enthalpy> replaces <volume (thermodynamics)> with <pressure> as a natural variable. This identity concerns equilibrium states; it does not identify $T\,dS$ with actual irreversible <heat> transfer. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S4#S4.SS4.SSS1[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 4.4.1: enthalpy]{ref}

For a <closed system> undergoing a <quasistatic process> at constant <pressure>, require fixed amount and composition, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work>, no <friction>, and unchanged bulk <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>. Then the actual <first law of thermodynamics> balance gives
$$
\delta Q=dU+p\,dV=dH-V\,dp,\qquad
dp=0\ \Longrightarrow\ Q_p=\Delta H.
$$
The symbol $Q_p$ means transferred <heat> for this fixed-<pressure> process. If the piston example in <heat> additionally meets these conditions, its $20\,\mathrm{kJ}$ of incoming <heat> equals $\Delta H$, while $\Delta U=15\,\mathrm{kJ}$ and $p\Delta V=W=5\,\mathrm{kJ}$. The example shows exactly what <enthalpy> adds to <internal energy>: the expansion <work> required at the imposed <pressure>.

Within a single <thermodynamic phase>, <heat capacity at constant pressure> gives
$$
C_p=\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial T}\right)_p,\qquad
\Delta H=\int_{T_1}^{T_2}C_p(T)\,dT
$$
with the amount and composition fixed. Constant $C_p$ gives $\Delta H=C_p\Delta T$; dividing by <mass> gives <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>. At equilibrium <melting>, <enthalpy> can instead change through <latent heat> while <temperature> stays fixed. The <sensible heat> example therefore adds two single-<thermodynamic phase> <enthalpy> changes and one <latent heat> contribution.

The usefulness of <enthalpy> lies in matching the imposed constraint: a fixed-<pressure> experiment naturally reports $\Delta H$, while fixed-<volume (thermodynamics)> heating with no other <work> reports $\Delta U$. Neither equality is universal. Additional <work> or changing <pressure> alters the relation between supplied <heat> and $\Delta H$; irreversible <entropy> production also prevents identifying actual $\delta Q$ with $T\,dS$. The separate <Joule-Thomson coefficient> branch uses <enthalpy> to describe throttling under its own assumptions.

= Joule-Thomson coefficient
{c}
{parent=Enthalpy}
{title2=$\mu=(\partial T/\partial p)_H$}

The temperature change per pressure change at fixed enthalpy is $\mu=(\partial T/\partial p)_H=[T(\partial V/\partial T)_p-V]/C_p$. A positive coefficient means cooling under an adiabatic throttle pressure drop.

= Virial criterion for Joule-Thomson cooling
{parent=Joule-Thomson coefficient}

For $p=k_BT[n+B_2(T)n^2]$, the coefficient is $\mu=N[TB_2'-B_2]/[C_p(1+2B_2n)]$. On the stable dilute branch it is positive precisely when $d(B_2/T)/dT>0$.

= Specific enthalpy
{title2=$h$}
{parent=Enthalpy}

Specific enthalpy is <enthalpy> per unit mass. For a simple compressible fluid of fixed composition, the <first law of thermodynamics> gives
$$
dh=T\,ds+\frac{dp}{\rho},
$$
where $T$ is <temperature>, $s$ is <specific entropy>, $p$ is <pressure>, and $\rho$ is <mass density>. Along an <isentropic flow>, $dh=dp/\rho$. For a <perfect gas> with constant <specific-heat ratio> $\gamma$, $h=\gamma p/[(\gamma-1)\rho]$.

= Polytropic enthalpy
{parent=Specific enthalpy}
{title2=$Q=(n+1)p/\rho$}

For $p=K\rho^{1+1/n}$ with $K,n>0$, the specific <enthalpy> relative to vacuum is $Q=(n+1)K\rho^{1/n}=(n+1)p/\rho$. It obeys $\nabla Q=\nabla p/\rho$ and $nD_tQ=-Q\nabla\cdot\mathbf u$. The vacuum reference is fixed when these equations are combined with the <polytropic equation of state>.

= Enthalpy Maxwell relation
{parent=Enthalpy}

Since $T=(\partial H/\partial S)_p$ and $V=(\partial H/\partial p)_S$, equality of mixed derivatives gives $(\partial T/\partial p)_S=(\partial V/\partial S)_p$.

= Heat capacity at constant pressure
{parent=Enthalpy}
{title2=$C_p=(\partial H/\partial T)_p$}

= Constant-pressure heat capacity
{synonym}

For a fixed-composition <closed system>, the <heat capacity> at fixed <pressure> is $C_p=(\partial H/\partial T)_p$, with <enthalpy> $H$. Under the <quasistatic process> assumptions in <heat capacity>, only <pressure>-<volume (thermodynamics)> <work> is permitted, so $dp=0$ gives $\delta Q=dH=C_p\,dT$. Thus supplied <heat> is $Q=\Delta H$, or $Q=C_p\Delta T$ within a single <thermodynamic phase> when $C_p$ is constant. For an <ideal gas>, the expansion <work> explains why this exceeds the required <heat> at fixed <volume (thermodynamics)> for the same <temperature> increase. Its per-unit-<mass> counterpart is <specific heat capacity at constant pressure>.

= Difference between constant-pressure and constant-volume heat capacities
{title2=$C_P-C_V$}
{parent=Heat capacity at constant pressure}

For a simple compressible system,
$$
C_P-C_V
=T\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_P
\left(\frac{\partial P}{\partial T}\right)_V.
$$
This follows by expressing the entropy as a function of temperature and volume and using a Maxwell relation.

= Diatomic ideal-gas enthalpy
{parent=Thermodynamics}

With translational and rotational modes active but vibration frozen, a diatomic ideal gas has $E=5Nk_BT/2$ and $H=7Nk_BT/2$.

= Irreversible adiabatic piston compression
{parent=Thermodynamics}

After a sudden increase to constant external pressure $p_1$, an insulated gas obeys $\Delta E=p_1(V_0-V_1)$ rather than a reversible adiabatic power law.

= Constant-pressure heating of an ideal gas
{parent=Thermodynamics}

At fixed pressure, $Q=\Delta H$ and the expansion work is $p\Delta V=Nk_B\Delta T$.

= Thermodynamic cycle
{parent=Thermodynamics}
{wiki}

A thermodynamic cycle returns a working substance to its initial state. Its net internal-energy change is zero, so the net work output equals net heat input.

= Thermal efficiency
{title2=$\eta=W/Q_{\rm in}$}
{parent=Thermodynamic cycle}
{wiki}

The thermal efficiency of a heat engine is the net work output divided by heat input. For a cycle absorbing $Q_{\rm in}$ and rejecting the positive amount $Q_{\rm out}$,
$$
\eta=1-\frac{Q_{\rm out}}{Q_{\rm in}}.
$$

= Otto cycle
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamic cycle}
{wiki}

The ideal Otto cycle has adiabatic compression, constant-volume heat addition, adiabatic expansion, and constant-volume heat rejection. For an ideal gas with compression ratio $r=V_1/V_2$ and specific-heat ratio $\gamma$,
$$
\eta=1-\frac1{r^{\gamma-1}}.
$$

= Diesel cycle
{c}
{parent=Thermodynamic cycle}
{wiki}

The ideal Diesel cycle consists of reversible adiabatic compression, constant-pressure heat addition, reversible adiabatic expansion, and constant-volume heat rejection.
